function heilfastenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Heilfasten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">A nine-day therapeutic fast can be viewed as a transformative experience within the process model of the Enneagram, interacting in various ways with the nine Enneagram principles. Each day of fasting carries the distinct quality of another principle.</p>
      <img src="../assets/heilfasten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Neuntägige Fastenerfahrung im Rahmen des Enneagramm-Prozessmodells – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">1st Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type One</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Strict self-discipline and focus on the right path. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Concentration on adhering to fasting rules and self-criticism upon deviations. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Pride in the decision to fast, accompanied by inner tension. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> Initial fatigue and mild headache, combined with a sense of determination.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">2nd Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Two</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Empathy and concern for others. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Considering how fasting improves one's own well-being and benefits others. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Joy and satisfaction in helping others by setting an example, but also mild self-sacrifice. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> Mild physical weakness and hunger pangs, accompanied by a desire for social interaction.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">3rd Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Three</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Motivation and goal orientation. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Focus on the positive results of fasting and personal efficiency. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Pride in progress, possibly with a touch of impatience. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> Improved energy and concentration, but also possible insomnia.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">4th Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Four</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Deep reflection and emotionality. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Engaging with one's own feelings and the meaning of fasting. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Intense emotional highs and lows, accompanied by creativity and self-reflection. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> Fluctuating energy levels, feelings of cold or warmth.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">5th Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Five</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Intellectual clarity and withdrawal. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Analyzing physical and psychological changes during fasting. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Detached calm, possibly combined with inner retreat. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> Stabilization of energy levels, mental clarity, and reduced hunger pangs.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">6th Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Six</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Concern and vigilance. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Doubts and safety concerns regarding the health effects of fasting. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Ambivalence between trust and anxiety, accompanied by a need for support. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> Mild nervousness, possibly digestive discomfort. <em>From day 6 onward, the body breaks down 95% fat through autophagy &ndash; compared to 50% fat and 50% protein during the first 5 days.</em></p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">7th Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Seven</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Optimism and joy. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Planning activities and enjoying the fasting experience. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Euphoria and enthusiasm, but also a tendency toward distraction. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> High energy and urge to move, occasional restlessness.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">8th Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Eight</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Determination and strength. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Focusing on control and endurance. <strong>Feelings:</strong> A sense of power and superiority, possibly linked with aggressiveness. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> High physical energy and resilience.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">9th Day of Fasting &ndash; Enneagram Principle of Type Nine</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>State of mind:</strong> Serenity and acceptance. <strong>Thoughts:</strong> Harmony and peace through the fasting experience. <strong>Feelings:</strong> Inner calm and contentment, possibly mild disinterest. <strong>Bodily sensations:</strong> Stable energy levels and well-being, overall balanced.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Day 10 &ndash; Breaking the Fast</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Breaking the fast traditionally takes place by eating an apple &ndash; a quiet, conscious moment of beginning anew. After nine days of an inner journey through all Enneagram principles, the faster returns to everyday life, tuned anew.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> A fasting experience within the framework of the 27 subtypes of the Enneagram can express itself in a corresponding, intensified, or even opposite way depending on the subtype variant (Normal Type, Reinforcing Type, Countertype). A nine-day fast can be an individually distinct, yet universally transformative experience for every Enneagram type. Nevertheless, within the process model of the Enneagram, there are 9 universal experiences during fasting &ndash; independent of the respective type or subtype. The author experienced this 9-day &bdquo;Enneagram fast&ldquo; himself directly before creating this overview during a therapeutic fast from August 1, 2024 to August 9, 2024.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"psychologisches-abwehrverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Psychological Defense Behavior"},
        {route:"psychologische-verhaltensmuster-der-9-typen", label:"Psychological Behavioral Patterns"},
        {route:"essverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Eating Behavior of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function psychologischeVerhaltensmuster9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Psychologische Verhaltensmuster der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">These psychological behavioral patterns are deeply rooted in the personality and influence how each of the 9 types thinks, feels, and acts. The circle diagram assigns the types to their three triads: <strong>Gut Triad</strong> (Types 8, 9, 1), <strong>Heart Triad</strong> (Types 2, 3, 4), and <strong>Head Triad</strong> (Types 5, 6, 7).</p>
      <img src="../assets/psychologische-verhaltensmuster-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Psychologische Verhaltensmuster der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Obsessive-Compulsive (Gut Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 has a strong tendency to want to do everything right and without errors &mdash; a compulsive urge for order that is deeply rooted in the gut center. They tend to criticize themselves and others when their own high standards are not met. Behind this lies a strong need for self-control and morally correct action &mdash; the inner judge never sleeps.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Dependent-Histrionic (Heart Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 feels drawn to people who need support and often offers them help &mdash; driven by a deep longing for redemption. They expect gratitude and emotional recognition for the help provided and may neglect their own needs in order to be there for others. The heart center of Type 2 ceaselessly seeks validation through giving.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Impatient, Ambitious Workaholic (Heart Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 has a strong need for recognition and status through achievement. They adapt easily to different situations in order to appear successful &mdash; always presenting their best side to be admired. The driving force lies in the heart center: Who am I if I do not perform? This question propels Type 3 incessantly forward.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Depressive-Masochistic (Heart Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 seeks individuality and takes pride in being different. They experience emotions deeply and can be melancholic or dramatic &mdash; their heart center is particularly sensitive. Type 4 tends toward intense self-reflection regarding their own identity and the question: Who am I really? &mdash; a question that finds no definitive answer and therefore continues endlessly.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Schizoid, Avoidant (Head Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 has a strong need to understand the world intellectually and dive deeply into topics. They prefer distance and independence, withdrawing to conserve resources and avoid emotional overwhelm. The head center is Type 5's home &mdash; there they feel safe, needing no one.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Anxious-Paranoid (Head Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 has a strong need for security and protection &mdash; often coupled with mistrust toward the new and unknown. They align themselves with groups, authorities, or ideologies to find stability, tending to carefully weigh decisions against potential risks and spot potential danger early on. The head center of Type 6 always considers the worst-case scenario.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Narcissistic (Head Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 tries to avoid unpleasant feelings through a constant search for fun and variety. They have a positive mindset and focus on life's many possibilities &mdash; switching quickly between interests so boredom never sets in. The head center of Type 7 is already planning the next pleasure while the current one is still underway.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Sociopathic-Sadistic (Gut Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 has a strong need for power and control &mdash; over themselves and others. They communicate openly and confrontationally, often without regard for others' feelings, fiercely defending themselves and the people important to them. The gut center of Type 8 is pure force: Whoever threatens me feels resistance. Whoever is loyal to me receives protection.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Passive-Aggressive (Gut Triad)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 has a strong need for inner and outer peace and avoids confrontation. They tend to adapt to the wishes of others to preserve harmony &mdash; often ignoring their own needs to avoid disturbance. The gut center of Type 9 is present, but often lulled to sleep: Their own strength makes itself known &mdash; but only softly, and rarely heard.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"heilfasten-der-9-typen", label:"Therapeutic Fasting of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"auffaellige-merkmale-der-9-typen", label:"Noticeable Features of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"suchtverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Addictive Behavior of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function frisurenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Frisuren der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Hair speaks too. If you know what to look for, you can often read a surprising amount about the wearer's Enneagram type from the style of a haircut &ndash; its grooming, its strictness or looseness, how much or how little it stands out.</p>
      <img src="../assets/frisuren-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Typische Frisuren der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Groomed and Precise</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 wears neat, tidy hairstyles &ndash; often classic or conservative styles that reflect precision and care. Possibly strictly tied-back hair or accurately cut styles. For the Perfectionist, no hair is out of place: Every strand sits exactly as it should. The hairstyle is not a self-portrayal &ndash; it is an expression of seriousness and respect, toward oneself and others.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Soft and Inviting</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 prefers soft, inviting hairstyles &ndash; frequently loose, natural styles that radiate warmth and friendliness. Possibly also hairstyles that are easily accessible and low-maintenance. The Helper's hairstyle shouldn't create distance &ndash; it should invite you to come closer and trust. Nothing hard, nothing off-putting. Type 2's hair embraces you before their arms even do.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Stylish and Trend-Conscious</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 wears stylish, fashionable hairstyles &ndash; well-groomed and presentable styles that emphasize self-confidence and success. Possibly hairstyles that follow current trends, occasionally with the frequent use of hair gel. The Achiever knows: First impressions count, and hair is part of the stage. The hairstyle sits perfectly &ndash; always. And if a trend changes, Type 3 changes their hairstyle.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Creative and Distinctive</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 chooses creative, unique hairstyles &ndash; unusual and artistic styles that express individuality and distinctiveness. Frequently unconventional, sometimes extravagant colors or cuts. The Individualist's hairstyle is no whim and no mere adaptation &ndash; it is a statement. It says: I am not like everyone else. And this isn't an outcry, but a quiet, deep commitment to their own uniqueness.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Functional and Uncomplicated</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 prefers functional, simple hairstyles &ndash; frequently low-maintenance styles that don't require much attention. Possibly also hairstyles that emphasize practicality. The Investigator/Thinker doesn't want hair that requires upkeep, demands attention, or consumes time. The hairstyle should function, not stand out. Ideally: cut once, forgotten for a long time &ndash; and back to being lost in thought.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Conservative and Reliable</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 wears conservative, reliable hairstyles &ndash; classic or inconspicuous styles that convey security and stability. Possibly hairstyles that are considered appropriate in professional or social contexts. The Loyal Skeptic doesn't want to attract attention, cause offense, or make a mistake with their hair. The hairstyle is proven &ndash; and what is proven provides security.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Vibrant and Experimental</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 wears lively, dynamic hairstyles &ndash; styles that express energy and a thirst for adventure. Possibly changing hairstyles or experimental styles. The Enthusiast gets bored quickly &ndash; even with their hair. What works today doesn't have to be right tomorrow. Changing hairstyles isn't an effort for them, it's a pleasure: Something new again, a fresh start again.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Strong and Striking</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 wears strong, striking hairstyles &ndash; dominant styles that emphasize self-confidence and strength. Possibly short or powerful hairstyles. The Challenger doesn't need high-maintenance hair &ndash; they need hair that matches their presence. Clear, decisive, without gimmicks. Type 8's hairstyle takes up space, just as they themselves take up space: naturally and without apology.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Relaxed and Natural</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 prefers relaxed, natural hairstyles &ndash; frequently harmonious, unobtrusive styles that convey serenity. Possibly hairstyles that require little effort and are easy to maintain. The Peacemaker doesn't want hair that creates work or draws attention to itself. It is best when the hair just lies there &ndash; naturally, calmly, without having to think about it.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"psychologische-verhaltensmuster-der-9-typen", label:"Psychological Behavioral Patterns"},
        {route:"auffaellige-merkmale-der-9-typen", label:"Noticeable Features of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function auffaelligeMerkmale9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Auffällige körperliche Merkmale der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The resonance principle &bdquo;as within, so without&ldquo; assumes that our outer reality is a mirror image of our inner states &ndash; also called the &bdquo;law of mirrors.&ldquo; Every Enneagram type carries its inner nature outward in a very characteristic way: in facial features, gaze, gestures, and body posture.</p>
      <img src="../assets/auffaellige-merkmale-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Auffällige körperliche Merkmale der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Principle-Oriented: The Perfectionist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Sharp, clear, strict, and sometimes very tense facial features. Frowning &ndash; the constant focus on details causes a vertical furrow between the eyes or drawn-together eyebrows. A serious, strict, or highly concentrated expression (the &bdquo;angry index finger&ldquo;!), a rigid, sometimes angry gaze, and a very upright, tense posture with tension in the face and neck. All of this reflects the structured and orderly, sometimes rigid and cold nature of Type 1.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Love-Oriented: The Giver/Helper</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Soft, warm, and gentle facial features, open arms and gestures. A friendly, inviting, and frequent smile on the lips, agreeable facial expressions. Large eyes that often express compassion, and a gentle, inviting gaze turned outward. These features reflect the warm-hearted, caring, sometimes empathetic, open, and warm nature of Type 2 &ndash; a person who physically turns toward the other before having said a single word.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Success-Oriented: The Achiever</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">A self-confident, energetic, and enthusiastic expression. A well-groomed, well-styled, and dynamic appearance. A radiant, often cold smile &ndash; very presentable, energetic movements. An assertive, very direct, and focused gaze. These features reflect the goal-oriented, focused, performance-oriented, sometimes somewhat calculating and cool nature of Type 3. The outward appearance is no side effect &ndash; it is part of the strategy.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Self-Oriented: The Individualist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Aesthetic, symmetrical, fine, and graceful facial features that express melancholy or depth. An individual, elegant, and intense outer appearance in facial expression and demeanor. A mysterious, sad expression. A deeply emotional, expressive gaze &ndash; turned inward, at times dramatic, depending on variable mood. These features reflect the emotional depth as well as the creative and aesthetic nature of Type 4.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Knowledge-Oriented: The Investigator/Thinker</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Unemotional facial features with a neutral, reserved expression &ndash; the so-called &bdquo;poker face&ldquo; &ndash; and often a lost-in-thought expression. A thoughtful, sometimes distant, inquiring gaze. Minimal but precise gestures. A restrained smile. An analytical, observing, and emotionally sparse gaze turned inward, a withdrawing, introverted, occasionally stooped posture. These features reflect the profound thinking and the inquiring, objectively observing nature of Type 5.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Security-Oriented: The Loyal Skeptic</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">An attentive, questioning facial expression, a friendly but cautious smile, weighing facial expressions. Tense jaw muscles. A watchful, cautious, occasionally tense posture. A cautious, alert, or concerned expression, tension around the eyes. A fearful, insecure gaze &ndash; constantly on guard, glancing around quickly. These features reflect the cautious and security-driven, sometimes skeptical nature of Type 6.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Fun-Oriented: The Enthusiast/Optimist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">A radiant, sometimes wide and lively smile, childlike, naive facial features. An energetic, open, cheerful facial expression with rapid shifts in expression. Fast, wide-ranging movements. A lively, sparkling gaze. These features reflect the optimistic, fun, life-affirming, cheerful, light, and enthusiastic &ndash; sometimes quickly bored and pain-avoiding &ndash; nature of Type 7.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Power-Oriented: The Challenger</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Strong, striking, sometimes even rough (raw) facial features. An assertive, challenging, and present, powerful posture. A fearless, dominant expression. A penetrating, often intense gaze. Strongly pronounced, energetic gestures. These features reflect the dominant, decisive, self-confident, present, and powerful &ndash; sometimes even intimidating &ndash; nature of Type 8.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Harmony-Oriented: The Peacemaker</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Soft, blurred, and sometimes sluggish facial features. A serene, self-forgetful posture. A relaxed, harmonious expression. A calm, often serene and peaceful smile. A peripheral, self-forgetful, unfocused &ndash; at times expressionless &ndash; gaze. These features reflect the calm serenity and the peace-loving, soft, and yielding nature of Type 9.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> The law of mirrors in relation to the 9 Enneagram principles or types can express itself correspondingly, intensified, or even in the exact opposite way within the framework of the 27 subtypes of the Enneagram, depending on the subtype variant (normal type, intensifying type, counter-type). Furthermore, the individual's level of consciousness plays a defining role here.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"frisuren-der-9-typen", label:"Hairstyles of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"haendedrucke-der-9-typen", label:"Handshakes of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function haendedruckeDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Händedrücke der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Each Enneagram type possesses a certain expression of life due to its Enneagram personality, which can manifest, among other things, in the characteristic expression of its handshake.</p>
      <img src="../assets/haendedrucke-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Die Händedrücke der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Firm and Precise</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 values order and correctness, which shows in a controlled and firm handshake. The perfectionist does not offer their hand casually &ndash; they do it consciously, with the right dosage of strength and dignity. Too limp would be impolite, too firm would be overbearing. The handshake of Type 1 is a small calling card of their character: reliable, clear, and without frills.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Warm and Gentle</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 is warm-hearted and caring, which is conveyed through a gentle yet friendly handshake. The helper does not just shake a hand &ndash; they receive it. Sometimes they add the second hand, hold on for a moment longer, and look into the eyes. The handshake of Type 2 is a small embrace: You are welcome, I am here for you.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Confident and Energetic</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 strives for recognition and success, which is reflected in a powerful and confident handshake. The achiever knows that the first impression counts &ndash; and their handshake is part of the staging. Firm, direct, with eye contact. It signals: Here is someone who knows what they want. The handshake of Type 3 opens doors before a word is spoken.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Expressive and Variable</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 is creative and emotional, which can express itself in a sometimes variable but always conscious handshake. The individualist brings their mood into the handshake as well &ndash; sometimes intense and holding on, sometimes tender and reserved, depending on how they feel in that moment. No handshake of Type 4 is like another. It is always real.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Withholding and Thoughtful</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 is analytical and withdrawn, which shows in a somewhat hesitant or cautious handshake. The researcher shakes hands because it is expected &ndash; but they do so with a certain inner distance. The contact is brief, correct, without exuberance. Behind the withholding lies no disinterest &ndash; only the desire not to offer more closeness than they are currently ready to give.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Firm and Trustworthy</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 seeks security and loyalty, which is expressed through a reliable and stable handshake. The loyalist gives a handshake you can trust &ndash; not too hard, not too soft, but dependable. It is a handshake that says: I am here, I stand by my word. For Type 6, the handshake is also a kind of silent promise.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Lively and Energetic</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 is adventurous and optimistic, which shows in a lively and dynamic handshake. The enthusiast shakes hands with momentum &ndash; perhaps even with a spontaneous pat on the shoulder or a broad smile to go with it. Their handshake is contagious: Here is someone who is glad to meet you and is already looking forward to the next adventure.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Dominant and Powerful</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 is assertive and strong, which manifests in a powerful and sometimes overwhelming handshake. The challenger extends their hand like a statement: I am present, I am strong, I do not back down. Their handshake fills the room. No hesitation, no half measures &ndash; whoever shakes Type 8's hand feels it for a moment afterward.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Gentle and Calming</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 strives for harmony and peace, which is illustrated by a calm and gentle handshake. The peace-loving person extends their hand without pressure, without dominance &ndash; inviting, open, relaxed. Their handshake says: All is well, I am not threatening, we can be with each other in peace. It is one of the most pleasant handshakes of all &ndash; because it has nothing to prove.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Handshake behavior within the framework of the 27 Enneagram subtypes can express itself correspondingly, amplified, or even in complete opposition depending on the subtype variant (normal type, amplification type, counter-type). Additionally, the respective degree of awareness plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the more fulfilling and harmonious the expression of their handshake behavior will be within their respective type spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"auffaellige-merkmale-der-9-typen", label:"Striking Characteristics of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"suchtverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Addictive Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"niesverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Sneezing Behavior of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function suchtverhalteDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Suchtverhalten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Everyone knows patterns that repeat &mdash; behaviors you reach for when the pressure becomes too great or the inner emptiness makes itself felt. The Enneagram shows which type-specific addictive structures lie behind these patterns.</p>
      <img src="../assets/suchtverhalten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Das Suchtverhalten der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Obsessive Perfectionism</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 can tend to obsessively fixate on order, discipline, and control. This can manifest in an unhealthy perfectionism that can result in stress and burnout. Sometimes Ones also reach for substances to cope with the pressure of their high standards. The inner judge that never rests becomes the actual addiction &mdash; the search for what is error-free, for what is finally right, for the moment when one finally feels good enough.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Co-dependency and Emotional Eating</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 can develop a tendency toward co-dependency by focusing excessively on the needs of others while neglecting their own. Type 2 can also tend toward emotional eating or substance abuse to satisfy their own emotional needs when they do not feel personally valued. The helper's addiction is often the addiction to validation &mdash; to the moment when someone says: I need you. Without this feedback, they reach for substitutes.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Workaholism and Performance Addiction</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 can be addicted to success, recognition, and status. Threes tend to lose themselves in work and achievement and can become workaholics. In extreme cases, they even reach for performance-enhancing substances to achieve their goals &mdash; the so-called "Lance Armstrong Syndrome." The actual addiction is the addiction to validation through performance: If I achieve enough, I am worthy. A cycle that spins on its own.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Emotional Intensity and Inner Emptiness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 can tend to lose themselves in their emotions and their longing for authenticity. Fours may use substances or behaviors to seek more intense emotional experiences or to distract themselves from their inner emptiness. The aesthete is sometimes addicted to the feeling itself &mdash; to the depth, the passion, the pain that at least shows that one is alive. The ordinary feels like numbness to them.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Isolation and Intellectual Escape</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 can develop an inclination toward isolation and withdrawal, often into a world of knowledge and information. Fours can also become addicted to certain intellectual pursuits or digital media to avoid social interactions. The thinker's addiction is the addiction to the safe interior: As long as I think and observe, I do not have to engage. Knowledge itself becomes a fortress behind which one entrenches oneself.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Anxiety Management and Security Addiction</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 can be driven by their anxiety and their need for security. Sixes may use substances or behaviors to numb their fears or to feel generally safer. This can lead to a dependence on tranquilizers or security rituals. The skeptic's deepest addiction is the addiction to certainty &mdash; to the final proof that everything will be alright. It does not exist, and so the wheel keeps turning.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Excessive Consumption and Distraction</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 can be addicted to new experiences, pleasures, and adventures. Sevens tend to consume excessively &mdash; whether food, alcohol, drugs, or entertainment &mdash; to avoid negativity, boredom, and inner emptiness. The enthusiast's addiction is the addiction to run from addiction: If I only move fast enough, I won't catch up with myself. The next kick comes before the last one has been processed.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Control and Power Addiction</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 can be addicted to control, power, and intensity. Eights tend to seek high-risk behaviors and may also reach for substances that give them a sense of strength, dominance, and invincibility. The challenger's actual addiction is the addiction to invulnerability &mdash; to the feeling of having nothing and no one to fear. The vulnerability lying underneath remains untouched.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Comfort Addiction and Conflict Avoidance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 can be addicted to comfort and conflict avoidance. Nines tend to distract themselves with television, digital media, food, or other soothing activities to distance themselves from uncomfortable feelings and tension. The mediator's addiction is the addiction to peace &mdash; even if this peace is bought at the cost of lulling one's own will and liveliness to sleep.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Addictive behavior within the framework of the 27 Enneagram subtypes can express itself accordingly, in an intensified way, or even in the exact opposite manner depending on the subtype variant (normal type, reinforcing type, countertype). In addition, the respective level of consciousness plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the less they tend toward addictive behavior within their type spectrum. Broadly speaking, the greatest addiction remains the ego's addiction to thinking, feeling, and acting, corresponding to the 3 Enneagram triads: Head (Types 5, 6, 7), Heart (Types 2, 3, 4), and Gut (Types 8, 9, 1).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"haendedrucke-der-9-typen", label:"Handshakes of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"niesverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Sneezing Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"gifte-des-geistes", label:"Poisons of the Mind"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function niesverhalteDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Niesverhalten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Through sneezing, the body primarily performs a physical, and in the background an energetic, self-cleansing. This releases physical and psychological blockages, allowing energy to flow better overall. Frequent sneezing sometimes also brings about a certain reset. The different Enneatypes react very differently to the involuntary, unexpected sneeze reflex, which leads to the explosive expulsion of air through the nose, often simultaneously through the mouth.</p>
      <img src="../assets/niesverhalten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Das Niesverhalten der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Precise Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 lets out a single, well-controlled sneeze &mdash; followed by a proper apology. No uncontrolled sneezing; always prepared with a tissue. The Perfectionist doesn't just sneeze randomly: Even this involuntary reflex is executed in as orderly a manner as possible. The apology afterward is sincere &mdash; one doesn't want to be a burden to anyone. And the tissue? It was already on hand, of course.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Considerate Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 sneezes quietly, almost apologetically &mdash; just don't disturb anyone. Help is offered immediately: &bdquo;Can I get you a tissue?&ldquo; The Helper reflexively turns the sneeze into a gesture of care. As soon as their own reflex is over, their attention is already back on others. Did someone sneeze too? Does anyone need a tissue? One's own little inconvenience becomes an opportunity to be present and helpful.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Impressive Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 sneezes loudly and powerfully &mdash; with impact. This is followed by a confident comment about their own sneezing to maintain the image of control and efficiency. The Achiever lets no weakness show, even when sneezing. What others would experience as an embarrassing moment is confidently staged: &bdquo;Excuse me &mdash; but efficient, right?&ldquo; A trademark sneeze.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Dramatic Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 accompanies their sneeze with a creative expression of surprise and drama. This is followed by deep reflection on the uniqueness of this sneeze. The Individualist experiences even the sneeze reflex as something special. Was that really just a sneeze &mdash; or is there more to it? Perhaps a sign. Perhaps the body expressing itself. In any case: a moment worth feeling for a brief instant.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Analytical Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 sneezes discreetly and quietly &mdash; followed by a thoughtful moment to ponder the cause and health implications of the sneeze. The Investigator does not let a sneeze reflex throw them off track. Instead: brief internal analysis. Where does this come from? Dust? Allergies? An early sign of a cold? The sneeze is already forgotten &mdash; the thinking has just begun.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Cautious Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 always covers their sneeze with a hand or a tissue &mdash; to ensure no germs are spread. Afterward, the environment is checked for possible allergens. The Loyalist takes no risks, even when sneezing: Hygiene is a duty, caution is a virtue. And who knows &mdash; maybe there's something in the air? Better keep an eye on the situation before moving on.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Spontaneous Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 sneezes happily and unexpectedly &mdash; often accompanied by a laugh or a joking comment. It doesn't interrupt the enthusiasm for the next adventure. The Enthusiast spontaneously turns the sneeze into a little joke. &bdquo;Bless me!&ldquo; &mdash; and on we go. No pausing, no brooding. The moment is already over, and the next experience is already waiting.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Dominant Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 sneezes powerfully and dominantly &mdash; taking up space and attracting attention. There are no apologies: a confident expression of physical presence. The Challenger sneezes the way they do everything: with full force, without hesitation, without regard for possible stares. The body has spoken &mdash; and that is perfectly fine. Anyone who is bothered by it has a problem. Not them.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Gentle Sneezing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 sneezes quietly and peacefully &mdash; barely noticeable. This is followed by an apologetic look to ensure no one was disturbed. The Peacemaker tries to take up as little space as possible, even when sneezing. The reflex came &mdash; they couldn't help it &mdash; but the apologetic look afterward shows: I hope that didn't throw anyone off balance. Everything should remain calm, even after a sneeze.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Sneezing behavior within the framework of the 27 subtypes of the Enneagram can express itself correspondingly, intensified, or even exactly the opposite, depending on the subtype variant (normal type, intensifying type, counter-type). In addition, the respective level of consciousness plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the more fulfilling and harmonious the expression of their sneezing behavior within their respective type spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"suchtverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Addictive Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"essverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Eating Habits of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"arbeitsverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Work Behavior of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function essverhalteDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Essverhalten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">What and how we eat is rarely just a matter of taste. It reflects our inner structure, our fears, our longings—and our Enneagram type. Every type brings its own distinct attitude toward food to the table.</p>
      <img src="../assets/essverhalten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Das Essverhalten der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Disciplined and Controlled</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 tends to eat very disciplined and controlled. It pays attention to healthy and balanced nutrition and can impose strict diets on itself to meet its high standards. For the Perfectionist, eating is not pure enjoyment, but also a question of virtue: what is right? What is healthy? What should I allow myself? This inner bar can be liberating—or become a silent burden when every bite is under observation.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Emotional and Caring</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 tends to eat emotionally, especially when it feels stressed or personally unappreciated. It can tend to spoil others with food while putting itself in the background. For the Helper, the kitchen is often a place of love—it cooks for others, sets the table, ensures everyone is well-fed. What it sometimes forgets: to ask itself what it actually wants.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Mindful and Performance-Oriented</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 pays attention to its appearance and performance, which often shows in a mindful and sometimes restrictive way of eating. It can tend to view food as a means of enhancing performance. The Achiever eats strategically: what gives me energy? What harms my figure? What fits my image? Food as an optimization tool—and sometimes also as a stage on which to display how disciplined one is.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Individual and Mood-Dependent</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 often has very individual eating behavior that is strongly influenced by its emotional states. It can tend toward extreme diets, such as exotic regimens or special eating patterns, to emphasize its uniqueness. For the Individualist, eating is expression—of mood, identity, belonging to oneself. An ordinary lunch in the cafeteria can feel to Type 4 like a rejection of their own essence.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Pragmatic and Purpose-Oriented</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 can have a rather pragmatic and sometimes negligent attitude toward eating. It may skip meals or prefer simple, easy-to-prepare foods to have more time for its intellectual interests. For the Thinker, eating is above all one thing: necessary. It interrupts the flow of thought, costs time and energy—so the meal is gladly made as efficient as possible, to get back to what really matters as quickly as possible.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Traditional and Familiar</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 often has rather traditional eating behavior and tends to stick to proven eating habits. It prefers familiar, trusted dishes and tends to hesitate to try new or unknown foods. What is familiar is not boredom for the Loyalist—it is security. A dish you know cannot disappoint you. And in the circle of family or friends, the well-known tastes twice as good.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Diverse and Pleasure-Seeking</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 loves diversity and enjoyment, including when it comes to food. It can tend to eat excessively, especially when stressed or bored, and loves trying new and exciting foods. For the Enthusiast, eating is an adventure—preferably the new cuisine, the unknown restaurant, the dish never tried before. The plate as a space of experience. The challenge: to pause even when it is no longer about enjoyment, but about distraction.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Powerful and Hearty</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 often has powerful and intense eating behavior. It prefers rich, hearty meals and often exercises great portion control. It tends to view eating as an act of enjoyment and as an expression of its strength. The Challenger eats as it lives: with full presence, without hesitation, without half-measures. A good meal is a feast for it—and it does not let guilt accompany it while it indulges.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Comforting and Careless</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 can tend toward comforting and sometimes careless eating behavior. It may eat emotionally to calm itself and often prefers familiar, soothing, and simple foods. For the Peacemaker, eating is comfort—it calms, it connects, it creates peace. In stressful moments, it gladly reaches for the familiar, something reliably good. The challenge lies in staying consciously present even while eating, and not disappearing into autopilot.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Eating behavior within the framework of the 27 Enneagram subtypes can express itself either correspondingly, amplified, or even in exactly the opposite way, depending on the subtype variant (Normal Type, Reinforcing Type, Countertype). The respective level of consciousness also plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the more authentic the expression of their eating behavior becomes within their respective type spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"niesverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Sneezing Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"arbeitsverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Work Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"verhaeltnis-zu-geld-der-9-typen", label:"Relationship to Money"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function arbeitsverhalteDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Arbeitsverhalten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">How someone works reveals almost everything about their Enneagram type: what drives them, what holds them back, how they behave in a team, what causes conflict. Every type brings its own distinct energy to the workplace.</p>
      <img src="../assets/arbeitsverhalten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Das Arbeitsverhalten der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Conscientious and Detail-Oriented</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 works conscientiously, in an organized manner, and with great attention to detail. It strives for high standards and is often critical of both its own work and that of others. It is reliable and has a strong drive to do things correctly. Type 1's inner critic is also present at the desk: good is not good enough if it can still be better. This makes it the most dependable colleague on the team—but also the harshest critic, especially of itself.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Supportive and Helpful</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 is supportive and helpful. It tends to put the needs of its colleagues above its own and often seeks validation through appreciation and gratitude. It is good at building relationships and working within a team. The Helper thrives in work environments where its care is seen and valued—without that feedback, it can feel burnt out and invisible, even though it gives everything for others.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Goal-Oriented and Efficient</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 is goal-oriented, efficient, and ambitious. It strives for success and recognition and often works hard to achieve its goals. It is good at motivating itself and others and can be very effective in leadership positions. For the achiever, work is the most natural expression of its essence—it lives for results, for milestones, for the moment when the project is completed and everyone applauds.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Creative and Meaning-Seeking</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 brings creativity and originality to its work. It seeks deep meaning and authenticity in its tasks. However, it can also be moody and have difficulty adapting when the work does not fulfill its creative needs. The Individualist needs to feel that its work means something—a routine job devoid of meaning is a kind of inner death for it. Given room to unfold, it delivers the incomparable.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Analytical and Independent</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 is analytical, focused, and independent. It prefers to work in peace and without interruptions. It gathers and analyzes information thoroughly before making decisions and is often an expert in its field. The Thinker needs retreat to be productive—too much team dynamics and small talk costs it energy that it would rather invest in deep reflection. Given its space, it delivers analyses of incomparable thoroughness.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Reliable and Security-Oriented</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 is reliable, conscientious, and security-oriented. It is good at assessing risks and bringing foresight to its work. It values stability and clear structures and works well in supportive and cooperative environments. The Loyalist is the type who finds the weak points in the plan before they become a problem—an invaluable asset on the team once it learns to trust its own competence.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Versatile and Imaginative</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 is versatile, imaginative, and energetic. It loves variety and new positive impulses and often brings in fresh, innovative ideas. However, it can also tend to spread itself too thin and have difficulty completing projects. The Enthusiast is the spark of every team—it excites, it inspires, it opens doors that others haven't even seen yet. The challenge lies in staying the course once the initial excitement fades.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Assertive and Leadership-Oriented</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 is assertive, decisive, and leadership-oriented. It readily takes on responsibility and is not afraid of confrontation. It works efficiently and directly, stands up for its convictions, and protects its team. The Challenger leads not through hierarchy but through strength and presence—it sees problems as opponents to be defeated and carries others along with its determination.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Cooperative and Balanced</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 is cooperative, patient, and adaptable. It creates a harmonious work environment and is good at mediating conflicts. However, it can sometimes have difficulty asserting its own needs and opinions. The Peacemaker is the glue of the team—it ensures that everyone is heard and no one is left behind. Its challenge: to represent its own point of view just as clearly as it represents everyone else's positions.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Work behavior within the framework of the 27 Enneagram subtypes can express itself either correspondingly, amplified, or even in exactly the opposite way, depending on the subtype variant (Normal Type, Reinforcing Type, Countertype). The respective level of consciousness also plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the more fulfilling and harmonious the expression of their work behavior within their respective type spectrum becomes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"essverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Eating Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"verhaeltnis-zu-geld-der-9-typen", label:"Relationship to Money"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function verhaeltnisZuGeldDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Verhältnis zu Geld der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Money is never just money &ndash; it is a mirror. What we do with it, how we hold onto it or let it go, whether it frightens us or inspires us: all of this reveals something about our Enneagram type. Each type brings its own patterns, fears, and longings into the handling of money.</p>
      <img src="../assets/verhaeltnis-zu-geld-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Das Verhältnis zu Geld der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Order and Ethical Diligence</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 often sees money as a means of creating order and security. They are disciplined and careful with money, avoid waste, and value a fair and ethical use of resources. The inner critic of Type 1 also guards the bank account: Is this money being spent the right way? Can I truly justify this? This attitude makes them reliable and forward-thinking &ndash; though sometimes unnecessarily rigid toward themselves.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Giving as a Way of Life</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 may tend to spend money to help others and take care of them. They find fulfillment in spending money on charitable causes or gifts &ndash; but can sometimes have difficulty prioritizing their own financial needs. Behind this pattern often lies the deep conviction: My worth lies in what I do for others. Whoever keeps money for themselves is selfish. The healing of Type 2 lies here as well: giving must not be a one-way street.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Success and Status</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 often sees money as a symbol of success and status. They are willing to work hard to achieve financial prosperity and enjoy investing in things that reflect their status and performance. However, they may also tend to save money to achieve long-term goals. For the achiever, money is less an end in itself than feedback: it shows that they have arrived, that their work is seen, that they count.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Ambivalence and Meaning</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 often has an ambivalent attitude toward money. They may view money as a means to fulfill aesthetic and emotional needs and tend to spend it on unique and meaningful experiences and objects. However, they can also struggle with financial discipline. The aesthetic wants money to be an expression of their inner self &ndash; routine expenses for everyday things often feel meaningless to them, while a perfect experience or a special piece seems worth any price.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Thriftiness and Independence</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 is generally cautious and thrifty with money. They tend to save money and invest wisely to ensure financial security. They prefer to spend money on things that provide them with knowledge and practical advantages. For the thinker, money means one main thing: independence. Whoever is financially secure does not need to depend on others &ndash; and that is a deeply desirable state for Type 5.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Security and Provision</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 often has a security-oriented relationship with money. They value financial stability and are cautious with investments. They tend to save money and minimize risks to insure themselves against uncertain future scenarios. When it comes to money, the skeptic always thinks a few steps ahead: What if something goes wrong? What if income dries up? This provision is one of their great strengths &ndash; as long as it does not turn into paralyzing anxiety.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Freedom and Experience</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 enjoys spending money on new experiences and pleasures. They tend to be impulsive and spend money on travel, leisure activities, and other pleasurable experiences. They may have difficulty saving money or making long-term plans. For the enthusiast, money is freedom capital &ndash; it buys options, adventures, the next opportunity. The thought of leaving it sitting in an account feels like wasted lifetime to them.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Power and Independence</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 sees money as a means to power and independence. They are often successful in acquiring money and are not afraid to use it to achieve their goals and strengthen their position. They are determined and confident in dealing with financial matters. The challenger negotiates, invests, and fights for resources &ndash; not out of greed, but because money means agency to them. Whoever has money can shape things. Whoever has none is dependent. And dependency is the worst thing possible for Type 8.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Comfort and Relaxedness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 can have a relaxed and often nonchalant relationship with money. They value comfort and may tend to spend money on things that give them a sense of peace and well-being. They may have difficulty focusing on long-term financial goals or making clear financial decisions. The peacemaker does not want conflict &ndash; not even with their own bank balance. As long as it is enough and everything remains quiet, they are satisfied. The challenge lies in actively taking care of one's own financial future, even when it is uncomfortable.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Within the framework of the 27 Enneagram subtypes, the relationship to money can express itself correspondingly, amplified, or even in complete opposition depending on the subtype variant (normal type, amplification type, counter-type). Additionally, the respective degree of awareness plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the more fulfilling and harmonious the expression of their relationship to money will be within their respective type spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"arbeitsverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Working Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"sexualverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Sexual Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function sexualverhaltenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Sexualverhalten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The famous Enneagram teacher Claudio Naranjo studied sexual behavior in the context of the Enneagram types. Each type brings its own characteristic patterns, fears, and longings into its sexual behavior.</p>
      <img src="../assets/sexualverhalten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Das Sexualverhalten der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Structured and Controlled</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 may strive for perfection during sex, which can lead to controlling behavior and self-criticism. The perfectionist brings their inner line of judgment into intimacy as well &ndash; they want to do everything right, not disappoint the partner, and commit no errors. This can have an inhibiting effect, but also a very attentive one: Type 1 can focus in a very disciplined and dedicated manner on the partner's well-being once they learn to silence the inner critic.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Warm-hearted and Caring</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 often puts the partner first and can be very giving and caring during sex. The helper seeks emotional validation and appreciation in intimacy &ndash; giving themselves completely and hoping to receive love back through this giving. Their sexual behavior is warm-hearted and dedicated, but often tied to a silent need: Do you see how much I am there for you? Will I get this back?</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Goal-oriented and Effective</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 may view sex as a performance and strives to be perceived as a good lover. The achiever brings their core orientation into the bedroom as well: they want to shine, impress, and be the best. This can be very stimulating &ndash; yet it also raises the question of whether true surrender lies behind the staging. The more conscious the Type 3, the more the performance stance dissolves and makes room for genuine connection.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Intense and Emotional</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 often experiences sexuality intensely and passionately, with a tendency toward drama and emotional depth. The aesthetic seeks a deep, meaningful connection in physical encounters &ndash; sexuality is not a fleeting moment for them, but a space where the innermost self becomes visible. They can be devoted and passionate, but require authenticity: superficiality or routine is the end of all erotics for Type 4.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Withholding and Analytical</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 can approach sexuality with detachment and analysis, often with a need for emotional distance. The thinker may find it difficult to surrender completely &ndash; preferring intellectual intimacy whenever possible. Physical closeness requires trust and security from them, which they build up slowly. If this step succeeds, a person opens up behind the cool facade who can be present with great attention and tenderness.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Hesitant and Insecure</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 can experience insecurity and doubt during sex, while simultaneously seeking security and trust. The skeptic brings their core fear into intimacy as well: Am I good enough? Is this safe? Can I let go? They can be sexually hesitant and require validation and stability from the partner. Once they find this security, they open up with a loyalty and reliability that is second to none.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Spontaneous and Playful</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 often approaches sexuality playfully and with a sense of adventure, holding a preference for variety and new experiences. The enthusiast views sex as fun and pleasure &ndash; bringing lightness, humor, and a joy of discovery. They may have difficulty committing and tend to replace depth with alternation. The more conscious the Type 7, the more they discover that true freedom does not lie in flight &ndash; but in arriving completely.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Powerful and Dominant</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 has strong and intensive sexual behavior, often marked by dominance and control. The challenger can be very passionate and direct, often seeking power and influence in the sexual dynamic. Behind this intensity, however, lies a deep longing: to be loved without having to show weakness. Anyone who succeeds in providing Type 8 with this safe space experiences a person who can open up with impressive vulnerability.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Gentle and Harmonious</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 has relaxed and harmonious sexual behavior that responds to the partner's needs. The peacemaker tends to avoid conflict, seeking peace and connection in the sexual relationship. In the process, they can forget themselves &ndash; putting their own wishes aside to make the other person happy. The more conscious the Type 9, the more they learn to bring in their own voice as well: connection arises between two present individuals, not between one and their echo.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Within the framework of the 27 Enneagram subtypes, sexual behavior can express itself correspondingly, amplified, or even in complete opposition depending on the subtype variant (normal type, amplification type, counter-type). Additionally, the respective degree of awareness plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the more fulfilling and harmonious the expression of their sexual behavior will be within their respective type spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"verhaeltnis-zu-geld-der-9-typen", label:"Relationship to Money"},
        {route:"kussverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Kissing Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function kussverhaltenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Kussverhalten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The famous Enneagram teacher Claudio Naranjo once developed an interesting perspective on kissing behavior in the context of the Enneagram types during a seminar in Berkeley, California, in the 1980s. According to Naranjo, a person's kissing behavior can provide insight into their Enneagram type.</p>
      <img src="../assets/kussverhalten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Das Kussverhalten der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Structured and Controlled</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 1 kisses in a structured and controlled manner &ndash; possibly with restraint to maintain perfection. For the perfectionist, a kiss is not a spontaneous moment of surrender, but an action carried out with careful consideration. They bring themselves in fully, but in their own way: precise, respectful, without overstepping boundaries. The Type 1 kiss is a silent promise &ndash; expressing passion not through intensity, but through reliability. Whoever is kissed by a Type 1 feels: they mean this seriously.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Warm-hearted and Caring</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 2 kisses warmly and caringly &ndash; a kiss expressing closeness and connection. The helper's entire essence is contained within the kiss: they surrender completely and without reservation. Their kiss is a concentrated form of an embrace &ndash; they want the other person to feel: You are welcome, you are loved, you are safe with me. Type 2 does not kiss for themselves &ndash; they kiss for the other person. And that is precisely what makes their kiss unmistakably warm.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Goal-oriented and Effective</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 3 kisses in a goal-oriented and effective way &ndash; possibly somewhat demonstratively. The achiever brings their core structure into even the most intimate moments: they know what they want, and they execute it. Their kiss is not a probing attempt, but a statement. It can be impressive &ndash; full of self-confidence, with clear intention. Yet the question remains: Do they kiss because they feel &ndash; or because they know how to kiss? The more conscious the Type 3, the truer the answer.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Intense and Emotional</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 4 kisses intensely and emotionally &ndash; a kiss conveying depth and passion. For the aesthetic, a kiss is never routine. It is expression, revelation, art. Type 4 kisses the way they feel: with everything they have. They bring the entire depth of their inner life into that moment &ndash; the longing, the beauty, the pain. Whoever is kissed by a Type 4 does not forget it. It is not just one kiss among many &ndash; it is the one that counts.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Withholding and Analytical</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 5 kisses withholding and analytically &ndash; rather cautiously and distantly. This does not mean they do not feel &ndash; it means closeness is precious to them and therefore rare. The thinker approaches physical intimacy with a certain inner observation: What is happening here? What does this mean? Their kiss is rare, but when it comes, it comes with awareness. And whoever is patient enough to overcome the distance discovers a tenderness behind it that very few would suspect.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Hesitant and Seeking Trust</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 kisses hesitantly and uncertainly &ndash; a kiss reflecting the search for security and trust. For the skeptic, intimacy is always also vulnerability &ndash; and vulnerability requires trust. Their hesitation is not a lack of interest, but a form of honesty: they only open up when they feel secure. Whoever has brought Type 6 to take that step has achieved something rare. And the kiss that follows is sincere &ndash; without posturing, without games.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Spontaneous and Playful</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 7 kisses spontaneously and playfully &ndash; a kiss displaying joy and a spirit of adventure. For the enthusiast, a kiss is a celebration &ndash; unplanned, exuberant, with a smile hidden inside. They kiss because the moment demands it, because it is wonderful, because it brings them joy. Their kiss is rarely heavy &ndash; it carries the lightness of the moment within it. Sometimes fleeting, sometimes sweeping, but always alive. Whoever kisses a Type 7 kisses someone who has never forgotten what it feels like the first time.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Powerful and Dominant</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 8 kisses powerfully and dominantly &ndash; a kiss expressing intensity and control. The challenger brings their core energy into every moment of encounter with others: strength, directness, unmasked presence. Their kiss is not a question &ndash; it is a statement. And yet: Behind this strength lies a vulnerability that Type 8 rarely shows. Whoever looks deep enough also finds shelter in their kiss &ndash; the silent message: I let you close because I trust you.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Gentle and Harmonious</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 kisses gently and harmoniously &ndash; a kiss emphasizing peace and connection. The peacemaker brings what defines them into their intimacy: a deep longing for connection without tension, for closeness without drama. Their kiss is a moment of quiet togetherness &ndash; soft, calm, with a warmth that spreads slowly. They do not want to impress; they want to arrive. And whoever surrenders to it finds one of the deepest kisses there are &ndash; because they are fully present, with no hidden agenda.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;padding:1rem 1.2rem;background:var(--surface-2, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));border-radius:8px;">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> Within the framework of the 27 Enneagram subtypes, kissing behavior can express itself correspondingly, amplified, or even in complete opposition depending on the subtype variant (normal type, amplification type, counter-type). Additionally, the respective degree of awareness plays an important role. The more conscious a person is, the more fulfilling and harmonious the expression of their kissing behavior will be within their respective type spectrum.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"sexualverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Sexual Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"hochsensibilitaet-der-9-typen", label:"High Sensitivity of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function hochsensibilitaetDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Hochsensibilität der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">These traits are frequently pronounced in a strong form in highly sensitive people, as the universal primal principles manifest themselves within a person in an excessively one-sidedly intense way. These qualities can be both a blessing and a curse in individual cases.</p>
      <img src="../assets/hochsensibilitaet-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Hochsensibilität der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Fine Perception, Perfectionism, Sense of Justice</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 1 possesses an extraordinary ability to hear out details in a simple yet detailed way, reproduce them, and put them into words. Naturally conscientious — its internal sensor registers every deviation from the ideal, every inconsistency, every imbalance. This sensitivity is its greatest gift: it makes it an incorruptible observer, a reliable corrector, a person who recognizes quality before others even sense it. The downside is internal exhaustion through constant stimulus filtering — Type 1 always hears everything, even what others tuned out long ago. A blessing and a curse at the same time: whoever perceives everything can improve the world — and cannot recover within it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Pronounced Altruism and Emotional Resonance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 2 feels and transmits extraordinary emotions with an empathy that others can hardly comprehend. It recognizes foreign feelings before they are spoken out loud — a look, a posture, a pause in conversation are enough for it to know what the other person needs right now. This quality makes it a natural helper, comforter, and emotional anchor for others. The flip side: it soaks up the emotions of its environment like a sponge and often carries feelings that are not its own at all — without knowing where the boundary between itself and the other runs. A blessing and a curse: whoever feels everything can heal — and can lose themselves in the process.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Intensive Emotional Sensing in Relationships</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 3 perceives relationships and stimuli deeply and emotionally intensely — far more than its outwardly often cool-appearing demeanor suggests. Behind the facade of the achievement-oriented person lives a person who experiences connections with great intensity and reacts extremely finely to emotional signals in their social environment. This sensitivity enables an intuitive sense for the dynamics between people — it reads rooms before others have opened the door. The danger: it overworks itself because it feels the emotional burden of its environment while simultaneously trying to show strength outwardly. A blessing and a curse: whoever senses everything and still delivers can move great things — and burns out from the inside while doing so.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Empathy and Long Emotional Reverberation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 4 is very empathetic and can understand fellow human beings at a depth that remains closed to others. Experiences echo within it for a long time — music, art, encounters, separations leave traces that do not simply dissolve. This depth is its artistic capital: it feeds its creativity, its expressive power, its ability to transform the unspeakable into language or image. The downside is emotional flooding: what others check off as past lives on in Type 4 — sometimes as beauty, sometimes as pain, often as both simultaneously. A blessing and a curse: whoever feels so deeply can create art that touches people — and finds no off-switch for themselves.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Long-Term Memory and Systemic Thinking</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 5 possesses a very pronounced long-term memory and the ability to think in larger contexts — it is a profound thinker with pronounced, far-branching trains of thought. It links information that others have long forgotten into new patterns and insights. This quality makes it an analyst, researcher, visionary. The flip side: it is overstimulated in a world that constantly pelts it, and therefore withdraws far back — not out of coldness, but out of the deep need for stillness to be able to think at all. A blessing and a curse: whoever thinks so broadly can recognize connections that others never see — and needs a solitude for that which not everyone understands.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Creating Trust and Security</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 6 is very reliable, helpful, and friendly-loyal — a person in whose presence others feel safe because trust is not just promised, but embodied. Its fine sense of risks, inconsistencies, and hidden dangers makes it a natural guardian and trust anchor in groups. It notices when something is wrong — long before it becomes obvious. The downside: this alertness exhausts it. A highly sensitive Type 6 carries the worries of its environment even when no one asked it to. A blessing and a curse: whoever is so vigilant holds communities together — and finds hardly a moment of genuine peace themselves.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Enthusiasm, Imagination, and Sensitivity to Pain</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 7 is optimistic, laughs and cries easily, and possesses an increased sensitivity to pain — it lives on the surface of emotions with an intensity that carries others along. Its high enthusiasm, versatile interests, and great imagination make it a creative motor in any environment. It ignites with ideas and ignites others with them. The flip side: it feels pain — physically as well as mentally — with an immediacy that sometimes rolls over it. Its optimism is often also a protection strategy against what it perceives so intensely. A blessing and a curse: whoever burns so brightly illuminates everything around themselves — and needs plenty of space to recover.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Psychological Stability and Independence</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 8 is usually psychologically very stable, very independent, and feels good even when alone — it needs no external validation to be sure of itself. This inner firmness is its special form of high sensitivity: while others waver in emotional storms, it remains grounded. At the same time, it perceives injustice, weakness, and threat with an immediacy that immediately sets it into action. Its body awareness is intense — it senses itself and its boundaries very clearly. A blessing and a curse: whoever is so grounded can provide support to others — and sometimes overlooks their own vulnerability hidden deep inside.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Stillness, Essentiality, and Connecting Presence</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The highly sensitive Type 9 often seeks stillness and can recognize the essential in a simple way — it possesses a pronounced need for harmony and is very mediating, friendly, and connecting. Amidst all the noise of the world, it hears what is truly important. This silent perception makes it a natural mediator: it sees all perspectives simultaneously and can build bridges where others see only trenches. The downside: overstimulation hits it hard — it then withdraws into itself and appears absent, even though it is actually just overwhelmed. A blessing and a curse: whoever sees the essential so clearly can foster peace — and sometimes loses sight of their own center in the process.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"kussverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Kissing Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"empfindliche-punkte", label:"Sensitive Points"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function superpowerDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Superpower der 9 Typen", "Charts · Spirituelle Sicht")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Search no longer in the outside world for your supposed happiness — recognize your desire (your core motivation) to look for yourself in the outside world. By becoming increasingly aware of your intrinsic motivation step by step, you recognize the desperate attempt of your ego structure and abandon it: the dead end of trying to receive love within the framework of external life circumstances, while actually achieving the exact opposite.</p>
      <img src="../assets/superpower-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Superpower der 9 Enneatypen aus spiritueller Sicht – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Superpower: True Perfection</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true perfection is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 1 seeks perfection in the outside world: in rules, structures, correctness. The spiritual insight is that this perfection is not an achievement to be earned, but a quality that is already there — in the moment, in stillness, in being. Perfection is not the result of improvement, but the nature of essence itself. When Type 1 stops trying to correct the world and begins to see it as it is, it discovers in every single moment an order it could never have created. That is its superpower: the ability to recognize the perfect within the imperfect.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Superpower: True Love</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true love is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 2 seeks love through giving, through being needed, through being reflected in the grateful gaze of another. The spiritual insight is that love does not need to be earned — it is the substance from which it is made. When Type 2 stops trying to win love through caregiving and begins to recognize it as its own core essence, its caregiving becomes free: no longer an exchange, but pure flowing. That is its superpower: love not as a means, but as an origin.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Superpower: True Success</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true success is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 3 measures its worth by results: titles, recognition, visible progress. The spiritual insight is that human worth cannot be earned because it was never lost. When Type 3 stops trying to prove itself through performance and begins to act from out of its own worth, its action becomes more powerful, genuine, and sustainable. That is its superpower: success not as a goal, but as a natural expression of inner wealth.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Superpower: True Individuality</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true individuality is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 4 seeks itself in the depth of feelings, in the uniqueness of experience, in the longing for what is missing. The spiritual insight is that it does not need to become unique — it already is, irredeemably and irrevocably. No amount of work on oneself makes it any more what it already is. When Type 4 stops searching for its distinctiveness and begins simply to embody it, art that moves others is created. That is its superpower: uniqueness as a gift, not a task.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Superpower: True Knowledge</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true knowledge is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 5 gathers knowledge as protection: the more it understands, the safer it feels in a world it experiences as demanding and exhausting. The spiritual insight is that the deepest knowledge does not come from books, but from the direct experience of consciousness itself. When Type 5 stops accumulating knowledge and begins to open itself to immediate experiencing, it discovers an intelligence greater than any concept. That is its superpower: knowledge as living presence, not an archive.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Superpower: True Safety</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true safety is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 6 seeks safety in structures, authority, contracts, and loyal allies — in everything that appears reliable. The spiritual insight is that no external safety can ever be complete because life itself is unpredictable. The only safety that cannot be taken away is that of one's own ground — the quiet certainty within that remains when everything else changes. That is its superpower: trust not as naivety, but as inner anchoring.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Superpower: True Joy of Life</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true joy of life is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 7 seeks joy in the next experience, the next plan, the next horizon — always one step ahead of the present. The spiritual insight is that joy is not a goal to be achieved, but the nature of consciousness itself in a state of openness. When Type 7 stops searching and begins to surrender completely to the present moment, it finds a joy there that needs no external cause. That is its superpower: joy of life as a fundamental tone, not an achievement.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Superpower: True Power</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true power is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 8 seeks power through control, influence, and the ability to shape the world — it does not want to be dependent, not at the mercy of others. The spiritual insight is that the greatest power is not control over others, but mastery over oneself: the ability to remain calm amidst chaos, to decide wisely amidst temptation. When Type 8 stops demonstrating strength and begins to be it, an authority arises that needs no loudness. That is its superpower: power from within, not over the outside world.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Superpower: True Harmony</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Realize that true harmony is already here and now within you — for that is your true inner strength. Type 9 seeks harmony through adaptation, conflict avoidance, and quietly merging with what others need. The spiritual insight is that harmony is not a state that must be produced through yielding — it is the fundamental vibration of being itself. When Type 9 stops dissolving itself to create peace and begins to live from its own center, it carries a stillness within that actually pacifies others — not through adaptation, but through presence. That is its superpower: harmony as inner being that radiates outward.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"hochsensibilitaet-der-9-typen", label:"Hypersensitivity of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"wolkenformationen-der-9-typen", label:"Cloud Formations of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"gifte-des-geistes", label:"Poisons of the Mind"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function wolkenformationenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Cloud Formations of the 9 Types", "Charts · Archetypes")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Assigning nine cloud formations to the nine Enneagram principles is a creative way to connect the varied qualities of these clouds with the different personality types of the Enneagram. In its countless forms, the sky mirrors the whole spectrum of human character — from clear structure to the silent blanket of equanimity.</p>
      <img src="../assets/wolkenformationen-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Cloud Formations of the 9 Types – Chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Cumulus Clouds: Order and Moral Integrity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cumulus clouds are often symmetrical and well-defined, matching the Perfectionist's striving for order and clarity. They symbolise Type 1's high moral integrity and diligence. The cumulus is the picture-book cloud: clearly outlined, brilliant white, reaching upward. It forms from rising warm air that shapes itself into a clearly defined body — chaos giving itself structure. Meteorologists use cumulus clouds as reliable indicators: their shape reveals the day's weather. They don't lie. The passion of anger shows itself as inner tension when the horizon grows untidy and the contours blur.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Stratocumulus Clouds: Care and Warmth</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Stratocumulus clouds are often broad and give a sense of shelter and warmth — much like the Helper, who looks after others' wellbeing and offers support. Stratocumulus clouds cover the sky in soft, interlocking layers — no edges, no sharpness, everything flowing together. They are the most common cloud form on Earth: millions of square kilometres of sky are covered by them daily, a quiet roof over the world. Beneath them, the day feels muted and sheltered. The passion of pride shows itself as the deep need to be the protective layer that shields others from the cold.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Altocumulus Clouds: Efficiency and Recognition</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Altocumulus clouds are arranged in regular patterns and can signal sunny, clear days. They represent the Achiever, who impresses through efficiency and drive and seeks a high degree of recognition. Altocumulus clouds form precise rows and patterns at medium altitude — the so-called "mackerel sky" pattern that meteorologists read as a sign of atmospheric stability and orderly currents. They are aesthetic, orderly, and in their regularity often herald good weather. The passion of vanity shows itself as delight in presenting a beautiful, orderly image — the pattern everyone admires.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Lenticular Clouds: Uniqueness and Inner Depth</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Lenticular clouds are rare and have an unusual, often lens-shaped appearance. These unique clouds mirror the Individualist's distinctiveness and creativity, the wish to stand apart from the crowd. Lenticular clouds form when air currents hit mountains or mountain ranges and oscillate in standing waves — the result is a lens-shaped, smooth, sometimes multi-layered form that looks as if it had been painted. It does not move, even though wind blows straight through it. Pilots avoid them because of extreme turbulence inside. The passion of envy shows itself as the wish to be as unmistakable as this cloud — seen, but never explained.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Cirrus Clouds: Distance and Analytical Clarity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cirrus clouds sit high in the sky and are often fine and wispy. They symbolise the Observer, analytical, who often directs their thoughts toward higher spheres. Cirrus clouds exist at altitudes of eight to thirteen kilometres — where most other clouds can no longer form. They are made not of water droplets but of tiny ice crystals that refract sunlight into fine veils. From up there, the view is clear and wide. Cirrus clouds often herald weather changes that arrive only days later — a knowledge possible only from a distance. The passion of avarice shows itself as thrift with closeness: lovely to look at, but unreachable.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Nimbostratus Clouds: Loyalty and Reliability</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Nimbostratus clouds are widespread, often associated with long-lasting rain. They symbolise the Loyalist's steadiness and reliability, standing firm even when circumstances are difficult. Nimbostratus clouds cover the entire sky in a uniform, dense grey — no gaps, no variation, no end in sight. They bring no dramatic downpour but steady, even, lasting rain — for hours, sometimes days. You can count on them: once nimbostratus arrives, it stays. The passion of fear shows itself as the feeling of needing to keep watching the sky — what if the rain never stops?</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Cumulonimbus Clouds: Energy and Love of Adventure</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cumulonimbus clouds are impressive and energetic, often associated with violent weather phenomena. They represent the Enthusiast, lively, adventurous, always in search of new experiences. The cumulonimbus is the largest cloud formation on Earth: it can stretch from a few hundred metres above the ground into the stratosphere — up to 18 kilometres high. Inside it rage updrafts of over 200 km/h; lightning flashes, thunder rolls, hailstones form, melt, and re-form. The cumulonimbus is the opposite of stillness: it is spectacle, energy, transformation. The passion of gluttony shows itself as an insatiable upward drive.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Wall Clouds: Power and Assertiveness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Wall clouds hang low and appear threatening, often heralding severe weather events. They symbolise the Challenger's power and assertiveness, confrontational and strong. Wall clouds form at the base of supercell thunderstorms — rotating storm systems among the most powerful weather phenomena on Earth. They hang low and dark, often with visible rotation, as if the sky itself were clenching a fist. Their appearance is an unmistakable signal: something is coming that nothing can stop. The passion of lust shows itself as the will to concentrate the entire atmospheric energy within oneself.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Stratus Clouds: Harmony and Quiet Balance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Stratus clouds are even and often cover the entire sky, conveying a sense of stability. They represent the Peacemaker's striving for harmony and balance. Stratus clouds are the calmest of all clouds: a uniform, low layer that covers the sky like a soft blanket — no drama, no contours, no movement. At most they bring fine drizzle or fog. Beneath stratus clouds the world grows quiet: no hard shadow, no glaring light, everything diffuse and evenly lit. The passion of sloth shows itself as the deep wish to be this blanket — muting everything, smoothing everything, letting nothing escalate.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal perspectives – volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 subtypes, healing paths and the bigger picture – volume 3, the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"wolfsarten-der-9-typen", label:"Wolf Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"eulenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Owl Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function wolfsartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Wolf Species of the 9 Types", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The assignment of nine wolf species to the nine Enneagram principles is a creative method to connect the diverse aspects and characteristics of these wolves and their traits with the various personality types of the Enneagram. Since time immemorial, the wolf has stood for instinct, community, and inner strength — each species in its very own way.</p>
      <img src="../assets/wolfsarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Wolfsarten der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Gray Wolf: Order and Structure in the Pack</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Gray Wolf displays strong territorial behavior and social hierarchy within the pack, indicating an order and structure very similar to Type 1. The Gray Wolf is the architect of the pack: It knows the boundaries of its territory down to the meter, marks them regularly, and defends them consistently. The hierarchy in the pack is clearly regulated — not through brute force, but through a social order that everyone acknowledges. Gray Wolves eat last and drink first only if the situation permits: duty before privilege. The passion of anger manifests as inner tension when order and justice are violated.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Arctic Wolf: Care and Cooperative Togetherness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Arctic Wolf lives in extreme environments and demonstrates strong cooperative behavior within the pack — corresponding to Type 2's need to care for others. The Arctic Wolf survives the Arctic only through radical cooperation: The entire group cares for the offspring together, shares prey even with weaker pack members, and holds together even in extreme frost. Everyone in the pack has a role, but no one is left alone. The Arctic Wolf is white as snow — no hiding, full visibility: I am here, I help, I belong. The passion of pride shows as a deep desire to be indispensable to the survival of others.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Red Wolf: Adaptability and Will to Survive</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Red Wolf is known for its adaptability and survival skills, reflecting the success and recognition that drive Type 3. The Red Wolf is one of the rarest wolves in the world — and a master of adaptation. It has survived centuries of persecution by adjusting its behavior, territory, and hunting strategy to the given situation. Today it exists almost exclusively in captive breeding programs — a witness to how far the will to survive can reach. The Red Wolf seeks no confrontation; it finds ways. The passion of vanity shows as identification with one's own success — the will to prevail despite everything.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Maned Wolf: Uniqueness and Rare Nature</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Maned Wolf is characterized by its striking appearance, its special lifestyle, and its highly individual behavior — corresponding to the distinctiveness of Type 4. The Maned Wolf is neither a true wolf nor a fox — it is unique: the only species of its genus. With long black legs, reddish-brown fur, and a black mane, it is unmistakable. It lives as a solitary creature in the Cerrado savannas of Brazil, roaming widely and alone through its territory. It avoids other Maned Wolves, except during mating season. The passion of envy shows as a longing for deep connection while simultaneously clinging to one's own distinctiveness.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Arabian Wolf: Retreat and Quiet Observation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Arabian Wolf lives in extreme desert regions and displays a reserved behavior that corresponds to Type 5. The Arabian Wolf is the smallest wolf in the world — and the loneliest. It mostly lives alone or in small pairs in the deserts of the Arabian Peninsula and Israel, having adapted to heat and drought like no other canid. It hunts at night, stays hidden during the day, and needs little to survive: minimal resources, maximum efficiency. The passion of avarice shows as extreme thriftiness with everything — energy, space, trust.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Eurasian Wolf: Loyalty and Pack Cohesion</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Eurasian Wolf shows strong social bonds, great loyalty, good cooperation, and intense security behavior within the pack, very similar to Type 6. The Eurasian Wolf is the wolf of Europe and Asia — widespread, enduring, deeply rooted socially. Its pack is a community with clear roles and strong mutual interdependence: The safety of the individual depends on the cohesion of all. Threats are registered collectively and answered collectively. The Eurasian Wolf is vigilant, but never rash. The passion of fear shows as hyper-alertness to anything that could threaten the safety of the pack.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Indian Wolf: Curiosity and Liveliness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Indian Wolf mostly lives in packs and shows great curiosity in diverse habitats — known for the adventurous and enthusiastic nature of Type 7. The Indian Wolf is smaller and lighter than its European relatives — and more agile. It inhabits open grasslands and semi-deserts of India, having adapted to a life that requires constant movement: wide ranges, changing hunting grounds, new territories. It is more playful than other wolf species and frequently shows playful behavior even as an adult. The passion of gluttony shows as an insatiable appetite for new spaces and experiences.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Timber Wolf: Dominance and Leadership</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Timber Wolf is the largest wild wolf in the world, living in strongly structured packs consisting of an alpha pair and their offspring — traits that correspond to the assertiveness of Type 8. The Timber Wolf of North American forests is a wolf that shapes landscapes: Where it lives, it regulates ungulate populations and thus the entire ecology of its habitat — the so-called trophic cascade effect. It needs no permission; it shapes. In the pack, the alpha pair leads with a combination of strength and experience that is not questioned. The passion of lust shows as an unslaked will for control and creation.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Iberian Wolf: Harmony and Balance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Iberian Wolf often displays calm and reserved behavior and lives harmoniously in smaller packs — corresponding to the peace-loving and balancing nature of Type 9. The Iberian Wolf is smaller and shyer than the Gray Wolf — an animal that has learned through centuries of persecution to remain as invisible as possible. It avoids conflict with humans, lives in small family packs, and stays hidden in the mountain regions of Portugal and Spain. It defends its territory quietly and without unnecessary escalation. The passion of sloth shows as a deep desire not to disturb the peace of the forest — to simply be, in hiding.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"wolkenformationen-der-9-typen", label:"Cloud Formations of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"eulenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Owl Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"baerenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bear Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function wetterNaturphaenomeneDer9TypenPage() {
  const img = (file, alt) => `<img src="../assets/${file}" alt="${alt}"
        style="width:100%;max-width:640px;display:block;margin:1rem auto 1.2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />`;
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Weather and Nature Phenomena of the 9 Types", "Charts · Archetypes")}
      <h1 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.2rem 0 0.5rem;line-height:1.2;">Weather and Nature Phenomena of the 9 Types</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Nature expresses the nine core energies of the Enneagram through its own cycles: frost and rain, sun and fog, starry polar night and storm, rainbow and volcano, the still lake. Each weather phenomenon carries its own principle — its own movement, its own quality of force, stillness, or change. Nine natural phenomena, nine Enneagram principles.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Frost / Crystal Formation: Frozen Form</h3>
      ${img("wetter-1-frost.jpg", "Hoarfrost crystals on frozen branches")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Frost forms when moisture, under falling temperature, doesn't freeze chaotically but arranges itself into an exact, geometrically pure structure — every ice crystal follows the same inner blueprint, nothing is random, nothing looks "untidy." That is exactly the signature of Type 1: the inner drive to bring chaos into a correct, flawless form before it may be considered "finished." Frost is also cold and hard until it is warmed — an image for the strictness with which Ones judge themselves first, then the world: nothing may melt until the form is right. And just as hoarfrost turns into clear, flowing water at the first rays of sun, the strictness of Ones dissolves once they meet themselves with warmth instead of criticism — perfection is then not abandoned, but brought to life.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Warm Spring Rain: Nourishing</h3>
      ${img("wetter-2-fruehlingsregen.jpg", "Raindrops on fresh spring grass")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Spring rain doesn't fall as a storm but gently and persistently — it awakens growth in everything it touches, without staying visible itself. It seeps into the ground, down to the roots, and keeps working unseen long after the clouds have moved on. That is the core movement of Type 2: placing themselves in service of others' growth, often unnoticed, without demanding recognition for it — giving closeness that lets the other bloom. The downside appears when rain never stops: ground that is constantly drenched can flood, roots can rot. The tendency of Twos to give so much that their own boundaries blur carries the same risk — they themselves dry out, because they never let themselves be watered. Maturity for Type 2 means letting their own rain fall for themselves too.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – The Midday Sun: Full Radiance</h3>
      ${img("wetter-3-mittagssonne.jpg", "Sunflower in full bloom in bright sunlight")}
      <p class="vb-intro">The sun at its zenith is the moment of maximum visibility and power — shadows shrink to a minimum, every shape, every color, every achievement becomes visible. A sunflower follows this sun all day long, turning toward it, opening itself fully to it — built for radiance. That mirrors the drive of Type 3 precisely: to shine, to succeed, to make ability and achievement visible, turning toward recognition the way the blossom turns toward the light. But midday sun also blinds: in its glaring light the fine contours blur, one sees less clearly precisely because the light is so intense. Likewise, the self-presentation of Threes can obscure who they are "in their own shadow," beyond the performance and the stage. Maturity for this type arrives when they know their worth even under a clouded sky.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Fog / Autumn: Melancholic, Mysterious</h3>
      ${img("wetter-4-nebel-herbst.jpg", "Misty autumn lake with reflected trees")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Fog over an autumn lake blurs the boundaries between water, shore, and sky — the familiar becomes strange and poetic at once, because one only senses the contours of the world rather than seeing them clearly. Autumn as the season of transition, of impermanence in full color, speaks directly to Type 4: for this type, the feeling of depth arises precisely from the awareness of loss, from knowing that every beauty also fades. The muted, golden-brown mood of a foggy autumn morning carries exactly that mix of longing, wistfulness, and quiet beauty that becomes, for Fours, the very feeling of being alive. But fog also lifts — and behind it often lies surprising clarity. An image for the fact that the emotional intensity of Fours need not be a final state, but a passage toward genuine, undramatic presence.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Starry Sky / Polar Night: Stillness, Depth</h3>
      ${img("wetter-5-polarnacht.jpg", "Starry sky with aurora during the polar night")}
      <p class="vb-intro">The polar night is stillness, distance, endless expanse — a space where one can observe for days without being seen, where withdrawal is not a lack but the condition for clarity. That is exactly the retreat impulse of Type 5: creating distance in order to understand before engaging; thinking things through in peace before acting. The stars and the aurora itself stand for what Fives collect — knowledge, patterns, insight, observed from a safe distance, without the noise of day that would outshine the faint lights in the sky. The challenge in this image: a night that lasts too long, from which no one returns to the daylight of connection. Maturity for Type 5 means sharing the clarity they've gathered with others, instead of keeping it only for themselves.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Wind / The Approaching Storm: Vigilance, Warning</h3>
      ${img("wetter-6-unwetter.jpg", "Lightning over mountains, gathering storm clouds")}
      <p class="vb-intro">The wind that announces what's coming, the falling barometer, the clouds piling up over the mountains — that is pure vigilance, reading the signals before the actual danger has even arrived. That is exactly the gift of Type 6: they sense risk before others see it, they are the "weather-sensitive" ones of any group, the first to notice when something is brewing. The worry-and-fear dynamic of this type shows itself in the fact that the rising wind is not yet a storm — but Sixes often already experience it as though the catastrophe has already happened, and prepare accordingly. Courage for this type doesn't mean having no fear, but: feeling the wind, taking it seriously — and still not declaring it a hurricane too soon.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Rainbow / Summer Thunderstorm: Dynamic, Colorful</h3>
      ${img("wetter-7-regenbogen.jpg", "Rainbow against the evening sun after a summer shower")}
      <p class="vb-intro">A summer thunderstorm is intense, brief, changeable — and afterward, when the sun breaks through the clouds again, the rainbow appears as a reward, visible proof that joy and color can arise again immediately out of turmoil. That is the movement of Type 7: rapid shifts, many colors and options in view at once, the ability to draw something positive and bright out of almost any situation. The downside: a rainbow is fleeting, it vanishes the moment one tries to hold onto it or chase after it — an image for the difficulty many Sevens have in truly staying with one experience, feeling, or commitment, instead of moving on to the next colorful stimulus. Maturity for this type shows when they learn to stay even under an overcast sky, without immediately fleeing toward the next rainbow.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Volcanic Eruption / The Storm: Raw Force, Transformation</h3>
      ${img("wetter-8-vulkan.jpg", "Volcanic eruption with lava and ash cloud")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Raw, unfiltered force that breaks through unstoppably — a volcanic eruption destroys everything in its immediate path, yet also creates new, exceptionally fertile ground on which more will later grow than before. That is Type 8: intensity, assertiveness, the instinct to take control the moment weakness or threat is sensed anywhere. The eruption in a sense clears the atmosphere, but also brings devastation if the force stays uncontrolled and no one channels it. The maturation of Type 8 lies precisely in consciously directing their own "lava force" — protection and building up instead of mere destruction, true leadership instead of pure dominance. A volcano that knows where its force should flow transforms a landscape for the better.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – The Still Lake / The Vast Sea: Absolute Calm, Connection</h3>
      ${img("wetter-9-stiller-see.jpg", "Still lake at sunset with calm water reflection")}
      <p class="vb-intro">The mirror-smooth surface of a still lake at sunset shows the sky, the shores, the trees — perfect, with no movement of its own that would disturb this peace. That is exactly the deepest wish of Type 9: connection, harmony, non-conflict, the feeling of being one with the surroundings instead of asserting oneself against them. But a lake with no current at all can stagnate over time — just as Nines can "forget" themselves, their own wishes and priorities, in order to preserve outer peace at any cost. The vast sea in the background is a reminder that beneath the calm surface lies enormous force and depth — their own presence, their own will, which Nines often must first consciously discover and claim in order to be truly alive.</p>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal perspectives – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 subtypes, paths of healing, and the bigger picture – Volume 3 completing the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"wolkenformationen-der-9-typen", label:"Cloud Formations of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"berge-der-9-typen", label:"Mountains of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"baerenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bear Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function architekturRaumgestaltungDer9TypenPage() {
  const img = (file, alt) => `<img src="../assets/${file}" alt="${alt}"
        style="width:100%;max-width:640px;display:block;margin:1rem auto 1.2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />`;
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Architecture & Interior Design of the 9 Types", "Charts · Archetypes")}
      <h1 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.2rem 0 0.5rem;line-height:1.2;">Architecture & Interior Design of the 9 Types</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">How we build and inhabit spaces mirrors our inner stance. Every architectural style carries its own way of being in the world — clarity or warmth, display or retreat, drama or stability. Nine architectural and interior concepts, nine Enneagram principles.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Minimalism / Bauhaus: Clean Lines, Functional Perfection</h3>
      ${img("architektur-1-bauhaus.jpg", "Bauhaus building with clear geometric lines")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Bauhaus architecture refuses any ornament that serves no function — every line, every surface is reduced to exactly what it needs to be, and no more. That is precisely the signature of Type 1: a deep-seated longing for the one correct, right form, in which nothing is superfluous and nothing is missing. Clean edges, right angles, no flourishes — order isn't experienced here as restriction, but as the only honest answer to how a space should be. The downside: a space that knows only function can feel cold, a home without softness. Maturity for Type 1 shows when the clear form remains, but warmth and life are allowed to enter it.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Cozy Cottage Style / Hygge: Warm, Inviting</h3>
      ${img("architektur-2-hygge.jpg", "Cozy hygge living room with warm light and plenty of seating")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Cottage style and the Danish feeling of hygge live from soft textiles, warm light, and above all: from seating for others. A sofa meant for four, a table with room for one more chair — this space is built from the very start for visitors, for closeness, for arriving together. That is exactly Type 2: creating a home in which others feel safe and welcome, often before their own needs are even spoken. The danger lies in the space eventually being furnished more for guests than for its own resident. Maturity means reserving the coziest corner for oneself too.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Penthouse / Modern Glass Architecture: Impressive, Forward-Looking</h3>
      ${img("architektur-3-glasfassade.jpg", "Modern glass facade of a skyscraper")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Glass facades and penthouses are architecture that wants to be seen — they reflect light, status, and progress, often visible from far across the rest of the city. Nothing is accidental: every sightline, every skyline view is placed to impress. That is Type 3: a home as a stage, as visible proof of success that needs no explanation because it speaks for itself. The downside of this glass perfection: whoever is always representing often has no place left to simply be imperfect. Maturity for Type 3 arises when worth no longer depends on the impression the space makes, but rests within.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Gothic / Boho-Chic / Art Nouveau: Dramatic, Individual</h3>
      ${img("architektur-4-jugendstil.jpg", "Art Nouveau facade with elaborate, individual details")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Art Nouveau facades and Gothic buildings refuse repetition — every ornament is unique, every window tells its own small story, every detail carries expression rather than mere function. That is the deepest language of Type 4: a space must make one's own uniqueness visible, must carry meaning, history, feeling within it, or it remains empty. Dramatic colors, unusual shapes, gathered found objects — every element expresses an inner truth. The danger: a space so devoted to being special that it can never rest, never truly arrive. Maturity shows when the beauty remains without needing constant new drama to sustain it.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Brutalism / Tiny House: Self-Sufficient, Reduced to Essentials</h3>
      ${img("architektur-5-brutalismus.jpg", "Brutalist concrete building with clear, massive forms")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Raw, unclad concrete, honest in its materiality — Brutalism shows exactly what it is, without facade, without decoration that would distract from the essential. The Tiny House carries the same message: a space radically reduced to what is truly needed, self-sufficient, independent of unnecessary excess. That is Type 5: retreat into a protected, clearly bounded space in which every resource — time, energy, possessions — is consciously allotted so nothing is wasted. The downside: a space so devoted to self-sufficiency that it no longer lets others in. Maturity for Type 5 means opening one's own fortress to others now and then.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Fortress Architecture / Classic Brick Row Houses: Solid, Reliable</h3>
      ${img("architektur-6-festung.jpg", "Massive fortress with thick stone walls")}
      <p class="vb-intro">A fortress is built to withstand any attack — thick walls, few, well-controlled entrances, a place where one feels safe because one knows exactly what protects it. Classic brick row housing carries the same message on a more civic scale: solid construction that has held for generations and leaves nothing to chance. That is Type 6: security arises through structure, through reliability, through knowing what will hold in an emergency. A home that gives support because it has been tested and proven. The danger: walls so thick they also lock out trust. Maturity for Type 6 shows when security no longer comes only from outside, but from within as well.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Lofts / Glamping Architecture: Open, Playful, Bright</h3>
      ${img("architektur-7-loft.jpg", "Open, bright loft with flowing transitions between spaces")}
      <p class="vb-intro">A loft knows no fixed walls — kitchen, living, and work flow into one another, the space can always be used and experienced anew, nothing is ever final. Glamping architecture carries the same lightness outdoors: comfort without confinement, openness instead of limits, always the possibility of spontaneously moving on. That is Type 7: a space that keeps options open, that places light, air, and freedom of movement above fixed structure, because commitment feels like confinement. The downside: so much openness can also mean a real sense of home never settles in. Maturity for Type 7 arises when an open space also invites staying, not only moving on.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Industrial Style / Monumental Architecture: Massive, Imposing</h3>
      ${img("architektur-8-industrial.jpg", "Industrial interior with exposed brick, steel, and large windows")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Raw brick, exposed steel beams, massive window fronts — industrial style hides nothing of its power, it puts it on display. Monumental architecture goes further still: buildings that command respect purely through their scale, leaving no doubt about who commands the space. That is Type 8: a home as an expression of strength, unconcealed presence, control over one's own territory. Nothing is minimized or softened, every structure stays visible. The danger: a space so massive that vulnerability finds no place in it. Maturity for Type 8 shows when softness is allowed behind the strong facade.</p>

      <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Organic Architecture (Hundertwasser/Steiner): Integrated into Nature</h3>
      ${img("architektur-9-organisch.jpg", "Hundertwasser building with colorful, organically curved facade")}
      <p class="vb-intro">Hundertwasser's buildings know no straight line, no right angle that would interrupt nature — facades grow like tree trunks, roofs carry soil and plants, the house becomes part of the landscape rather than a contradiction to it. That is exactly Type 9: a space that isn't asserted against its surroundings but merges with them, in which boundaries stay soft and permeable because harmony matters more than separation. Soft shapes, warm colors, no element pushing itself into the foreground. The downside: a house without clear contour can also mean its resident dissolves within it. Maturity for Type 9 arises when the organic softness remains, but one's own presence within it becomes clearly felt. More on Hundertwasser as sexual Type 9 (SX9w8) in the portrait linked below.</p>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal perspectives – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 subtypes, paths of healing, and the bigger picture – Volume 3 completing the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"wetter-naturphaenomene-der-9-typen", label:"Weather and Nature Phenomena of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"beziehungen", label:"Relationships of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"arbeitsverhalten-der-9-typen", label:"Work Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"beruehmte-hundertwasser", label:"Portrait: Friedensreich Hundertwasser (SX9w8)"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function eulenartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Eulenarten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The assignment of nine owl species to the nine Enneagram principles is a creative method to connect the diverse aspects and characteristics of these owls and their traits with the various personality types of the Enneagram. Owls have been symbols of wisdom, silence, and inner vision since antiquity &mdash; each species in its very own way.</p>
      <img src="../assets/eulenarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Eulenarten der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Barn Owl: Precision and Perfection</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Barn Owls have a clear, structured hunting pattern and are known for their efficiency and precision. Their methodical nature and their striving for perfection reflect the characteristics of the Type 1 Reformer. The Barn Owl's face is an acoustic precision instrument: The heart-shaped disc focuses sound waves like a parabolic antenna and directs them to asymmetrical ears &mdash; allowing it to locate a mouse under snow in absolute darkness purely by the sound of its steps. No living creature has more precise hearing. The Barn Owl does not make mistakes &mdash; it cannot afford to. The passion of anger manifests as inner tension when the hunt does not meet the inner standard.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Great Grey Owl: Care and Quiet Helpfulness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Great Grey Owl is known for its caring nature toward its young and its ability to survive in a variety of environments and help other owls raise young when needed. The Great Grey Owl is one of the largest owls in the world &mdash; and one of the quietest. It hunts silently, warns others of danger, and demonstrates pronounced brood care: Great Grey Owl males regularly bring food to their nesting partner even under harsh conditions. It serves without pushing. The passion of pride shows as a deep need to be needed &mdash; the protector in silence.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Snowy Owl: Performance and Adaptability</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Snowy Owls are impressive hunters that survive in the harsh conditions of the Arctic. Their ability to adapt to extreme conditions and hunt successfully reflects the performance-oriented Type 3. The Snowy Owl is the only animal that hunts year-round in the Arctic &mdash; at temperatures down to minus 50 degrees, in winter without light. It draws no boundaries between night and day, between storm and calm: It hunts when prey is there. Its white plumage is not ornament, but efficiency &mdash; perfect camouflage in the snow. The passion of vanity shows as the will to triumph visibly.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Long-Eared Owl: Uniqueness and Inner Depth</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Long-Eared Owl possesses a striking appearance with its prominent ear tufts, which clearly distinguish it from other owls. Its distinctiveness and being different correspond to the Individualist Type 4. The Long-Eared Owl is the raccoon among owls: When it feels threatened, it stretches into a slender branch, presses its plumage tightly to its body, and becomes almost invisible between bark and trunk. This ability for transformation &mdash; being visible when it wants, invisible when necessary &mdash; mirrors the inner world of Type 4. The passion of envy shows as a longing to be seen without giving up the protective mask.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Ural Owl: Observation and Analytical Silence</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Ural Owl behaves quietly and guardedly, using its precise powers of observation selectively to search for prey &mdash; which corresponds to the observing analyst Type 5. The Ural Owl is the largest owl of the boreal forests &mdash; and one of the most difficult to observe. It sits motionless for hours on a high branch, turning its head in small, precise movements and waiting. It does not chase after prey &mdash; it waits until it knows enough, and then acts with absolute certainty. Its gaze is not aggressive, but registering. The passion of avarice shows as thriftiness with energy and presence.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Little Owl: Fidelity and Social Vigilance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Little Owls are social and often live near human settlements. Their loyalty to their partners and their readiness to protect their family reflect the characteristics of the reliable Type 6. The Little Owl is the owl that lives closest to humans: It nests in old orchards, barns, and wall holes right next to the farmyard. It knows every corner of its territory by heart and reacts to any change with visible alarm. Its piping call at twilight is a signal: I am here, I am watching, everything is under control. The passion of fear shows as permanent alertness that never completely rests.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Northern Hawk-Owl: Liveness and Joy of Hunting</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Northern Hawk-Owl is a medium-sized owl that lives in open forests and meadows. It is known for its acrobatic flight maneuvers and fast hunting techniques, demonstrating its lively, adventurous nature. The Northern Hawk-Owl is the only owl that is predominantly diurnal &mdash; it hunts in sunlight, visible to all. It flies in long, undulating arcs, swoops lightning-fast onto prey, and is in constant motion. Unlike its nocturnal relatives, it seeks not silence, but light. The passion of gluttony shows as an insatiable hunger for action and the next target on the horizon.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Turkmenian Eagle Owl: Dominance and Territoriality</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Turkmenian Eagle Owl is a very assertive owl. Territorial aggression and its strong vital presence, along with presence and assertiveness, reflect the Challenger Type 8. The Turkmenian Eagle Owl is one of the largest owls in the world &mdash; its territory can encompass hundreds of square kilometers, and it defends it with a consistency that even large birds of prey avoid. Its call in the night is unmistakably deep and far-reaching: a signal that does not invite, but warns. It does not share its space &mdash; and has no need to. The passion of lust shows as an unconditional will to control its own domain.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Eurasian Scops Owl: Quiet Harmony and Adaptation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Eurasian Scops Owls are small and inconspicuous, preferring peaceful and quiet environments. Their peaceful and even-tempered nature corresponds to the harmony-seeking nature of the peace-loving Type 9. The Eurasian Scops Owl is the smallest owl in Europe &mdash; and the most inconspicuous. It nests in tree cavities left behind by others, quietly hunts insects and small creatures, and stands out in no environment. Its call is a soft, rhythmic whistle that blends harmoniously into the soundscape of the night. It needs no attention and seeks no conflict. The passion of sloth shows as a deep desire for the night to remain quiet and for everything to take its accustomed course.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"wolfsarten-der-9-typen", label:"Wolf Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"papageienarten-der-9-typen", label:"Parrot Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"vogelarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bird Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function papageienartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Papageienarten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The mapping of nine parrot species to the nine Enneagram principles is a creative method for connecting the diverse aspects and characteristics of these parrots with the different personality types of the Enneagram. Each species fascinatingly mirrors the essence of a type—from the analytical Grey Parrot to the peace-loving Cockatiel.</p>
      <img src="../assets/papageienarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Papageienarten der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Grey Parrot: Intelligence and Precision</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Grey Parrot</strong> is known for its extraordinary intelligence and its ability to precisely imitate words and concepts. It can be very orderly and prefers a structured environment—traits that correspond to the perfectionistic Type 1. The Grey Parrot is considered the most cognitively gifted bird in the world: researchers like Irene Pepperberg have shown that it does not merely imitate words but understands their meaning and can think abstractly—quantities, colors, shapes, absence. It distinguishes between right and wrong, correct and incorrect. When something is off, it points it out. The passion of anger manifests as inner unrest when things do not go as they should.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Sulphur-Crested Cockatoo: Care and Social Bonding</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Sulphur-Crested Cockatoo</strong> is very social and shows strong bonds to its companions. It is extremely curious and can be very affectionate, which makes it known for its caring nature and its need for social interaction—traits that correspond to the helper Type 2. Sulphur-crested cockatoos suffer from loneliness in a way that can only be described as emotional pain: they pluck out their own feathers, scream, become depressed. Their capacity for bonding is extraordinary—they choose a partner and remain faithful to it for life. The passion of pride manifests as the deep need to be needed and loved.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Blue-Fronted Amazon: Self-Confidence and Performance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Amazon parrots are self-confident and possess a strong personality. They are very curious and learn quickly what makes them successful within their group—traits that reflect the achievement-oriented Type 3. The <strong>Blue-Fronted Amazon</strong> is a bird with a stage instinct: it loves an audience, it loves reactions, and it learns at a speed that puts other parrots to shame. Its plumage is flashy, its performance is loud, its presence is immediate. It calculates—not in a cold sense, but in the sense of a creature that always knows what a social situation currently calls for. The passion of vanity manifests as a hunger for admiration and resonance.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Hyacinth Macaw: Depth, Uniqueness, and Intense Bonding</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Hyacinth macaws are known for their vivid colors and their unique, intense personality. They maintain deep emotional bonds with their partners and possess a strong presence as rare creatures—traits that correspond to the individualist Type 4. The <strong>Hyacinth Macaw</strong> is the largest flying parrot in the world—and one of the rarest. Its cobalt-blue plumage has no equivalent in the bird world. It forms pair bonds of an intensity and exclusivity that regularly astonishes observers: hyacinth macaws spend nearly all their time together, away from the group. The passion of envy manifests as a deep longing for complete connection.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Cockatoo: Observation and Detached Interest</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cockatoos are very intelligent and love learning new things. They can be extremely curious and have a strong observational, detached interest in their environment—which fits well with the analytical Type 5. The <strong>Cockatoo</strong> solves problems with a patience and methodology reminiscent of a scientist: it turns an object in every direction before acting. In the wild, it was discovered that cockatoos use tools—and pass on this knowledge within their group without demonstrating it. They share knowledge selectively. The passion of avarice manifests as thriftiness with closeness and affection—trust must be earned.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Senegal Parrot: Loyalty and Caution</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Senegal parrots—formerly called Poicephalus—are very loyal and possess strong bonds to their partners or caregivers. They are cautious and reserved toward strangers, which underscores the reliable and loyal Type 6. The <strong>Senegal Parrot</strong> chooses those it trusts with great care—and once it has decided, this bond is nearly unbreakable. It meets new people or situations with demonstrative skepticism: it observes first, then tests, and only reveals itself much later. The passion of fear manifests as permanent vigilance—an inner security system that never fully switches off.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Rose-Ringed Parakeet: Liveliness and Adventurousness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Rose-ringed parakeets are lively and adventurous. They live for the exceptional, for new experiences, and possess a contagious enthusiasm for life—typical traits of Type 7. The <strong>Rose-Ringed Parakeet</strong> is one of the most adaptable parrots of all: it originates in South Asia and Africa, but has since established feral populations in over 100 cities worldwide—London, Amsterdam, Cologne, Tokyo. It finds food everywhere, nesting opportunities everywhere, company everywhere. Every new place is an opportunity. The passion of gluttony manifests as an inexhaustible appetite for the new.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Moluccan Cockatoo: Strength and Dominance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Moluccan cockatoos are very self-confident and dominant. They often display leadership qualities and possess a strong presence—typical traits of Type 8. The <strong>Moluccan Cockatoo</strong> is one of the largest cockatoos in the world and one of the loudest birds of all: its call can be heard from over a kilometer away. It raises its crest in moments of excitement or dominance—a visual signal that is unmistakable. It accepts no subordination that it has not chosen itself. When it loves, it loves absolutely; when it fights, it fights absolutely. The passion of lust manifests as a hunger for influence and complete control over its environment.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Cockatiel: Gentleness and Harmony</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cockatiels are gentle and peaceful companions. They strive for harmony and avoid conflict—which fits well with the peace-loving, gentle Type 9. The <strong>Cockatiel</strong> is the most commonly kept parrot in the world—not because of its beauty or intelligence, but because of its nature: it is uncomplicated, pleasant, easygoing. It sings quietly to itself, grooms its partner with gentle beak touches, and remains noticeably calm even in restless environments. It needs no attention—it simply is. The passion of sloth manifests as a deep wish for everything to stay as it is—quiet, warm, together.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"eulenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Owl Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"vogelarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bird Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"blumenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Flower Types of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function blumenartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Blumenarten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The assignment of nine flower species to the nine Enneagram principles is a creative method to connect the diverse aspects and characteristics of these flowers and their traits with the various personality types of the Enneagram. Each flower carries an archetypal essence within itself — from the flawless purity of the lily to the quiet enlightenment of the lotus.</p>
      <img src="../assets/blumenarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Blumenarten der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Lily: Purity and Perfection</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The lily symbolizes purity, perfection, and integrity — values that fit well with the high standards and striving for perfection of Type 1. The lily is one of the oldest cultivated plants in the world: In Egypt, Greece, and the Christian West, it stands for spotlessness and moral perfection. Its geometrically precise petals open in an order that knows no deviation. The fragrance is clear, strong, and unmistakable — leaving no room for ambiguity. For Type 1, the lily is a mirror: beauty as the result of inner correctness, not by chance. The passion of anger manifests as pain when reality falls short of the ideal.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Peony: Love and Care</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The peony stands for compassion and generosity — traits that highlight the helpful and loving character of Type 2. No other flower opens so completely: The peony unfolds dozens of petals in concentric layers, a gesture of total opening. It smells sweet and inviting and has been given as a gift of affection for centuries. In folk medicine, it was a healing plant for heart and mind. Type 2 recognizes itself in it: in the overwhelming giving, in the desire to envelop others with beauty and warmth. The passion of pride shows as the deep need to be indispensable.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Calla: Elegance and Success</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The calla symbolizes elegance and success — reflecting the ambitious and goal-oriented nature of Type 3. The calla is the flower of architects and designers: its calyx is a single, flawless line, no superfluous detail. In wedding arrangements and on stages, it is a staple — always where appearance matters. Pablo Picasso painted it, Diego Rivera's Frida Kahlo loved it. The calla does not let itself be drowned out by other flowers: It stands, and all others step back. The passion of vanity shows as total identification with one's image — the desire to appear unforgettable.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Iris: Individuality and Depth</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The iris stands for creativity and individuality — reflecting the unique and artistic character of Type 4. The iris exists in over 300 species and almost every color of the spectrum — no other flower shows so much variation within a single genus. Van Gogh painted the iris with an obsession that surprised even himself: He called it "the violet soul of the South." Its triple-petal system has symbolized the connection between heaven, earth, and the underworld since antiquity — an entity that exists between worlds. The passion of envy shows as the feeling of being unique yet not fully understood.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Violet: Quiet Wisdom and Observation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The violet symbolizes modest wisdom and quiet thoughtfulness — fitting well with the reserved and reflective nature of Type 5. The violet grows in quiet corners, under trees, on shady slopes — never in the spotlight. It is one of the smallest blossoms in the garden and at the same time one of the most tenacious: It spreads quietly, roots deeply, and returns every year. In antiquity, it was considered the plant of philosophers — food for the mind, protection for thought. The violet makes no fuss. It is simply there, and whoever looks closely discovers a beauty that does not impose itself. The passion of avarice shows as thriftiness with presence.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Forget-Me-Not: Fidelity and Reliability</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The forget-me-not stands for fidelity and constancy — highlighting the reliable and loyal character of Type 6. The name says it all: This small blue flower is the universal symbol for remembrance, fidelity, and the promise not to forget. In medieval legends, a knight who picked flowers for his beloved and fell into the river threw the flowers to her while falling, saying: "Forget me not!" The forget-me-not does not ask for attention — it grows in large carpets, each blossom tiny on its own, together impossible to miss. The passion of fear shows as a deep desire not to be abandoned.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Sunflower: Joy and Optimism</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The sunflower symbolizes joy and optimism — fitting well with the lively and positive nature of Type 7. The sunflower literally does what its name promises: Young sunflowers turn daily toward the sun, from east to west — a phenomenon called heliotropism, which biologists describe as an active search for light. It grows faster than almost any other garden plant, reaches heights of over three meters, and bears heads that themselves look like small suns. Vincent van Gogh painted them as a symbol of friendship and life. The passion of gluttony shows as an insatiable hunger for brightness and experience.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Gladiolus: Strength and Determination</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The gladiolus stands for strength and determination — fitting the powerful and assertive nature of Type 8. The name comes from the Latin "gladius" — the sword. No coincidence: The gladiolus grows out of the ground like a blade, straight, upright, unbending. In ancient Rome, gladiators were showered with gladioli after victory — the flower of fighters, of conquerors. Its stem bears the blossoms in a line from bottom to top, as if showing a way: straightforward, determined, without detours. The passion of lust shows as an unslaked hunger for power and influence — the blade that always points upward.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Lotus: Harmony and Inner Peace</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The lotus flower symbolizes peace and harmony — fitting well with the balanced and harmonious nature of Type 9. The lotus flower grows in the mud and rises daily flawless above the water's surface — an image that stands for enlightenment and the path through suffering in Buddhist and Hindu traditions. Its secret: The surface of its petals is structured in such a way that dirt simply beads off — the lotus effect, which science only fully understood in the 20th century. For Type 9, the lotus flower is a deep image: It rests in the murky waters without being touched by them. The passion of sloth shows as a deep desire for this peace — untouched, floating, whole.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"papageienarten-der-9-typen", label:"Parrot Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"hunderassen-der-9-typen", label:"Dog Breeds of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"vogelarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bird Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function kampfkunstartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Kampfkunstarten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Martial arts are disciplines that teach the skills and techniques of serious physical confrontation with an opponent. Each of the martial arts listed here carries, at its core, primarily one of the Enneagram patterns — a pattern that matches exactly the innermost philosophy of that particular martial art.</p>
      <img src="../assets/kampfkunstarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Kampfkunstarten der 9 Enneagrammtypen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Karate: Precision and Incorruptible Form</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Karate is the martial art of the exact line. Every technique has a single correct execution — the angle of the fist, the position of the foot, the timing of the exhale on a strike are defined down to the smallest detail and repeated for years in the kata, the fixed movement forms, until no deviation remains possible. There is no "roughly right" — only right or wrong. This matches the inner world of Type 1 precisely: the striving for the one correct way, the discipline to refine one's own execution until it matches the inner ideal. The karateka does not practice to win, but to perfect the form — victory over the opponent is merely the logical consequence of perfected technique. The passion of anger shows here as controlled, channeled force: every strike is an expression of order, not of rage.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Wrestling: Physical Contact and Caring Control</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Wrestling is the oldest of all martial arts — and the most intimate. Unlike distanced striking arts, there is no distance here: you hold the opponent, feel their weight, their breath, their next move before it happens. The wrestler wins not by injuring, but through control over the other's body — through gripping, guiding, taking to the ground, without striking. This matches the relational dynamic of Type 2: the desire for close, direct contact, the need to "read" the other so well that you sense their next move before they know it themselves. Wrestling is care and dominance at once — you hold on to the other, ostensibly to subdue them, but really to stay in connection. The passion of pride shows as the need to be indispensable in the hold.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Kung Fu: Virtuosity and Impressive Effect</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Kung Fu (more precisely: Wushu) is the martial art of a thousand styles and visible mastery. Hardly any other martial art has such diverse, acrobatic, and visually impressive movement forms — from the tiger style to the crane style to the monkey style. Kung Fu masters have become icons for centuries through legends, films, and Shaolin performances: Bruce Lee made Kung Fu world-famous because it unites power, elegance, and showmanship in a single language of movement. This matches Type 3 exactly: the desire to impress through visible excellence, to perfect one's own ability until it commands admiration. Kung Fu is never merely functional — it is always also performance, an image of skill designed to have an effect. The passion of vanity shows as identification with the flawless, admired execution.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Judo: One's Own Path Through the Other's Force</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Judo literally means "the gentle way" — its founder Jigoro Kano developed it on the principle that one does not fight the opponent's force, but absorbs it, redirects it, and makes it usable for one's own throw. A smaller, lighter judoka can defeat a much stronger opponent by understanding their movement and energy and channeling it in a new, self-determined direction. This is the deeply individualistic logic of Type 4: not seeking direct confrontation, but shaping something entirely one's own, unmistakable, out of what comes from outside — even out of pain, even out of another's force. Every throw in Judo is a small transformation: foreign energy becomes one's own movement. The passion of envy shows as the deep feeling of having to find one's own, unmistakable path only through dealing with what is missing or standing in one's way.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Taekwondo: Distance, Reach, and Technical Clarity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Taekwondo is the martial art of distance. Unlike wrestling or judo, it explicitly seeks distance from the opponent — its characteristic, wide-sweeping leg techniques allow an attack to land without entering direct bodily contact. Taekwondo is also one of the most clearly systematized martial arts: a well-thought-out belt system, precisely catalogued techniques, a high proportion of theoretical and analytical understanding of movement mechanics. This matches the world of Type 5: the need for distance as protection, the preference for clear, well-thought-out systems, the security that comes from precise analysis rather than physical closeness. The taekwondoin strikes from a distance — precise, calculated, without entering a scramble. The passion of avarice shows as sparing use of closeness: contact only at the precisely calculated moment.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Bare-Knuckle Boxing: Vigilance, Guard, and Tested Trust</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Classic bare-knuckle fighting — the forerunner of modern boxing, handed down in many cultures as a pure, weaponless confrontation of fist against fist — lives by a single core principle: constant vigilance. Whoever lowers their guard even for a moment gets hit. The bare-knuckle fighter continuously watches the smallest signals in the opponent's face and shoulder posture to anticipate the next strike. This matches the basic stance of Type 6: permanent inner vigilance, scanning the environment for danger, the need to test before opening up. Bare-knuckle fighting is not an art of grand gestures, but of small, constant corrections — step by step, guard by guard, when in doubt checking once too often rather than once too little. The passion of fear shows as an inner alertness that never fully switches off.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Kickboxing: Speed, Variety, and Explosive Energy</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Kickboxing combines punches and kicks into a fast, varied, combination-rich fighting style that constantly opens new options — combinations of boxing, karate, taekwondo, and Muay Thai flow together here. It is one of the most energetic, tempo-intensive combat sports there is: high strike frequency, changing distances, constant movement. No moment resembles another, no fight follows a fixed pattern. This matches the vitality of Type 7: the need for variety, tempo, and an abundance of possibilities, the aversion to rigid, monotonous repetition. The kickboxer doesn't seek the one perfect technique, but the greatest possible range of options — fists, feet, knees, combinations without end. The passion of gluttony shows as an insatiable hunger for ever-new stimuli and movement possibilities.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Boxing: Direct Force and Uncompromising Confrontation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Boxing is the purest, most direct form of physical confrontation that exists in combat sport — no kicks, no throws, no evasive detours into other techniques, only the fist meeting the opponent in a straight line. Two people stand facing each other, without distraction, without detour, measuring their strength in its rawest form. Boxing demands enduring pain and striking back, placing one's own will above exhaustion. This matches the basic stance of Type 8: the preference for direct, unvarnished confrontation, the mistrust of detours and tactics that obscure the actual question of strength. The boxer does not negotiate — he steps up, endures, and strikes back. The passion of lust (excess) shows as the drive to feel and prove one's own strength to the limit and beyond.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Aikido: Harmony Instead of Confrontation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Aikido is the only major martial art that has no attack of its own — its founder Morihei Ueshiba explicitly developed it as the "way of harmonizing energy." The aikidoka does not attack; they dissolve the opponent's attacking energy by joining with it, redirecting it, and channeling it into a circular, harmless throw — without meeting force with force. At the ideal end of the technique stands not a defeated opponent, but a restored order in which no one was seriously hurt. This matches the deepest longing of Type 9: to resolve conflict not through confrontation but through harmonization, not to break the other's energy but to transform it into something peaceful. Aikido is martial art as reconciliation — strength that does not assert itself but integrates. The passion of sloth shows as the deep wish that, at the end of every movement, calm and harmony are restored.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"hunderassen-der-9-typen", label:"Dog Breeds of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"blumenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Flower Types of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function grimmsMaerchenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Grimm's Fairy Tales of the 9 Types", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Hardly any narrative form carries archetypal knowledge as directly as the fairy tale. In a few clear images, the Brothers Grimm's tales tell of exactly those inner struggles, longings, and paths to redemption that the Enneagram also describes — envy, pride, sloth, greed, fear, not as abstract concepts but as witches, wolves, enchanted princes, and brave little tailors. That is exactly why fairy tales matter so much for children: they make the basic principles of this world tangible early on, on a level no textbook can reach — even when they are sometimes uncomfortable, even cruel. Because they speak of reality, not a sanitized version of it. Reading fairy tales aloud has become rarer than it should be — yet children need precisely these early, archetypal maps of the soul.</p>
      <img src="../assets/grimms-maerchen-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Grimms Märchen und Enneagramm-Strukturen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />

      <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem;justify-content:center;margin:0 0 2rem;padding:1rem;background:var(--paper);border-radius:10px;border:1px solid var(--line);">
        <span style="font-size:0.8rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--gray-mid);width:100%;text-align:center;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">Quick jump — go directly to a type</span>
        ${[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9].map(n => `<a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('grimm-typ-${n}').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'})" style="font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.3rem 0.7rem;border-radius:6px;background:linear-gradient(160deg, ${typeColor(n)}22, ${typeColor(n)}0d);color:${typeColor(n)};text-decoration:none;border:1px solid ${typeColor(n)};">Type ${n}</a>`).join("")}
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-1" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – The Frog King · King Thrushbeard · The Iron Stove</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">All three tales speak of transformation through fidelity to principle — of redemption that comes not through feeling but through keeping to the right path. This is exactly the innermost logic of Type 1.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Frog King:</strong> The princess promises, out of desperation, to accept the frog as a companion — and must later keep that promise against her own disgust. Only when she truly honors her word, not because she wants to but because it is right, does the spell break. A primal image of the Type 1 principle: not feeling but the obligation one has entered decides.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>King Thrushbeard:</strong> An arrogant princess who mocks every suitor is led through a harsh lesson in humility — disguised as a beggar, she must experience her own arrogance firsthand before she can find true, unaffected integrity. The purification of false pride into genuine righteousness is a central Type 1 motif.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Iron Stove:</strong> An enchanted prince can only be redeemed if a faithful companion completes a series of difficult, precisely defined tasks — and even when she breaks a single rule, she must make amends through persistent, correct action. Redemption as the reward for persistence despite setbacks.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-2" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Little Red Riding Hood · Rapunzel · Sweet Porridge</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">These three tales circle around care, closeness, and nourishment — and their shadow side: giving, binding, and nurturing that spins out of balance. A core theme of Type 2.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Little Red Riding Hood:</strong> A girl lovingly brings food to her sick grandmother — and falls prey to the wolf through her own naivety and boundlessness. An image of how the pure need to give and provide can blind one to danger and to one's own limits.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Rapunzel:</strong> A sorceress locks a girl into a tower out of supposed care and lets no one near her — the shadow side of Type 2 energy in its purest form: love that turns into possession and control, care that locks the other away instead of letting them grow.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sweet Porridge:</strong> A magic pot that cooks porridge endlessly on command spins out of control and floods the entire village. A fitting image for unchecked giving and nurturing that — once set in motion — no longer knows how to stop itself.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-3" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Our Lady's Child · Doctor Knowall · The Master Thief</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">All three stories deal with image, deception, and the question of what creates real worth — achievement, appearance, or truth. The central conflict of Type 3.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Our Lady's Child:</strong> A girl opens a forbidden door out of curiosity but persistently denies the deed to preserve her image of innocence — and loses her voice for it. Only honest confession, the shedding of the facade, brings redemption. Image versus truth — a core theme of Type 3.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Doctor Knowall:</strong> A simple farmer presents himself, through clever staging, as an all-knowing scholar — and succeeds purely through convincing performance, not through actual knowledge. A tale about the power (and danger) of pure self-presentation.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Master Thief:</strong> A cunning hero proves his abilities through spectacular feats performed for an admiring public — his worth measured by the effect on an audience, not by quiet virtue. Achievement as a stage on which one must shine.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-4" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Snow White · The Fisherman and His Wife · Jorinde and Joringel</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Three stories about uniqueness, insatiable longing, and the pain of loss — the basic melodies of Type 4.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Snow White:</strong> The stepmother cannot bear the daughter's unique beauty and pursues her to a seeming death. A tale about envy of what is special in another, about isolation, the loss of one's own center — and revival through genuine, unaffected love.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Fisherman and His Wife:</strong> A woman wishes for ever more — from cottage to palace, from king to pope, finally wanting to be like God himself — and loses everything in the end. The endless hunger for what is missing as the core of the story.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Jorinde and Joringel:</strong> Two lovers are brutally separated by an enchantment; the longing for the lost connection stretches over years — until persistence and a magical sign make reunion possible. Longing, loss, and holding on to what was once whole.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-5" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Hansel and Gretel · The Brave Little Tailor · Rumpelstiltskin</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Three stories in which intellect, knowledge, and cunning triumph over raw force — the survival strategy of Type 5.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Hansel and Gretel:</strong> Children abandoned in the forest survive not through strength but through sharp thinking — breadcrumbs as an orientation system, a clever ruse to escape the witch. Survival through thought, not physical power.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Brave Little Tailor:</strong> A small, physically inferior tailor defeats giants and a unicorn purely through cleverness, language, and skillful deception. Intellect as the superior weapon against brute force — a primal image of the Type 5 principle.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Rumpelstiltskin:</strong> Knowledge of the little man's secret name becomes the queen's only salvation — knowledge as the key to power, as the one decisive detail that determines victory or defeat.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-6" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Phobic: The Wolf and the Seven Young Kids · Counterphobic: The Story of the Youth Who Went Forth to Learn Fear</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6 appears in the Enneagram in two opposite expressions — the phobic (cautious, vigilant) and the counterphobic (actively confronting fear) variant. Both are found here exemplified.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Wolf and the Seven Young Kids (phobic):</strong> The mother goat warns urgently of the wolf, who disguises himself as her — the young goats must learn to examine every sign carefully before trusting it. Vigilance and mistrust as a survival strategy, safety through constant checking.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Story of the Youth Who Went Forth to Learn Fear (counterphobic):</strong> A young man who literally cannot feel fear actively seeks it out — he confronts ghosts, corpses, and monsters in order to finally learn to shudder. Not avoiding fear but walking straight toward it in order to conquer it — the counterphobic variant of Type 6.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-7" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Hans in Luck · Brother Lustig · Gambling Hansel</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Three figures who meet the seriousness of life with lightness, wit, and constant reframing — the basic stance of Type 7.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Hans in Luck:</strong> Hans keeps trading his wages for something apparently lesser — until he ends up empty-handed and yet feels like the happiest man in the world. The art of reframing every loss positively and never letting one's own good mood be shaken.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Brother Lustig:</strong> A wily, eternally hungry, eternally cheerful ex-soldier swindles his way through life with wit, cheek, and a healthy dose of rule-breaking — he even deals with the devil and Saint Peter. A joy for life that no authority can constrain.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Gambling Hansel:</strong> A figure who treats life as a game, always in motion, always searching for the next diversion — an image of the restlessness and multifacetedness with which Type 7 escapes boredom.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-8" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Strong Hans · The Young Giant · Sharing Joy and Sorrow</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Three stories about raw strength, protective instinct, and how power connects with loyalty — central themes of Type 8.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Strong Hans:</strong> A boy with superhuman strength defeats robbers and a monster in order to free and protect the weaker. Strength that does not exhaust itself in destruction but places itself in the service of the weak — the positive core of Type 8.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Young Giant:</strong> A physically overpowering young man resists exploitation by his father and later by others who want to misuse his strength for their own ends. The theme of justice and self-assertion through strength against oppression.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sharing Joy and Sorrow:</strong> A strong man promises his bride to share both joy and sorrow with her — and is literally tested on this promise. Loyalty and the protector principle, which does not deny its own vulnerability either.</p>

        <h3 id="grimm-typ-9" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – The Blue Light · Lazy Heinz · The Twelve Lazy Servants</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Three stories about withdrawal, sloth, and the underestimated, often overlooked inner strength — the tension field of Type 9.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Blue Light:</strong> A loyal soldier is carelessly dismissed and overlooked after years of service — until he discovers a hidden magical power that finally secures him his due. An image of the quiet, often passed-over strength that slumbers in withdrawal and must first be awakened.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Lazy Heinz:</strong> A figure who, with remarkable ingenuity, evades every effort and would rather go to greater lengths to avoid work than simply do the work itself. Sloth as the central motif, resistance to any activity that might disturb inner peace.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>The Twelve Lazy Servants:</strong> An entire group of servants who reinforce each other's avoidance of work and escape action with ever-new excuses. Collective sloth and conflict avoidance through persistent inaction.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"bagua-zonen-der-9-typen", label:"Bagua Zones of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"kampfkunstarten-der-9-typen", label:"Martial Arts of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"limericks", label:"The 9 Enneagram Limericks"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function enneagrammEmotionalitaetPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Enneagram and Emotionality", "Charts · Emotions & Awareness")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Emotionality in the Enneagram is deliberately understood as value-free. It is neither good nor bad — it is energy in search of a direction. In a conscious state, emotionality can be a rare quality: a blessing for others and for oneself, a source of warmth, depth, and living connection. In unconscious phases of life, however, the very same emotionality can just as easily become a curse — for the people around us, and suddenly for the person themselves. It is the same force that carries or overwhelms, connects or entangles — depending on how much awareness stands beside it.</p>
      <img src="../assets/enneagramm-emotionalitaet.jpg" alt="Enneagram and Emotionality – Chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Two faces of the same force</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Each of the nine types carries its own emotional coloring — its own way in which feeling arises, shows itself, gets held back, or finds expression. This coloring is in itself neutral. It is only the degree of awareness that decides whether it becomes a gift or a burden. In a conscious state, emotionality becomes an instrument of perception: it opens a person to the distress and joy of others, gives depth to expression, and creates real closeness. In an unconscious state, the very same emotionality runs unfiltered — it floods, manipulates, confuses, or freezes, often without the person noticing at first.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The chart shows, for each type, this double possibility: the quality that emerges when emotionality is lived consciously — and the distortion that emerges when it unconsciously takes the wheel. It is the same raw material, only with a different degree of inner observation.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem;justify-content:center;margin:1rem 0 2rem;padding:1rem;background:var(--paper);border-radius:10px;border:1px solid var(--line);">
        <span style="font-size:0.8rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--gray-mid);width:100%;text-align:center;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">Quick jump — go directly to a type</span>
        ${[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9].map(n => `<a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('emo-typ-${n}').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'})" style="font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.3rem 0.7rem;border-radius:6px;background:linear-gradient(160deg, ${typeColor(n)}22, ${typeColor(n)}0d);color:${typeColor(n)};text-decoration:none;border:1px solid ${typeColor(n)};">Type ${n}</a>`).join("")}
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 id="emo-typ-1" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 — The Emotionality of Rightness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 1, emotionality rarely shows itself raw; it is usually filtered through an inner court: before a feeling is shown, it is checked for whether it is appropriate. Lived consciously, this becomes warm, reliable care — feeling that lands exactly where it is truly needed, without exaggeration but with real seriousness.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP1):</strong> Consciously, a quiet, caring diligence shows itself in everyday life — feeling expressed through order and reliability. Unconsciously, this can become permanent inner tension, a constant undercurrent of irritation about what isn't right, discharging as irritability or silent resentment.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO1):</strong> Consciously, emotionality becomes committed responsibility for the whole — compassion that stands up for fair conditions. Unconsciously, it tips into moral outrage, a feeling of one's own righteousness that diminishes others.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX1):</strong> Consciously, a passionate, almost missionary intensity of feeling for the other emerges — emotionality as the drive to make the world better for the partner. Unconsciously, this becomes control disguised as care, a feeling that imposes its own view of things on the other.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-2" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 — The Emotionality of Devotion</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 2, emotionality is oriented outward from the start: toward the other person's feeling, their need, the relationship. Lived consciously, this becomes genuine empathy — feeling that carries without possessing. Unconsciously, the same feeling becomes a means of being needed.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP2):</strong> Consciously, a loving, almost childlike warmth shows itself within the close circle — feeling that creates a sense of shelter. Unconsciously, this becomes clinginess and a quiet demand for attention hidden behind charm.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO2):</strong> Consciously, a generous, community-building compassion emerges that includes many. Unconsciously, it tips into a feeling of indispensability — emotionality becomes proof of one's own importance to the group.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX2):</strong> Consciously, a deep, devoted intensity shows itself in closeness to a single person — seductive warmth that is genuinely present with the other. Unconsciously, this becomes emotional possessiveness, a feeling that wants to own the other in order to feel seen itself.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-3" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 — The Emotionality of Achievement</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 3, feeling is often regulated before it is allowed — it must not get in the way. Lived consciously, this becomes infectious, motivating emotional energy that carries others along. Unconsciously, feeling becomes an afterthought, suppressed in favor of function and success.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP3):</strong> Consciously, a calm, matter-of-fact warmth shows itself, expressed through reliability and quiet care. Unconsciously, feeling is entirely subordinated to functioning — exhaustion and inner emptiness that must not become visible from the outside.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO3):</strong> Consciously, inspiring, team-building energy emerges — feeling that ignites others for a shared goal. Unconsciously, emotionality becomes performance, a means of being admired, without genuine vulnerability being shown.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX3):</strong> Consciously, intense, magnetic passion shows itself, genuine enthusiasm for the partner and the shared project. Unconsciously, this becomes a feeling aimed above all at impressing — closeness that remains tied to success and attractiveness.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-4" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 — The Emotionality of Depth</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 4, emotionality is the true mother tongue — feeling is experienced and expressed here more directly than in any other type. Lived consciously, this becomes a rare ability to transform feeling into beauty, art, and genuine encounter. Unconsciously, the same intensity becomes a trap with no apparent way out.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP4):</strong> Consciously, a quiet, steady emotional depth shows itself, one that carries even heavy feelings without breaking under them. Unconsciously, this becomes tough, silent suffering — a feeling of deprivation that is rarely shared and quietly wears the person down.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO4):</strong> Consciously, an ability emerges to transform one's own suffering into something that offers others comfort and understanding. Unconsciously, it tips into the feeling of being uniquely and unbearably misunderstood in one's pain — shame that withdraws or accuses.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX4):</strong> Consciously, volcanic, immediate emotional honesty shows itself, enabling real, unembellished closeness. Unconsciously, this becomes dramatic swings up and down, a feeling that seeks closeness by provoking conflict and intensity.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-5" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 — The Emotionality of Withdrawal</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 5, feeling is at first not expressed but observed — often from a safe inner distance. Lived consciously, this becomes a rare emotional clarity: feeling that does not flood but is precisely recognized and then consciously shared. Unconsciously, the same distance becomes a wall.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP5):</strong> Consciously, a calm, self-contained sufficiency shows itself, one that offers others a sense of security. Unconsciously, this becomes emotional shutdown — withdrawal into a small, controlled refuge from which feeling barely reaches the outside.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO5):</strong> Consciously, a matter-of-fact but genuine interest in others' inner lives emerges — feeling expressed through understanding. Unconsciously, it tips into superior distance, a feeling that hides behind knowledge and observation instead of showing itself.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX5):</strong> Consciously, a surprisingly intense, almost rapturous devotion to a single person or subject shows itself. Unconsciously, this becomes consuming inner absorption that hardly shows on the surface, leaving the other in the dark.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-6" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 — The Emotionality of Vigilance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 6, feeling is closely tied to safety — it arises as a response to perceived threat or connection. Lived consciously, this becomes loyal, dependable warmth and a fine sense for the feelings of others. Unconsciously, the same sensitivity becomes a source of constant inner unrest.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP6):</strong> Consciously, warm, familial connectedness shows itself, creating safety within the close circle. Unconsciously, this becomes anxious clinginess — a feeling that clings to trusted people out of fear of being alone.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO6):</strong> Consciously, loyalty and emotional commitment to the group emerge, genuine sense of duty. Unconsciously, it tips into mistrust and a search for clear authorities to which one's own feeling can anchor itself.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX6):</strong> Consciously, courageous, forceful emotional intensity shows itself, seeking closeness directly and unguardedly. Unconsciously, this becomes impulsive vehemence — a feeling that answers its own fear with attack rather than retreat.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-7" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 — The Emotionality of Enthusiasm</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 7, feeling instinctively seeks the light, pleasant side — heavy emotions are quickly reframed or glossed over. Lived consciously, this becomes infectious zest for life that pulls others out of difficult moments too. Unconsciously, the same lightness becomes escape.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP7):</strong> Consciously, practical, grounded cheerfulness shows itself, combining safety with enjoyment. Unconsciously, this becomes restless distraction — a feeling constantly seeking the next pleasant stimulus in order not to have to feel unease.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO7):</strong> Consciously, generous, idealistic enthusiasm emerges, carrying others along for a good cause. Unconsciously, it tips into self-sacrifice with hidden resentment — feeling that gives, but inwardly keeps score of what it gets back.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX7):</strong> Consciously, intense, enchanting enthusiasm shows itself, infecting others with its vision. Unconsciously, this becomes impulsive idealization — a feeling that falls in love quickly, only to be disappointed just as quickly and move on.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-8" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 — The Emotionality of Strength</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 8, feeling is rarely named as such — it usually shows itself as action, as energy, as presence. Lived consciously, this becomes strong, protective devotion that gives others a sense of safety. Unconsciously, the same force becomes an overwhelming weight.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP8):</strong> Consciously, robust, uncomplicated care shows itself — feeling expressed through action and provision. Unconsciously, this becomes thick-skinnedness, a covering-over of one's own vulnerability through ever more control.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO8):</strong> Consciously, loyal, self-sacrificing strength emerges in service of a group or cause, a feeling of responsibility that carries. Unconsciously, it tips into authoritarian dominance — emotionality that tolerates no opposing view.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX8):</strong> Consciously, intense, devoted passion shows itself, loving the partner wholly and unconditionally. Unconsciously, this becomes possessive intensity — a feeling that confuses closeness with control.</p>

        <h3 id="emo-typ-9" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 — The Emotionality of Harmony</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 9, one's own feeling is often set aside first to preserve peace — one's own emotionality steps back behind that of others. Lived consciously, this becomes a rare, encompassing capacity for compassion that can hold many perspectives at once. Unconsciously, the same capacity becomes disappearing into the feelings of others.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP9):</strong> Consciously, quiet, carrying calm shows itself, radiating a sense of shelter without much fuss. Unconsciously, this becomes emotional numbing — withdrawal into habit and distraction in order to avoid feeling one's own emotion at all.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO9):</strong> Consciously, a warm, connecting presence emerges that holds groups together without pushing itself to the foreground. Unconsciously, it tips into self-effacement — a feeling that shows up for everyone else, but not for oneself.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX9):</strong> Consciously, intense, merging devotion to a beloved person shows itself — feeling that fully gives itself over to the other. Unconsciously, this becomes self-abandonment, a loss of one's own identity within the other's feelings and preferences.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("sprache-unserer-beziehungen", "How the 9 types experience closeness, bonding, and feeling in relationships — and what each type needs to truly arrive emotionally.", "Die Sprache unserer Beziehungen")}
        ${bookTip("die-sprache-unserer-sexualitaet", "Emotionality and instinct working together — how feeling and desire connect in the 9 types and their subtypes.", "Die Sprache unserer Sexualität")}
        ${bookTip("die-verborgene-dynamik-der-27-subtypen", "The 27 subtypes in detail — the normal, boosting, and counter variant of every Enneagram type explained in depth.", "Die verborgene Dynamik der 27 Subtypen")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"basisemotionen", label:"Basic Emotions of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"zornverhalten", label:"Anger Behavior of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"leidenschaft-und-wunde", label:"Passion and Wound"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function enneagrammInstinktPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Enneagram and Instinct", "Charts · Emotions & Awareness")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Instinct in the Enneagram is the immediate, bodily drive — the energy that acts before the mind has finished thinking. It, too, is value-free in itself: neither good nor bad, but pure life force in search of a direction. Lived consciously, instinct becomes healthy presence, timely action that steps in at the right moment, and a reliable feel for what is needed now. Lived unconsciously, the same energy becomes impulsivity, reaction without pause — the body acts before the person understands why.</p>
      <img src="../assets/enneagramm-instinkt.jpg" alt="Enneagram and Instinct – Chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Two faces of the same force</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Each of the nine types carries its own instinctual coloring — its own way in which bodily energy arises, gets held back, or discharges into action. This coloring is in itself neutral. It is only the degree of awareness that decides whether it becomes a reliable inner compass needle or a blind reflex. In a conscious state, instinct becomes an instrument of perception: it grounds a person in action, anchors them in the body, and gives presence along with a fine sense for the right moment. In an unconscious state, the very same instinctual force runs unfiltered — it overwhelms, hardens, freezes, or exhausts itself, often without the person noticing at first.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The chart shows, for each type, this double possibility: the quality that emerges when instinct is lived consciously — and the distortion that emerges when it unconsciously takes the wheel. It is the same bodily energy, only with a different degree of inner observation.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem;justify-content:center;margin:1rem 0 2rem;padding:1rem;background:var(--paper);border-radius:10px;border:1px solid var(--line);">
        <span style="font-size:0.8rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--gray-mid);width:100%;text-align:center;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">Quick jump — go directly to a type</span>
        ${[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9].map(n => `<a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('ins-typ-${n}').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'})" style="font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.3rem 0.7rem;border-radius:6px;background:linear-gradient(160deg, ${typeColor(n)}22, ${typeColor(n)}0d);color:${typeColor(n)};text-decoration:none;border:1px solid ${typeColor(n)};">Type ${n}</a>`).join("")}
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 id="ins-typ-1" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 — The Instinct of Rightness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 1, instinct is closely coupled to inner resistance: the body tenses where something isn't right, before the mind has even named it. Lived consciously, this becomes precise, hands-on drive that corrects at the right moment without overcorrecting.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP1):</strong> Consciously, an alert, physically grounded sense of order in one's own surroundings shows itself — instinct that takes practical action. Unconsciously, this becomes chronic muscular tension, a body permanently braced.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO1):</strong> Consciously, determined, big-picture drive emerges that addresses wrongs directly. Unconsciously, it tips into reflexive lecturing, a body that draws itself up to correct others.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX1):</strong> Consciously, intense, almost fiery energy shows itself, sweeping the partner along into shared change. Unconsciously, this becomes impulsive impatience — a body that pushes before the other is ready.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-2" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 — The Instinct of Devotion</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 2, instinctual energy flows immediately outward, toward the other — the body moves before the mind decides to help. Lived consciously, this becomes warm, reliable presence that senses where it's needed.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP2):</strong> Consciously, playful, physically warm affection shows itself within the close circle — instinct that seeks and gives closeness directly. Unconsciously, this becomes restless busyness, a body that must constantly be doing something for others to settle itself.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO2):</strong> Consciously, energetic, community-building drive emerges that keeps many people in view at once. Unconsciously, it tips into excessive activism — a body that exhausts itself to stay indispensable.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX2):</strong> Consciously, magnetic, devoted physical presence shows itself, entirely focused on one person. Unconsciously, this becomes instinctive courting of attention that knows no pause.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-3" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 — The Instinct of Achievement</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 3, instinct is closely tied to pace and efficiency — the body sets itself in motion as soon as a goal is visible. Lived consciously, this becomes effortless, infectious drive that moves others into action.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP3):</strong> Consciously, tireless, down-to-earth productivity shows itself, creating security through steady doing. Unconsciously, this becomes drivenness — a body that cannot switch off because stillness feels like danger.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO3):</strong> Consciously, dynamic, team-leading energy emerges that moves a shared project forward. Unconsciously, it tips into restless status-striving — a body in permanent self-optimization.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX3):</strong> Consciously, magnetic, self-assured physical presence shows itself that attracts without staging. Unconsciously, this becomes restless self-presentation, putting energy into effect rather than genuine closeness.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-4" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 — The Instinct of Intensity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 4, instinct seeks the special, the intensely lived — the body reacts strongly to beauty and pain alike. Lived consciously, this becomes deep, embodied expressive power that turns experience into form.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP4):</strong> Consciously, tough, steady bodily strength shows itself, carrying even hard phases through. Unconsciously, this becomes quiet exhaustion, a body that silently endures deprivation instead of expressing it.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO4):</strong> Consciously, the ability emerges to turn one's own intensity into expression that touches others. Unconsciously, it tips into demonstrative withdrawal, a body that visibly removes itself from the fray.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX4):</strong> Consciously, volcanic, immediate bodily energy shows itself, bringing real intensity into relationship. Unconsciously, this becomes dramatic escalation, a body that seeks closeness through intensification.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-5" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 — The Instinct of Withdrawal</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 5, instinctual energy is first held back, conserved, observed — the body restrains itself before acting. Lived consciously, this becomes calm, economical strength that is exactly present when needed.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP5):</strong> Consciously, quiet sufficiency shows itself, a body that gets by with little while staying fully present to itself. Unconsciously, this becomes near-total withdrawal from the body, a life lived mostly in the head.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO5):</strong> Consciously, matter-of-fact, observant presence emerges that contributes in a targeted, selective way. Unconsciously, it tips into detached passivity, a body that prefers analyzing to acting.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX5):</strong> Consciously, surprisingly intense devotion to a person or subject shows itself once trust exists. Unconsciously, this becomes consuming inner absorption that barely shows itself physically at all.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-6" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 — The Instinct of Vigilance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 6, the body is constantly tuned to signals of danger or safety — instinct shows itself as fine alertness. Lived consciously, this becomes fast, accurate responsiveness that protects without paralyzing.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP6):</strong> Consciously, provident, down-to-earth vigilance shows itself, establishing safety in daily life. Unconsciously, this becomes nervous tension, a body permanently braced for the next problem.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO6):</strong> Consciously, loyal, reliable presence emerges in service of the group. Unconsciously, it tips into suspicious overwatchfulness, a body that keeps a wary eye on authorities.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX6):</strong> Consciously, courageous, direct bodily strength shows itself, turning fear into confrontation rather than retreat. Unconsciously, this becomes impulsive readiness to attack, a body that strikes before it has checked.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-7" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 — The Instinct of Movement</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 7, the body wants to keep moving, take in new stimuli, miss nothing — instinct shows itself as a constant pull forward. Lived consciously, this becomes lively, infectious energy that brings momentum into any situation.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP7):</strong> Consciously, practical, pleasure-loving physicality shows itself, combining safety with experience. Unconsciously, this becomes restless busyness, a body that needs distraction so as not to feel unease.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO7):</strong> Consciously, generous, inspiring energy emerges in service of a good cause. Unconsciously, it tips into self-overextension, a body that keeps giving until its reserves run dry.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX7):</strong> Consciously, intense, fascinating presence shows itself that spontaneously sweeps others along. Unconsciously, this becomes impulsive stimulus-seeking, a body that leaps from one enthusiasm to the next.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-8" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 — The Instinct of Strength</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 8, the body itself is the message: powerful, present, immediate. Lived consciously, this becomes protective strength that holds space without taking it from others.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP8):</strong> Consciously, robust, unshakeable presence shows itself, creating safety for self and others. Unconsciously, this becomes uncompromising toughness, a body that never lets vulnerability show at all.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO8):</strong> Consciously, powerful, loyal leadership energy emerges in service of a group. Unconsciously, it tips into dominant controlling behavior, a body that claims space instead of sharing it.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX8):</strong> Consciously, devoted, immediate passion shows itself, wholly embracing the partner. Unconsciously, this becomes possessive intensity, a body that confuses closeness with taking over.</p>

        <h3 id="ins-typ-9" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 — The Instinct of Calm</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 9, instinctual energy often withdraws to preserve peace — the body dampens itself before conflict can arise. Lived consciously, this becomes carrying, grounded presence that radiates safety without imposing.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP9):</strong> Consciously, cozy, physically grounded ease shows itself, creating a sense of shelter. Unconsciously, this becomes inertia — a body that numbs itself in habit and comfort.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO9):</strong> Consciously, connecting, balancing energy emerges that holds groups together. Unconsciously, it tips into self-effacement, a body that shows up for everyone else while forgetting its own energy.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX9):</strong> Consciously, intense, merging bodily presence shows itself, fully oriented toward the beloved person. Unconsciously, this becomes energetic fusion to the point of self-abandonment, a body that loses its own boundaries in the other.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("die-sprache-unserer-sexualitaet", "Instinct and emotionality working together — how desire and bodily energy connect in the 9 types and their subtypes.", "Die Sprache unserer Sexualität")}
        ${bookTip("die-verborgene-dynamik-der-27-subtypen", "The 27 subtypes in detail — the normal, boosting, and counter variant of every Enneagram type explained in depth.", "Die verborgene Dynamik der 27 Subtypen")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"enneagramm-emotionalitaet", label:"Enneagram and Emotionality"},
        {route:"enneagramm-intellekt", label:"Enneagram and Intellect"},
        {route:"leidenschaft-und-wunde", label:"Passion and Wound"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function enneagrammIntellektPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Enneagram and Intellect", "Charts · Emotions & Awareness")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Intellect in the Enneagram is the ability to pause, order, and understand before acting or feeling. It, too, is value-free in itself: neither good nor bad, but mental clarity in search of a direction. Lived consciously, intellect becomes genuine insight, an overview that recognizes connections without getting lost in them. Lived unconsciously, the same ability becomes rumination, distance, a mind that thinks instead of living — reasoning that pushes itself between the person and the world.</p>
      <img src="../assets/enneagramm-intellekt.jpg" alt="Enneagram and Intellect – Chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Two faces of the same force</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Each of the nine types carries its own intellectual coloring — its own way of using the mind to order the world. This coloring is in itself neutral. It is only the degree of awareness that decides whether it becomes clear insight or a flight from one's own experience. In a conscious state, intellect becomes an instrument of perception: it creates overview, recognizes patterns, and links feeling and action with understanding. In an unconscious state, the very same mind runs unfiltered — it ruminates, justifies, distances, or freezes in analysis, often without the person noticing at first.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The chart shows, for each type, this double possibility: the quality that emerges when intellect is lived consciously — and the distortion that emerges when it unconsciously takes the wheel. It is the same mental force, only with a different degree of inner observation.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.5rem;justify-content:center;margin:1rem 0 2rem;padding:1rem;background:var(--paper);border-radius:10px;border:1px solid var(--line);">
        <span style="font-size:0.8rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--gray-mid);width:100%;text-align:center;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">Quick jump — go directly to a type</span>
        ${[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9].map(n => `<a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('int-typ-${n}').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'})" style="font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:700;padding:0.3rem 0.7rem;border-radius:6px;background:linear-gradient(160deg, ${typeColor(n)}22, ${typeColor(n)}0d);color:${typeColor(n)};text-decoration:none;border:1px solid ${typeColor(n)};">Type ${n}</a>`).join("")}
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 id="int-typ-1" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 — The Intellect of Discernment</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 1, the mind is trained to separate right from wrong — an inner rulebook checks every situation. Lived consciously, this becomes clear, differentiated judgment that sees nuance instead of only black and white.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP1):</strong> Consciously, practical, solution-oriented thinking shows itself, creating order in daily life. Unconsciously, this becomes an inner critic that scans every detail for flaws.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO1):</strong> Consciously, systemic thinking emerges that keeps fair structures for everyone in view. Unconsciously, it tips into self-righteous rule-mongering, a mind that measures others against its own standard.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX1):</strong> Consciously, committed, persuasive thinking shows itself that wins others over for a better cause. Unconsciously, this becomes missionary zeal, a mind that no longer allows any other viewpoint.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-2" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 — The Intellect of Relationship</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 2, the mind thinks primarily in relationships — who needs what, who feels how. Lived consciously, this becomes fine social sense that recognizes needs without losing itself.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP2):</strong> Consciously, warm, everyday-close understanding of the people in the close circle shows itself. Unconsciously, this becomes constant thinking-for-others that leaves the mind hardly any room of its own.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO2):</strong> Consciously, strategic thinking emerges for the good of many, an overview of social networks. Unconsciously, it tips into calculated maneuvering over who owes whom what.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX2):</strong> Consciously, intuitive sense for the one person shows itself, a mind entirely tuned to them. Unconsciously, this becomes ruminating circling around the question of whether one is loved enough.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-3" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 — The Intellect of Effectiveness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 3, thinking is goal-directed and efficient — the mind plans the shortest route to success. Lived consciously, this becomes clear, pragmatic strategy that brings others along rather than merely functioning.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP3):</strong> Consciously, down-to-earth, efficient organizational talent shows itself, creating security through smart action. Unconsciously, this becomes constant optimization-thinking that never comes to rest.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO3):</strong> Consciously, strategic thinking emerges that cleverly leads a team toward a shared goal. Unconsciously, it tips into image calculation, a mind that precisely computes every effect.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX3):</strong> Consciously, magnetic, self-assured thinking shows itself that wins the partner over through real substance. Unconsciously, this becomes permanent self-staging in the mind before any action even happens.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-4" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 — The Intellect of Meaning</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 4, the mind seeks not efficiency but meaning — what makes this experience unique? Lived consciously, this becomes deep, symbolic understanding that turns experience into meaning.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP4):</strong> Consciously, quiet, steady reflection shows itself that can put even hard things into words. Unconsciously, this becomes tough rumination over one's own inadequacy.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO4):</strong> Consciously, the ability emerges to put one's own experience into words that comfort others. Unconsciously, it tips into constant comparison, a mind that keeps re-proving its own difference.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX4):</strong> Consciously, immediate, honest thinking shows itself that creates closeness through clarity. Unconsciously, this becomes dramatizing mental cinema that tests every relationship for intensity.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-5" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 — The Intellect of Understanding</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 5, thinking is home ground — the mind is used here more directly and more readily than anywhere else. Lived consciously, this becomes rare clarity, an overview that makes complex connections simple.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP5):</strong> Consciously, focused, independent expertise shows itself that achieves much with little effort. Unconsciously, this becomes near-total retreat into thinking, far from one's own body and feeling.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO5):</strong> Consciously, expertise emerges that willingly shares knowledge with others. Unconsciously, it tips into superior distance, a mind that entrenches itself behind expertise.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX5):</strong> Consciously, intense, deepened engagement with a person or subject shows itself. Unconsciously, this becomes secret, walled-off thinking that is hardly shared at all.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-6" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 — The Intellect of Contingency</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 6, the mind thinks in scenarios — what could go wrong, how can one prepare? Lived consciously, this becomes smart, realistic foresight that assesses risk without getting lost in it.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP6):</strong> Consciously, careful, forward-looking planning in daily life shows itself, creating security. Unconsciously, this becomes exhausting rumination over every worst-case scenario.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO6):</strong> Consciously, loyal, systemic thinking emerges that creates reliability for the group. Unconsciously, it tips into distrust of authorities, a mind constantly searching for proof.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX6):</strong> Consciously, courageous, direct thinking shows itself that turns uncertainty into action rather than rumination. Unconsciously, this becomes impulsive black-and-white thinking that no longer allows any middle ground.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-7" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 — The Intellect of Possibility</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 7, the mind leaps from idea to idea, linking, associating, seeing options everywhere. Lived consciously, this becomes sparkling, creative clarity that opens perspectives instead of confusing.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP7):</strong> Consciously, practical, solution-loving thinking shows itself that smartly combines enjoyment and security. Unconsciously, this becomes jumpy restlessness, a mind that never stays long with one thought.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO7):</strong> Consciously, idealistic, connecting thinking emerges that excites others for a vision. Unconsciously, it tips into self-overextension through too many parallel plans.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX7):</strong> Consciously, fascinating, visionary thinking shows itself that infects others. Unconsciously, this becomes impulsive idealization, a mind that excites quickly and moves on just as fast.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-8" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 — The Intellect of Clarity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 8, thinking is direct and unfiltered — the mind gets straight to the point without detours. Lived consciously, this becomes strategic clarity that quickly cuts to the heart of a complex situation.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP8):</strong> Consciously, robust, down-to-earth judgment shows itself, organizing security for self and others. Unconsciously, this becomes uncompromising black-and-white thinking that tolerates no gray areas.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO8):</strong> Consciously, far-sighted strategic thinking emerges in service of a group or cause. Unconsciously, it tips into authoritarian self-righteousness, a mind that tolerates no opposing view.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX8):</strong> Consciously, passionate, decisive thinking shows itself, entirely focused on the partner and the shared venture. Unconsciously, this becomes possessive controlling thought.</p>

        <h3 id="int-typ-9" style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 — The Intellect of Mediation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In Type 9, the mind instinctively seeks the connecting perspective — it can understand many viewpoints at once. Lived consciously, this becomes wise, balancing clarity that mediates without losing itself.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Self-Preservation (SP9):</strong> Consciously, calm, practical thinking shows itself that smoothly orders daily life. Unconsciously, this becomes mental inertia, a mind that prefers distraction to deciding.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Social (SO9):</strong> Consciously, connecting thinking emerges that brings different standpoints together. Unconsciously, it tips into self-effacement, a mind that sets its own opinion aside to avoid conflict.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sexual (SX9):</strong> Consciously, intense, empathetic understanding of the beloved person shows itself. Unconsciously, this becomes fusion in thinking, a mind that dissolves its own view entirely into the other's.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("die-verborgene-dynamik-der-27-subtypen", "The 27 subtypes in detail — the normal, boosting, and counter variant of every Enneagram type explained in depth.", "Die verborgene Dynamik der 27 Subtypen")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal perspectives – volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"enneagramm-emotionalitaet", label:"Enneagram and Emotionality"},
        {route:"enneagramm-instinkt", label:"Enneagram and Instinct"},
        {route:"drei-zentren", label:"The Three Centers"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function baguaZonenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Bagua-Zonen der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The Bagua zones of Feng Shui divide a house, an apartment, or even a single room into nine life areas — and these nine areas correspond remarkably closely to the nine Enneagram patterns. To improve one of the nine areas in a targeted way, one can consciously enhance that particular zone with Feng Shui measures — and in doing so, understand the inner principle behind it.</p>
      <img src="../assets/bagua-zonen-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Bagua-Zonen der 9 Enneagrammtypen im Feng Shui – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Fame: Integrity Made Visible</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Bagua zone "Fame" stands in Feng Shui for reputation and the way one is perceived in the world — not as loud success, but as the good name that grows out of right action. This is exactly the logic of Type 1: he does not strive for fame for its own sake, but to be seen as upright, correct, and trustworthy. His reputation should be the fruit of his inner order, not the goal of his actions. In Feng Shui, this zone is traditionally strengthened with fire, with light, and with symbols of one's own values — fitting for a type whose reputation is inseparably tied to his inner moral compass.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Relationships: Connection as the Center of Life</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Relationships" zone stands for love, closeness, and the quality of all intimate bonds — and that is exactly the center around which Type 2's life revolves. No other type directs their attention so consistently toward the wellbeing of others, toward the success of connection, toward the fine sense of what a partner or friend needs right now. In Feng Shui, this zone is activated through pair symbols, warmth, and consciously created closeness — a principle Type 2 already lives: they create closeness before anyone asks for it, because relationship is not an addition to life for them, but its foundation.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Career: The Visible Path Forward</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Career" zone traditionally lies at the entrance area in Feng Shui — where the life path begins and where movement, flow, and progress arise. This matches the core motivation of Type 3 precisely: he is the type who wants to move forward, who sets and achieves goals, who defines his worth through visible achievement and progress. Where others hesitate, Type 3 is already moving on — standstill is his greatest discomfort. In Feng Shui, this zone is strengthened through flowing water, clarity, and open paths — images for a person whose energy constantly pushes forward.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Helpful People: Allies for One's Own Path</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Helpful People" zone stands for mentors, companions, and those rare people who truly understand and support us — not the crowd, but the few, genuine connections. That is exactly what Type 4 seeks: not superficial contacts, but deep, authentic encounters with people who see their uniqueness without trying to bend it. They don't need a mass of support, but the one kindred spirit who confirms them in being exactly who they are. In Feng Shui, this zone is activated through symbols of support from outside — fitting for a type who often walks their path alone, yet is deeply grateful for the few who truly accompany them along the way.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Knowledge: The Retreat of Understanding</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Knowledge" zone stands for self-cultivation, study, and quiet inner retreat — the space where one withdraws in order to understand. Hardly any type embodies this principle as clearly as Type 5: he withdraws in order to gather knowledge, penetrate connections, and build an inner map of the world before turning back to it. In Feng Shui, this zone is traditionally kept quiet, undisturbed, and minimal — a place for books, for silence, for concentrated thought. For Type 5 this is no matter of chance but a necessity: only in protected retreat can he gain the clarity he later shares with others again.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Family: The Safe Circle</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Family" zone stands for belonging, rootedness, and the supportive social network that provides stability — and that stability is exactly what Type 6 longs for most. Security does not arise for him through going it alone, but through reliable bonds to a group, a family, a system he can trust and to which he in turn remains loyal. In Feng Shui, this zone is strengthened through family photos, symbols of heritage, and stable, grounded materials — a principle Type 6 understands instinctively: roots are not a step backward, but the foundation from which trust can grow in the first place.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Children: Creativity, Play, and Joy for the Future</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Children" zone stands in Feng Shui for creativity, playfulness, and new projects — for everything that arises from pure joy of life and points toward the future. That is the innate world of Type 7: he lives out of a childlike capacity for enthusiasm, loves creating something new, letting ideas sprout, and experiencing life as an adventure rather than a duty. In Feng Shui, this zone is activated through color, symbols of fertility, and space for spontaneous expression — very much in keeping with a type who never quite says goodbye to their playful, curious inner childhood.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Wealth: Resources as the Foundation of Freedom</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Wealth" zone stands for material prosperity, resources, and the abundance from which one remains capable of acting — and that is of central importance to Type 8. It is rarely about possession as an end in itself, but about independence: whoever has their own resources need not submit to anyone and can protect those who depend on them. In Feng Shui, this zone is strengthened through symbols of growth, consciously cultivated abundance, and stable, powerful elements — fitting for a type for whom strength always also means taking responsibility for one's own territory and one's own people.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Tai-Chi (Health): The Quiet Center of Everything</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The "Tai-Chi" zone sits at the center of the Bagua — it stands for health, balance, and the resting midpoint from which all eight other life areas are held together. It is fitting that Type 9, of all types, corresponds to this center: he is the mediator, the balancer, the one who naturally keeps the whole picture in view and brings others together rather than losing himself in a single life area. In Feng Shui, this central zone is deliberately kept open, quiet, and undisturbed — an image that mirrors Type 9's deepest longing: inner peace as the supporting ground from which everything else finds its place.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"kampfkunstarten-der-9-typen", label:"Martial Arts of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"blumenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Flower Types of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"superpower-der-9-typen", label:"Superpower of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function hunderassenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Hunderassen der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The assignment of nine dog breeds to the nine Enneagram principles is a creative method to connect the symbolic qualities and characteristics of these different dogs with the various personality types of the Enneagram. Each breed carries an unmistakable nature within itself — from the precise Border Collie to the gentle Bernese Mountain Dog.</p>
      <img src="../assets/hunderassen-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Hunderassen der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Doberman: Performance, Vigilance, Success</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Border Collie is known for working hard, being highly trainable, and setting high standards in its work — similar to Type 1, which strives for perfection and order. It is considered the most intelligent dog in the world: In studies, a Border Collie learned over a thousand words and distinguished objects by their shape. Its gaze — the famous "Eye" — is a hypnotic fixating stare at the sheep that freezes it in immobility. No other dog has developed this technique. For the Border Collie, there is only one way to complete a task: the right way. The passion of anger manifests as inner restlessness when things do not run as they should.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Basset Hound: Depth, Uniqueness, Expression</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Golden Retriever is an exceptionally friendly, affectionate, and ever-ready companion who knows how to please and support its owner — exactly what fits the caring nature of Type 2. Golden Retrievers have been used as therapy dogs, guide dogs, and rescue dogs — not because of their physical strength, but because of their unconditional devotion. They sense pain and grief in humans and respond with bodily contact and closeness. Their pride lies not in success, but in being loved for simply being there. The passion of pride manifests as a deep desire to be indispensable.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Dalmatian: Energy, Playfulness, Curiosity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Dobermans are athletic, intelligent, and eager-to-work dogs that often excel in demanding roles such as guard or working dogs. Performance and ambition reflect the success and striving of Type 3. The Doberman was bred by a tax collector who wanted a dog that inspired respect while being intelligent enough to read situations. The result: a dog that can be trained perfectly for the role expected of it. Its appearance is calibrated — no superfluous fur, no superfluous character. The passion of vanity shows as total identification with the role.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 4 – Basset Hound: Tiefe, Einzigartigkeit, Ausdruck</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Basset Hounds often look melancholy and sometimes make a thoughtful or sad impression — which fits well overall with the characteristics of Type 4. They possess an unmistakable anatomy: short legs, an elongated body, and long drooping ears that enhance their sense of smell by stirring up scent molecules from the ground. No other dog looks like a Basset — and no other dog wears its nature so openly on its face. Its gaze is an entire psychology. The passion of envy manifests as a longing for connection paired with a simultaneous feeling of being different.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Shiba Inu: Independence and Inner World</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Shiba Inu is known for its independence and powers of observation. Its quiet and analytical nature fits well with Type 5, which is often withdrawn and inquisitive. The Shiba Inu is a thousands-of-years-old Japanese hunting dog — one of the few dogs that has hardly changed through domestication. It is cat-like: cleans itself, decides for itself, and chooses its proximity carefully. It does not show affection on command. Its trust must be earned, and once established, it is deep and quiet. The passion of avarice manifests as thriftiness with energy, time, and affection — none of which is wasted.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Shetland Sheepdog: Loyalty and Vigilance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Shetland Sheepdogs are extremely loyal and vigilant, always ready to protect their family. This reflects the reliability and faithfulness of Type 6. Shelties were bred on the windswept Shetland Islands — a harsh environment that produced dogs that prefer to look twice before acting once. They bark at the unfamiliar, inspect situations, and observe patterns. But within their group, they possess a reliability that is hard to surpass: they follow their family like a shadow. The passion of fear shows as hypervigilance — an inner early warning system that never completely shuts off.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 7 – Dalmatiner: Energie, Verspieltheit, Neugier</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Dalmatians are lively and curious dogs that enjoy playing and experiencing new adventures. Their cheerful and adventurous nature fits Type 7 well. The Dalmatian is impossible to miss — its spotted coat is a visual invitation. It was bred as a carriage dog that could run alongside horses for hours without tiring: a dog built for movement, speed, and the next destination. It was a regular in the circus; in the fire department, it became a mascot. The passion of gluttony shows as an insatiable hunger for experience, contact, and the next adventure.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Rottweiler: Strength and Protective Instinct</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Rottweilers are strong, self-confident dogs with a pronounced protective instinct that fits well with the assertive and protective nature of Type 8. The Rottweiler descends from Roman drover dogs that drove cattle herds over the Alps. In Rottweil, they were later used as butchers' dogs — pulling carts and carrying their master's money in pouches around their necks because no one dared to rob a Rottweiler. The Rottweiler is not an aggressor — it is a protector. The passion of lust shows as an unslaked hunger for control and safety for those entrusted to it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Bernese Mountain Dog: Gentleness and Even-Temperedness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Bernese Mountain Dogs are known for their gentle and even-tempered nature. They are friendly and peaceful, which fits well with the harmony-seeking and mediating nature of Type 9. The Bernese Mountain Dog was bred in the Swiss Alps as an all-rounder — it herded cattle, pulled carts, and served as a companion dog. Its nature is that of a mediator: it does not argue, it does not push, and it takes up no more space than necessary. In a group of dogs, it is the one that dissolves conflict merely by being there. The passion of sloth shows as a deep desire for everything to remain as it is — big, warm, quiet, together.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"blumenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Flower Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"pferdearten-der-9-typen", label:"Horse Breeds of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"baerenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bear Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function pferdearteDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Pferdearten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The assignment of nine horse breeds to the nine Enneagram principles is a creative method to connect the various aspects and characteristics of these horses with the personality types of the Enneagram. Each breed impressively embodies the essential nature of its type — from the noble precision of the Lipizzan to the peaceful even-temperedness of the Connemara Pony.</p>
      <img src="../assets/pferdearten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Pferdearten der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Thoroughbred: Born to Win</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">With its legendary precision and discipline, the Lipizzan embodies the Reformer type in an ideal manner. This breed is known for its skills in classical dressage, which require supreme precision and years of patient practice. The Lipizzan symbolizes the striving for perfection and the adherence to high standards. Its calm, controlled power and the ability to execute complex movements with grace reflect the passion of Type 1: the inner drive toward perfection, which is sometimes experienced as anger at the imperfect. No other horse has inscribed itself so deeply into the arts of horsemanship — the High School is its realm, and in this realm there is no "good enough."</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Shire Horse: Power Without Apology</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The American Quarter Horse stands for helpfulness and the joy of serving others — just like the Helper type of the Enneagram. These horses are friendly, versatile, and ready to assist with many tasks. They are known for their cooperative nature and their willingness to serve others. Their pride lies in giving and in the feeling of being needed. As one of the most versatile working horses of North America — equally at home on ranch, rodeo, and racetrack — they symbolize the warm affection and care that characterize Type 2.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Connemara Pony: Harmony as Home</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Thoroughbred is the breed identified with success, performance, and competition — a perfect mirror of the Achiever. Thoroughbreds are known for their speed and success in racing. They embody ambition, goal-orientation, and the desire to be successful. Their entire nature is geared toward performance: every muscle fiber, every breath strives toward the finish line. The passion of vanity — the self-image as a winner — is archetypally anchored in this breed. They were not bred to be as happy as possible, but to be as fast as possible — and it shows.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Arabian: Beauty and Uniqueness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Arabian is known for its beauty, elegance, and distinct personality — traits that reflect the Individualist type. Arabians possess a distinct personality, elegance, and individuality. They radiate creativity, uniqueness, and grace. Their deep, expressive gaze and sensitive character make them unmistakable. The passion of envy — the feeling of being different while simultaneously longing for connection — expresses itself in their intimate yet headstrong nature. The Arabian is not a horse for everyone; whoever leads it must understand it, not try to dominate it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Mustang: Freedom and Inner Knowledge</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Mustang embodies independence, autonomy, and wisdom — the core qualities of the Observer. Mustangs are wild horses that often live withdrawn and independent lives. They symbolize the desire for autonomy and knowledge, as well as the ability to survive alone. The Mustang observes the world from a distance, retreats into its inner world, and accumulates knowledge that it guards like a treasure. Millennia in the wilderness have engraved an intelligence into it that no breeder can replicate: It knows where water is, where danger lurks, where the herd is safe. Its passion — avarice with time, energy, and trust — manifests in the careful dosing of its proximity.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Clydesdale: Loyalty as Strength</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Clydesdale stands for loyalty, reliability, and a sense of community — exactly the qualities that distinguish the Loyalist. Clydesdales are strong, reliable draft horses. They stand for loyalty, dependability, and the willingness to work hard and support their community. They do not use their colossal strength for themselves, but in service of the group — field work, transport, carrying loads without complaining. The passion of fear — the constant checking whether everything is safe — transforms in the Clydesdale into irreplaceable fidelity: Anyone who has ever led a Clydesdale knows that it does not run away.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Andalusian: The Celebration of Movement</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Andalusian stands for liveliness, enthusiasm, and the joy of adventure — a radiant image of the Enthusiast type. Andalusians are lively, energetic, and versatile. They love adventure and are known for their cheerful and enthusiastic nature. With flowing mane and springy step, they dance through life — every moment a new experience, every encounter a new opportunity. Their history as a horse of Iberian knights and fiesta participants has given them a natural sense of spectacle: The Andalusian performs. The passion of gluttony expresses itself in their inexhaustible appetite for movement, stimulation, and contact.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 8 – Shire Horse: Macht ohne Entschuldigung</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">With its imposing size, power, and assertiveness, the Shire Horse impressively symbolizes the Challenger type. Shire Horses are powerful and dominant horses that embody strength and assertiveness. They are brave and ready to take on challenges. Their mere presence moves entire landscapes — these horses know no flinching. As the heaviest horses in the world, they were used to haul war equipment, drive factories, and move harbor loads. The passion of lust manifests as an unslaked hunger for power, influence, and the feeling of shaping the world — or at least pulling the plow.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 9 – Connemara Pony: Harmonie als Heimat</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Connemara Pony is known for its calm and even-tempered nature — an apt image of the Peacemaker. Connemara Ponies are calm and balanced. They are friendly and patient, strive for harmony, and are easy to manage. Hailing from the rugged boglands of Ireland, these robust, frugal ponies manage with everything and wish to be a burden to no one. They adapt to any rider, any terrain, any weather — without theater. Their passion of sloth shows as a deep desire not to let inner peace be disturbed by conflict: A Connemara causes no trouble and expects none.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"hunderassen-der-9-typen", label:"Dog Breeds of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"nagetierarten-der-9-typen", label:"Rodent Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"baerenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bear Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}


function nagetierartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Nagetierarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Rodents are the most successful mammal group on Earth—over 40 percent of all mammal species are rodents. They live on every continent, in every climate, in every niche. Their recipe for success is diversity: every species has developed a completely unique principle to survive and thrive. Nine rodent species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/nagetierarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Nagetierarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Prairie Dog: Order Underground</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The prairie dog builds the most sophisticated social systems among all rodents: its underground towns—so-called villages—can encompass hundreds of kilometers of tunnels, with clearly separated areas for sleeping, giving birth, storage, and waste. Every area has its place, and that place is maintained. Furthermore, prairie dogs communicate with one of the most complex animal languages known: researchers have discovered that their calls do not just mean "danger," but also encode the size, color, shape, and speed of an intruder—they literally describe them. A prairie dog call conveys: A large, round, slow, brown human is approaching from the east. Precision of information as a lifesaver. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The prairie dog lives in a world where everything has its place—and ensures that everyone knows that place. Order is not an effort for it. It is survival.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Ground Squirrel: Watching Over the Community</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The ground squirrel is a small rodent of the Eurasian steppes—and one of the most selfless animals of the savannah: it stands on its hind legs to scan the landscape, and upon sighting a bird of prey, immediately lets out a warning call that brings the entire colony to safety. This warning call makes the ground squirrel itself the most visible target in the area—it endangers itself to save others. Ground squirrels live in tight family groups, share their burrows, groom one another, and the females of a group mutually assist in raising the young. What drives these animals is not personal advantage—it is the bond to the group, which is stronger than the bond to one's own survival. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The ground squirrel steps into the light so that others are safe in the shade. This reversal—making oneself visible in order to protect—is the deepest form that caretaking can take.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Wood Mouse: Adaptability as a Recipe for Success</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The wood mouse is the most successful rodent in Europe—and the most unobtrusive. It lives in forests, fields, gardens, cellars, marshes, high mountains, and dunes. It eats seeds, fruits, insects, fungi, roots—whatever is available. It nests in burrows, tree hollows, beneath stones, in compost heaps. It is nocturnal and crepuscular, travels kilometers in a single night, and can flee, climb, and swim at speeds of up to 13 km/h. The wood mouse is not the largest, not the strongest, not the most striking mouse—it is the most versatile. And that is precisely why it is among the most widespread mammals on Earth. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The wood mouse does not ask whether an environment suits it. It adapts—lightning-fast, competently, without fuss. Success through total flexibility.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Dwarf Hamster: Intensity in a Small Format</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The dwarf hamster is one of the smallest rodents in the world—and one of the most willful. In the wild, it is a solitary creature with firm territorial convictions: it tolerates no other animal in its domain, scent-marks it meticulously, and defends it with a determination that mocks its physical size. Its coat changes depending on the species and season—some dwarf hamsters turn white in winter, a metamorphosis that is rare even in the rodent world. It carries enormous amounts of food in its cheek pouches for short distances—supplies that it stores carefully and sorts according to an inner system. It lives intensely, sleeps little, and moves constantly. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The dwarf hamster shows that uniqueness is not a matter of size. Its territory is its expression—clearly defined, deeply felt, non-negotiably its own.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Mole: Master of the Hidden World</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The mole spends its entire life underground—it is almost blind, its eyes are covered by skin, and its ears are not visible. But it has developed senses that surpass all aboveground creatures: it senses the vibrations of an earthworm through the soil from several meters away. Its tunnel network is an architectural masterpiece: with a digging speed of up to 20 meters per hour, it creates extensively branched networks complete with sleeping chambers, storage rooms, and "hunting runs"—tunnels it travels regularly to harvest worms that fall inside. What few know: moles paralyze earthworms with toxin in their saliva and store them alive in supply chambers—a cool, living storehouse for the winter. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The mole knows everything about its world—and its world lies where no one else is looking. It needs no light to see.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Muskrat: Safety Through Community</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The muskrat builds its lodges on ponds and rivers with a single principle: the entrance is always underwater. No land predator can enter without diving. This security architecture is carefully engineered, the result of an evolutionary strategy that prioritizes survival through caution. Muskrats live in tight family units—several generations share a lodge, guard the entrance together, and warn one another of danger with precise vocalizations. Foreign muskrats are driven out of the territory; family members, on the other hand, are supported for life. The boundary is clear: loyalty to the group, mistrust toward the unknown. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The muskrat has built its lodge so that no stranger can enter unannounced—and its family always knows that the way down and into the water is the safest path home.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Beaver: The Architect of Joy</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The beaver is the most engineering-minded animal in the world: it fells trees, dams rivers, creates ponds where none existed, and builds lodges out of wood, mud, and stone that last for decades. What sets it apart: it does not build because it has to. It builds because building is its nature—its instinct is so deeply ingrained that captive beavers attempt to erect "dams" out of any available material, even when no water is flowing. The sound of running water is the trigger; building is the response; the joy in doing so is tangible. Beaver families are tight and loyal—young remain with the parents for two years and help with construction before dispersing. What they leave behind: beaver dams that alter entire ecosystems, create wetlands, and foster biodiversity. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The beaver transforms whatever it touches—not out of duty, but out of a deep-seated drive to shape the world. Every trunk a new project. Every reservoir a new adventure.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Nutria: Uncompromisingly Robust</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The nutria—also known as the coypu—is an immigrant from South America that has long since made itself at home in Europe, North Africa, and North America, even though nobody invited it: it came, it ate, it stayed. Nutrias are large, robust rodents with bright orange teeth—colored by iron-rich enamel that renders them harder and more resistant than ordinary incisors. They systematically excavate riverbanks, feed on reeds, aquatic plants, and roots with a thoroughness that causes bank erosion—and do so with an indifference toward the consequences that is both impressive and unsettling. In their native South America, they are part of a balanced ecosystem; in Europe, they have no natural predators and establish their territory at their own discretion. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The nutria does not ask whether it is welcome. It is there—with orange teeth, full physical commitment, and no apologies. It sets its own boundaries.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Capybara: The Most Peaceful Animal in the World</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The capybara is the largest rodent in the world—and the most relaxed. It lives in South America, along rivers and lakes, in groups of ten to thirty animals, and possesses the remarkable quality of living in peace with nearly all other animals. Photos and videos of capybaras have gone viral worldwide—because monkeys, birds, cats, ducks, and even caimans regularly sit on their broad backs without the capybara so much as batting an eye. Biologists explain this through its extraordinary stress resistance: its nervous system is calibrated for calm like almost no other animal's. It swims, wallows in mud, grazes on grass—and in doing so radiates a serenity that literally soothes other creatures. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The capybara has no enemy because it needs none. Its peace is so complete that other animals feel it and let themselves be infected by it. It is the living argument for harmony as a primal force.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, Charts, and Type Descriptions – The First Volume of the Trilogy That Brings the Enneagram to Life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-Depth Type Profiles and Archetypal Perspectives – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, Paths to Healing, and the Big Picture – Volume 3 as the Conclusion of the Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"baerenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bear Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"spinnenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Spider Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"insektenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Insect Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function baerenarteDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("B\u00e4renarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Bears are among the most powerful and enigmatic animals on Earth—revered, feared, and understood as a symbol of inner strength in nearly every culture around the globe. Every species possesses a completely distinct nature: its own strategy, its own way of living, its own principle. Nine bear species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/baerenarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="B\u00e4renarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Polar Bear: Perfection in White</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Polar Bear lives in the most extreme environment a terrestrial predator can inhabit: on the drifting ice of the Arctic, in temperatures down to minus 50 degrees Celsius, on an ice sheet that constantly moves, fractures, and reshapes itself. Its adaptation to these conditions is so complete that no other mammal surpasses it: its fur is not white, but hollow and transparent—each individual hair directs sunlight down to the black skin underneath, which absorbs the heat. Its paws are as large as snowshoes and designed so that it glides silently across the ice. It can smell seals beneath a blanket of snow over a kilometer away. The polar bear trains its hunting technique—young polar bears spend hours practicing stalking and striking. Perfection is not a gift; it is the result of repetition. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The polar bear does not merely survive despite its environment—it has adapted so completely to it that it achieves perfection within it. No other animal could do better here. And that is enough for it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Black Bear: The Adaptable One</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The American Black Bear is the most social and adaptable bear in the world: it lives in forests, swamps, grasslands, and suburbs—finding its way in almost any environment left to it by humans. It is the only bear that consistently avoids conflict: compared to brown bears or grizzlies, it is significantly less aggressive, almost always shows flight behavior when encountering humans, and seeks the path of least resistance. What distinguishes it: it possesses a remarkable capacity for social learning. Black bears living near human settlements quickly learn how garbage cans work, how doors are opened, and how to raid campsites—passing this knowledge on to their cubs. It helps itself by understanding others. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The black bear survives not through dominance, but through the ability to fit in, learn, and adapt. It yields—and thereby receives more than those who fight.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Kodiak Bear: Record as Second Nature</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Kodiak Bear is the largest terrestrial predator on Earth: males reach weights of over 680 kilograms and a shoulder height of 1.5 meters—standing nearly four meters tall when reared up. They inhabit the Kodiak Archipelago in Alaska, where a rich ecosystem—salmon rivers, abundant vegetation, mild maritime air—produces animals that face no natural predators at their size. The Kodiak bear is not aggressive for the sake of its size—it is calm, methodical, and decisive. During salmon runs, dominant males occupy the prime positions at waterfalls, catching salmon with an efficiency that other bears achieve only after years of learning. They invest in their skill—and are rewarded for it. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The Kodiak bear is not the largest because it wants to exaggerate. It is the largest because it made the absolute optimum out of everything its environment provided. Size as a consistent next stage of development.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Asian Black Bear: The Mark It Bears</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Asian Black Bear—also known as the Moon Bear—bears an unmistakable white V-shaped mark on its chest: a crescent moon that earned it the name "moon bear" in parts of Asia. No other bear looks like it. The Asian black bear lives mainly in trees—it is the most adept climber among the large bears and builds sleeping nests at heights inaccessible to other bears. High up there, alone, it withdraws from conflict. It is a solitary creature with a distinct preference for a lonely life—drawing a parallel to the solitary lifestyle that corresponds to the introspective character of Type 4. In East Asian mythology, it is regarded as an animal of the moon, of mystery, and of the inner world. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The Asian black bear wears its mark upon its chest—visible, unmistakable, without explanation. It ascends where others cannot reach. And there it rests, far above the noise of the world.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Spectacled Bear: The Quiet Observer of South America</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Spectacled Bear is the only bear native to South America—and one of the most reclusive: it inhabits the hard-to-reach cloud forests of the Andes, mostly as a solitary creature, and is so shy that researchers have only been able to observe it closely in recent decades. Its characteristic light markings around the eyes—which give it its name—differ from animal to animal: an individual pattern that renders it unique. The spectacled bear is primarily herbivorous and spends much time feeding in tree canopies—high above, invisible, in tranquility. It constructs feeding platforms from bent branches upon which it sits and eats—a form of tool use long thought impossible for bears. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The spectacled bear knows how to remain invisible and chose a habitat where most do not even look for it. What it knows, it taught itself—high above, alone, in absolute peace.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Brown Bear: Community as an Anchor</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Brown Bear is the bear most familiar to people—from European forests, national parks, fairy tales, and myths. It is cautious, situationally aware, and extraordinarily capable of learning: brown bears remember places where they were disturbed, avoid them for years, and adjust their behavior based on their experiences with humans. They are known for their fidelity to annual migration routes and spawning grounds—the same riverbank, the same season, the same strategy. Brown bears are solitary creatures that gather during salmon season—developing a remarkable hierarchy and tolerance in the process: mothers with cubs take precedence, and experienced bears pass down social rank. The bond between a mother and her cubs is among the most intense in the animal kingdom: she stays with them for up to three years. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The brown bear survives through trust—trust in its wealth of experience, in the community, and in the proven path. Anyone who offers it this trust finds a reliable companion.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Sloth Bear: Joy in Living, Joy in Suction</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Sloth Bear is the most playful and loudest bear in the world: it possesses a flexible snout with long lips and a toothless front palate, with which it sucks up termites and ants with a vacuuming sound audible up to 180 meters away. This bear makes noise—smacking, huffing, sucking, whistling. Living in the forests of India and Sri Lanka, it is known for its active, lively behavior: it climbs, plays, rolls around, and tirelessly explores its territory. Sloth bear mothers carry their cubs on their backs—the only bears to do so—and play with them extensively long after other bear mothers would have released their young into independence. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The sloth bear developed a favorite pastime out of hunting termites and ants—and takes so much joy in it that its celebration can be heard across half the forest. Life is a feast. And it possesses the tools to enjoy it completely.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Grizzly Bear: The Untamed Force</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Grizzly Bear is the ultimate symbol of wild power: mighty, direct, without detour. A full-grown grizzly can clear a tree trunk with a single stroke of its paw, roll over a boulder, and swipe a salmon out of the water with such precision that the fish barely has time to react. It dominates its territory—not through aggression for its own sake, but through a presence that leaves other bears without even the thought of contesting its position. What few know: grizzlies are excellent fathers in the sense that males tolerate young bears within their territory and occasionally even protect them—not out of sentimentality, but out of the firm confidence that their own position is undisputed. Those who are strong enough do not need to prove themselves. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The grizzly is not aggressive. It is sovereign—and those who understand this distinction understand why the best spot in every river belongs to it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Sun Bear: Gentle in the Greatest Heat</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Sun Bear is the smallest bear in the world—living in the tropical rainforests of Southeast Asia, where it is rarely seen, rarely heard, and rarely disturbed. It leads a quiet, frugal life: honey, fruit, insects—it seeks the sweetness of life, never conflict. Its name derives from the golden-yellow crescent on its chest, which glows like a patch of sunlight. The sun bear is famous for its long tongue—up to 25 centimeters—with which it licks honey out of narrow crevices where no other tongue can reach. It maintains a deep social bond with its mother: sun bear mothers play with their cubs longer than almost any other bear species, and the young display a particularly distinct capacity for facial mimicry—mirroring the facial expressions of their mother and playmates, which researchers interpret as a possible form of empathy. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The sun bear does not seek the great battle. It seeks the sweet moment—and finds it because it never stops gently searching for it.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deep-dive type profiles and archetypal observations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"nagetierarten-der-9-typen", label:"Rodent Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"milchkuharten-der-9-typen", label:"Dairy Cattle Breeds of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"vogelarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bird Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function milchkuhartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Milchkuharten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">The cow has been humanity's closest animal companion for over 10,000 years&mdash;nourishing cultures, shaping religions, and forming landscapes. In the process, every breed has developed an unmistakable nature: its own performance, its own posture, its own principle. Nine dairy breeds, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/milchkuharten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Milchkuharten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; German Holstein-Friesian: The Benchmark of the Dairy Industry</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The German Holstein-Friesian is the highest-performing dairy breed in the world&mdash;with a single-minded consistency that is second to none. Top cows produce over 14,000 liters of milk per year, several times the output of an average cow, achieved through decades of precise breeding toward a single standard: maximum milk yield combined with optimal udder health. Its black-and-white coat pattern is so iconic that it serves globally as the quintessential image of a cow&mdash;no other animal embodies its function quite like the Holstein. It is not a beef breed, nor a dual-purpose breed&mdash;it is completely aligned with its singular task. Keeping Holsteins requires precision: exact feeding plans, regular veterinary checkups, clean free-stall barns with defined routines. It delivers its maximum when everything is right&mdash;and immediately indicates when something is off. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The Holstein has no Plan B. It gives everything for what it does&mdash;according to a standard that accepts no exceptions. And that is why it remains unmatched.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Jersey: Care in Its Purest Form</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Jersey cow is the friendliest dairy breed in the world: small, gentle, with large dark eyes that touch even people who normally have no special affection for cattle. Jerseys are known for seeking contact with humans, enjoying being petted, and responding to their names. They produce less milk than the Holstein&mdash;but their milk is the richest of all breeds: over 5 percent fat, creamy, and golden-yellow due to its high carotene content, making it ideal for butter, cream, and cheese. Their milk does not merely nourish&mdash;it pampers. The Jersey is a breed raised on small family farms, thriving in close proximity to humans rather than in industrial herds. It needs affection and gives it back. Farmers describe it as the cow you can truly get to know. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The Jersey does not give the most&mdash;it gives the best. And it does so from a closeness that transforms every relationship you enter into with it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; German Simmental (Fleckvieh): Performance in Every Respect</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The German Simmental (Fleckvieh) is the most versatile high-performance breed in Germany&mdash;and that is what makes it unique: it delivers both outstanding milk yields and premium meat quality. While other breeds specialize in a single task, the Simmental excels at both&mdash;and at the highest level. With a market share of over 40 percent, it is the most widely kept breed in Germany, Austria, and parts of Southern Europe. Its robust build, reliable fertility, and longevity make it the most economically successful breed on many farms. Simmental cows demonstrate remarkable adaptability: they thrive in the Alpine foothills just as well as in the North German Plain, in intensive barn management as well as on pasture. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The Simmental does not ask whether to focus on milk or meat. It delivers both&mdash;convincingly, reliably, everywhere. Success is not a goal for it. It is its natural state.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; German Black Pied Cattle: The Original Behind the Shadow</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The original German Black Pied Cattle is the ancestral breed from which decades of crossbreeding with American-Canadian Holstein-Friesian lines produced today's high-performance Holstein&mdash;and in the process, almost vanished. Today, it is considered an endangered breed, with only a few thousand purebred animals remaining in Germany. The original Black Pied is more robust than its descendants, longer-lived, and features a milk composition that is particularly prized for artisanal cheese production. Those who know this cow appreciate the original&mdash;the authentic, unvarnished reality behind what mass production turned it into. It is not a high-performance machine. It is an animal with history, depth, and a character that resists uniform standardization. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The German Black Pied was overtaken by progress&mdash;and is precisely for that reason irreplaceable. What it carries cannot be reproduced by selective breeding: the distinctiveness of the original.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Angeln Cattle / German Red: The Quiet Connoisseur</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Angeln Cattle&mdash;also known as German Red&mdash;is one of the oldest German dairy breeds and one of the least known. Deep red, medium-weight, thrifty. It requires no special conditions, no elaborate feeding, no intensive management&mdash;it utilizes what is available and in return produces milk that is held in high esteem in artisanal dairying: high protein content, excellent cheese-making properties, and a flavor derived from extensive pasture grazing. It is not loud, not flashy, not record-breaking&mdash;it quietly accumulates its wisdom across generations within itself. The Angeln is a breed for people who look more closely. Those who know it appreciate what no glossy brochure shows: the quality that emerges from restraint. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The Red Cattle does not impress through sheer volume. It convinces through what lies beneath the surface&mdash;and that reveals itself only to those who truly inquire.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; German Brown Swiss (Braunvieh): Reliability Across Generations</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The German Brown Swiss (Braunvieh) is the most reliable dairy breed of the Alpine region: robust, long-lived, loyal to its herd and to the pastures on which it grew up. Brown Swiss cows are famous for their longevity&mdash;many animals are milked for over ten years, significantly longer than high-performance breeds. They are sure-footed on steep slopes, adept at climbing alpine pastures, and adaptable to the extreme conditions of the mountains. Their milk has a high casein content, which makes it the premier cheese milk&mdash;Allgäuer Emmental, mountain cheese, and many Alpine specialties rely on Brown Swiss milk. This cow has its fixed place in the landscape&mdash;and it does not abandon it. It delivers year after year, reliably, without caprice. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The Brown Swiss is the cow upon which the mountain pasture builds. Not because it yields the absolute highest volume&mdash;but because it is always there when you need it. Year in, year out, without exception.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; German Red Pied: Joy of Life on Four Legs</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The German Red Pied is one of the most colorful dairy breeds in Germany&mdash;its characteristic red-and-white coat stands out on any pasture, and its temperament matches this striking appearance: it is active, curious, and eager to explore. Red Pied cows are known for preferring pasture grazing; they need movement and develop stress more quickly in tight barn systems than calmer breeds. They are dual-purpose animals&mdash;suitable for both milk and meat&mdash;and therefore versatile in application. Their milk yield is solid, their adaptability high, and their playful instinct as calves noticeable. Red Pied calves are the liveliest on any pasture&mdash;they run the furthest, explore with the most curiosity, and are the most likely to disrupt the calm of older cows. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The Red Pied demonstrates what joy of life means as an operational principle. It yields the most when it feels freest&mdash;on spacious pastures, with plenty of room and the next horizon in sight.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Limousin: Strength You Can Feel</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Limousin is not a traditional dairy breed&mdash;it is a world-class beef breed, originating from the Limousin high plateau in France, where a harsh climate and sparse pastures forged a breed that survives because it is stronger than its environment. Limousin cattle possess impressive musculature, a golden-brown coat, and a presence that commands immediate attention on any farm. They are known for their dominance within the herd: they assert themselves, claim the best grazing spots, and can sometimes appear intimidating to people who do not know them. But under good management, with respect and clear handling, they are good-natured, productive, and long-lived. Their meat is considered among the highest quality in Europe&mdash;fine-grained, well-marbled, and favored by top chefs. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The Limousin does not ask for permission. It takes the space that is rightfully its own&mdash;and those who know how to handle it reap the very best it has to offer.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Hinterwald: Peace in the Smallest Format</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Hinterwald Cattle is the smallest cattle breed in Central Europe&mdash;and one of the most beautiful: white with golden-brown markings, polled or with delicate horns, carrying an expression of complete tranquility on its face. It originates from the Southern Black Forest, where it has lived for centuries on steep, narrow pastures inaccessible to larger breeds. The Hinterwald requires very little: little feed, little space, little effort. It thrives where others fail. It is one of the most frugal breeds of all&mdash;and simultaneously one of the longest-lived. Hinterwald cows often reach over 15 years of age, reliably yielding a modest yet aromatically rich milk throughout their lives. Within the herd, they are peaceful, lower in rank than larger breeds, but stress-resistant and well-balanced. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The Hinterwald needs no grand stage. It needs its slope, its grass, its community&mdash;and in that, it is complete. What it gives, it gives wholeheartedness. Without drama, without demand, without end.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deep-dive type profiles and archetypal observations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"baerenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bear Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"vogelarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bird Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"milchkuharten-der-9-typen", label:"Dairy Cattle Breeds of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function vogelartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Vogelarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Birds are the only creatures that truly call the sky their home—encompassing over 10,000 species, on every continent, in every climate. In doing so, every species has developed an unmistakable nature: a look, an attitude, a way of moving through the world. Nine bird species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/vogelarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Vogelarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Eagle: The Gaze That Misses Nothing</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The eagle sees four times sharper than a human. From an altitude of 300 meters, it can spot a mouse in the grass—it does not just see more, it sees more precisely, clearly, without distortion. Its eye has two foveas—two focal points instead of one—and can perceive colors in the ultraviolet spectrum that are invisible to humans. It circles, it waits, it scans the world with a concentration that permits no distraction—and when the moment comes, it dives at speeds of up to 300 km/h into the depths. No hesitation, no detour, no second attempt required. The eagle expands its nest—called an eyrie—over decades, adding new material year after year until it can weigh tons. It improves what it has—a little more perfect every year. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The eagle does not judge the world—it sees it as it is. And then it acts with a precision that requires no apology.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Budgerigar: Connection as a Way of Life</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The budgerigar is the most social bird in existence—in the wild, it lives in flocks of thousands, communicates incessantly, maintains lifelong partnerships, and visibly grieves when its mate dies. It learns its partner's voice and imitates it—not to conform, but to show: I hear you. I am with you. Budgerigars do not drink alone: they wait for one another. Budgerigars do not sleep alone: they huddle together, shoulder to shoulder. What many do not know: budgerigars can speak—not just mimic, but communicate contextually. The world record stands at over 1,700 words that a budgerigar understood and used appropriately according to the situation. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The budgerigar does not exist for itself. It exists in relation to others—and in this interconnectedness, it finds its complete expression.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Golden Pheasant: The Grand Entrance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The golden pheasant is one of the most magnificent birds in the world: the male wears a plumage of gold, scarlet, deep blue, and emerald green—so elaborate that 18th-century naturalists believed it could not be real. But it is real—and it knows it. During courtship display, it unfurls its feathers, walks in circles around the female, and displays every single feather in its best position. Its vibrant colors don't happen by chance—this splendour is the result of hundreds of thousands of years of sexual selection: only those who impress pass on their genes. The female, by contrast, is an inconspicuous brown—she chooses the best, and the golden pheasant cock has made himself the very best there is. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The golden pheasant shows that appearance is not superficiality—it is the result of perfection. One who shines so brightly has something to shine with. And he knows exactly when to do so.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Swan: Beauty and Depth</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The swan is Europe's most elegant bird—and one of its most intense. It forms lifelong partnerships; if its partner dies, it grieves visibly, loses weight, withdraws, and some swans die shortly after their mates. This depth of bond is rare in the animal kingdom—it calls to mind a form of love that transcends instinct. Yet the swan is no peaceful bird: it defends its nest and family with a determination that puts large dogs to flight. Beneath the flawless white surface lies a surprising intensity. What was once unknown about its call: the swan actually sings—the so-called "swan song," a low, almost mournful call it emits shortly before death. This is where the idiom "swan song" originates. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The swan lives the entire spectrum—from the flawless surface to depths others cannot fathom. It is not what it appears to be. It is much more.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Owl: The Knowledge of the Night</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The owl is the observer among birds: it can rotate its head 270 degrees, see in near-total darkness, and hear so precisely that it can locate prey under a blanket of snow purely by sound. Its eyes cannot roll—it moves its entire head instead of its eyes. This gives it a gaze that appears as if it is processing everything simultaneously. Owls hunt in silence: their feathers have a velvet-like structure that dampens all air resistance—their flight is virtually silent. Prey never hears them coming. Owls are solitary creatures with fixed territories that they hold for years. In almost every culture across the world, they are the symbol of wisdom—not because of cleverness, but because of their silence. One who observes like that, without being seen, must know a great deal. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The owl gathers its knowledge in the darkness that others fear—and then acts with a precision that is only possible when one has truly seen.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Stork: The Returning One</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The stork returns every spring—to the same nest, to the same meadow, often to the same partner. Researchers have documented stork pairs at the same nest over 20 years: they wintered separately in Africa, each taking their own path—and met again in April at their shared eyrie, as if they had never been apart. This loyalty to community, to a familiar place, to a well-matched partner is the trademark of the stork. It migrates in groups—thousands of storks travel together, utilizing thermal updrafts, helping one another, flying distances together that none could endure as well alone. The stork is considered a bringer of good fortune in many cultures—because it returns. Because it can be relied upon. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The stork is not there because it has to be. It is there because it has always been there—and because those who count on it know that it will come.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Hummingbird: Life to the Fullest</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The hummingbird is the most intense living creature in the world: its heart beats up to 1,260 times per minute. It flies forward, backward, sideways, hovering—the only bird capable of doing so. It visits up to 2,000 flowers a day, hovering, sipping, moving on—never for longer than a moment, but entirely present in every single one. To cover its energy consumption, it eats double its body weight in nectar daily. At night, it drops its metabolism to a minimum—a state of torpor—in order to survive until the next day. Its life is a single, intense pulse between exhaustion and a new beginning. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The hummingbird cannot go slowly. It can only go all in. Every flower is a new promise—and it keeps it, for exactly the moment it needs.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Falcon: Power from Above</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The peregrine falcon is the fastest animal on Earth: in a hunting dive, it reaches speeds over 380 km/h—faster than a Formula 1 race car on a straightaway. It hunts other birds in flight, striking them with a single targeted claw and turning back before the prey even hits the ground. No other animal combines speed, precision, and dominance so completely in a single moment. The falcon claims its territory—in cities, it nests on church towers and skyscrapers, surveying the entire urban expanse from above. Other birds of prey avoid its airspace. What most people do not know: falcons have been kept by humans as hunting partners for millennia—not because they are docile, but because their power, when offered voluntarily, is priceless. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The falcon does not give away its power. But when it deploys it, the outcome is inevitable—and anyone who witnesses it knows what they have experienced.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Flamingo: Harmony in Pink</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The flamingo lives in the world's most inhospitable bodies of water—highly alkaline salt lakes in Africa and Latin America, where hardly any other animal can survive—and feels completely at home there. Its pink color has a clear cause: it stems from the carotenoids in the algae and crustaceans it filters—it literally becomes what it feeds on. Flamingos stand on one leg for thousands of hours—not out of effort, but because it is more relaxing for them than standing on two: their physical system locks into place on one leg and requires zero muscle power. It finds the easiest way—and stands there like a sculpture. In flocks of hundreds of thousands, flamingos move in sync, fly in sync, and perform the steps of their courtship dance in sync—as if they were a single organism. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The flamingo needs no chaos to shine. It finds its place in the community—and in this harmony, it becomes what it is: unforgettable.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, Charts, and Type Descriptions – The First Volume of the Trilogy That Brings the Enneagram to Life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-Depth Type Profiles and Archetypal Reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, Paths to Healing, and the Big Picture – Volume 3 as the Conclusion of the Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"milchkuharten-der-9-typen", label:"Dairy Cow Breeds of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"froscharten-der-9-typen", label:"Frog Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"spinnenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Spider Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function froschartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Froscharten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Frogs are among the oldest living creatures on Earth—they have existed for over 250 million years and have since colonized almost every damp niche on the planet: desert, rainforest, mountains, polar regions. Each species carries its own intrinsic principle within it. Nine frog species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/froscharten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Froscharten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Glass Frog: Nothing to Hide</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Glass Frog</strong> is the most transparent frog in the world—literally: its belly side is completely transparent. Anyone looking at it from below can see the heart, liver, intestines, and the blood vessels through which the blood flows. No other vertebrate lays itself open so completely. This transparency is not a deficit—it is an evolutionary survival strategy: seen from above on green leaves, the glass frog is nearly invisible; seen from below, one looks right through it into the sky. It vanishes into its own clarity. The glass frog tends to its eggs with impressive care—the male stays with the clutch for days, moistening it with his own body and warding off parasites. What it protects, it protects completely. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The glass frog has no mask and needs none. It lives in such a way that it has nothing to hide—thereby becoming something that nature itself could not improve.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – White's Tree Frog: Gentleness as Strength</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>White's Tree Frog</strong> (also known as the Australian green tree frog or Dumpy tree frog) is the most gemütlich frog in the world. Its greenish-white, almost waxy skin, relaxed facial expression, and phlegmatic temperament have earned it the reputation of being the friendliest frog there is. It adapts completely to its surroundings: it sleeps where it sits; it eats what comes; it shows no aggression, no franticness, no excessive vigilance. What distinguishes it: it is surprisingly robust. Its skin secretion contains antibacterial and antiviral substances that researchers are investigating as a basis for medications—it helps even when it doesn't know it's helping. It calls at dusk with a soft, deep bark—inviting, never intrusive. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The White's Tree Frog gives without making a fuss about it. Its gentleness is not a weakness—it is how it touches the world.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Red-Eyed Tree Frog: The Performance Is the Message</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Red-Eyed Tree Frog</strong> is the best-known frog in the world—and for good reason. Its bright red eyes, green body, orange feet, and blue flank stripes are so improbably colorful that it has been immortalized on posters, logos, and book covers ever since humans have had cameras. It is the face of the tropical rainforest. What hardly anyone knows: these colors are not a warning of poison—the red-eyed tree frog is harmless. Its bright colors are pure signal: startle coloration that briefly surprises predators at the moment of attack through the sudden sight of red eyes, thereby buying time. It lives by the performance. And it has developed the best one in the world. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The Red-Eyed Tree Frog shows what is possible when impact becomes art. It is not dangerous—but it looks like it could be. And that is quite enough.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – European Tree Frog: The Dreamer in the Reeds</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>European Tree Frog</strong> is Central Europe's most poetic frog. Small, bright green, with large adhesive discs on its toes, it sits alone on a leaf and sings—a trilling, clear song that can be heard far and wide on warm nights. It is often the only living creature calling in that night—as if it had the entire soundscape to itself. The tree frog lives an introverted life: it hunts alone, sleeps alone, and climbs through brush and reeds along its own routes. Only during the mating season does it briefly gather with others—and even then, it remains unmistakably itself. What few know: tree frogs can change their color from bright green to gray-brown—depending on mood, temperature, and light. They wear their inner world on the outside. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The European Tree Frog does not sing for the audience. It sings because it must—and because its song rings out in no other throat like in its own.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Desert Spadefoot Toad: Master of Retreat</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Desert Spadefoot Toad</strong> solves a problem that is actually unsolvable: it lives in an environment that kills frogs. No water. Extreme heat. Months without rain. Its solution is radical: it burrows up to a meter deep into the earth, wraps itself in a multi-layered mucus envelope that dries out into a parchment-like capsule, and shuts down its metabolism to a minimum—for up to seven years. No eating, no moving, no breathing in the true sense. Just waiting. When the rain comes, it rehydrates within hours, surfaces, and has a few weeks to eat, mate, and vanish again. Its entire life plays out in short, intense phases—interrupted by long phases of complete withdrawal. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The Desert Spadefoot Toad has learned that one does not always have to be present to survive. Sometimes withdrawal is the most intelligent decision—and surfacing when the time is right all the more impactful.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Common Frog: Loyalty to the Proven Path</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Common Frog</strong> is Europe's most reliable frog: every year in spring, right after the first thaw, it migrates back to the exact same pond where it hatched itself—sometimes across kilometers, across roads, through forests, despite all obstacles. Researchers have documented individual common frogs at the same spawning water for over ten years. It knows its place—and it returns there because it works. In autumn, it hibernates in groups deep in the mud of ponds and rivers, where the temperature remains stable. Community as a protection strategy: many bodies generate enough warmth to survive together. What distinguishes it is not heroism—it is reliability. It does what it has always done, and it does it on time. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The Common Frog does not trust the unknown. It trusts the path that has proven itself—and this path leads it, year after year, precisely to where life continues.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Poison Dart Frog: Color as a Declaration of Freedom</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Poison Dart Frog</strong> wears the loudest color of the rainforest: cobalt blue, fire orange, emerald green with black dots—so glaring, so improbable, that every predator instinct immediately says: Do not touch. And rightly so: some species carry enough poison to kill thousands of humans. What fascinates: the poison dart frog needs no hiding place, no camouflage, no strategy of invisibility. It strolls across the forest floor in broad daylight—in full public view, in full color. It carries its poison like a ticket to freedom. By the way, it derives its poison from its food—from specific ants and mites of the rainforest. In captivity, without this food, it loses its poison entirely. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The Poison Dart Frog has found the most radical solution to the problem of safety—it glows so brightly that no one attacks it. Freedom through color. Protection through attention.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Bullfrog: Territory Without Compromise</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Bullfrog</strong> is North America's largest frog—and the loudest: its call, which is barely distinguishable from a bull's roar, can be heard from several kilometers away. It claims its territory along the shore with an ambiguity-free clarity: other males that come too close are attacked and sometimes eaten. The bullfrog is not a picky eater—it eats everything that fits into its mouth: insects, mice, small snakes, other frogs, occasionally birds. Its mouth is comparatively huge. It sits motionless on the shore—a rock, a presence—waiting for what comes. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The Bullfrog does not explain its territory. It fills it out—with its voice, its size, its readiness to end any conflict immediately. Whoever comes close knows where they stand.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Marsh Frog: The Peace of the Moment</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Marsh Frog</strong> sits. On a stone, on a water lily leaf, on the edge of the pond—and it sits so completely, so totally in the moment, that it has become a symbol of contemplation. In Japan, the frog at the pond is a classic haiku motif—one of the most famous haiku in world literature was written in 1686 about the leap of a frog into the water: for a moment, stillness becomes visible through the sound that interrupts it. The marsh frog is highly adaptable—it thrives in almost any body of water, gets along with almost all conspecifics, seeks no conflict, and lets itself be disturbed by most threats only at the very last moment. It jumps into the water—and sits on the next stone shortly after. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The Marsh Frog resists the urge to change the world. It finds its peace within it—just as it is. And this peace is no deficiency. It is a gift.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"vogelarten-der-9-typen", label:"Bird Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"spinnenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Spider Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"reptilienarten-der-9-typen", label:"Reptile Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function spinnenartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Spinnenarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Spiders have existed for over 380 million years – longer than the dinosaurs, longer than flowering plants. On every continent, in every habitat, at every altitude. Every species has developed a completely unique strategy: for building, lurking, hunting, seducing. Nine spider species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/spinnenarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Spinnenarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Garden Orb-Weaver: The Web as a Work of Art</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Garden Orb-Weaver builds one of nature's most precise constructs: a radial capture web with up to 1,500 individual attachment points, perfectly symmetrical, built anew every morning, the old one consumed and recycled after a single night. She begins with a single bridge thread in the wind, waiting patiently for the draft that carries it to the opposite side – and builds from there according to an internal blueprint encoded in her DNA that has remained unchanged for millions of years. The web follows the logarithmic spiral – the same mathematical curve found in sea shells, spiral galaxies, and plant growth patterns. The Garden Orb-Weaver does not reinvent the wheel. She builds it with a precision that requires no explanation. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The web is not merely a tool. It is an expression of what is right – built according to standards deeper than conscious intention. When completed, it is flawless. And tomorrow, it will be made new again.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Crab Spider: The Waiting Hostess</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Crab Spider builds no web – she waits. On flower petals, inside blossoms, nestled among stamens, she sits entirely motionless, perfectly camouflaged: white on a white flower, yellow on a yellow flower. She can adjust her color within days. She does not hide – she becomes one with the location others seek out for sustenance, welcoming them. Bees, hoverflies, and butterflies approach willingly, drawn by the scent of the flower, and the Crab Spider strikes – without vast quantities of venom, relying instead on a single, precise bite to the neck. The prey feels virtually nothing. What appears to an observer as a lurking predator is simply her place: she resides where others desire to go. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The Crab Spider initiates no struggle. She creates a space – and others arrive of their own accord. Her secret is not brute force, but the ability to blend in completely.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Jumping Spider: Performance with Eye Contact</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Jumping Spider is the world's most athletic spider: she can leap 50 times her body length in a single jump – for a human, that would equal a jump across an entire football field. In doing so, she strikes prey with precision so swift that even high-speed cameras can barely capture it. What sets the Jumping Spider apart from other hunting spiders: she turns around before attacking, looks directly into the eyes of her prey – and then makes her decision. Her four large eyes provide a three-dimensional field of view similar to that of a human. Jumping spiders are also the only spiders that react to human glances: they look back. They know they are being watched. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The Jumping Spider does not hide behind a web. She stands in the center of the space, makes eye contact – and leaps with such precision that the outcome is inevitable. Performance you can see.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Peacock Spider: Beauty as a Survival Strategy</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Peacock Spider is the most brightly colored spider in the world – and the male has evolved a courtship dance considered by biologists to be among the most impressive behavioral displays in the animal kingdom. He unfolds his colorful abdomen, extends his legs, and begins to dance: rhythmically, precisely, with an enthusiasm wildly out of proportion to his tiny size (barely 5 millimeters). If the dance fails to impress, the female consumes him. If it succeeds, he has achieved something no other animal can: he has survived through pure uniqueness. Every Peacock Spider species bears a distinct pattern – no two are alike, and within a single species, males differ in color, movement, and flair. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The Peacock Spider displays what lies within – completely, without reservation, as a matter of life and death. Uniqueness is not decoration. It is the sole means of survival.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Wolf Spider: System-Driven Solitary</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Wolf Spider hunts like a wolf: alone, on the ground, combining endurance, keen observation, and flawless timing. She constructs no web – she actively pursues her prey, observes from a safe distance, and strikes when conditions are optimal. Her eight eyes are arranged in three rows, providing a nearly 360-degree view; her two large main eyes glow in the dark like tiny headlights – allowing one to spot Wolf Spiders at night using a flashlight. What distinguishes the Wolf Spider from other hunting spiders: she carries her egg sac attached to her body – and after hatching, the spiderlings climb onto the mother's back, remaining there until their first molt. This spider, who otherwise operates entirely alone, gives herself completely in this moment. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The Wolf Spider knows every detail of her territory. She needs no one to hunt – but when responsibility arises, she assumes it fully.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Walnut Orb-Weaver: Security Through Structure</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Walnut Orb-Weaver builds her webs with a rigor that stands out even among spiders: always in protected crevices, always with a retreat path, always positioned so she can vanish instantly if threatened. Her web is symmetrical and precise – yet she does not sit in the center where other spiders wait. She sits at the edge, hidden in the crevice, connected to the web via a single signal line: she senses every vibration without being exposed. This combination of presence and protection is her defining trait. She knows her environment intimately, always builds in the same locations, and never leaves her territory unless driven by necessity. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The Walnut Orb-Weaver demonstrates that security does not presuppose fear – it presupposes intelligence. She does not build despite dangers. She builds so that dangers cannot touch her.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Banana Spider: Large, Colorful, Everywhere</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Banana Spider – also known as the Giant Wood Spider – constructs the largest free-standing webs of all spiders: up to two meters in diameter, stretched between trees, across pathways, from branch to branch. Her silk is golden-yellow and so tear-resistant that researchers are studying it as a potential material for bulletproof vests. The Banana Spider herself is strikingly large, brightly colored, and moves with an energy that belies her size – repairing her web while it is in active use, leaping from section to section, yielding to nothing. What few realize: Banana Spiders sometimes live in harem-like structures – one female surrounded by several tiny males. Vibrant, social, always active. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The Banana Spider does not build a small, safe web. She spans it between the largest trees she can find – and if it tears, she immediately starts on the next.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Tarantula: Power Without Apology</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Tarantula is the largest spider in the world – and the one that commands the greatest reverence from humans. Her body can reach the size of a human hand; her chelicerae are strong enough to overpower mice, frogs, and small birds. Yet despite this, Tarantulas are not aggressive animals. They do not attack unless provoked. They inhabit the same burrows for decades, defending their territory with clarity – but without hysteria. Their venom is generally less dangerous to humans than a bee sting. What frightens people is not her danger – it is her sheer presence. She does not hide. She emerges when she chooses. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The Tarantula requires neither camouflage nor cunning. Her size is her communication – direct, unmistakable, without apology. Those who show respect have nothing to fear. Those who provoke quickly learn why it was a poor idea.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Golden Silk Orb-Weaver: Harmony as a Masterpiece</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Golden Silk Orb-Weaver spins the strongest natural fiber in the world: her silk possesses greater tensile strength than steel, higher elasticity than nylon, and lighter weight than cotton – and it genuinely shimmers golden in the sunlight. In Papua New Guinea, fishermen have used her threads to weave fishing nets for centuries. Her web is a permanent creation – she does not rebuild every day, but instead maintains, repairs, and expands. The same web can endure for months. What makes it exceptional: it is not merely strong, it is beautiful. The golden hue attracts bees seeking light – prey that arrives voluntarily because the web shines. The spider herself is calm, rarely aggressive, almost patient. She sits at the center of her work and waits. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The Golden Silk Orb-Weaver needs no haste, no noise, no exaggerated motion. She has crafted the strongest material in existence – and it radiates on its own. Harmony that endures.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – Volume 1 of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal explorations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"froscharten-der-9-typen", label:"Frog Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"reptilienarten-der-9-typen", label:"Reptile Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"insektenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Insect Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function reptilienartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Reptilienarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Reptiles are the oldest terrestrial vertebrates on Earth—they survived the dinosaurs, endured ice ages, and colonized every continent. Every species carries an ancient principle within itself that has proven its worth over millions of years. Nine reptile species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/reptilienarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Reptilienarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Green Iguana: Bearing as a Principle</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Green Iguana is one of the most perfectly formed reptiles in the world: upright, precise in every movement, with a body that seems engineered for eternity. It basks in the sun with a care that borders on ritual—always on the same branch, always in the same posture, always at the same time of day. It regulates its body temperature with surgical precision: too cold, and it becomes sluggish; too warm, and it immediately seeks shade. It knows its optimal window—and maintains it. The iguana is a pure herbivore and tolerates no compromises in its choice of food: rotten fruits, moldy leaves, or decaying twigs are ignored. Only the right nourishment enters its mouth. Its dewlap and dorsal crest are not weapons of attack—they are communication: signals for others that demonstrate who it is and where its boundaries lie. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The Green Iguana does not live in reaction against a world full of errors. It simply lives according to its own standard—and this standard is so clear and consistent that its environment respects it without the iguana ever having to fight.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Green Tree Python: Security as a Gift</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Among reptile enthusiasts, the Green Tree Python is considered one of the most caring snakes: females guard their eggs with a devotion that is unusual for snakes—for months they do not leave the clutch, warming it with muscular shivers and protecting it against any intruder. Young tree pythons are bright yellow or red—only over time does their coloration transform into their characteristic emerald green. This transformation mirrors something profound: that which nourishes and accompanies changes alongside those it protects. Moving through trees, the tree python glides with an elegance and gentleness that belies its size—it flows rather than crawls. It waits for prey not with aggression, but with patience: coiled upon a branch, head loosely hanging, relaxed until the decisive moment. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The Green Tree Python is right where it is needed—quiet, patient, completely present. Its strength lies not in muscular power alone, but in the willingness to remain when others leave.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Komodo Dragon: Efficiency Without Compassion</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Komodo Dragon is the largest living lizard in the world—and one of the planet's most impressive hunters. Reaching up to three meters in length, it can sprint briefly as fast as a human and has developed a hunting system impressive in its cold calculation: it bites, lets go—and waits. Its saliva is a cocktail of toxins, bacteria, and anticoagulants; the victim's wound opens further while the dragon follows at a safe distance. For hours. For days, if necessary. Until the victim collapses. The Komodo dragon does not negotiate. It calculates. Its sense of body size, hunger, and distance is precise—it never attacks when the risk is too high, and it never gives up when the target is attainable. It devours everything—deer, water buffalo, occasionally humans. Success is its sole criterion. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The Komodo dragon has no interest in morality. It has an interest in results. And it gets them—reliably, methodically, and without a thought of detours.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Basilisk: The Water Walker</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Basilisk is the only reptile in the world capable of running across water: with blazing speed and a special technique—its hind legs slap the water's surface so fast that air pockets form on which it briefly stands—it runs up to 1.5 meters per second across rivers and lakes. In Latin America, people call it the "Jesus Christ lizard" because of this gift. Even more remarkable: its appearance possesses an enigmatic uniqueness that renders it unmistakable. The crest on its head, the dorsal sail, the play of colors—no other animal looks quite like it. It is a solitary creature that knows and defends its territory, but does not hang out in groups. It has no flaw—it is truly as extraordinary as it appears. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The basilisk can do something no other animal can—and looks as if doing so were the most natural thing in the world. Uniqueness and capability merge into one.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Eastern Brown Snake: Quiet Master of Withdrawal</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Eastern Brown Snake—one of Australia's most venomous snakes—stands out for what it does not do: it makes no noise, displays no vivid color, and seeks no confrontation. It is the most inconspicuous snake in an environment full of inconspicuous snakes—and precisely for that reason, so dangerous. Those who encounter it often notice it only at the last moment. Carrying the second most toxic venom of any land snake in the world, it nevertheless uses it only as a last resort. First it flees. Then it freezes. Only when its escape path is blocked and no other option remains does it strike—swiftly, precisely, without warning. The brown snake lives in hiding, hunts at dusk, and rests under rocks and bark during the day. It gathers knowledge about its environment without being seen itself. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The most dangerous thing about the brown snake is not its venom—it is the knowledge it accumulates about its surroundings while no one observes it. It knows everything. And no one knows it is there.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Desert Gecko: Alert to the Very Pores</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Desert Gecko is a marvel of adaptation: the adhesive lamellae on its toe pads allow it to scale vertical glass surfaces through Van der Waals forces acting between millions of nanoscale setae and the surface. No glue, no suction cups. Pure physics, perfectly exploited. At night, it hears sounds that do not exist for the human ear and can move its eyes independently of each other—each eye surveying its own horizon. In the desert gecko, alertness exists as a fundamental state: it registers every change in its environment, adapts to temperature, prey, and threat levels in real time, and communicates with conspecifics through a complex repertoire of sounds—geckos are the loudest reptiles in the world relative to their body size. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The desert gecko is never fully relaxed—but it is also never paralyzed. It has learned to live with its vigilance and transformed it into a capability that secures its survival in any environment.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Anole: Life Is a Play of Colors</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Anole is one of the most vibrant and diverse reptiles in the world: over 400 species, a different one on every Caribbean island, each with its own color variation, dewlap color, and rhythm. Anoles change color—not like chameleons for camouflage, but as an expression of mood: vivid green signifies excitement, brown indicates stress or cold. Their dewlap, inflated in the sun, is one of the most colorful structures in the entire animal kingdom. They are small, lightning-fast, curious—and incapable of staying motionlessly quiet. Anoles constantly explore, constantly climb, constantly change positions. An anole sitting on the same branch for ten minutes is a sick anole. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The anole brings color to a world that would be grayer without it. Its restlessness is not a deficit—it is its way of discovering the world. Always the next branch, always the next color.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Crocodile: Primal Force Without Disguise</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Crocodile has remained virtually unchanged for 200 million years—because it was perfect from the beginning. It survived the dinosaurs, endured ice ages, and outlasted all mass extinctions. Its body structure is an engineering feat of evolution: eyes and nostrils are positioned on top, allowing it to vanish completely underwater while simultaneously breathing and watching. Its bite force surpasses that of any other animal—a jaw pressure of up to 16,000 Newtons, more than triple that of a lion. And yet the crocodile sits motionlessly on the riverbank for hours—a rock, a stone, an aesthetic void. Until prey comes within reach. Then, in a tenth of a second, it is there—without a running start, without warning, without a chance for defense. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The crocodile has no time for games. It knows its worth, knows its power—and needs no one to confirm it. It waits until the moment arrives. And then it is completely there.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Turtle: The Wisdom of Slowness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Turtle is the oldest and most peaceful reptile known to humanity—and in nearly every culture across the globe, a symbol of wisdom, longevity, and inner peace. In Iroquois cosmology, the turtle carries the Earth upon its back. In Chinese thought, it represents the unity of Heaven and Earth—its domed shell is Heaven, its flat plastron is Earth. It has no natural enemy from which it flees through speed—instead, it carries its home upon its back: at home everywhere and at all times, always protected, never homeless. Turtles live in community without competing. They share basking spots without fighting. They withdraw without fleeing. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The turtle knows what most living creatures forget: those who do not need to fight because they rest within themselves have found the deepest form of strength. Its shell is not a shield against the world—it is the expression that it does not need to conquer the world in order to live in it.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, Charts, and Type Descriptions – Volume 1 of the Trilogy That Brings the Enneagram to Life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-Depth Type Profiles and Archetypal Reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, Paths to Healing, and the Big Picture – Volume 3 as the Trilogy's Conclusion.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"spinnenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Spider Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"schildkroetenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Turtle Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"fischarten-der-9-typen", label:"Fish Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function schildkroetenartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Schildkr\u00f6tenarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Turtles are the oldest reptiles on Earth—they have existed almost unchanged for over 220 million years. While dinosaurs came and went, the turtle survived. Not through speed, not through size—but because each species has perfected a distinct principle. Nine turtle species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/schildkroetenarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Schildkr\u00f6tenarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Green Sea Turtle: The Inner Compass</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Green Sea Turtle</strong> always returns to the exact same beach where it hatched to lay its eggs—even if that beach is thousands of kilometers away. It navigates using Earth's magnetic field with a precision that puts GPS systems to shame. Researchers have tracked individual animals over decades and found that they lay eggs on the exact same stretch of beach, sometimes on the exact same meter as their mother and grandmother. No detours, no deviation. The Green Sea Turtle is named for its green body fat—not its scales—because it feeds exclusively on seagrass and algae, maintaining seagrass meadows like a lawnmower: through regular grazing, it keeps the meadows vital, supporting the entire marine food chain. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: It follows no external rulebook. It follows an inner compass that is always true—thereby enabling not just its own life, but the ecosystem around it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Green Turtle (Suppenschildkröte/Loggerhead/Sea Turtle): Devotion That Sustains</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Loggerhead Sea Turtle</strong> is the most social of the large sea turtles: it actively seeks contact with conspecifics, gathering in hundreds at feeding grounds and nesting beaches. Females return together, laying eggs side by side, and their hatchlings emerge in coordinated waves—together they have a higher chance of survival because predators cannot cope with the sheer volume. For centuries, it was the animal that sustained humans on long sea voyages—living provisions that survived for months without water and food. It served humans until it was hunted nearly to extinction. Its name stems from the fact that its meat actually tastes like veal—to its great misfortune. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The Loggerhead carries others—literally. Its devotion to the community is its strongest trait. And its greatest risk.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Leatherback Sea Turtle: Records as a Way of Life</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Leatherback Sea Turtle</strong> is the largest, fastest, and deepest-diving turtle in the world: up to 2.1 meters long, weighing 900 kilograms, diving down to 1,280 meters—deeper than any other sea turtle. It covers the longest migratory distances of all turtles: up to 20,000 kilometers per year, from nesting beaches in the tropics to jellyfish blooms in polar waters. It lacks a hard shell—its back is covered in a leathery skin—but it is thermoregulating: as the only turtle, it can keep its body temperature above the ambient temperature, remaining active even in freezing water. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The Leatherback does not break records for the sake of setting them. It is built to make the impossible ordinary—and then does so as if it were a matter of course.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Hawksbill Sea Turtle: Beauty as Destiny</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Hawksbill Sea Turtle</strong> is the most beautiful turtle in the world: its shell is a work of art made of amber, flamed plates—each one unique, no two animals alike. This very uniqueness nearly became its doom: for centuries, its shell was processed into "tortoiseshell" for jewelry, combs, eyeglass frames, and art objects—it was literally robbed of its identity. Today, it is critically endangered. It thus recalls a deep truth of the Type 4 principle: the uniqueness that attracts others can also become a burden when the world only extracts use from it. The Hawksbill lives at specific coral sites known only to it, feeds on sponges that other animals avoid—and is thus the only one keeping the ecosystem of the sponge reefs in balance. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: Its uniqueness is not an ornament—it is its contribution, which no one else can make.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Softshell Turtle: Hidden and Invincible</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Softshell Turtle</strong> has no hard shell—its back is made of flexible, leathery tissue. What sounds like a weakness is its strongest asset: it can burrow lightning-fast into riverbeds and mud, lying in wait completely invisible for prey. No one sees it. No one looks for it. It emerges when it is ready—and strikes with a speed the eye can barely follow. Softshell turtles can breathe through their skin when burrowed—they do not even need to surface. Its ability to retreat is absolute: it doesn't just pull its head in—it literally vanishes into its environment. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The Softshell Turtle controls when it is visible and when it is not. Its power lies not in its armor—but in its complete autonomy over when it appears.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Red-Eared Slider: Safety in Community</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Red-Eared Slider</strong> is the best-known aquatic turtle in the world—and one of the most vigilant: it basks on rocks and logs in groups, always ready to slide into the water lightning-fast at the slightest sign of danger. It tolerates no surprises. As soon as a shadow moves or a sound is heard, it is gone—it does not hesitate. This caution is not a sign of weakness, but a proven protective principle: whoever is always vigilant lives longer. At the same time, the Red-Eared Slider is highly social—it always basks in a group, and the larger the group, the safer it feels. Many eyes see more than two. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: Safety is not created by retreat, but by cohesion and vigilance. The Red-Eared Slider has perfected both.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Map Turtle: Curiosity Without Limits</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Map Turtle</strong> – named after the ridges on its shell – is one of the most active and curious land/freshwater turtles: it travels long distances, systematically explores its territory, eats everything that comes before its nose – plants, fruits, insects, mushrooms – and shows an openness that consistently surprises wildlife observers. It is enduringly active, even in the midday sun when other turtles rest in the shade. Its shell surface – these characteristic ridges that flatten out with age – gives it a distinctive silhouette that looks like a backbone forged from past adventures. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The Map Turtle does not rest as long as there is still something to explore. Life is a grand menu – and it wants to taste a bit of everything.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Snapping Turtle: Power Without Negotiation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Snapping Turtle</strong> is the most aggressive turtle in the world: its jaw develops a bite force that shatters wood, and unlike most turtles, it does not pull its head back into its shell – its shell is too small for that. Instead, it attacks. That is its strategy: no retreat, no hiding, no defense. If it feels threatened, it snaps. This snap can happen in 50 milliseconds – barely perceptible to the human eye. Yet in the water – its actual habitat – the Snapping Turtle is astonishingly peaceful: it dives down, avoids conflicts, hunts quietly. Only outside of water, terrain foreign to it, does it become a risk. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The Snapping Turtle has no mask. In its own territory, it is sovereign and calm – but whoever threatens it experiences its answer immediately and without warning.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – European Pond Turtle: Silent Constancy</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>European Pond Turtle</strong> is one of the oldest native reptile species in Central Europe – and one of the most unobtrusive. It lives in ponds, wetlands, and quiet river sections, basks on drifting logs, and slips silently underwater at any disturbance. It does not fight, it does not flee far – it simply waits until the danger has passed. Then it resurfaces as if nothing had happened. Pond turtles can live to be over 100 years old, displaying a remarkable adaptability to changing environments: they thrive in almost any clean wetland, tolerate heat and cold, and cope with plenty or little. Different animals in a body of water know each other – they share basking spots without fighting, taking turns quietly. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The Pond Turtle needs no center of attention. It only needs its body of water, its peace, and the next quiet morning. And in that stillness, it contributes more to the harmony of the ecosystem than most louder ones ever will.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"reptilienarten-der-9-typen", label:"Reptile Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"fischarten-der-9-typen", label:"Fish Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"walarten-der-9-typen", label:"Whale Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function fischartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Fischarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Fish have populated the Earth for over 500 million years—they were here before the dinosaurs and will still be here after us. Each species has developed a distinct principle that ensures its survival. Nine fish species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/fischarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Fischarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Rainbow Trout: Perfection in Motion</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Rainbow Trout</strong> is one of the most precise fish in existence: its streamlined body is a masterpiece of evolution, optimized for crystal-clear flowing water with the highest oxygen content. It lives exclusively in clean, cold water—the slightest pollution drives it away immediately. As a natural bio-indicator, its presence is considered a sign of an intact, healthy body of water. Its movements in the water are of extraordinary precision: it holds position with millimeter accuracy in the current shadow of rocks, waits for insects hitting the water surface, and snaps with a single, targeted leap. No waste, no improvisation. Its iridescent coat of colors—the characteristic rainbow stripe—is not showing off, but the natural recognition mark of its kind. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: Order and purity are not demands placed on others—they are the inner standard by which the rainbow trout lives. Anyone who pollutes its territory will never get to see it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Clownfish: Protection Through Devotion</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Clownfish</strong> lives in one of the most astonishing symbioses of marine life: it dwells in the tentacles of the sea anemone—an animal that instantly kills any other fish with its stinging cells. The clownfish is immune because it builds up a protective film of the anemone's mucus substance through a slow, patient approach. What it gives in return: it defends the anemone against its predators, cleans its tentacles, and attracts other fish with its colorful appearance—which then serve as food for the anemone. This mutual care and support is no side effect—it is the clownfish's life principle. Astonishingly, all clownfish are born as males; the dominant fish in a group becomes a female. If the group loses its female, the highest-ranking male immediately takes over its role. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The clownfish has no safe place for itself alone—but it creates a safe place for others. And precisely in doing so, it finds its own protection.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Salmon: Performance Until the Last Breath</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Salmon</strong> is the most enduring high performer among fish: born in fresh water, it migrates to the sea as a juvenile, lives there for three to five years—and then returns, with an infallible sense of smell, to the exact same river where it hatched. The return journey can span thousands of kilometers against the current, over waterfalls, through shallows. The salmon does not eat on this journey. It lives off its body reserves until it reaches the spawning ground, deposits its eggs, and dies. Its final act is its greatest: the decomposing body fertilizes the riverbed and ensures the survival of the next generation. No other fish embodies readiness for commitment so uncompromisingly. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The salmon does not ask if the effort pays off. It knows where it must go—and it arrives, no matter what lies on the way. Success is not an option. It is the goal.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Mandarin Fish: Uniqueness as a Way of Life</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Mandarin Fish</strong> is the most colorful fish in the world: a luminous mosaic of blue, orange, green, and turquoise, in patterns that no two animals share identically. What makes it even more remarkable: these colors are not produced by pigments—rather by a unique cell structure that refracts and reflects light. The mandarin fish is one of the few fish that have no scales; its skin is covered with a toxic slime layer that deters predators. It lives in hidden reefs, shy and ready to retreat, but emerges in the evening at sunset to mate—in a dance so precisely choreographed that pairs recognize each other after just one encounter. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The mandarin fish does not need to adapt. It wears its uniqueness on its skin—literally. Whoever wants to see it must come to it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Anglerfish: Knowledge from the Darkness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Anglerfish</strong> lives in the deepest, darkest layers of the ocean—at depths up to 2,000 meters, where no sunlight reaches, the pressure would crush humans, and the temperature barely rises above zero degrees. It carries its own light source: a bioluminescent organ above its head that it extends like a fishing rod to lure prey into the darkness. The anglerfish barely moves—it observes, waits, analyzes. When prey arrives, it reacts with a mouth full of backward-curving teeth that allow no escape. Astonishingly, the female is up to ten times larger than the male. The male literally fuses with the female during mating and becomes part of her body. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The anglerfish needs no outside world to survive. It creates its own light in the darkness—and uses it to lure precisely what it needs.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Coral Guard: Loyalty as a Shield</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Coral Guard</strong>—also known as the cleaner wrasse—takes on one of the most important roles in the coral reef: it runs so-called cleaning stations where larger fish stop to be freed from parasites, dead skin, and wound germs. What is remarkable: these larger fish—which would normally eat the coral guard—stay calm, open mouths and gills, and completely trust the small fish. It literally enters their mouths and leaves them unharmed. This trust is no stroke of luck: it is the result of millions of years of old cooperation in which both sides have learned that loyalty protects more than strength. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The coral guard protects others—and is thereby protected itself. Safety is not created by isolation, but by trust that is earned.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Parrotfish: The Reef as a Playground</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Parrotfish</strong> is one of the liveliest inhabitants of the coral reef: colorful like a parrot, with a beak-like mouth apparatus made of fused teeth with which it literally bites off coral, digests it, and excretes it as fine white sand. The white sandy beach of many tropical islands consists largely of the digestion product of parrotfish. At night, it sleeps in a self-produced slime bubble—a kind of sleeping bag that protects it from parasites and predators. During the day, it is constantly in motion: nibbling here, swimming there, exploring every corner of the reef. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The parrotfish transforms what it touches—in the literal sense. Its hunger does not lead to destruction, but to creation. Curiosity as a way of life that changes the world.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Grouper: Ruler of the Territory</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Grouper</strong> is one of the most dominant predatory fish of the sea: a massive, fast ambush predator that claims its territory with a clearly recognizable presence and uncompromisingly drives away intruders. It can reach considerable sizes—some species exceeding two meters and 400 kilograms—while maintaining an agility that contradicts its size. What few know: groupers also change their sex—but in reverse to the clownfish: they start as females and, when they are large and strong enough, become males. Strength and status are not given away—they are worked for and earned. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: The grouper does not ask for permission who occupies the territory. It enforces it—with presence, power, and the clarity that requires no explanation.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Koi: Harmony in Every Basin</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Koi</strong> is the most peaceful and beloved ornamental fish in the world: in Japan, it is considered a symbol of luck, longevity, endurance, and harmony. It lives in community, gets along with almost all other fish species, and its calm movement in the water—slow, flowing, without haste—has had a meditative character for centuries for people sitting at ponds. Koi can live to be over 200 years old, recognize their caretakers, and let themselves be petted by them. They are highly adaptable: they thrive in almost any clean body of water, tolerate cold and warmth, and get by with little. Their peace does not scare them—it is their natural state. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The koi needs no drama to exist. It brings calm into every basin it inhabits—simply by being as it is. And in this stillness, it touches something every human knows: the longing for peace.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"schildkroetenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Turtle Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"walarten-der-9-typen", label:"Whale Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"insektenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Insect Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function walartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Walarten der 9 Typen", "Charts \u00b7 Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Whales are the largest and most enigmatic creatures on Earth—they dive deeper than we could measure, sing songs that reach thousands of kilometers, and live in communities that remind us of human cultures. Nine whale species, nine Enneagram principles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/walarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Walarten der 9 Typen \u2013 Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Blue Whale: The Measure of All Things</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Blue Whale</strong> is the largest animal that has ever lived on Earth: up to 33 meters long, up to 190 tons heavy—heavier than the largest dinosaur, heavier than 30 elephants. Its heart is as large as a small car, its aorta wide enough for a human to crawl through. And despite this unimaginable size, it moves with a precision that continues to fascinate researchers to this day: it travels on exact same routes between its summer and winter grounds, year after year, without deviation. Everything about it is designed for the maximum—but this maximum is not excess, rather the expression of an inner order that knows no compromises. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The blue whale is not the measure because it is the largest—it is the largest because it never stopped being completely true to its principle.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Humpback Whale: The Singer of the Sea</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Humpback Whale</strong> sings. Not just somehow—but with a precision and complexity that musicologists still find fascinating today. Its songs last up to 20 hours, fully structured like compositions with themes, repetitions, and transitions. What is particularly touching: humpbacks clean each other, travel as pairs over thousands of kilometers, and mothers accompany their calves with a devotion that moves even experienced marine biologists. Even more astonishing: humpbacks have been observed multiple times protecting other whale species—even seals—from killer whale attacks without personal advantage or cause. They simply help. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The humpback whale does not ask if it pays off. It sings because connection is its essence—and it helps because it cannot be otherwise.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Fin Whale: The Eternal High Performer</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Fin Whale</strong> is the second-fastest whale in the world—and one of the largest: up to 27 meters long, 80 tons heavy, yet with a top speed of over 45 kilometers per hour. It was called the "greyhound of the sea" by whaling captains: it was so fast that historical sailing ships simply could not catch it. The fin whale never pauses for long—it is constantly on the move, migrating from polar seas to tropical regions and back, covering tens of thousands of kilometers. It does not freeze, it does not stop, it does not wait. It is what it does. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: The fin whale is not successful because it pushes itself—rather because performance is not a means for it, but the most natural expression of its essence.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Narwhal: The Unicorn of the Sea</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Narwhal</strong> is the most enigmatic animal of the Arctic Ocean: it carries a spiral tusk up to three meters long—actually a forward-growing canine tooth—and even now, no one knows for sure what its exact purpose is. It is no fighting instrument, no hunting tool, no simple signal device. It is unique. The narwhal lives in small, deeply bonded groups, dives up to 1,500 meters deep, and avoids humans with exceptional shyness. For centuries, it was revered as a magical creature by Inuit cultures, and its horn found its way into European pharmacies as a "unicorn horn"—as a universal remedy whose value exceeded that of gold. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: Some beauty eludes explanation. The narwhal does not exist to be understood. It exists because it is itself—and in a way that the world does not forget.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Sperm Whale: The Thinker of the Deep Sea</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Sperm Whale</strong> has the largest brain that has ever existed on Earth: up to 7.8 kilograms, six times heavier than a human's. It dives alone down to 3,000 meters deep—in complete darkness, under a pressure that would deform steel—and stays there for up to two hours. No other animal spends more time alone at greater depths. What it looks for there: giant squids that hardly any other animal ever gets to see. Sperm whales communicate via a complex click pattern—called coda—which researchers have been studying as a possible language for years. In 2024, scientists began an AI project to decode this language. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The most valuable lies in the depths. The sperm whale dives where no one else goes—and brings back what no one else has.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Gray Whale: The Guardian of the Community</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Gray Whale</strong> undertakes one of the longest migrations of all mammals: up to 20,000 kilometers per year between the lagoons of Baja California and the Arctic seas—back and forth, calmly and persistently, year after year, on the same routes its ancestors have traveled for millennia. It avoids conflicts, shuns disputes, and its calves are born in shallow lagoon waters, far away from open seas and dangers. Gray whale mother-calf pairs are known for their special intimacy—the calf stays close to the mother for months, and both communicate quietly and steadily. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The gray whale finds safety not through strength, but through loyalty—to the community, to the proven path, to the connection one can rely on.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Dolphin: The Joy of Living</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Dolphin</strong> is the most playful marine animal in the world—and one of the most intelligent. Dolphins surf in the bow waves of ships, jump out of the water for no reason, play with each other, invent new games, and pass them on. They are the only animals other than humans in which researchers observe true "purposeless play" in adulthood—joy for the sake of joy. Dolphins can recognize themselves in mirrors, quickly learn new skills, and their social networks are so complex that researchers had to develop specialized analysis methods to understand them. They are also the only animals that keep humans in distress afloat—presumably because they can, because they want to. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The dolphin needs no justification for its joy. Life is the game—and it plays it with complete devotion.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Killer Whale: Power with System</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Killer Whale</strong> – Orca – is the most effective predator of the sea: it hunts sharks, rays, dolphins, and whales up to ten times its size, doing so with a strategic precision that impresses even military planners. Orcas have hunting tactics passed down from generation to generation—regional "cultures" that differ from one another like corporate cultures. They work in teams with clearly assigned roles: some herd the prey, others charge, while others secure the retreat path. Orcas can control their voice so precisely that they use echolocation as a weapon—stunning prey with targeted sound waves. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: Power is not an excess—it is a tool. The orca uses it when necessary, with full physical commitment and without hesitation. And not a second sooner.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Bryde's Whale: Silent Greatness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Bryde's Whale</strong> – named after the Norwegian consul Johan Bryde – is one of the least known large whales: no record holder, no spectacular hunter, no winner in any of the major comparisons. It is the whale that simply lives. It inhabits tropical and subtropical seas, follows no strict migration pattern, and moves without the compulsion to move—it is wherever the food is, wherever it is warm, wherever it is good. Bryde's whales are solitary or live in small, loose groups. They are neither loud nor aggressive, neither flashy nor record-breaking. They are simply whole. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: Bryde's whale needs no category in which it is the best. It only needs the peace in which it belongs to itself—and in that peace, it is complete.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist \u2013 Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"fischarten-der-9-typen", label:"Fish Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"insektenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Insect Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"baumarten", label:"Tree Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function insektenartenDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Insektenarten der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Insects are the most successful living creatures on Earth—they have existed for 400 million years, make up over 80 percent of all animal species, and have colonized almost every habitat. And every species follows a distinct principle. Nine insects, nine Enneagram types.</p>
      <img src="../assets/insektenarten-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Insektenarten der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Ant: Order as a Way of Life</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Ant</strong> is the most disciplined creature on Earth. An ant colony functions without a top-down hierarchy—every ant knows what to do and does it. No laziness, no evasion, no improvisation. Ants build structures that, relative to their body size, surpass the pyramids of Giza. They lay paths, optimize transport routes, and organize provisions with an efficiency that logistics experts still study today. Astonishingly, ants do not wage meaningless wars—they only attack when the colony is threatened and retreat in an orderly fashion when the battle is lost. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: Order is not a restriction—it is the prerequisite for the whole to function. The ant does not live for itself. It lives for what is right.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Bee: Devotion to the Community</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Bee</strong> is the most selfless insect in the world. In its short six-week life, a worker bee flies up to 800 kilometers, visits hundreds of thousands of flowers—and in the end produces exactly one teaspoon of honey. Not for itself. For the hive. Bees communicate through the waggle dance, one of the most complex animal languages we know—to show others where the best flowers are. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The bee does not ask what it gets. It asks what is needed—and delivers it. Without the bee, no pollination, no fruit, no harvest. It holds the ecosystem together without knowing it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Field Cricket: Attention Through Performance</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Field Cricket</strong> is the loudest insect relative to its body size: the male's chirping reaches up to 100 decibels—louder than a jackhammer three meters away. And that with a body of barely two centimeters. Its chirping is not noise—it is performance: precise frequencies tuned to the females' auditory organs. Every cricket has its own rhythm, its own pattern. Crickets chirp faster when it is warmer—a correlation so reliable that the air temperature can be calculated from the chirping frequency. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: Performance is audible. The cricket does not draw attention to itself because it is flashy—but because its performance is. Success carries itself.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Butterfly: Transformation as Identity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Butterfly</strong> is the only creature that reinvents itself completely: as a caterpillar it crawls, as a pupa it literally dissolves—its body liquefies inside the pupal shell—and as a butterfly something completely new emerges. This metamorphosis is not growth, but a transformation of identity. Butterflies communicate through colors and patterns visible to the human eye—but also through ultraviolet light that only insects can perceive. There is an inner layer of beauty that is not meant for others. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: Whoever truly wants to become themselves must be willing to dissolve completely. The butterfly shows: transformation is not a loss—it is the deepest form of expression.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Praying Mantis: Silent Observation, Deadly Precision</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Praying Mantis</strong> is the most philosophical hunter of the insect world: it hardly moves, waits patiently, observes everything—and then strikes with a precision the human eye can scarcely follow. Its strike takes 50 to 70 milliseconds—faster than a human eye can blink. It is the only insect that can turn its head, giving it a three-dimensional field of view. Praying mantises have a unique frontal ocellus eye that measures distances with the highest accuracy. The female eats the male after mating—it takes what it needs while maintaining complete control. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: Whoever observes everything before acting needs only a single strike.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Termite: Security Through Community</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Termites</strong> build the most astonishing structures in the animal kingdom: termite mounds in Africa can reach up to nine meters high—which corresponds, relative to body size, to a human structure over a kilometer high. These buildings are air-conditioned: an internal ventilation system keeps the temperature constant at 30 degrees, regardless of the outside temperature. Every termite has a clearly defined role—worker, soldier, queen—and none deviates from it. The survival of the individual is inextricably linked to the survival of the colony. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: Security is not created by individual achievement, but by the trust that everyone fulfills their role. The termite mound holds because everyone builds.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Dragonfly: Master of Freedom</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Dragonfly</strong> is the most agile flying insect in the world: it can fly in all six directions—forward, backward, sideways, hovering—while controlling its four wings completely independently of one another. Its hunting strategy is spectacular: it calculates in flight where its prey will be and flies there—not where it is. With a hit rate of over 95 percent, it is the most successful hunter in the animal kingdom. Dragonflies have 360-degree vision through their huge compound eyes. They literally see the world from all sides simultaneously. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: Freedom does not mean a lack of planning—rather the ability to set off in any direction at any moment. The dragonfly already knows where the prey will be.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Hornet: Power Without Apology</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Hornet</strong> is the most powerful insect in Europe: its mandibles can chew wood, its sting is several times more painful than a bee's—and it attacks without hesitation when it sees its territory threatened. What is barely known: hornets are highly intelligent. They build intricately constructed paper nests from chewed wood fibers, actively regulate the temperature inside the nest, and communicate via pheromones with a precision researchers have only decoded in recent years. Hornets do not kill bees without reason—they only hunt when they need food. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: Power is not a problem—it is a resource. The hornet uses it when necessary. And not a second sooner.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Ladybug: Silent Harmony</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>Ladybug</strong> is the most popular insect in the world—in almost all cultures it is considered a symbol of luck, a messenger of peace, a sign that everything will be fine. It is neither loud nor aggressive, neither the largest nor the fastest. It eats aphids and thereby keeps the ecosystem in balance—invisible, silent, without a fuss. It wears its bright red back with black spots as its only warning: I taste bad. That is enough. Ladybugs overwinter in groups of thousands, tightly packed together, in complete silence—one of nature's quietest assemblies. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The most harmonious contribution is often the one no one notices—until it is missing. The ladybug keeps the world in balance simply by being there.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"walarten-der-9-typen", label:"Whale Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"flugzeugmodelle-der-9-typen", label:"Airplane Models of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"baumarten", label:"Tree Species of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function flugzeugmodelleDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Flugzeugmodelle der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Nine aircraft—each a masterpiece of engineering, each with an unmistakable character and purpose. From fighter jets to rescue helicopters, vintage biplanes to long-range business jets: What Enneagram principle lies behind these nine aircraft types?</p>
      <img src="../assets/flugzeugmodelle-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Flugzeugmodelle der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Airbus A-380: Precision on a Grand Scale</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Airbus A-380 is the largest passenger aircraft in the world—and the most precise. Developed by over 1,500 engineers in seven countries, it carries up to 853 passengers over distances of more than 15,000 kilometers, with a noise level that, despite its size, is quieter than many smaller aircraft. Every rivet, every weld seam, every system redundancy follows a meticulously crafted plan. What distinguishes the A-380 is not mere size, but the consistency with which it fulfills its mission: reliable, quiet, efficient—everything in its proper place. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: Greatness entails responsibility. The A-380 is not the best because it is the largest—it is the best because, in its size, it makes no compromises on precision.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Perhaps the most impressive proof of this connection is Prince Al-Waleed bin Talal—Saudi billionaire, one of the richest people in the world, and according to Detlef Rathmer's Enneagram assessment, a Self-Preservation One with a Nine-wing (SP1w9). He had an Airbus A-380 converted into a flying palace: featuring a throne room, a concert grand piano, bedrooms, a garage for his car, and a lounge area equivalent to a five-star hotel. This is not mere ostentation—this is the Self-Preservation One in its clearest expression: being fully prepared, carrying everything necessary along, depending on no external resource. A concert grand piano on board—that is deliberately chosen, a statement of the Type 1 principle: the grand piano is perhaps the most precise instrument in classical music, built to hundredths of a millimeter, tuned to physical laws that tolerate no compromise. Whether Al-Waleed plays himself or simply appreciates the presence of this instrument as an expression of perfection—it belongs to his basic equipment. He does not arrive like a king. He travels as a king.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Eurocopter EC-145: Help That Arrives</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Eurocopter EC-145 is the rescue helicopter of German air rescue—the red vehicle with the DRF logo that reaches people in their direst hour when no other vehicle can arrive in time. It lands on mountain peaks, highways, narrow courtyards, and sea beaches. Its sole purpose: to reach the other person as quickly as possible and help. The EC-145 has no weapon systems, no luxury features, no status symbol appeal. It is entirely oriented toward the other. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The most important aircraft is not the fastest or the largest—it is the one that arrives when no one else comes.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Bombardier Global 6000: Success on the Move</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Bombardier Global 6000 is a world-class business jet: an 11,000-kilometer range, up to 17 passengers, a cabin with stand-up headroom, sleeping berths, and a conference area—all at a travel time that beats any commercial flight by hours. It flies CEOs to acquisition negotiations, heads of state to summits, and investors to their next deals. The Global 6000 is not just a means of transport—it is a signal: this person's time is too valuable to share with others. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: Efficiency is not convenience—it is respect for one's own potential. Anyone flying directly to the destination wastes no time on layovers.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Larry Ellison—founder and longtime CEO of Oracle, one of the world's wealthiest people—is a Social Type Three with a Four-wing (SO3w4). He owns several Bombardier business jets and uses them for what they represent to the Social Three: both stage and tool. The Social Three does not merely want to be successful—he wants to be perceived as successful, set benchmarks, and inspire others. Ellison is famous not only for building Oracle, but also for winning the America's Cup, purchasing a Hawaiian island, and becoming a Tesla shareholder—always aiming to be the best at what he does. The Four-wing lends him an unusual depth: he is not only ambitious, but also aesthetic—his yacht, his estate, his jet are always expressions of a personal style.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Piper Cub: Freedom in Simplicity</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Piper Cub is the most romantic of all aircraft: yellow, open, slow, hovering just above the treetops. Produced since 1937 and virtually unchanged, it is the plane of free spirits—the farmers flying over their fields, the artists exploring landscapes from a bird's-eye view, the dreamers for whom flying is not transportation, but expression. The Piper Cub flies with open windows, the wind blowing directly into the cockpit. It is not fast. It is not comfortable. It is unique in its simplicity and, for that very reason, unforgettable. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: The most beautiful aircraft is not the most powerful—it is the one that makes the pilot truly feel the sensation of flight. With the Piper Cub, you touch the sky personally.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Boeing E-3 Sentry (AWACS): The Flying Observer</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Boeing E-3 Sentry is no fighter plane—it is a flying intelligence center. AWACS stands for Airborne Warning and Control System: a modified Boeing 707 featuring a massive radar dome on its back, mapping, evaluating, and coordinating the entire air picture of a conflict from high altitude. It fires no weapons, drops no bombs—it sees everything, analyzes everything, and passes this knowledge along. An E-3 crew member spends their mission in front of screens filled with data points, translating them into situational awareness and strategy. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: Knowledge is power—but only when understood. The AWACS wins no battles through brute force, but through complete overview.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Lockheed C-130 Hercules: The World's Most Reliable Carrier</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Lockheed C-130 Hercules has been in production since 1954—and remains so today. No other military aircraft has survived so many variants, operational countries, and decades. The Hercules lands on unpaved desert strips, Antarctic ice, and high-altitude airfields in the Andes. It supplies troops, delivers relief supplies after disasters, and evacuates civilians from crisis zones. Over 70 nations trust it. It lacks elegant lines—yet it always arrives. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The most reliable aircraft in history is not the most beautiful or the fastest. It is the one that, for 70 years, has never stopped fulfilling its duty.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Extra EA-300: Pure Joy of Flying</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Extra EA-300 is the world's most spectacular aerobatic plane: engineered for maneuvers that no other aircraft survives, boasting a load capability of +10/-10 G and a roll rate that turns pilots into world champions. It was designed by Walter Extra in Dinslaken—a German engineer who redefined stunt flying as a high-performance sport. Extra EA-300 pilots execute knife-edge flights, inverted maneuvers, torque rolls, and flat spins that take the audience's breath away. The plane lives for the moment, for the joy of skill. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The sky is not a pathway—it is a playground. The Extra EA-300 turns every horizon into a new possibility.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">John Travolta—actor, dancer, and passionate pilot—is a Sexual Seven with an Eight-wing (SX7w8). He owns multiple aircraft, flew Boeing 707s and Gulfstream jets himself for decades, and established flying as a second life identity alongside acting. The Sexual Seven with an Eight-wing lives adventure intensely and physically—not as a spectator, but as an actor. Travolta did not just want to play heroes in movies; he wanted to be one himself: at the controls, in the air, with full physical engagement. The Eight's fire gives him the determination to push boundaries—even in the cockpit. The Extra EA-300 with its ±10 G is precisely the right plane for someone for whom the horizon is never distant enough.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – F-16 Fighting Falcon: Untamed Air Superiority</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The F-16 Fighting Falcon is one of history's most successful fighter jets: over 4,600 built, deployed in over 25 nations, with a combat record of over 70 aerial victories without a single loss in air-to-air combat. Developed in the 1970s with a clear maxim: superiority through agility. The F-16 is smaller than many of its rivals, yet faster, more nimble, and more direct. In the cockpit, the pilot sits in a 30-degree reclined seat designed to tolerate forces up to 9 G—more than any other jet of its era. It fights to win. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: Dominance stems not from size—but from the resolve to exploit every advantage in confrontation. The F-16 does not ask. It acts.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Cessna 172: Peace Above the Clouds</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Cessna 172 is the most-produced aircraft in history: over 44,000 units since 1956, still in production today. It is the primary plane in which most pilots worldwide learned to fly—forgiving, stable, and gentle. It is not fast, not glamorous, nor built for extreme maneuvers. But it flies anywhere, with almost anyone, safely and calmly. A Cessna 172 pilot gliding through calm air above a landscape experiences something rare: complete silence amid movement. The plane requires little effort—it naturally wants to fly straight ahead. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The world's most harmonious aircraft requires no conquest of the sky. It simply glides along—and finds its own peace in the process.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, Charts, and Type Descriptions – Volume 1 of the Trilogy That Brings the Enneagram to Life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-Depth Type Profiles and Archetypal Reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, Paths to Healing, and the Big Picture – Volume 3 as the Trilogy's Conclusion.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"brillenmodelle-der-9-typen", label:"Eyeglass Models of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"insektenarten-der-9-typen", label:"Insect Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Eras of World History"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function brillenmodelleDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Brillenmodelle der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Eyeglasses are more than a vision aid—they are the very first thing others perceive about us. Which eyewear model aligns with which Enneagram principle? Detlef Rathmer made himself available as a model and tried on all nine styles.</p>
      <img src="../assets/brillenmodelle-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Brillenmodelle der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Ray-Ban Clubmaster: Classic and Timeless</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Ray-Ban Clubmaster is one of the most iconic pairs of glasses in the world—and has been since 1965. Its hallmark feature is the combination of a bold top browline frame and a rimless bottom: a clear line, nothing superfluous, instantly recognizable. The Clubmaster carries history within it: it was the choice of intellectuals, architects, and teachers—people who value form and posture. It appears orderly without being stiff. Precise without appearing cold. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: The most beautiful glasses are those that do exactly what glasses ought to do—without drawing unnecessary attention to themselves. The Clubmaster is no mere accessory. It is a stance.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Malcolm X—civil rights leader, orator, and one of the most poignant moral voices of the 20th century—wore the Clubmaster at a time when glasses were not yet a fashion accessory, but a symbol of seriousness and conviction. In Detlef Rathmer's assessment, Malcolm X was a Social Type One with a Nine-wing (SO1w9): a person with an absolute moral compass who dedicated his life to fighting for what was right—relentless in his conviction, yet possessing a depth and dignity that transcended mere outrage. The Nine-wing lent him that calm, almost priestly presence that rendered his speeches so impactful: he did not shout—he declared. He was assassinated in 1965 at age 39, right in the midst of a period of transformation he had helped set in motion. The Clubmaster fit him like no other eyewear: clear, principled, uncompromised.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Cat-Eye: Warm and Inviting</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cat-Eye glasses originated in the 1950s and quickly became a symbol of warmth, charm, and approachable grace. Their upswept outer corners lend the face a friendly, inviting expression—as though the frame itself were smiling. It carries feminine connotations without being exclusive, representing the desire to be liked and to bring joy to others. Interestingly, the Cat-Eye has been experiencing a renaissance for years—precisely because it radiates warmth in a world of cool minimalist aesthetics. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: Eyewear that opens the face rather than merely framing it. The Cat-Eye renders the wearer more accessible—and that is its true purpose.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Oakley: Performance and Dynamics</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Oakley was founded in 1975 in Southern California—originally for BMX riders and motocross racers who required protection and high performance. Today, Oakley is the brand of world-class athletes: downhill skiers, triathletes, and Tour de France winners wear Oakley. Aerodynamic, scratch-resistant, and engineered with technologies originally designed for space travel. An Oakley signals: I am ready. I am focused. I am driven by performance. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: Eyewear as equipment, not ornament. Oakley does not do things by halves—and neither do its wearers.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – John Lennon Round Glasses: Unmistakable Expression</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The round spectacles worn by John Lennon from the mid-1960s onward became one of pop culture's strongest visual symbols. Lennon did not choose them for fashion's sake—he chose them because they suited him: unconventional, gentle, slightly playful, profound. The John Lennon frame is not loud. It is distinct. It is worn by individuals who decline to follow the crowd, whose inner world is larger than the outer one. In the 1990s, the style regained popularity through alternative artists, poets, and musicians. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: Eyewear as self-expression. Not: What is trendy? But rather: Who am I? The round frame says: I see the world differently.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">John Lennon himself is, in Detlef Rathmer's view, a prime example of a Social Type Four with a Five-wing (SO4w5)—perhaps the purest embodiment of this subtype in pop history. The Social Four does not merely struggle for personal identity; he carries his pain and uniqueness out into the world as an artistic and societal mission. Lennon did not write intimate songs purely for himself: he wrote "Imagine" for humanity, "Working Class Hero" for the oppressed, and "Woman" as a declaration of love with universal resonance. The Five-wing granted him the depth and distance of an observer—analyzing the world before singing about it. And his round glasses were the visible sign of this inner orientation: I do not belong to the crowd. I see you—but from a different vantage point.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Aviator: Analytical Vision</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Aviator glasses were developed in 1936 by Bausch & Lomb for U.S. military pilots with a single objective: maximum field of vision, minimal distraction. The teardrop-shaped lenses cover the entire visual field, giving the wearer the sensation of holding everything in view. Tom Cruise popularized them in 1986's "Top Gun"—yet their true origin is analytical, not glamorous. They are the observer's eyewear: precise, functional, designed for total overview. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: Eyewear originally engineered to observe everything from high altitudes. The Aviator is no mere statement—it is an instrument of long-range vision.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Wayfarer: Reliable and Time-Tested</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Introduced in 1952, the Ray-Ban Wayfarer is the best-selling pair of sunglasses in history. It weathered fashion trends, decades of change, and the rise and fall of countless competitors—yet it remains, unchanged in its core proportions. The Wayfarer is the eyewear of trust: everyone knows it, everyone has worn a pair or known someone who did. It is not the most daring choice—it is the safest. And that is its strength. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: The best glasses are those that haven't let anyone down in 70 years. Reliability is not a virtue of the boring—it is the highest form of loyalty.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Colorful Retro Glasses: Joy and Liveliness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Colorful retro glasses are the exact opposite of understatement: rendered in orange, turquoise, purple, or tortoiseshell blends—they demand to be seen. Drawing on 1960s and '70s designs, they carry those eras into the present with enthusiasm. Anyone wearing vibrant retro glasses has made a clear choice: I turn life into an experience. Even seeing should bring joy. Elton John built his entire stage identity around his eyewear collection—hundreds of frames, each a celebration. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: Eyewear is allowed to celebrate. Life contains enough gray moments—the glasses don't need to add another.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The most famous face behind colorful glasses is undoubtedly Elton John—musician, global star, and owner of one of the most extraordinary eyewear collections in history: over 250,000 pairs, including heart-shaped, rhinestone-encrusted, light-up, and winged models. For Elton John, glasses were never just an accessory—they served as stage, costume, and identity all in one. He once described himself: "I'm the man with the crazy glasses." In terms of the Enneagram, Detlef Rathmer assesses Elton John as a Self-Preservation Seven with a Six-wing (SP7w6): the family man among Sevens, who lives out his joie de vivre not only on stage, but deeply anchored in belonging, loyalty, and the need for a secure nest. The Six-wing provides warmth and faithfulness—he has been married to his partner David Furnish for decades, actively supports AIDS research, and adopted two sons. Elton John's glasses declare: Life is a party—and everyone is invited.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Shield: Dominance and Protection</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Shield glasses—featuring a single, continuous lens instead of two separate ones—are the eyewear of dominance. Covering more of the face while revealing less, they act like armor. Originally developed for extreme sports and military applications, they became the preferred accessory for rappers, motorcycle racers, and bodyguards. A pair of Shield glasses says: I am watching—and you don't know exactly how. The imbalance between what the wearer sees and what others can perceive is intentional. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: Eyewear as territory. Whoever wears a Shield controls what emanates outward—while maintaining a complete overview.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Panto: Round Harmony</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Panto glasses—featuring a slightly flattened, soft, and balanced oval shape—are the most harmonious of all frame styles. Their rounded contours enclose no harsh corners or sharp edges. They suit nearly every face, every style, and every occasion. The Panto has no rivals. It is the eyewear of mediators: not overly conspicuous, not overly reserved, simply pleasantly present. In classical optometry, the Panto is considered the shape that most closely mirrors the natural contours of the human eye. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The most beautiful form is the one that nowhere creates friction. The Panto does not say "Look at me"—it says "I am here whenever you need me."</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, Charts, and Type Descriptions – Volume 1 of the Trilogy That Brings the Enneagram to Life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-Depth Type Profiles and Archetypal Reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, Paths to Healing, and the Big Picture – Volume 3 as the Trilogy's Conclusion.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"luxusuhren-der-9-typen", label:"Luxury Watches of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"flugzeugmodelle-der-9-typen", label:"Aircraft Models of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Eras of World History"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function luxusuhrend9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Luxusuhren der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Nine watches – each a masterpiece of horology, each the expression of an unmistakable character. Which Enneagram principle lies behind these icons on the wrist?</p>
      <img src="../assets/luxusuhren-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Luxusuhren der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Patek Philippe Calatrava: Timeless Perfection</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Patek Philippe Calatrava is the purest expression of what a watch can be: no superfluous detail, no decoration for decoration's sake – only the perfect interplay of form and function. Virtually unchanged in its basic form since 1932, it embodies the ideal of simple perfection. Patek Philippe – founded in Geneva in 1839 – is the house that coined the famous phrase: "You never actually own a Patek Philippe. You merely look after it for the next generation." The principle of Enneagram Type 1: Quality doesn't need attention – it speaks for itself, across generations. The Calatrava is not the loudest watch in the room. It is the most correct.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">One of the most famous wearers of Patek Philippe was Queen Elizabeth II – perhaps the most disciplined and duty-bound monarch of the 20th century. In terms of the Enneagram, according to Detlef Rathmer's assessment, a Self-Preservation One with a Nine-wing (SP1w9): a woman who dedicated her entire life to duty without ever breaking character – calm, steadfast, unshakeable. That she wore a Patek fits the picture. This watch is like her: timeless, proper, and without any need for approval.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Cartier Tank: Elegance as a Gift</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Cartier Tank was designed in 1917 by Louis Cartier – inspired by the bird's-eye view of a First World War tank. Ever since, it has been the most elegant gift in watchmaking: not a watch you buy for yourself, but one you give to another. Princess Diana wore it. Andy Warhol wore it. Jackie Kennedy wore it. Coco Chanel wore it. The Tank represents a luxury that doesn't boast – but is attentive, connecting, and given away. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: The most beautiful thing about possession is giving. The most elegant watch in the world was designed to shine on someone else's wrist.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Rolex Submariner: Success as a Symbol</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Rolex Submariner is the most famous luxury watch in the world – and that is no surprise. Developed in 1953 as a professional diver's watch, it quickly became an icon of success: James Bond wore it, yacht owners wore it, CEOs wore it. It is water-resistant to 300 meters, precise, robust – and recognizable. That is the decisive factor: Everyone knows what a Submariner is. Rolex doesn't sell watches; Rolex sells recognition. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: Success must be visible. The Submariner is the universal signal: I have made it – without saying a single word.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Hublot Big Bang: Art on the Wrist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Hublot Big Bang is the watch that broke all the rules of watchmaking and thereby invented a new language: rubber and gold, carbon and titanium, sapphire and ceramic – materials that no one had combined before. Jean-Claude Biver, who brought Hublot to its current significance, describes the Big Bang as a "fusion" – the encounter of the unexpected. It is unmistakable, polarizes, and is either loved or rejected – rarely ignored. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: Uniqueness is not a stylistic device – it is the goal. You don't wear the Big Bang because everyone knows it, but because it belongs to no one else but yourself.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Jaeger-LeCoultre Reverso: Mechanical Intelligence</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Jaeger-LeCoultre Reverso – designed in 1931 for polo players who wanted to protect their dial from falls – is the most ingenious mechanical solution in the history of watchmaking: The case simply swivels around. What began as a pragmatic idea became a tribute to mechanical thinking. The Reverso houses up to four complications in the tightest space. Jaeger-LeCoultre – the "Watchmaker of Watchmakers" – has developed more patented complications than any other house. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: The most beautiful solution is the most elegant one. No more mechanics than necessary – but every single gear right where it belongs.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Omega Seamaster: Reliability as a Promise</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Omega Seamaster has been the symbol of reliability under extreme conditions since 1948: Deep-sea divers trust it, polar explorers trust it, the British Royal Navy trusted it. James Bond – in the films a tough, reliable agent – has worn a Seamaster since 1995. Omega was founded in 1848 in La Chaux-de-Fonds and supplied watches to NASA astronauts, including Buzz Aldrin during his moonwalk in 1969. The Seamaster works where other watches fail. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: A watch that never fails is worth more than one that looks dazzling. Reliability is not a compromise – it is the highest promise.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">A remarkable connection: Michael Schumacher – seven-time Formula 1 World Champion and, according to the Enneagram, a Self-Preservation One with Nine-Wing – was an Omega brand ambassador for years before later becoming the face of the Mercedes-Benz S-Class as well. Both brands stand for the same principle: not the loudest, but the most reliable. That a self-preservation One embodied precisely these two brands says a lot about what drives this subtype: unwavering inward discipline, reliable substance over loud self-promotion.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Audemars Piguet Royal Oak Offshore: Freedom and Adventure</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Audemars Piguet Royal Oak Offshore is the loudest, most extroverted watch in the luxury class – large, eye-catching, in colors and materials that other houses would never have dared to use. It was introduced in 1993 as "The Beast" – the animal that surpasses the already revolutionary Royal Oak. Jay-Z, LeBron James, Serena Williams, Arnold Schwarzenegger – the Offshore is the watch for people who want to show their joy for life. Audemars Piguet – founded in 1875 in the Vallée de Joux – is the oldest luxury watch manufacturer still family-owned. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: The world is too diverse to wear a boring watch. The Offshore celebrates life – loud, colorful, unmissable.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">That several famous Type Sevens wear the Royal Oak Offshore is no isolated case. LeBron James – one of the greatest basketball players of all time – is, according to Detlef Rathmer's assessment, a Self-Preservation Seven with an Eight-wing (SP7w8): the entrepreneur among Sevens, who invests his energy into expansion and building. Serena Williams – 23-time Grand Slam champion – is a Sexual Seven with an Eight-wing (SX7w8): intense, passionate, with the Eight-fire underneath. And Kevin Hart, one of the world's most successful comedians, is likewise a Sexual Seven with an Eight-wing (SX7w8): irrepressible energy, charisma, and the urge to fill every room. Three Sevens – three different subtypes, but the exact same watch. The Enneagram shows itself even on the wrist.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Panerai Luminor: Military Might</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Panerai Luminor has an unusual history: Originally secret equipment for the Italian Navy's combat swimmers in World War II, it was not sold to the public for decades. Only in 1993, after the military contract ended, did the first Panerai watches hit the open market. The case measures 44–47mm – unusually large, unmistakably dominant. Sylvester Stallone discovered it on the set of "Daylight" in 1996 and had custom series made for himself – a man who stands for strength and assertiveness. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: A watch originally developed for combat divers doesn't need delicacy. Power is not a style – it is an attitude.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Vacheron Constantin Patrimony: Quiet Harmony</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Vacheron Constantin Patrimony is from the oldest continuously operating watch manufacture in the world – since 1755, without a single year's interruption. The Patrimony is their quietest, most harmonious line: a simple dial, a slender case, no complication for complication's sake. It doesn't draw attention to itself. It is simply there – even, enduring, perfectly balanced. Vacheron Constantin bears the motto "Faire mieux si possible, ce qui est toujours possible" – "Do better if possible, and that is always possible." The principle of Enneagram Type 9: The deepest tranquility needs no ornament. A watch that has existed for 270 years has proven that endurance is the strongest form of beauty.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal observations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"luxusautos-der-9-typen", label:"Luxury Cars of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"brillenmodelle-der-9-typen", label:"Eyewear Models of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Epochs of World History"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function luxusautosDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Luxusautos der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Nine luxury cars&mdash;each a masterpiece of engineering and design, each an expression of an unmistakable character. Which Enneagram principle lies behind these icons of the automotive world?</p>
      <img src="../assets/luxusautos-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Luxusautos der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Koenigsegg Jesko Absolut: Utter Perfection</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Koenigsegg Jesko Absolut from Ängelholm, Sweden, is the most consistent pursuit of perfection known to the automotive world: Over 1,600 hp, a theoretical top speed of 330 mph (531 km/h)&mdash;the fastest street-legal production car ever built. Every detail is optimized for aerodynamic efficiency; nothing is left to chance. Christian von Koenigsegg founded his company in 1994 with the conviction that the perfect car was possible&mdash;and then actually built it. Only 125 units were produced, each handcrafted, each a manifesto of precision. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: Not the fastest at any cost&mdash;but the most correct. The urge to make something as perfect as possible, even if no one besides the maker notices the difference. And perhaps telling: No prominent owners of the Jesko Absolut are publicly known&mdash;the 125 buyers are deliberately kept anonymous. This, too, aligns with the Type 1 principle: Perfection needs no audience.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Rolls-Royce Phantom: Caring Luxury</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">For over a century, the Rolls-Royce Phantom has been the symbol of luxury that does not show off&mdash;it envelops. No other car in the world offers its occupants a comparable sense of silence, warmth, and security. The body is soundproofed like a concert hall. The headliner can be ordered with genuine fiber-optic starlight ceilings. What distinguishes the Phantom is not speed, but the promise: You are the most important person. Rolls-Royce has never marketed the Phantom as a "car"&mdash;but as a "magic carpet." The principle of Enneagram Type 2: Luxury as care. The most beautiful thing is not the fastest or the loudest&mdash;it is that which makes the other person feel completely sheltered and cared for.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">It is no isolated case that the Rolls-Royce Phantom is so frequently chosen by Enneagram Type 2 personalities. Lewis Hamilton&mdash;Formula 1 World Champion and, according to Enneagram analysis, a Social Type Two with a strong Three-wing&mdash;drives a Phantom. Jay-Z, who can be described as a Sexual Type Two with a distinct Three-wing, does as well. And Oprah Winfrey, a Self-Preservation Two with a strong Three-wing, is also among the known Phantom enthusiasts. What unites these three seemingly different individuals at first sight: They all combine warmth and approachability with a sense of style and charisma&mdash;precisely what the Phantom embodies. The car as a mirror of the type.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Bugatti Chiron Super Sport 300+: The Fastest in the World</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Bugatti Chiron Super Sport 300+ is the first production vehicle in the world to break the 300 mph barrier&mdash;reaching over 490 km/h during tests at the Ehra-Lessien track in 2019, making it the fastest Bugatti of all time. Only 30 units were built, each priced at a net base price of 3.5 million euros. Boasting 1,600 hp and a quad-turbocharged W16 engine, it accelerates from 0 to 200 km/h in 5.8 seconds and 0 to 300 km/h in 12.1 seconds. Bugatti&mdash;founded in 1909 by Ettore Bugatti in Molsheim, Alsace&mdash;understands itself as fine craftsmanship disguised as an automobile. The Chiron is not transportation; it is a statement: I am the best.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">One of the most famous owners of this vehicle is Cristiano Ronaldo&mdash;arguably the world's most famous football player and, from an Enneagram perspective, a Social Type Three, the ultimate high achiever. Ronaldo was the very first person to test-drive a Bugatti Chiron in Madrid in 2017 before regular customer deliveries even began&mdash;a story that fits the Three principle perfectly: not waiting until the world recognizes you, but always staying one step ahead. It comes as no surprise that he later acquired one of the 30 Super Sport 300+ models: Anyone who aims to be the best drives the fastest. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: Peak performance is not a matter of ego&mdash;it is a matter of drive. The Chiron demonstrates what is possible when all resources are aligned toward a single goal: being number one.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Pagani Huayra: Art on Four Wheels</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Pagani Huayra is perhaps the most unique car in the world&mdash;not because of its performance (700+ hp), but because of its soul. Horacio Pagani, son of an Argentine baker, drew inspiration from Leonardo da Vinci and the Italian Renaissance to build cars he describes as "paintings with an engine." Every Huayra is unique in its details: Hand-engraved titanium screws, and leather and carbon interiors customized to the owner's specifications. The name originates from Huayra-tata, the Inca god of wind. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: Beauty is not decoration&mdash;it is the purpose. Sitting inside a Pagani, one feels uniqueness first, not speed.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Tesla Roadster: Knowledge as Drive</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Tesla Roadster (2nd Generation) is the purest embodiment of an idea: What becomes possible when you rethink the automobile purely from knowledge&mdash;without an internal combustion engine, without old assumptions? Elon Musk and the Tesla engineering team did not build the fastest petrol car; instead, they proved that electricity can be faster, more efficient, and smarter: 0&ndash;100 km/h in under 1.9 seconds, over 1,000 km of range. The Roadster is a scientific statement. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: Innovation arises not from tradition, but from the willingness to radically challenge existing knowledge&mdash;and to consistently execute those insights, even when everyone else doubts.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Mercedes-Benz S-Class: Reliability as a Promise</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">For decades, the Mercedes-Benz S-Class has served as the benchmark for the safest, most reliable, and best-engineered vehicle in the world. Hardly any other car has introduced more safety innovations into production: Airbag, ABS, ESP, Pre-Safe systems&mdash;many were first tested in the S-Class. It is the vehicle of choice for heads of state, armored diplomatic transport, and long-distance travelers. Not because it demands attention&mdash;but because it never fails. Mercedes-Benz carries the slogan "The best or nothing"&mdash;meaning not prestige, but reliability. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: Security is not a luxury&mdash;it is the foundation for everything else. Whoever can rely on their vehicle can focus on their path.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">A well-known S-Class fan was Michael Schumacher&mdash;seven-time Formula 1 World Champion and, according to Enneagram classification, a Self-Preservation Type One with Nine-Wing. This fits together well on a second look: a man whose success rested on technique, discipline, and precise preparation&mdash;and who privately preferred the world's most reliable, solid saloon car. This is precisely the pattern of the self-preservation One: unwavering care in work and in private life alike, security through quality rather than through flash. The S-Class was his private sanctuary&mdash;the same precision as on the racetrack, minus the speed limit risk.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Lamborghini Aventador SVJ: Pure Joie de Vivre</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Lamborghini Aventador SVJ is not a car&mdash;it is an experience. Vibrant green, deafeningly loud, with scissor doors opening upward like the arms of someone cheering. Ferruccio Lamborghini founded the brand in 1963 out of a mixture of defiance and enthusiasm&mdash;originally a tractor manufacturer, he purchased a Ferrari and wrote to Enzo Ferrari with suggestions for improvement. When Ferrari ignored him, he built his own car. The Aventador SVJ holds the Nürburgring lap record for production vehicles. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: Life is too short for boring cars. The Aventador celebrates the joy of driving, of experiencing, of breaking boundaries&mdash;and invites everyone to watch.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Ferrari SF90 Stradale: Untamed Power</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Ferrari SF90 Stradale is Ferrari's first plug-in hybrid vehicle&mdash;and simultaneously the most powerful production car in the brand's history: 1,000 hp, combined from a V8 internal combustion engine and three electric motors. The name SF90 references the 90th anniversary of Scuderia Ferrari. What sets the SF90 apart is not merely performance, but dominance: It does not just want to be fast&mdash;it wants to command the field. Enzo Ferrari once said: "I have no time for compromises." The principle of Enneagram Type 8: Power is not an end in itself&mdash;it is the expression of the will to set one's own terms and enforce them without asking for permission.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">It is fitting that this brand was founded by a quintessential Type Eight: Enzo Ferrari&mdash;recognized in Enneagram circles as a Type Eight, and assessed by Detlef Rathmer as a Sexual Type Eight with a Seven-wing (SX8w7)&mdash;was a man who knew no compromise and accepted no authority above himself. He sold road cars solely to fund racing&mdash;and dismissed drivers whom he considered too weak. His famous quote: "I have no time for compromises." The Sexual Eight invests its full intensity into the relationship with what it passionately loves&mdash;for Enzo Ferrari, that was motor racing. The SF90 Stradale is the most living legacy of this character.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Bentley Continental GT: Harmony and Open Space</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Bentley Continental GT is the most luxurious grand tourer in the world&mdash;not a sports car, not a show-off, but a Grand Tourer: a car built to cover vast distances in utter tranquility and elegance. Founded in 1919 by W.O. Bentley in London, Bentley stood from the beginning for "the best car in the world"&mdash;not through extreme output, but through the seamless interplay of all elements. The Continental GT boasts 630 hp, yet makes little fuss about it. It is quiet, spacious, and cozy&mdash;the objective is not speed, but arrival. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: Harmony arises when everything is in its place and nothing works against each other. And the most beautiful journey is the one where you forget that you are driving at all.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, Charts, and Type Descriptions – Volume 1 of the Trilogy That Brings the Enneagram to Life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-Depth Type Profiles and Archetypal Reflections – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, Paths to Healing, and the Big Picture – Volume 3 as the Trilogy's Conclusion.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"berge-der-9-typen", label:"Mountains of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"luxusuhren-der-9-typen", label:"Luxury Watches of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Eras of World History"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function bergeDer9TypenPage() {
  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("Berge der 9 Typen", "Charts · Archetypen")}
      <p class="psycho-intro">Nine famous mountains of the world – each unique in shape, character, and symbolism. And each a mirror of one of the nine Enneagram principles. This mapping is no geography lesson, but an archetypal consideration: what do these mountains embody that reveals something about ourselves?</p>
      <img src="../assets/berge-der-9-typen.jpg" alt="Berge der 9 Typen – Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Mount Fuji, Japan: Perfect Form</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Mount Fuji is one of the most beautiful and symmetrical mountains in the world – an almost perfect volcanic cone, 3,776 meters high, the subject of art, poetry, and meditation for centuries. No mountain in Japan is more revered. What distinguishes it is not only its height, but its shape: pure, clear, unmistakable. In Japanese culture, it stands for sublimity, perfection, and inner order. It is an ideal in mountain form. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: beauty lies not in excess, but in the right measure. And in loyalty to a form that does not bend.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Matterhorn, Switzerland/Italy: Help and Connection</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Matterhorn is arguably the most iconic mountain of the Alps – striking, pointed, unforgettable in its shape. And yet it is no lonely summit: it stands on the border between two countries and two cultures, Switzerland and Italy, and connects them. Mountain guides have a special tradition at the Matterhorn: the first ascent in 1865 was only possible because a local guide, Michel Croz, helped the team. No summit without support. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: connection is not a weakness—it is the prerequisite for what people can achieve together.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Mount Everest, Nepal/Tibet: The Highest</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Mount Everest, at 8,849 meters, is the highest mountain on Earth – the ultimate measure of achievement, ambition, and the striving for the summit. Since its first ascent in 1953 by Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay, it has been the worldwide symbol of human drive for achievement. Every year, hundreds attempt the climb – and whoever reaches the top becomes part of an exclusive story. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: the highest point is not the goal in itself—but it shows what people are capable of when they align themselves with a goal with all their might.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Mont Blanc, France/Italy: Beauty and Uniqueness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Mont Blanc, at 4,808 meters the highest mountain in the Alps, is known for its artistic allure. It was the subject of Romantic poetry – Shelley, Byron, Rousseau wrote about it. It stands between two cultures and belongs fully to neither. It is beautiful, yet inhospitable; sublime, yet dangerous. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: what is beautiful is often also vulnerable. And whoever feels deeply lives closer to the heights and depths at the same time.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Mount Kilimanjaro, Tanzania: Knowledge and Observation</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Kilimanjaro is the highest mountain in Africa (5,895 m) and a research paradise: on its slopes exist five different climate zones, from tropical rainforest to arctic glacier crown. Scientists have documented the effects of climate change here for decades – the shrinking glaciers are a global warning signal. It stands alone, visible far across the savanna, a silent observer of the continent. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: knowledge comes from observation. And the mountain that sees everything keeps its knowledge within itself—until someone comes who truly wants to understand.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Eiger, Switzerland: Loyalty and Overcoming Fear</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Eiger (3,967 m) is famously notorious for its North Face – one of the most dangerous climbing walls in the Alps, over 1,800 meters of sheer rock and ice terrain. For decades, it was considered unconquerable. The first ascent in 1938 was an act of collective caution, reliability, and mutual trust: the rope between the climbers is the decisive symbol. The Eiger teaches that courage is not the absence of fear—but the ability to keep going anyway, when a reliable partner is on the other end of the rope. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: security does not come from the absence of danger—but from trust and loyalty.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Aoraki/Mount Cook, New Zealand: Adventure and Joy</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Aoraki/Mount Cook (3,724 m) is the highest mountain in New Zealand – and one of the most diverse adventure areas on Earth. Here you can experience glacier hikes, helicopter flights, climbing tours, and stargazing all in a single day. The mountain trained Edmund Hillary, who later climbed Everest. For the Māori, it is an ancestor, a living being full of spirit and history. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: life is an adventure to be explored. Not the summit alone counts—but the abundance of experiences along the way.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Denali/Mount McKinley, Alaska: Strength and Mastery</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Denali (6,190 m) is the highest mountain in North America – and one of the toughest mountains in the world. Not because of its absolute height, but because of the extreme conditions: temperatures down to minus 60 degrees, arctic storm gusts, months of darkness. Whoever conquers Denali does so not through finesse, but through raw endurance, physical strength, and the unconditional will not to give up. The mountain does not yield—the person must grow. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: strength does not mean having no boundaries—rather knowing them, respecting them, and crossing them anyway.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Uluru/Ayers Rock, Australia: Peace and Inner Stillness</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Uluru is not a mountain in the classical sense – it rises as a giant sandstone monolith from the Australian desert, 348 meters high, 9.4 kilometers in circumference. For the Anangu, the original custodians of the land, it is a sacred place full of Dreamtime stories that connect all areas of life. It changes its color with the light – orange, red, violet, gold – yet always remains the same. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: true calm is not emptiness—but a depth from which everything finds its place.</p>

      </div>
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"baumarten", label:"Tree Species of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"luxusautos-der-9-typen", label:"Luxury Cars of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Epochs of World History"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function neunJahreszyklenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("9-jahreszyklen")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts · Life Course</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Nine-Year Cycles of Life</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">A human life can be divided into nine sections of nine years each – from birth to fulfillment in old age. Each of these nine cycles brings its own developmental tasks, its own challenges, and its own inner movement. And each cycle can be assigned to one of the nine Enneagram principles: what does each life phase reveal about the principle it embodies in its rawest form?</p>
      <img src="../assets/9-jahreszyklen-des-lebens.jpg" alt="The 9 Nine-Year Cycles of Human Life – diagram"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The Process Model of the Enneagram</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Alongside its well-known use as a personality system – nine character types, each with its own passion, fixation, and animal correspondence – the Enneagram contains a second, far less well-known layer: the <strong>process model</strong>. This reading goes back to the original meaning of the symbol as handed down by G. I. Gurdjieff, long before Oscar Ichazo and Claudio Naranjo developed the nine personality types as we know them today. In this older tradition, the Enneagram describes not a fixed identity, but the <strong>sequence of a complete process</strong>: any process – whether baking a cake, carrying out a project, or an entire human life – passes, according to this model, through nine consecutive stages before it can complete itself and a new cycle can begin.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Applied to a person's biography, this means: it is not only one's own Enneagram type that shapes a life, but also a <strong>temporal sequence</strong> that every person moves through regardless of their own type – from the pure possibility of birth (Principle 1) through growth, expansion, and maturity to fulfillment in old age (Principle 9). This perspective should be understood as a complementary, internal interpretive model, not as an exact scientific timetable – yet it helps explain why certain life themes, such as confronting one's own mortality or the late-life search for inner wisdom, tend to surface in comparable decades across very different people, regardless of which of the nine personality types they themselves belong to.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – First Cycle, Years 0–9: Phase of New Beginnings (Birth + Childhood)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The first nine-year cycle begins with birth itself – the most radical of all beginnings. A human being leaves the sheltered, warm interior of the womb and steps unprepared, unprotected, entirely on its own into a world that is completely foreign to it. In these first nine years, the foundation is laid on which the entire rest of life will rest: the first attachments, the first words, the first rules, the first sense of safety or insecurity in the world. Children in this phase learn quite literally how the world works – through imitation, through trial and error, through the unconditional openness with which small children absorb everything happening around them. Nothing in this phase is yet fixed; everything is still becoming. That makes it, at once, the most vulnerable and the most malleable time of the entire life. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: at the beginning stands not perfection, but pure, unformed possibility – out of which a viable order first develops through constant, repeated practice.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Second Cycle, Years 9–18: Phase of Growth (emotional + social maturity)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The second cycle leads out of sheltered childhood into the stormiest biological and social transformation of human life: puberty. The body changes fundamentally, the brain undergoes profound restructuring during this time, and for the first time attention noticeably detaches from the family and turns toward peers, toward first romantic feelings, toward the question of who one actually is beyond one's own family. Emotional and social maturity do not arise in a vacuum, but through friction – in conflicts with parents, in friendships that break apart and re-form, in the painful experience of rejection as much as in the first moments of genuine belonging. These nine years are a time of trying on identities, in which a young person learns to recognize themselves in the mirror of others. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: growth happens in relationship – a person becomes aware of themselves only by rubbing up against others, feeling with them, and striving for their affection.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Third Cycle, Years 18–27: Phase of Expansion (partner, career, independence in the outer world)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">With the 18th year of life begins the phase in which a person truly turns outward for the first time and wants to prove what is in them. Education or study, the first job of one's own, the first apartment of one's own, the first serious partnerships – in these nine years, an existence is built that is no longer carried by the parents, but by one's own strength. It is the phase of the greatest energy and the greatest pace in the entire life: one tries out professions, moves to new cities, builds a network of one's own, perhaps starts a first family. The focus lies almost exclusively on achievement, visibility, and building an identity that can assert itself in the outer world. Setbacks are rarely experienced in this phase as crises, but as motivation to do better next time. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: a person's worth is shown – or so one believes in this phase of life – in what they build and achieve in the outer world.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Fourth Cycle, Years 27–36: Phase of Maturation (beginning of inner maturation processes + emotional growth)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Around the 27th year of life, the purely outward orientation of the previous phase slowly begins to shift. For the first time, more serious questions arise: is the path I have taken really mine? Does the career, the relationship, the life I have built actually correspond to who I am? These nine years are marked by a beginning inner maturation process that is no longer measured solely by outer successes, but increasingly by emotional depth and self-knowledge. Many people experience in this phase the first truly decisive life crises – separations, career reorientations, the birth of one's own children – which force them to look at themselves anew and more honestly. It is a phase of reconciling the outer image one has built up until now with what is actually true on the inside. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: true maturity does not arise through further outward growth, but through an honest encounter with one's own inner truth – even when it hurts.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Fifth Cycle, Years 36–45: Phase of Saturation (beginning discovery of inner knowledge/wisdom)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The fifth cycle marks, for many people, the midpoint of life – the point at which more years lie behind than necessarily still lie ahead. The pure accumulation of outer experiences, positions, and possessions begins in this phase to lose its appeal; instead, a quiet but persistent search begins for something deeper – for inner knowledge, for wisdom, for what truly matters beyond career and outer status. Many people, in these nine years, begin to engage more intensively with philosophy, spirituality, or simply with taking stock of their own life. It is a phase of saturation in the best sense: one has experienced enough to know what really counts – and begins to focus more consciously and selectively on what actually has substance. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: true knowledge does not arise through the accumulation of ever more experience, but through withdrawing into quiet contemplation of what one has already lived through.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Sixth Cycle, Years 45–54: Phase of Decline (developing courage against the fear of death)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In the sixth nine-year phase, the body begins to change noticeably, and with it one's own mortality moves into awareness – often, for the first time, truly inescapably. It is the phase in which parents often die, in which one's own health shows its first limits, in which one recognizes that the time remaining is in fact finite. This confrontation with one's own finitude is deeply unsettling – and, at the same time, the precondition for a new kind of courage: the courage to no longer evade the fear of death, but to look it directly in the eye. Whoever consciously lives through this phase gains clarity about what truly matters in the life that remains – and what may be let go. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: true security does not arise from repressing one's own mortality, but from the courage to meet it directly.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Seventh Cycle, Years 54–63: Phase of Withdrawal (developing sobriety in the fullness of life)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The seventh cycle often falls into a phase of outer abundance: the career has usually reached its peak or is already winding down, children are grown, material security has often been achieved. And yet, in these nine years, an inner movement begins that seems to run counter to the outer abundance: withdrawal. Not out of resignation, but out of a growing inner sobriety – the recognition that not every opportunity must be seized, not every offer accepted. One begins to choose more deliberately where to invest one's remaining energy, and learns to keep measure amid the fullness of life instead of being swept along by it. It is the phase in which the capacity for enjoyment matures into genuine wisdom about the right measure. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: true freedom shows itself not in seizing everything life offers, but in consciously and soberly choosing what truly nourishes.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Eighth Cycle, Years 63–71: Phase of Old Age (developing weakness + neediness)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">With entry into the eighth nine-year phase, formal retirement begins for most people – and with it a completely new experience: the conscious development of weakness and neediness, after a whole life in which strength, independence, and control were often at the forefront. The body becomes noticeably more vulnerable, one's own strength declines, and one becomes increasingly dependent on the support of others – an experience that is deeply challenging for many people who had to be self-reliant and strong their whole lives. And yet a quiet strength of its own lies in this phase: the ability to accept help without being ashamed of it, and to accept one's own vulnerability as part of a complete human life instead of fighting it. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: true strength, in the end, is shown not by never being weak, but by acknowledging one's own neediness without being broken by it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Ninth Cycle, Years 72–81: Phase of Fulfillment (wisdom, love, peace, transcendence)</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The ninth and final of the classic nine-year cycles is the phase of fulfillment – those years of life in which, ideally, everything that has been lived through comes together into one great, reconciled whole. Wisdom, love, peace, and a sense of transcendence stand at the center of this phase: the ability to look back on one's own life with all its highs and lows and to accept it as it was. Many people report, from this phase of life, a new, deep serenity – an inner calm that depends neither on outer successes nor on physical strength, but grows out of the sum of a life lived. It is a phase in which the opposites that persisted throughout life – between wanting and letting go, between self and world – lose their sharpness and dissolve into something greater. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: at the end of a fully lived cycle stands not exhaustion, but peace – the quiet certainty that everything has found its place.</p>

      </div>

      <div style="background:var(--paper-deep,#ede8dc);border-left:3px solid var(--copper);border-radius:0 10px 10px 0;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;margin:1.6rem 0;">
        <p style="font-size:0.86rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink);margin:0;">A cycle is a period of life with a starting point and an ending point. A human life can be divided into nine cycles of nine years each, in which life presents the most varied challenges and holds certain developmental phases in store. The age ranges are meant as rough orientation, not as rigid boundaries – every person moves through these phases at their own pace.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"heilungsweg", label:"The Healing Path of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"kindheitsperspektiven", label:"Childhood Perspectives"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Epochs of World History"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function temperamentenlehreAntikePage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("temperamentenlehre-antike")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts · Character Studies</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The Four Temperaments of Antiquity</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">As early as antiquity, physicians such as Hippocrates and Galen distinguished four human temperaments, derived from the theory of the four bodily humors: the Choleric (yellow bile), the Sanguine (blood), the Melancholic (black bile), and the Phlegmatic (phlegm/water). Each temperament is assigned a season, an element, and specific qualities &ndash; hot, cold, moist, dry. This overview maps the four ancient temperaments onto the nine Enneagram types.</p>
      <img src="../assets/temperamentenlehre-antike.jpg" alt="The Four Temperaments of Antiquity – diagram"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />

      <div style="background:var(--paper-deep,#ede8dc);border-left:3px solid var(--copper);border-radius:0 10px 10px 0;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;margin:0 0 1.6rem;">
        <p style="font-size:0.86rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink);margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> In addition to the four classical temperaments of this already-ancient theory, I have added to the Phlegmatic the quality of the <strong>Anxious type (Angustic)</strong> &ndash; that is, a person whose driving force is fear (Type 6), including the fear of not being successful (Type 3).</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background:var(--paper-deep,#ede8dc);border-left:3px solid var(--copper);border-radius:0 10px 10px 0;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;margin:0 0 1.8rem;">
        <p style="font-weight:700;font-size:0.92rem;margin:0 0 0.6rem;color:var(--ink);">Primary desire for:</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.86rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--ink);margin:0;">
          <strong>Recognition + Status</strong> &ndash; Types 1 + 8<br/>
          <strong>Compassion</strong> &ndash; Types 4 + 5<br/>
          <strong>Respect + Security</strong> &ndash; Types 3 + 6 + 9<br/>
          <strong>Attention</strong> &ndash; Types 2 + 7
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Choleric (yellow bile): Summer, Fire, Anger &ndash; hot + dry</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In the ancient theory of temperaments, the Choleric is the temperament of yellow bile &ndash; assigned to summer, the element of fire, and the passion of anger. The ancient physicians already described the Choleric as hot and dry in temperament: quickly ignited, energetic, with an inner heat that pushes outward. Character-wise, the Choleric is described as quickly angered, wrathful, irritable, and excitable &ndash; a description that has changed little in over two thousand years. Together with Type 8, the One's primary desire is directed toward recognition and status: the Choleric doesn't just want to act, they want their actions to be seen and respected. This combination of fiery energy and the desire for visible recognition makes the One a type that tolerates no mistakes &ndash; neither in themselves nor in others &ndash; and who usually channels their inner heat into controlled discipline rather than letting it erupt unfiltered. The principle of Enneagram Type 1: the fire of anger is not suppressed, but poured into a form that creates order rather than destroying it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Sanguine (blood): Spring, Air, Heart &ndash; hot + moist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In the ancient theory of the four humors, the Sanguine represents blood &ndash; assigned to spring, the element of air, and the organ of the heart. Its temperature is considered hot and moist: warm, lively, in constant motion. The ancient sources describe the Sanguine as courageous, hopeful, loving, cheerful, and active &ndash; a person who receives life with open arms and infects others with their warmth. Together with Type 7, the Two's primary desire is directed toward attention: the Sanguine flourishes when they are the center of attention, when their affection is returned, and their presence is noticed. This combination of heartfelt warmth and the wish to be seen makes the Two a type that actively seeks and shapes relationships, feels alive in closeness to others, and experiences emotional coldness as the greatest threat. The principle of Enneagram Type 2: love is not awaited, but actively given &ndash; in the hope of being seen oneself through devotion.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Anxious/Phlegmatic: Winter, Water, Brain &ndash; cold + moist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">For Type 3, the chart combines two qualities: the classical Phlegmatic with the author-added quality of the Anxious type. The Phlegmatic classically represents the element of water, winter, and the brain, tempered cold and moist. The added Anxious quality describes a person whose deeper driving force is fear &ndash; for the Three, specifically the fear of not being successful. Character-wise, the Anxious type is described as fearful, worried, caring, and friendly &ndash; a description that, in the Three, is usually well hidden behind a smooth, controlled surface. Together with Types 6 and 9, the Three's primary desire is directed toward respect and security: the outward success the Three displays serves not only recognition, but above all the soothing of a deeper fear of worthlessness and failure. The principle of Enneagram Type 3: behind the flawless, successful facade lies the quiet fear of not being safe, not being respected, without performance.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Melancholic (black bile): Autumn, Earth &ndash; cold + dry</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">In the ancient theory of temperaments, the Melancholic represents black bile &ndash; assigned to autumn and the element of earth. Its temperature is considered cold and dry: heavy, muted, turned inward. The ancient sources describe the Melancholic as discouraged, sleepless, irritated, thoughtful, and sad &ndash; a description that at a glance seems bleak, but at the same time reveals the depth and capacity for reflection of this temperament. Together with Type 5, the Four's primary desire is directed toward compassion: the Four does not seek applause or success, but to be truly understood and compassionately accompanied in their pain, longing, and uniqueness. This earthbound, heavy melancholy is at the same time the fertile ground for the artistic and emotional depth for which Enneagram Type 4 is known. The principle of Enneagram Type 4: from the heavy, dark earth of melancholy grows the deepest capacity for genuine feeling and genuine empathy.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Melancholic (black bile): Autumn, Earth &ndash; cold + dry</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Five, too, carries in this chart the temperament of the Melancholic: black bile, autumn, the element of earth, tempered cold and dry. Unlike the Four, however, this melancholic undertone expresses itself in the Five less as emotional depth than as inner distance: discouraged, sleepless, irritated, thoughtful, sad &ndash; the same ancient character traits, but in the Five translated into withdrawal, observation, and quiet mental work rather than open emotional expression. Together with Type 4, the Five's primary desire is directed toward compassion &ndash; even if the Five often hides this desire behind a facade of self-sufficiency and intellectual distance. The earthbound heaviness of the Melancholic shows up in this type as a need for spaces of retreat, where one can think and process undisturbed before even seeking contact with others. The principle of Enneagram Type 5: the heaviness of earth is not radiated outward, but turned inward &ndash; into a fortified, mental place of retreat.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Anxious/Phlegmatic: Winter, Water, Brain &ndash; cold + moist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 6, like the Three and the Nine, carries the combined quality of Phlegmatic and the added Anxious trait: the element of water, winter, brain, tempered cold and moist. In no other type does the fear-driven force of the Anxious type lie so openly on the surface as in the Six, whose entire Enneagram principle revolves around basic fear and the search for security. Character-wise, the Anxious type is described as fearful, worried, caring, and friendly &ndash; a description that fits precisely the vigilant, loyal, but also brooding nature of the Six. Together with Types 3 and 9, the Six's primary desire is directed toward respect and security: it seeks reliable structures, trustworthy allies, and certainty in a world that fundamentally seems unpredictable to it. The cold, moist water quality of the Phlegmatic shows up in the Six as a watchful mind that never quite comes to rest, constantly running through dangers in order to preempt them. The principle of Enneagram Type 6: out of the vigilance of fear grows care &ndash; for oneself and for those to whom one is loyal.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Sanguine (blood): Spring, Air, Heart &ndash; hot + moist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Like the Two, the Seven also carries the temperament of the Sanguine: blood, spring, the element of air, heart, tempered hot and moist. Character-wise, the Sanguine is described as courageous, hopeful, loving, cheerful, and active &ndash; in the Seven, this description expresses itself as boundless optimism, constant mobility, and an infectious joy of life that is hard to slow down. Together with Type 2, the Seven's primary desire is directed toward attention: it wants not only to experience things, but to share its experiences, to sweep others along, to stand at the center of its own adventure story. The airy, light quality of the Sanguine shows up in the Seven as an inability to linger long in one place or on one thought &ndash; it is always drawn onward, to the next possibility, to the next source of enthusiasm. The principle of Enneagram Type 7: the warm, airy lightness of the Sanguine becomes a flight forward &ndash; into ever new, bright possibilities.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Choleric (yellow bile): Summer, Fire, Anger &ndash; hot + dry</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">Like the One, the Eight also carries the temperament of the Choleric: yellow bile, summer, the element of fire, the passion of anger, tempered hot and dry. Character-wise, the Choleric is described as quickly angered, wrathful, irritable, and excitable &ndash; in the Eight, however, this fire quality expresses itself not as controlled discipline as in the One, but as immediate, unfiltered force that knows no detours. Together with Type 1, the Eight's primary desire is directed toward recognition and status: it does not want to be overlooked, controlled, or kept small, but wants to place its strength unmistakably in the world. The fire of the Choleric temperament shows up in the Eight as a direct, confrontational energy that despises weakness and seeks control over its own territory. The principle of Enneagram Type 8: the heat of anger is not disciplined, but directly unleashed &ndash; as unrestrained force that commands respect rather than asking for it.</p>

        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Phlegmatic/Anxious: Winter, Water, Brain &ndash; cold + moist</h3>
        <p class="vb-intro">At the top of the chart stands the Nine, with the purest expression of the Phlegmatic, complemented by the Anxious quality: the element of water, winter, brain, tempered cold and moist. Character-wise, the Phlegmatic is described as calm, unemotional, and passive &ndash; a description that corresponds exactly to the peace-loving, conflict-averse basic nature of the Nine, who would rather give in than cause unrest. Together with Types 3 and 6, the Nine's primary desire is directed toward respect and security: it seeks a stable, conflict-free place in the world, where it is not pushed, not disturbed, not drawn into disputes. The cold, moist water quality of the Phlegmatic shows up in the Nine as a deep inner inertia toward its own urgency &ndash; its own needs are often set aside in favor of peace in the outer world. The principle of Enneagram Type 9: in the cool stillness of the Phlegmatic rests a force that does not fight, but creates peace through mere presence and equanimity.</p>

      </div>

      <div style="background:var(--paper-deep,#ede8dc);border-left:3px solid var(--copper);border-radius:0 10px 10px 0;padding:1.1rem 1.3rem;margin:1.6rem 0;">
        <p style="font-size:0.86rem;line-height:1.65;color:var(--ink);margin:0;"><strong>Note:</strong> What does the human body need to survive? Air to breathe, drink, food, and warmth &ndash; corresponding to the four basic qualities of the temperaments: air, water, earth, and fire.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"9-jahreszyklen", label:"The 9 Nine-Year Cycles of Life"},
        {route:"kindliche-temperamente", label:"Childhood Temperaments"},
        {route:"drei-zentren", label:"Three Centers"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function baumartenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("baumarten")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts · Archetypen</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Tree Species</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">An Enneagrammatic-Archetypal Perspective: What each tree species reveals about the core essence of an Enneagram type – with astonishing facts that bring the principle to life.</p>
      <img src="../assets/baumarten.jpg" alt="The 9 Tree Species – chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – The Oak: Unshakable Integrity</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The oak has been regarded for thousands of years as a symbol of strength, endurance, and righteousness. What few people know: an oak can live for over 1,000 years – but its wood only becomes truly hard as it ages. The longer it grows, the firmer its inner structure becomes. In a storm, it prefers to give up branch and twig rather than bend its trunk – a tree that surrenders parts under pressure before breaking its axis. Every oak produces up to 70,000 acorns over the course of its life – of which only a single one grows into a tree. The greatest care, the greatest selection. The principle of Type 1: That which truly sustains does not become hard through rapid growth, but through an unshakable posture over time.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – The Birch: Healing and Care</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The birch is a so-called pioneer plant: it is the first to grow on clear-cut or burned land, providing the soil with structure, protection, and nutrients so that other, more demanding trees can thrive later on. The birch works for others, not for itself. Its sap, which rises in spring, was used for centuries as a remedy: anti-inflammatory, detoxifying, revitalizing. Astonishingly, a birch releases up to 70 liters of water into the atmosphere daily. And its white bark reflects light into the forest – a tree that literally gives brightness. The principle of Type 2: Preparing the ground so that others can blossom – that is the highest form of giving.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – The Cedar: Majestic Ascent</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The cedar is among the most impressive trees in the world: up to 50 meters tall, with a canopy 30 meters wide. Its wood has been prized for 5,000 years – in ancient Egypt for mummification, in Lebanon for the construction of Solomon's Temple. What makes it special: it protects itself from insects, moisture, and rot through natural oils in the wood – substance from within itself. Its principle of growth is remarkable: the cedar divides the space around itself with quiet decisiveness, claiming light and height without seeking attention in the process. The principle of Type 3: Convincing through genuine substance – and filling the space that is rightfully yours.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 4 – Die Weide: Anmutige Tiefe</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The willow is the most melancholic and at the same time the most resilient tree. Its branches hang low, almost weeping – but its roots are driven by a restless search energy: they grow toward water, traversing vast distances underground and finding paths even through hard soil. Astonishingly, a cut willow can grow from a simple twig in the ground into a complete tree – no tree regenerates as fully from so little. Willows weep, but they do not die. Incidentally, the active ingredient in aspirin comes from willow bark – the pain itself carries the healing within it. The principle of Type 4: Pain is not loss, but substance. That which feels deeply, lives deeply – and reinvents itself from within.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 5 – Die Kiefer: Stille Ausdauer</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The pine is one of the most long-lived and adaptable trees in the world. The oldest known pine – a Bristlecone Pine in North America – is over 5,000 years old and still living today. Pines thrive on rock, in sand, high in the mountains – in places where other trees give up. Astonishingly, the pine communicates with other pines via an underground fungal network, passing nutrients on to weaker neighbors – a tree of quiet, invisible knowledge exchange. Its resin, used for centuries as a remedy, is released only upon injury: the pine gives of itself when tapped – not on its own. The principle of Type 5: Knowledge is accumulated quietly and persistently. It is shared – but on one's own terms.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – The Maple: Faithful Constancy</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The maple is one of the most reliable trees of the temperate zones: seasonally precise, structurally strong, subtly magnificent. No tree turns a more brilliant color in autumn – and in doing so, it demonstrates a remarkable inner order: every leaf follows the same proportions, every coloration occurs at the exact same time. Maple wood is one of the hardest woods of all and is used for musical instruments, sports equipment, and dance floors – material that withstands stress while retaining its tone. Maple syrup is produced only through a precise seasonal mechanism: during a narrow window of frost and thaw, the sap rises. Reliability that knows its exact moment. The principle of Type 6: Loyalty is not a matter of feeling – it is structure that reveals itself at the right moment.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – The Apple Tree: Overflowing Abundance</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The apple tree is the most cheerful and generous tree – it blossoms abundantly, scents the air far and wide, and in good years bears more fruit than can be harvested. There are over 20,000 known apple varieties worldwide – no other fruit tree has produced such diversity. The wild apple, ancestor of all cultivated varieties, still grows in the forests of Kazakhstan today: a primal energy that has expressed itself in ten thousand directions. Astonishingly, apple trees cannot bear fruit alone – they need bees, bumblebees, and insects as pollinators. The life of the apple tree is a collective work. The principle of Type 7: Life wants to be shared. Whoever gives brings forth more – in more forms than previously imagined.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – The Elm: Strength with Depth</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The elm is one of the structurally strongest trees in Europe. Its wood is so tough yet flexible at the same time that it is the only timber that does not rot even underwater – it has been used for millennia for bridge piers, mill wheels, and shipbuilding. Astonishingly, elm wood is virtually impossible to split because its fibers twist and intertwine – no wedge can penetrate it. While other trees split, the elm holds together. Its roots reach wide and deep, occupying space without being loud. And its blossoms are the first in spring, even before the leaves emerge – the elm acts before others wake up. The principle of Type 8: True strength sustains – it holds together what breaks apart for others.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 9 – Der Olivenbaum: Ewiger Frieden</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The olive tree is humanity's oldest cultivated tree and a symbol of peace in almost every culture across the world. What few people know: it can live for over 2,000 years, growing so slowly that its wood becomes hard as stone. Even if an olive tree is cut down to the trunk, it sprouts anew. In the Garden of Gethsemane in Jerusalem stand olive trees that, according to DNA analysis, are over 900 years old and grow in the exact same root region where olives stood in the time of Jesus. No tree has a deeper history of silent survival. The oil it gives has nourished for millennia – while it asks for nothing itself: barren soil, little water, intense heat. The principle of Type 9: Peace is not a state you force – it is the result of a deep, root-deep patience that carries across generations.</p>


      </div>

      <div style="margin:3rem 0 0;padding-top:2rem;border-top:2px solid var(--gold);">
        <p class="eyebrow">Charts · Archetypen</p>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:1.4rem;">The Tallest Trees in the World</h2>
        <p class="psycho-intro">The same archetypal lens, a different reference set: Which Enneagram principle is embodied by the most famous and massive trees on Earth?</p>
        <img src="../assets/hoechste-baeume-welt.jpg" alt="Die höchsten Bäume der Welt – Schaubild"
          style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
        <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 1 – General Sherman Tree: Vollkommene Form</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">The General Sherman in Sequoia National Park is the largest living tree on Earth by volume: over 1,400 cubic meters of wood mass, 84 meters tall, with a trunk circumference of 31 meters. What sets it apart from the merely huge is its proportionality: it does not appear misshapen, but almost symmetrically complete – massive and at the same time ordered. It is still growing. Each year, it adds wood mass equivalent to a full-grown oak. Yet it barely changes its shape: it gets larger, but remains itself. The principle of Type 1: Magnitude without loss of form. The measure remains, even as everything grows.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Grizzly Giant: Protection for Many</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">The Grizzly Giant in Yosemite National Park is one of the oldest and most gnarled giant sequoias – nearly 2,700 years old, with a crooked, almost human-looking trunk that reaches out over the forest like an extended arm. Its interior is hollow and harbors countless animals, insects, and fungi: it grants space without claiming more space for itself. Astonishingly, the Grizzly Giant is used permanently as a home by over 20 different animal species. It is not the largest – but it is the one where everyone finds shelter. The principle of Type 2: The greatest tree is not the one that grows the tallest, but the one that offers the most protection.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Hyperion: The Tallest</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">Hyperion is the tallest known living tree in the world – a Coast Redwood in Northern California, 115.92 meters tall, discovered only in 2006. Its exact location is kept secret to protect it from tourists. It has a single goal pursued with an energy that is biologically almost inexplicable: to be at the top. It draws barely more nutrients from the soil than its neighbors – yet it grew taller than all of them. Astonishingly, a tree of this height must pump water over 100 meters upward, against gravity, every day. The principle of Type 3: Being at the top through genuine performance – and expending the energy of the ascent at any cost.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 4 – L’Arbre du Ténéré: Der Einsamste</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">L'Arbre du Ténéré was a solitary acacia in the middle of the Sahara – the most isolated tree in the world, marked on maps for decades as the only landmark within a radius of 400 kilometers. Caravans navigated by it as if by a star. In 1973, it was knocked down by a drunk truck driver – the only tree for miles around, and of all things, it was hit. Today, a metal sculpture monument stands in its place. No tree in the world was more unique. The principle of Type 4: Not size, not height – but unmistakability makes one unforgettable. And sometimes the most unique thing is also the most vulnerable.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Methuselah: The Living Memory</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">Methuselah is a Great Basin Bristlecone Pine in the White Mountains of California – proven to be over 4,850 years old, making it one of the oldest living organisms on Earth. Its exact location is also kept secret. It grows extremely slowly, consumes virtually no resources, and lives in a barren high-mountain zone where almost nothing survives. What makes its wood special: it is so dense and resinous that it is practically decay-proof – even dead parts of the tree remain standing for centuries. It has stored more history than any book: its tree rings are an archive of climate history. The principle of Type 5: The one who endures the longest has observed the most. Knowledge as the only imperishable material.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Sentinel Tree: Watchtower of Stability</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">The Sentinel Tree – a giant sequoia in Sequoia National Park – bears its name for a reason: „Watchtower" or „Guard." It has stood there for millennia, survived countless storms, ice ages, and fires, remaining almost unchanged in its form. Sequoias have an extraordinary property: their bark is so thick and fire-resistant that wildfires can hardly harm them. They survive through steadfastness, not by dodging danger. The principle of Type 6: Safety does not come from avoiding danger – but from a skin that holds firm.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – General Grant Tree: Joy on a Grand Scale</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">The General Grant Tree, the second-largest tree in the world by volume, was officially named the „Nation's Christmas Tree" by the U.S. Congress in 1926. Every year since 1926, a solemn ceremony takes place at its base. It is not the tallest, nor the oldest – but it is the one where people gather, celebrate, and marvel. Astonishingly, General Grant is also an official national memorial for fallen soldiers – a tree that unites grief and joy within itself. The principle of Type 7: What makes life grand is not height – but the moments when people come together in wonder.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typ 8 – Coast Redwood: Unbesiegbare Kraft</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">The Coast Redwood is the tallest tree species in the world – some specimens exceed 110 meters. It grows faster than any other tree species in its size class, pushing upward with a decisiveness that seems almost aggressive. Astonishingly, Coast Redwoods require the Pacific fog to grow, actively condensing it from the air to cover up to 40 percent of their water needs themselves – a tree that takes what it needs directly from the air. Its wood is naturally fire- and mold-resistant. The principle of Type 8: Power does not mean depending on others – but taking the resources you need and growing in the process.</p>

          <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Baobab: Silent Harmony of Nations</h3>
          <p class="vb-intro">The Baobab – also known as the monkey-bread tree – is one of the stranger and most touching trees in the world. It can live for up to 3,000 years, stores up to 120,000 liters of water in its hollow trunk, and supplies entire landscapes with moisture and nourishment during dry seasons. Its trunk can grow so wide that village communities live inside it, hold religious services, and set up marketplaces – the tree as a communal space. It blossoms at night, pollinated invisibly and quietly by bats. In many African cultures, it is considered the „Upside-Down Tree" – its bare winter branches look like roots reaching into the sky, as if the Earth and the sky were interchangeable for it. The principle of Type 9: Peace does not mean absence – but presence for all.</p>

        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:2rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "In-depth type profiles and archetypal perspectives – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths to healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"affenarten", label:"Ape Species of the Types"},
        {route:"berge-der-9-typen", label:"Mountains of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Epochs of World History"},
        {route:"enneagramm-zimmerpflanzen", label:"Enneagram Meets Houseplants"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function affenartenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("affenarten")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Archetypes</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Primate Species</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">An enneagrammatic-archetypal consideration: What each monkey/primate species reveals about the core essence of an Enneagram type – viewed in comparison among the primate species.</p>
      <img src="../assets/affenarten.jpg" alt="The 9 Primate Species &ndash; chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />

      <div style="background:var(--surface-2,#f5f0e8);border-left:4px solid var(--gold);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1rem 1.2rem;margin:0 0 2rem;" class="vb-section">
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 0.5rem;color:var(--copper);">Note on how to read this mapping</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0;color:var(--anthracite);">These mappings arise through the <strong>comparison of primate species among one another</strong>: which primate species embodies which Enneagram principle most aptly when comparing only primates to each other? The result may deviate from the general animal correspondences in the Enneagram zoo – and that is no contradiction, but a matter of the reference set.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem;font-weight:700;margin:0.75rem 0 0.25rem;color:var(--anthracite);">Three examples that illustrate this:</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0.4rem 0 0;color:var(--anthracite);"><strong>1. Orangutan:</strong> In the comparison of primate species, it corresponds to <strong>Type 5</strong> – among all primates, it is the most withdrawn, observational, and solitary. In the general animal comparison across all 27 subtypes, however, the orangutan corresponds to the <strong>Self-Preservation Eight</strong>. No contradiction: the Self-Preservation Eight leans strongly toward Type 5, being the withdrawn, introverted Eight – among all animals, the orangutan therefore matches the Self-Preservation Eight, while among all primates it matches the Five principle.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0.5rem 0 0;color:var(--anthracite);"><strong>2. Chimpanzee:</strong> In the primate comparison, it corresponds to <strong>Type 1</strong> – structured, rule-governed, attentive to social order. In the general animal comparison, however, the chimpanzee corresponds to the <strong>Sexual Seven</strong>: full of energy, rivalry, and intensity of life. Both apply – depending on what you compare it with.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0.5rem 0 0;color:var(--anthracite);"><strong>3. Gorilla:</strong> In the primate comparison, it stands for <strong>Type 8</strong> – strength, protection, leadership through presence. In the general animal comparison, the gorilla corresponds to the <strong>Self-Preservation Seven</strong>. That also makes sense: my teacher, Dr. Claudio Naranjo, called the Self-Preservation Seven the "hidden Eight" – it often has a strong Eight wing, appearing robust and assertive. Among the primates, the gorilla embodies the Eight principle; among the 27 subtypes, it belongs to the Self-Preservation Seven, whereas the white-faced capuchin carries the pure Seven principle.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0.75rem 0 0;color:var(--anthracite);">The rule states: <strong>What you compare determines the result.</strong> Within the primate world, the relative principle applies – not the absolute correspondence in the overall system. Both have their own independent truth content.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Chimpanzee: Order Through Rules</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Chimpanzees live in highly structured groups with clear hierarchies, rules, and social norms. They are sensitive to rule violations and show a pronounced sense of fairness – in experiments, they even refuse rewards when they see that a conspecific receives less. Chimpanzees patrol territory boundaries, correct misconduct, and actively ensure the maintenance of group order. Among all primates, they are the ones who insist most strongly on adherence to social rules. The principle of Type 1: the world has a correct order – and it is one's own task to ensure that it is maintained.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Bonobo: Care as a Way of Life</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Bonobos are the most empathetic and peaceful great apes. Their society is matriarchal, and conflicts are resolved through physical contact, touch, and mutual affection – rather than through aggression. Bonobos share food voluntarily, even with strangers. They carry sick or weaker animals, helping each other without recognizable self-interest. Nowhere in the primate world is the principle of care so strongly pronounced as in the bonobo. The principle of Type 2: connection arises through devotion – through a genuine interest in the well-being of the other.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Japanese Macaque: Intelligence in the Service of Success</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Japanese macaques are known for their learning ability, adaptability, and inventiveness. As the first primates to develop bathing in hot springs, they show how cultural innovations arise and spread through imitation within the group. Japanese macaques adapt to extreme climates – from tropical to deeply snowy – and survive through flexibility and hard work. They are sociable, eager to perform, and capable of making the best of any situation. The principle of Type 3: whoever can adapt and perform finds their way everywhere.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Gibbon: Unique Expression</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Gibbons are the artists among the apes. Their song – complex, melodic, unmistakable – is unique in the animal kingdom and serves not only for territory communication, but is an expression of identity and relationship. Each pair develops its own distinct duet song. Gibbons move through the foliage with an elegance reminiscent of dance – no other primate species moves with such expressive locomotion. They live in fixed pair bonds and, in their uniqueness, are hardly comparable to other apes. The principle of Type 4: what is one's own, what is unmistakable, is no whim – it is the deepest expression of essence.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Orangutan: Silent Intelligence</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Orangutans are the most solitary of all great apes. They live predominantly alone, avoid company, observe from a distance, and solve problems through calm, persistent reflection rather than activity. Their intelligence is demonstrated in tool use, planning capability, and remarkable patience. They are not slow – they are thorough. Among all apes, they consume the least energy and prefer observing to acting. The principle of Type 5: understanding precedes action. Whoever truly wants to get to know the world must first look at it in peace.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Baboon: Security Through Community</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Baboons live in large, closely knit groups based on mutual watchfulness and loyalty. They have pronounced alarm and warning systems, react quickly to dangers, and stick together – especially against predators. Within the group, everyone depends on everyone: whoever leaves the group is lost. Baboons are predictable in their loyalty and unpredictable in their alertness. They constantly test whether the order still holds – and intervene if it does not. The principle of Type 6: security does not arise alone – it arises through the web of the reliable.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – White-Faced Capuchin: Curiosity Without Limits</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">White-faced capuchins are the most curious, playful, and inventive monkeys of the New World. They examine everything, invent tools spontaneously, learn through trial and error, and are constantly in motion. No monkey appears so full of zest for life and so hungry for new experiences as the capuchin. They dance, play, bicker, reconcile, explore, and start all over again immediately. Boredom does not seem to be biologically planned for them. The principle of Type 7: life is an adventure – and the next interesting thing is always right around the corner.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Gorilla: Strength and Protection</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Gorillas are the most powerful primates in the world – and at the same time surprisingly gentle in their everyday life. A silverback leads his group not through arbitrariness, but through presence, decisiveness, and the ability to end conflicts before they escalate. He places himself between danger and the group, decides the path, and brings calm. The gorilla rarely roars – he doesn't have to. His presence alone sets boundaries. The principle of Type 8: true strength does not need to prove itself. It protects – and keeps chaos away.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Ring-Tailed Lemur: Harmony in the Community</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The <strong>ring-tailed lemur</strong> – also known as the katta – lives in harmonious groups with relatively flat hierarchies. It is social, adaptable, and oriented toward the well-being of the group. Ring-tailed lemurs sunbathe together, groom each other, sleep in groups, and show a pronounced capacity for relaxation. Conflicts are resolved quickly, and the harmony of the group takes precedence. The ring-tailed lemur does not stand out – it is part of the whole. The principle of Type 9: peace is not the absence of conflicts. It is an active stepping back that gives space to others.</p>

        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"tierentsprechungen", label:"Animal Correspondences (General)"},
        {route:"epochen-weltgeschichte", label:"Epochs of World History"},
        {route:"hunderassen", label:"Dog Breeds of the Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function epochenWeltgeschichtePage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("epochen-weltgeschichte")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Archetypes</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Epochs of World History</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">The 9 Enneagram principles in the epochs of history: each epoch carries a dominant time quality – and that mirrors the core essence of a type.</p>
      <img src="../assets/epochen-weltgeschichte.jpg" alt="Epochs of World History &ndash; chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – The Enlightenment (17th–18th Century)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enlightenment was the age of reason, order, and moral perfection – an era in which thinkers like Kant, Voltaire, and Locke sought to realign the world according to rational principles. Ignorance, arbitrariness, and superstition were to be replaced by science, law, and ethical grounding. It was a time of inner demand: humanity had to become better, more just, more enlightened. No century demanded so much moral seriousness of itself. The principle of Type 1: the world has a correct state – and it is the duty of the thinking person to work toward it.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Early Christianity (1st–4th Century)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Early Christianity was a movement of emergency aid, community, and selfless devotion. In a world of poverty, exclusion, and persecution, the early Christians offered protection, food, care, and community – often at their own expense and their own risk. Charity was not a commandment among others, but the foundation of the faith. The early church organized itself around giving: alms, diaconal service, hospitality. The principle of Type 2: the highest expression of life is service to others – out of love, not calculation.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Industrialization (18th–19th Century)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Industrialization was the age of progress, achievement, and economic advancement. For the first time in history, productivity itself became a value: whoever produced more, worked faster, ran a more efficient business, rose. Machines replaced muscle, factories replaced craft, numbers replaced tradition. Success became measurable, visible, comparable. The principle of Type 3: anyone who wants to survive in competition must deliver – visibly, efficiently, adaptably.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Romanticism (Late 18th to Late 19th Century)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Romanticism was a countermovement against the cool rationality of the Enlightenment – a cry from the soul that could not find itself in numbers and systems. Poets, painters, and composers celebrated the individual, the sublime, the melancholic. The principle of Type 4: what feels deeply is real – and what is real has a value that no logic can replace.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – The Renaissance (14th–17th Century)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Renaissance was the rebirth of knowledge – an era in which the ancient knowledge of antiquity was rediscovered, distilled, and supplemented through one's own observation. Leonardo da Vinci filled thousands of notebook pages with anatomy, mechanics, optics, and botany. Copernicus observed the sky and rethought the worldview. The Renaissance was the hour of science, curiosity, and the calm, systematic exploration of reality. The principle of Type 5: understanding is not a means to an end – it is the end itself.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – The Middle Ages (5th–15th Century)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Middle Ages were an era of belonging, loyalty, and security through structure. The feudal system gave everyone a fixed place: vassals swore loyalty, knights defended their lord, citizens adhered to guild rules, believers trusted the church. These hierarchies were not shackles, but protection – in an uncertain world, they offered orientation, community, and meaning. The principle of Type 6: whoever knows where they belong and whom they trust has an anchor – and the anchor holds when the storm comes.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – The Roaring Twenties (1920s)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Roaring Twenties were the decade of joy of life, ecstasy, and cultural explosion. After the horrors of the First World War, the Western world celebrated life with an intensity that bordered on compensation: jazz, dance, cabaret, cinema, fashion, new freedoms for women, new arts, new ideas. Berlin was glamorous and excessive, Paris shone, New York raged. The world no longer wanted to think – it wanted to experience. The principle of Type 7: life is too short for deprivation – every opportunity for enjoyment is a duty toward one's own soul.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – The Roman Empire (27 BC–476 AD)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Roman Empire was the greatest power structure of the ancient world – built on will, discipline, expansion, and the absolute claim to control. Rome thought in empires, not in borders. Legions, governors, legal systems – all of it was the expression of a single principle: whoever is stronger determines the order. The Roman Empire was not cruel out of a joy in cruelty, but out of the deep conviction that order requires power. The principle of Type 8: strength protects. Weakness invites attack. Whoever leads must be ready to deploy everything.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – The Pax Romana (27 BC–180 AD)</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Pax Romana – the Roman Peace – was one of the longest periods of relative stability in the history of the ancient world: over 200 years in which millions of people lived in relative safety, trade routes remained open, laws applied, and wars were the exception. No drama, no revolution – just the quiet, magnificent fact that life simply went on. The principle of Type 9: the greatest contribution is sometimes the one no one sees – the silence that allows others to grow, act, and live.</p>

        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions – the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations – Volume 2 of the Rathmer Trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture – Volume 3 as the conclusion of the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"kaffeearten", label:"Coffee Varieties of the Types"},
        {route:"getreidearten", label:"Grain Types of the Types"},
        {route:"tierentsprechungen", label:"Animal Correspondences"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function kaffeeartenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("kaffeearten")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Archetypes</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Coffee Varieties</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">An enneagrammatic-archetypal consideration: what each type of coffee reveals about the core essence of a type.</p>
      <img src="../assets/kaffeearten.jpg" alt="The 9 Coffee Varieties &ndash; chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Espresso: Pure Perfection</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Espresso is the purest, most concentrated expression of coffee &ndash; no frills, no dilution, no compromise. It is brewed under precise pressure, at an exact temperature, and with a precisely measured grind size. Anyone who prepares it at home knows: the smallest deviations change everything. Espresso knows no mediocrity &ndash; it is either successful or not. The principle of Type 1: Quality arises through discipline, accuracy, and the knowledge that there is a right way.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Latte Macchiato: Warmth for Others</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Latte Macchiato is the most caring of all coffees &ndash; soft, warm, inviting. It comforts, warms, and envelops. It is not a drink for hard work desks, but for cozy gatherings, for the other person to feel welcome. It steps back &ndash; the strong espresso hides in a glass of soft milk, barely recognizable, yet the actual core. The principle of Type 2: Whoever loves ensures well-being &ndash; even if their own fades into the background.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Cappuccino: Style and Impact</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Cappuccino is the coffee of polished public presentation &ndash; balanced, appealing, welcome everywhere. It combines espresso, milk, and foam in perfect proportion. In social and professional environments, it is the first choice: it appears neither too simple nor too eccentric, always striking the right tone. Garnished with latte art, it becomes a calling card. The principle of Type 3: What wants to convince must not only be good &ndash; it must also look good and appear at the right moment.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Affogato: Unique Depth</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Affogato is no ordinary coffee &ndash; it is an encounter of two worlds: hot, bitter espresso meets cold, sweet vanilla ice cream, and in this contrast, something unmistakable emerges that is neither one nor the other. It is dessert and coffee at once, defying every category, moody, deep, intense. Whoever orders it wants no norm &ndash; they want an experience. The principle of Type 4: In contrast lies beauty, in particularity lies identity.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Cold Brew: Knowledge Through Patience</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cold Brew is not a fast coffee &ndash; it takes 12 to 24 hours for cold water to slowly and silently permeate the coffee beans. No pressure, no heat, just time and precision. The result is a deep, multi-layered concentrate, smooth and intense at the same time, without the bitterness of a hot brew. The process is invisible &ndash; the impact all the greater. The principle of Type 5: Knowledge does not arise in a rush. It arises in silent, patient penetration &ndash; and only shows itself when the moment is ripe.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Americano: Reliable Structure</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Americano is the most reliable of all coffees &ndash; clear, simple, predictable. It consists of espresso and hot water, nothing more, and it never disappoints. It is not exciting, but it is always there, always stable, always as expected. In uncertain moments, one reaches for the Americano, not the unknown. It offers stability without promises it cannot keep. The principle of Type 6: What supports does not need to shine. It must be there &ndash; reliable, clear, without hidden motives.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Frappuccino: Pure Joy of Life</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Frappuccino is the happiest drink in the coffee universe &ndash; colorful, playful, packed with flavors, syrups, toppings, and variations. It is an experience, not a routine. No Frappuccino has to taste like another &ndash; today caramel, tomorrow matcha, the day after something new. It is the coffee of variety, fun, and enjoyment without seriousness. The principle of Type 7: Life is too short for boring drinks. Everything can be colorful, full, and exciting.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Café Cubano: Strength Without Apology</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Café Cubano is the strongest, most direct coffee &ndash; a short, punchy espresso whipped with raw sugar during the brewing process to create a thick, sweet crema. Sweet and strong at the same time &ndash; no apologies, no holding back. It hits like a fist that is nevertheless charming. In Cuba, it is drunk in one gulp, standing, with others. The principle of Type 8: Strength needs no framework of apology. Directness is not unfriendliness &ndash; it is respect.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Café au Lait: Silent Harmony</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Café au Lait is the gentlest, most peaceful coffee &ndash; coffee and hot milk in equal parts, no dominance, no sharpness, no fight for the foreground. It is the drink of the quiet morning, cozy togetherness, the balanced moment. In France, it belongs to everyday life like the baguette &ndash; natural, unexcited, always welcome. The principle of Type 9: In togetherness lies peace &ndash; when no one dominates, everyone can breathe.</p>

        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"getreidearten", label:"Cereal Varieties of the Types"},
        {route:"gewuerzarten", label:"Spice Varieties of the Types"},
        {route:"tee-enneagramm", label:"Enneagram Teas"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function getreideartenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("getreidearten")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Archetypes</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Grain Varieties</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">An enneagrammatic-archetypal consideration: what each type of grain reveals about the core essence of a type.</p>
      <img src="../assets/getreidearten.jpg" alt="The 9 Grain Varieties &ndash; chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;" />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 – Wheat: The Measure of All Things</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Wheat is the staple food par excellence—the grain against which culture, bread, and civilization measure themselves. It stands for reliability, yield, and perfection in cultivation: for centuries, agriculture has refined it, selected it, optimized it. The ear must be straight, the kernel full, the quality controlled. No grain is sorted as strictly by quality classes as wheat. The principle of Type 1: what is supposed to be good must hold up to the standard—and the standard is high.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 – Oats: Nurturing Care</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Oats are the most nurturing of all grains—nourishing, warming, stabilizing. They are the basis of porridge and oatmeal, of drinks that strengthen and soothe. They provide without making a fuss and can be found wherever strength is needed: in children's nutrition, in convalescence, in everyday life. Oats do not just fill the stomach—they give comfort. The principle of Type 2: being nourishing, being needed, giving strength to others—that is the deepest expression of love.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 – Rice: Adaptation as Success</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Rice is the most eaten grain in the world—adaptable, versatile, successful everywhere. It thrives in Asia as well as in the Camargue, in risotto as in sushi, in festive dishes as in quick everyday meals. It fits into every kitchen, every culture, every style—and is valued everywhere. No grain is so globally successful. The principle of Type 3: whoever can adapt, whoever hits the format the situation demands, is welcome everywhere—and successful everywhere.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 – Quinoa: Special Depth</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Quinoa is no ordinary grain—strictly speaking, it is not one at all, but a pseudo-grain, an amaranth relative from the Andes that refuses to fit into a simple category. For millennia it was the sacred food of the Incas, nearly forgotten, then rediscovered—unique in its nutrient density, its flavor, its origin. Quinoa is special. It knows it. The principle of Type 4: the ordinary is not an option. Uniqueness is not a claim—it is the essence.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 – Barley: Knowledge as Substance</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Barley is one of the oldest cultivated grains in the world—sober, robust, versatile. From it, malt is obtained, from which beer is brewed—a long, quiet process of transformation in which the grain unfolds its full potential through fermentation. Barley is no loud grain. It works in the background, is foundation rather than garnish. Its nutritional value is dense, its use broad—from the soup kitchen to the distillery. The principle of Type 5: what knows need not shine. It must unfold—slowly, deeply, in the right hands.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 – Rye: Reliable Strength</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Rye grows where other grains fail—on barren soils, in harsh climates, under difficult conditions. It is robust, resilient, loyal. From rye, the dark, dense bread is baked that has nourished ordinary people for generations—not glamorous, but reliable, nourishing, long-lasting. Rye is the grain of endurance, not of the grand entrance. The principle of Type 6: steadiness where others yield—not because it is easy, but because one knows what can be relied upon.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 – Corn: Joie de Vivre in Abundance</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Corn is the colorful, lavishly abundant grain—yellow, plump, sun-drenched. It grows quickly, thrives in warmth, bears lush cobs. It is popcorn and polenta, tortilla and bourbon, sweetness and satiety. Corn fills life with color and flavor. No grain has so many forms, so many uses, so many culinary transformations. The principle of Type 7: life should taste, shine, and be full—savoring every possibility before silence sets in.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 – Millet: Strength from Inner Power</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Millet is one of humanity's oldest and most resilient grains—it needs little water, withstands heat, survives times of drought where other crops fail. Millet has sustained entire civilizations. It is no delicacy—it is pure strength, direct and without detours. Its grains are small, but compressed full of energy. The principle of Type 8: strength needs no outer validation. It is simply there—primal, resilient, unimpressed by resistance.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 – Spelt: Gentle Harmony</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Spelt is an old, gentle grain—careful in its growth, careful in its effect. It is considered particularly easy to digest, easy to process, mild in flavor. No grain integrates so easily into different recipes: spelt adapts without giving itself up. It is good for digestion, peaceful in cultivation, undemanding in the soil. For centuries, it was the grain of monasteries and quiet agriculture. The principle of Type 9: being in harmony with what is—no friction, no resistance, only the gentle yes of flowing along.</p>

        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The archetypal essences of the 9 types – Volume 1 of the foundational work.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "The unfolding of personality patterns in their full depth – Volume 2.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "The concluding volume of the foundational work – synthesis and deepening.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"gewuerzarten", label:"Spice Varieties of the Types"},
        {route:"kaesesorten", label:"Cheese Varieties of the Types"},
        {route:"brotsorten", label:"Bread Varieties of the Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function gewuerzartenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("gewuerzarten")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Archetypes</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Spice Varieties</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">An enneagrammatic-archetypal consideration: what each spice reveals about the core essence of a type.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/gewuerzarten.jpg"
        alt="Die 9 Gew\xfcrzarten &ndash; Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:0 0 1rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Rosemary: Clarity and Order</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Rosemary is the spice of clarity &ndash; needle-shaped, upright, with a sharp, resinous scent that immediately provides orientation. It grows in rocks and dry conditions without much care, always maintaining its form. In Mediterranean cuisine, it stands for structure: it organizes meat, vegetables, and sauces, bringing clarity to complex dishes. No spice is so immediately recognizable, so unmistakable in scent and effect. The principle of Type 1: Clarity is not an aesthetic &ndash; it is an inner attitude expressed in everything one does.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Cloves: Warmth as a Gift</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Cloves are the warmest of all spices &ndash; sweet, intense, radiating a deep cordiality that immediately exudes security. In mulled wine, warming pastes, and holiday pastries: cloves are always present when something special is created, when people come together, and when care is given. Their aroma envelops, warms, and comforts. They have been part of healing cuisine and festive preparation for centuries &ndash; a spice that gives itself away. The principle of Type 2: True care has a scent &ndash; you feel it even before you see it.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Curry: Impact Through Versatility</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Curry is not a single spice, but a blend &ndash; and therein lies its power. It adapts, succeeding in India and Japan, the Caribbean and England, fine dining and street food. It is the spice of impact: whoever adds curry fundamentally changes a dish, immediately and unmistakably. It is colorful, vibrant, energetic, and present in any composition. The principle of Type 3: True impact does not need a single source &ndash; it arises from the interplay of all qualities.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Saffron: The Incomparable</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Saffron is the rarest and most expensive spice in the world &ndash; harvested from the threads of a single crocus blossom, hand-picked, unmistakable in color and aroma. A hint is enough to transform an entire dish &ndash; into a deep gold, into a scent that no other ingredient can replace. Saffron cannot be imitated. It is what it is &ndash; unique, complex, of a beauty that must be experienced rather than explained. The life feeling of Type 4: I am irreplaceable &ndash; and that is not a claim, but a fact.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Coriander Seeds: Concentrated Knowledge</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Coriander seeds are tiny, perfectly round, unassuming &ndash; and astonishingly multi-layered in their concentration. Their aroma only unfolds when crushed or heated: warm, slightly citrusy, nutty, almost philosophically complex. They are not eaten raw, but prepared, distilled, transformed into their essence. In herbalism, they are among the oldest known spice plants &ndash; their seeds were found in Egyptian tombs. The principle of Type 5: Knowledge is gathered, preserved, refined &ndash; and only shared when it exists in its clearest expression.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Oregano: Reliable Spice</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Oregano is the most reliable of all culinary herbs &ndash; in pizza, tomato sauce, and every Mediterranean dish, it is the loyal companion that never surprises and never disappoints. It is widely available, easy to use, and predictable in its effect. It makes no fuss, does not push itself to the foreground, yet something is missing without it. It has been part of folk medicine for millennia, a spice that provides stability and offers protection. The principle of Type 6: Whoever is always there, remaining predictable, creates the foundation upon which others can build.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Lemongrass: Joy of Life and Lightness</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Lemongrass is the most joyful of all spices &ndash; fresh, citrusy, bright, possessing a zesty lightness that immediately lifts the spirits. Coming from Asia, it brings tropical vibrancy to every kitchen and promises summer even when there is none. It is carefree, inviting, transforming soups, teas, and curries into something special without becoming heavy. Its scent uplifts the mood even before you taste it. The principle of Type 7: Lightness is not superficiality &ndash; it is a gift that returns breath to others.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Ginger: Strength Without Detours</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Ginger does not do things by halves &ndash; sharp, burning, direct, with a warmth that is immediately perceptible and leaves nothing in the middle. Even a small piece completely alters an entire dish. It is renowned across Asia, the Middle East, and natural medicine worldwide for its strength: promoting circulation, boosting immunity, warming from within. Ginger tolerates no half-heartedness &ndash; you either use it or you leave it alone. The principle of Type 8: Whoever truly lives, feels it &ndash; and whoever knows ginger knows exactly what is meant.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Basil: Silent Harmony</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Basil is the most peaceful of all spices &ndash; soft, aromatic, with a silent sweetness that holds everything together without dominating. It connects: tomato and mozzarella, oil and pesto, kitchen and garden. It clashes with nothing, argues with no other flavors, fitting in seamlessly while elevating the whole. In Mediterranean tradition, it stands for hospitality and peace. Freshly torn, it smells gently &ndash; a quiet invitation. The principle of Type 9: Whoever harmonizes creates more than any lone fighter &ndash; namely, a whole greater than the sum of its parts.</p>

        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "Overviews, charts, and type descriptions &ndash; the first volume of the trilogy that brings the Enneagram to life.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-2", "Deepened type profiles and archetypal considerations &ndash; Volume 2 of the Rathmer trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 2")}
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-3", "27 Subtypes, paths of healing, and the big picture &ndash; Volume 3 concluding the trilogy.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 3")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"kaesesorten", label:"Cheese Varieties of the Types"},
        {route:"brotsorten", label:"Bread Varieties of the Types"},
        {route:"tee-enneagramm", label:"Enneagram Teas"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function friedenSchliessenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("frieden-schliessen")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Relationships</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Frieden schlie\xdfen</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">From the speck in the eye and the art of encountering others with compassion instead of judgment.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/frieden-schliessen.jpg"
        alt="Making Peace"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <p class="vb-intro">We all learn and practice reading people. We see their patterns, recognize their Enneagram types, and understand faster and faster how someone ticks. Yet with this knowledge comes great responsibility: Once we know our counterpart's type, we stand at a crossroads &ndash; do we use it to be right, or to create connection?</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">An old wisdom hits the nail on the head: <em>We see the speck in our neighbor's eye, but we do not notice the beam in our own.</em></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The Trap of the Drawer-Ego</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">When we type someone, the temptation is great to go into judgment: Type 1 has to play the schoolmaster again. Type 3 just wants to show off again. Type 6 sees problems everywhere. We then use the Enneagram like a weapon &ndash; <em>You are in your pattern, therefore you are the problem.</em></p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Yet the truth is: No type is better or worse than another. Each of us has quirks, blind spots, and very personal trigger points. Every pattern is ultimately just a survival strategy of the ego &ndash; including our own.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The Royal Road: Gently Touching the Ego</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">How do we deal with it when we have seen through someone? A clever and deeply human approach states: <em>I do not have to forcefully assert my own ego at all costs.</em></p>
        <p class="vb-intro">If you know you are dealing with a Type 1 whose deepest need is for things to run right &ndash; why exhaust yourself fighting it? A simple &bdquo;From your perspective I can completely understand that, that's an important point&ldquo; immediately breaks the other person's defensive line.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">This is not caving in. This is not hypocrisy. It is conscious relationship design. By calming the other person's ego to a certain extent, we take the pressure off &ndash; and create space for a genuine encounter.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The Transformation</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">To do this, we must do something that our own ego finds difficult: step back. Recognize the beam in our own eye &ndash; the urge to be right, to defend ourselves, or to have to correct the other person.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">When we stop servicing the other person's trigger point and instead meet them with compassion, something astonishing happens: The world becomes a little more peaceful. Theoretical profiling turns into applied neighborly love.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The Impetus</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Observe yourself today in your encounters. Where do you desperately want to be right? Where do you see the speck in the other person? Try instead to internally recognize and gently validate your counterpart's pattern.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Watch what happens when you park your ego on a bench for a moment.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:2rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">Theoretical profiling becomes applied neighborly love. &ndash; Detlef Rathmer</blockquote>

        ${bookTip("die-sprache-unserer-beziehungen", "How the 9 types react in relationships &ndash; and how compassion instead of judgment enables true connection.", "Die Sprache unserer Beziehungen")}
        ${bookTip("leidenschaft-und-heilung", "27 Subtypes &ndash; their passions, blind spots, and the path to inner healing.", "Leidenschaft und Heilung")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"naehe", label:"Proximity & Distance"},
        {route:"beziehungen", label:"Relationships"},
        {route:"schaubilder-als-spiegel", label:"Charts as Mirrors"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function werWirSindPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("wer-wir-sind")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Essence</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Who We Are</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">The map is not the territory &ndash; why the Enneagram describes us, but does not define us.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/wer-wir-sind.jpg"
        alt="Who We Are &ndash; chart"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">&bdquo;The Enneagram explains how we function &ndash; not who we are.&ldquo;</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">At first glance, the Enneagram seems like just another psychological labeling system. Nine types, nine personality profiles, nine ways of seeing the world. Anyone who engages with it quickly finds their &bdquo;number&ldquo; &ndash; and soon catches themselves commenting on their own behavior or that of others with phrases like &bdquo;Typical Three!&ldquo; or &bdquo;That's such a Five reaction again.&ldquo;</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">But this is precisely where this quote comes in. The distinction between <em>how</em> and <em>who</em> is not hair-splitting &ndash; it touches the very core and transformative potential of this ancient model. It draws a clear line between personality (the &bdquo;how&ldquo;) and essence (the &bdquo;who&ldquo;).</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The &bdquo;How&ldquo;: The Machinery of Personality</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">When the Enneagram explains <em>how</em> we function, it reveals the unconscious mechanisms, coping strategies, and filters we have developed over the course of our lives. Each of the nine types describes a specific fixation &ndash; a kind of psychological lens through which we view reality. It shows how we react to stress &ndash; through withdrawal, attack, or adaptation. It makes visible which core fears and core needs control our actions unnoticed. And it exposes repeating behavioral patterns that we often mistakenly take for our innermost soul.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">In this sense, the Enneagram is a brilliant instruction manual for the human ego &ndash; a description of our mind's &bdquo;operating system.&ldquo; It tells us: <em>&bdquo;Look here: This is how your psychological structure functions when you are in autopilot mode.&ldquo;</em></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The &bdquo;Who&ldquo;: Freedom Beyond the Box</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The most common misunderstanding &ndash; even among advanced students &ndash; is using the Enneagram as a justification for one's ego: <em>&bdquo;I'm just an Eight, I have to be this dominant.&ldquo;</em> But the model does not want to put us in a box. It wants to show us <strong>which box we are already sitting in</strong> &ndash; so that we can break out of it.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Who we truly are &ndash; our essence, our true self, our consciousness &ndash; cannot be squeezed into a number. The true self is not the type, but the <em>observer</em>: that quiet presence that notices when the personality kicks in. When someone living the pattern of Type One notices inner anger rising over a mistake &ndash; and simply observes this impulse instead of blindly acting on it &ndash; then they are acting from the &bdquo;who,&ldquo; from pure being. They <em>use</em> the knowledge of the &bdquo;how&ldquo; to gain freedom.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram does not define us. It is a tool for <strong>disidentification</strong>: By understanding how we function, we stop confusing ourselves with the reactive apparatus of the ego fixation.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">From Autopilot to Presence</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram gives us a precise map of our mental and emotional habits. But the map is not the territory. When used correctly, we learn to observe the mechanisms of our type with a touch of humor and genuine compassion. We recognize: The number we carry in the Enneagram is only the clothing &ndash; never the person inside it.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:2rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">We are not our patterns. &ndash; Detlef Rathmer</blockquote>

        ${bookTip("nichts-und-alles", "A quiet book about the space beyond personality &ndash; consciousness, essence, and inner freedom.", "Nichts und Alles")}
        ${bookTip("du-bist-nicht-dein-zweifel", "Why we are not our thoughts and patterns &ndash; and how we can free ourselves from them.", "Du bist nicht dein Zweifel")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"schaubilder-als-spiegel", label:"Charts as Mirrors"},
        {route:"verwechslungen", label:"Common Mix-ups"},
        {route:"wurzeln-des-enneagramms", label:"Roots of the Enneagram"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function symmetrieDesEnneagrammsPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("symmetrie-des-enneagramms")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; System Architecture</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The Symmetry of the Enneagram</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Behind the nine different personality structures lies a remarkable inner order: opposing types often share the same core wound &ndash; yet develop opposite strategies to cope with it.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/symmetrie-des-enneagramms.jpg"
        alt="The Symmetry of the Enneagram: Opposite Type Pairs at a Glance"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram describes nine distinct personality structures. At first glance, these nine types appear very different. However, upon closer inspection of the system, one realizes that certain types exhibit surprising commonalities &ndash; especially those located directly opposite each other on the circle. They frequently revolve around the same core issue, yet develop different, sometimes even contrasting, strategies to handle it. This creates a remarkable symmetry within the Enneagram.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &amp; Type 8 &ndash; Two Faces of Anger</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Both types maintain a close relationship with the theme of anger. Both possess strong inner drive and a pronounced sense of will. However, they express this core energy in opposite ways:</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 1</strong> attempts to control, refine, and channel its impulses into morally correct avenues. Energy is disciplined and frequently directed inward against one's own shortcomings &ndash; anger turns inward.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 8</strong>, on the other hand, directs this exact force immediately outward. It resists limitations, enforces boundaries, and confronts resistance directly. Anger serves as both a means of expression and a shield.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">In the chart: Type 1 &ndash; angry (social); Type 8 &ndash; angry (anti-social). The same energy, two opposite directions of expression.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &amp; Type 7 &ndash; Two Paths of Benevolence</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Both orient themselves toward the positive and try to avoid uncomfortable experiences as much as possible. Their core impulse is benevolent &ndash; yet the direction differs:</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 2</strong> seeks its path through relationships, closeness, and helpfulness. It wants to create connection and stand up for others. Benevolence is directed outward &ndash; emotional, feeling, and relationship-oriented.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 7</strong> seeks the expansion of life through possibilities, experiences, ideas, and future prospects. Benevolence is directed inward and forward &ndash; intellectual, constructive, and freedom-seeking.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &amp; Type 6 &ndash; Two Responses to Insecurity</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Both address security and agency in their own way &ndash; yet their strategies run in opposite directions:</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 3</strong> counters insecurity with activity, achievement, and goal orientation. Action creates self-confidence and imparts a feeling of control. Fear is masked by uninterrupted doing.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 6</strong> directs its attention much more strongly toward potential risks and dangers. Fear is not masked; it is perceived, tested, and analyzed. Action remains possible &ndash; but only after careful examination. Fear initially blocks action before it begins.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &amp; Type 5 &ndash; Two Ways of Dealing with Inner Lack</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The connection between these two types runs particularly deep. Both carry an inner sense of lack or inner poverty &ndash; and react to it in starkly different ways:</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 4</strong> experiences this deficiency directly and suffers under it. It cannot tolerate the internal feeling of poverty &ndash; giving rise to longing, depth, and emotional intensity. The sense of lack is lived out sensitively and expressively.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Type 5</strong> reacts to the same core sensation with withdrawal and resignation. It tolerates the internal feeling of poverty and comes to terms with it: it reduces its needs, retreats, and tries to get by with limited internal resources.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; The Resting Center</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Type 9 occupies a special position within this symmetry. Nine acts as a balancing and mediating force. It attempts to connect opposites and harmonize tensions &ndash; appearing, in a sense, as the calm, resting center of the entire system.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:var(--ink);">What This Symmetry Means</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Examining these symmetries opens up a deeper view into the internal architecture of the Enneagram. Similar core themes, injuries, or inner tensions often lie hidden behind visible differences. The types differ less in the problem that occupies them and more in how they attempt to resolve that problem.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:1.5rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">Opposite types often carry the same core wound &ndash; and choose opposite strategies for it. One tries to cope with the problem through control, activity, or intensification; the other chooses the opposite path.</blockquote>

        <p class="vb-intro">When you recognize these connections, you begin to understand the Enneagram no longer just as a typology, but as a map of human consciousness and survival strategies. The nine types do not stand isolated next to one another &ndash; they are bound together by profound psychological links.</p>

        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The core structure of the nine types &ndash; who you truly are beyond role and pattern.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("der-code-deiner-persoenlichkeit", "The Enneagram with colored charts: types, patterns, and developmental paths at a glance.", "Der Code deiner Pers\xf6nlichkeit")}
        ${bookTip("nichts-und-alles", "Beyond all type patterns: the quiet foundation that connects all nine types with one another.", "Nichts und Alles")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"dialektische-struktur", label:"Dialectical Structure"},
        {route:"horney-triaden", label:"Horneyvian Triads"},
        {route:"wurzeln-des-enneagramms", label:"Roots of the Enneagram"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function koerperregulationPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("koerperregulation")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Body, Soul & Energy</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Enneagram & Body Regulation</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">The nine types as patterns of order for body, soul, and energy &ndash; not a diagnosis, but a map for understanding, self-awareness, and holistic balance.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/koerperregulation.jpg"
        alt="Enneagram & Body Regulation: The Nine Types as Patterns of Order"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <p class="vb-intro">Body and character are not separate worlds. Each Enneagram type brings its own way of regulating energy, holding tension, and expressing itself physically. This overview invites you to newly understand the connection between your own type pattern and bodily experience &ndash; not as a diagnosis, but as an invitation to self-awareness.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#5f5f5f;">Type 1 &ndash; Order &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Liver &middot; Gallbladder &middot; Tension</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Correctness, conscience, improvement, urge for perfection. Type 1 lives in an inner tension between how something is and how it ought to be. This tension finds its physical expression particularly in the liver and gallbladder &ndash; the classic detoxification and regulation organs. The body holds onto control where the psyche cannot let go.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Where may something flow that was previously controlled?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#7a2fa8;">Type 2 &ndash; Bonding &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Cardiovascular System &middot; Warmth &middot; Rhythm</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Love, affection, giving, suppression of needs. Type 2 directs warmth outward &ndash; and frequently neglects its own center. Physically, this shows in the cardiovascular system: rhythm, pulsation, one's own heartbeat. The question is whether the warmth flowing outward is also allowed to return inward.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Does the warmth stay in your own center, or does it constantly flow outward?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#1fa688;">Type 3 &ndash; Performance &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Metabolism &middot; Solar Plexus &middot; Energy</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Success, goal, efficiency, recognition, impact. Type 3 is oriented toward performance &ndash; and the body follows this program via metabolism and the solar plexus. Energy is generated for output, not for inner experience. The paradox: The more that is produced, the less is often perceptible of what is truly there.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Is energy truly present, or is only performance being produced?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#3cbf1f;">Type 4 &ndash; Grief &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Lungs &middot; Skin &middot; Boundary</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Individuality, depth, longing, loss, sensitivity. Type 4 perceives deeply and is permeable. Lungs and skin are its physical resonance organs: skin as the boundary between inside and outside, lungs as the rhythm of taking in and letting go. The question moving Type 4 internally also echoes physically: How much may I inhale without losing myself? How much can I exhale without holding onto lack?</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Can I inhale the world without losing myself in it? And can I exhale without holding onto lack?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#124fcc;">Type 5 &ndash; Withdrawal &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Nervous System &middot; Stimulus Processing &middot; Energy</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Knowledge, understanding, distance, withdrawal, observation. The nervous system is the physical home territory of Type 5. It processes stimuli before encountering them &ndash; sometimes for so long that the encounter itself fails to take place. The energy paradox of the Five is well-known: it often has more energy than it shows, and withdraws precisely when aliveness would be possible.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Does withdrawal serve gathering &ndash; or the avoidance of aliveness?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#8a5222;">Type 6 &ndash; Alarm &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Stress System &middot; Adrenal Glands &middot; Vegetative Regulation</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Security, duty, loyalty, doubt, trust, vigilance. Type 6 lives with an inner alarm system attuned to potential dangers. Physically, this manifests through the stress system, the adrenal glands, and vegetative regulation &ndash; an organism in permanent readiness. That is often helpful. But: A system that is always sounding the alarm loses its orientation function.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Does the system recognize real danger &ndash; or does it keep sounding the alarm out of habit?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#d4a800;">Type 7 &ndash; Excess &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Digestion &middot; Stimulus Processing &middot; Metabolism</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Joy, lightness, variety, enjoyment, evasion. The digestive system of Type 7 is its physical image: intake, processing, moving on. Everything is supposed to flow smoothly. Yet the question is whether the new is truly taken in and digested &ndash; or whether more and more is piled on before the previous is fully processed.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Is life truly being nourished &ndash; or is ever more new input simply being taken in?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#a00802;">Type 8 &ndash; Power &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Musculature &middot; Pelvis &middot; Striking Force</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Strength, control, protection, assertiveness, confrontation. Type 8 expresses itself physically in musculature, pelvis, and striking force &ndash; in direct access to energy, presence, and impact. This bodily strength can protect and move things. But it can also become a burden when it must be continuously proven instead of simply being available.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Is strength available &ndash; or must power constantly be proven?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 0.6rem;color:#cc6e00;">Type 9 &ndash; Integration &nbsp;<span style="color:var(--muted);font-weight:400;font-size:0.92rem;">Lymphatic System &middot; Immune System &middot; Basic Regulation</span></h2>
        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Themes:</strong> Peace, harmony, connectedness, balance. Type 9 is physically at home in the lymphatic and immune systems &ndash; the system of silent integration that does not work loudly, but holds everything together. Peace can be a living stillness. Or it can be numbing &ndash; a withdrawal from life that feels like silence, but is actually rigidity.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro"><em>Question for Reflection:</em> Is peace living stillness &ndash; or is it numbing?</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Holistic Perspective</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Human beings are a regulating whole. Body and soul are inseparably connected. This overview does not help with diagnosing, but with seeing: how patterns manifest in the body, where energy flows and where it stalls, where tension is held and where letting go would be possible. Each of the nine reflection questions is an invitation &ndash; not to analysis, but to honest perception of one's own body.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:2rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">This assignment is symbolic in nature &ndash; not as a diagnosis, but as an invitation to better understand body and character. It opens up clear lines of connection between one's own type and a conscious, felt way of living. &ndash; Detlef Rathmer</blockquote>

        ${bookTip("hinter-der-leidenschaft", "The hidden body patterns of the 27 subtypes &ndash; where passion inscribes itself in the body.", "Hinter der Leidenschaft")}
        ${bookTip("heilung-als-erinnerung", "Body, soul, and healing as an inseparable unity &ndash; the homeopathic view of the Enneagram.", "Heilung als Erinnerung")}
        ${relatedLinks([
          {route:"bewusstseinsuebungen", label:"Awareness Exercises"},
          {route:"spirituelle-uebungen", label:"Spiritual Practices"},
          {route:"energetische-bewegungen", label:"Energetic Movements"},
        ])}
      </div>
    </div>
  `);
}

function schopenhauerZitatPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("schopenhauer-zitat")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Philosophy & Enneagram</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">We Cannot Want What We Want</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Arthur Schopenhauer's famous quote strikes in an astonishing way at the very core of what the Enneagram describes regarding the hidden driving forces of personality.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/schopenhauer-zitat.png"
        alt="Schopenhauer Quote: Man can do what he wants, but he cannot want what he wants."
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <p class="vb-intro">Schopenhauer's statement is uncomfortable because it scrapes at a spot where human beings like to feel sovereign: their own volition.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:1.5rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.1rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">&bdquo;Man can do what he wants, but he cannot want what he wants.&ldquo; &ndash; Arthur Schopenhauer</blockquote>

        <p class="vb-intro">At first, this sounds like a philosophical sleight of hand. But it isn't. It is a sober observation. I can act, choose, make decisions. But that which arises within me in the first place as desire, defense, longing, fear, or urge is not something I simply chose. My volition is already there before I take a stance toward it.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">This Is Exactly Where Schopenhauer and the Enneagram Meet</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram does not primarily describe what a person does, but out of which inner necessity they do it. Two people can externally do the exact same thing while internally undergoing something completely different. One helps because they love. The other helps because they need to be needed. One works hard out of joy in the task itself. Another because, without achievement, they fall internally into a bottomless pit. One is silent out of wisdom. Another is silent out of fear of conflict.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Behavior is visible. The volition beneath it &ndash; the intrinsic motivation of the respective type &ndash; is hidden.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Typologically Colored Volition</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Schopenhauer would say: Human beings follow their character. The Enneagram would add: This character has a structure, a fixation, a core fear, a preferred illusion. We do not simply desire freely &ndash; we desire with a typological coloring:</p>
        <ul style="margin:1rem 0 1rem 1.2rem;line-height:1.9;color:var(--ink);font-size:0.97rem;">
          <li><strong>The One</strong> wants to be right.</li>
          <li><strong>The Two</strong> wants to be loved.</li>
          <li><strong>The Three</strong> wants to prove worth.</li>
          <li><strong>The Four</strong> wants to find themselves.</li>
          <li><strong>The Five</strong> wants to understand and protect themselves.</li>
          <li><strong>The Six</strong> wants security.</li>
          <li><strong>The Seven</strong> wants to keep options open and avoid pain.</li>
          <li><strong>The Eight</strong> wants to remain strong.</li>
          <li><strong>The Nine</strong> wants to preserve peace.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="vb-intro">The problem is not that we want things. The problem is that we mistake our wanting for freedom.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">&bdquo;That's Just How I Am&ldquo; &ndash; The Polite Form of Captivity</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Precisely herein lies the diagnostic acuity of the Enneagram. It shows us not only our strengths, but also the place where we are internally unfree &ndash; and usually still mistake this lack of freedom for personality. &bdquo;That's just how I am&ldquo; is often merely the polite form of captivity.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">A person says: <em>&bdquo;I only want to help.&ldquo;</em><br/>Perhaps that is true. Or perhaps they want to be indispensable.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Another says: <em>&bdquo;I only want to be honest.&ldquo;</em><br/>Perhaps that is true. Or perhaps they do not want to feel that they are vulnerable.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">A third says: <em>&bdquo;I just want my peace and quiet.&ldquo;</em><br/>Perhaps that is true. Or perhaps they have long since stopped truly risking themselves in life.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram begins where such sentences become questionable.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Dignity Through Visibility</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">It does not strip us of our dignity. On the contrary: It makes dignity possible in the first place because it makes the mechanical parts of our nature visible. As long as I mistake my automatic wanting for my true self, I am at its mercy. Only when I notice: &bdquo;Ah, that is my pattern again,&ldquo; does a small gap emerge. And in this gap, freedom begins.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Perhaps we truly cannot simply want what we want. But we can learn to recognize our wanting. We can listen to it without immediately obeying it. We can see which hunger speaks, which fear urges, which old strategy disguises itself once more as reason.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:2rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">Freedom does not mean: I reinvent myself every morning.<br/>Freedom means: I am no longer completely at the mercy of my pattern.<br/><span style="font-style:normal;font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--ink);">&ndash; Detlef Rathmer</span></blockquote>

        ${bookTip("du-bist-nicht-dein-zweifel", "When the pattern becomes visible, a gap opens up &ndash; and in this gap, freedom begins.", "Du bist nicht dein Zweifel")}
        ${bookTip("nichts-und-alles", "Beyond fixation: Awareness as the quiet witness of one's own volition.", "Nichts und Alles")}
        ${bookTip("hinter-der-leidenschaft", "The hidden driving force behind visible behavior &ndash; 27 portraits.", "Hinter der Leidenschaft")}
        ${relatedLinks([
          {route:"antriebskraefte", label:"Driving Forces"},
          {route:"ego-fixierungen", label:"Ego-Fixations"},
          {route:"interessante-erkenntnisse", label:"Interesting Insights"},
        ])}
      </div>
    </div>
  `);
}

function bewusstseinsuebungenPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("bewusstseinsuebungen")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Spiritual Practice</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Nine Consciousness Exercises as a Path Back to Presence</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Nine sequential steps through which consciousness gradually releases itself from its fixations &ndash; from the book <em>7 Ways to Yourself</em>.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/bewusstseinsuebungen.jpg"
        alt="Nine Consciousness Exercises &ndash; Detlef Rathmer"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">Nine Steps &ndash; Not a Type-Specific Program</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">These nine consciousness exercises align with the nine points of the Enneagram &ndash; yet they are not a type-specific exercise program. Not every type exclusively practices &bdquo;their&ldquo; exercise. Rather, they describe nine sequential steps through which consciousness can gradually release itself from familiar fixations. Each exercise touches upon a theme that is uniquely connected to a specific Enneagram point and its inner principle.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 9 &ndash; Presence: The Starting Point</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Every path begins with noticing. Nine stands for presence, mindfulness, and awareness &ndash; and thus for the foundation without which all further steps lead into a vacuum. As long as we are not present, we remain completely trapped in our automatic reactions. The first exercise is therefore: <strong>Experiencing presence.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 1 &ndash; Recognizing: Looking Inward</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Consciously directing attention to one's own inner world means perceiving reality as it actually is &ndash; before it is evaluated or corrected. Type 1 tends to orient itself toward standards and ideals. This exercise opens the space before that: <strong>Recognizing what is.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 2 &ndash; Accepting: Devotion to Being</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Two defines itself through relationships and helpfulness. The exercise of acceptance invites one to first receive reality without having to change it. This creates the capacity to receive life rather than constantly giving: <strong>Accepting what is.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 3 &ndash; Sensing: Feeling the Inner Body</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Three often directs its attention outward toward goals and outcomes. The exercise of sensing leads back into direct physical contact and enables experiencing one's worth beyond performance: <strong>Experiencing the body as home.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 4 &ndash; Preserving: Observing Feelings</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Four lives in close proximity to the emotional world, yet is frequently swept away by its emotions. Consciously perceiving and holding feelings without completely identifying with them creates inner stability: <strong>Preserving without identification.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 5 &ndash; Dissolving: Observing Thoughts</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Five lives strongly in the realm of thinking and inner analysis. Recognizing thoughts as temporary occurrences &ndash; not fighting them, but simply letting them pass &ndash; releases consciousness from the cycle of thought: <strong>Dissolving instead of holding on.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 6 &ndash; Breathing: Observing the Breath</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Six is closely tied to anxiety and future orientation. The breath continually returns attention to the present moment and teaches staying with oneself even in uncertainty: <strong>Being anchored in the breath.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 7 &ndash; Anchoring: The Stillness of the Inner Space</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Seven constantly seeks new possibilities and distractions. The exercise of anchoring helps prevent attention from wandering outward, allowing one to consciously experience inner expanse and calm: <strong>Stillness as homecoming.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 8 &ndash; Aligning: The Expanse of Space</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Eight possesses great strength and presence, yet often experiences the world as a place of tension and resistance. This exercise opens the view to a larger perspective: Consciousness is wider than any willpower. <strong>Aligning with the whole.</strong></p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Point 9 &ndash; Presence: The Homecoming</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The path returns to Point 9 &ndash; but on a different level. Presence is no longer a brief moment, but the supporting backdrop of all experiences. From this presence, thoughts, feelings, actions, and relationships can be consciously experienced without personality exerting its accustomed dominance: <strong>Presence as the foundational state.</strong></p>

        <p class="vb-intro" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">The nine exercises thus describe a path from automatic functioning to conscious presence. Each exercise dissolves a piece of the respective fixation and opens direct access to what lies beyond personality in the Enneagram: present, awake consciousness.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:2rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">Every spiritual development process begins at Point 9 &ndash; presence. Without awareness as a foundation, everything is nothing. &ndash; Detlef Rathmer</blockquote>

        ${bookTip("7-wege-zu-dir-selbst-lebenskunst-fuer-den-alltag", "The book for these exercises &ndash; seven spiritual art-of-living steps for daily life.", "7 Wege zu Dir selbst")}
        ${bookTip("heilung-als-erinnerung", "Healing as a return to presence &ndash; the spiritual dimension of inner work.", "Heilung als Erinnerung")}
        ${bookTip("nichts-und-alles", "Consciousness, silence, and inner vastness &ndash; the depth beyond personality.", "Nichts und Alles")}
        ${relatedLinks([
          {route:"spirituelle-uebungen", label:"Spiritual Practices"},
          {route:"schaubilder-als-spiegel", label:"Charts as Mirrors"},
          {route:"koerperregulation", label:"Body Regulation"},
        ])}
      </div>
    </div>
  `);
}

function schaubilderAlsSpiegelPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("schaubilder-als-spiegel")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Self-Knowledge & Maturation</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Charts als Spiegel</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">How we react to a presentation often reveals more about us than the presentation itself. No one absorbs charts neutrally &ndash; everyone reads and interprets them through their own structure.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/schaubilder-als-spiegel.jpg"
        alt="Charts als Spiegel der inneren Struktur"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />

      <div class="vb-section">
        <p class="vb-intro">What is presented as an overview is never just simply information. It is always also a mirror. And mirrors are demanding &ndash; they don't just show something; they immediately trigger a reaction.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Exactly therein lies their value. Because the reaction to a mirror often says more than the mirror itself.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">Defense Is Also an Answer</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Whoever scrolls past such an overview and immediately dismisses it internally &ndash; with the thought that it is irrelevant, alien, or spiritual nonsense &ndash; is already showing something of their inner position. This does not have to mean that this person is uneducated or understands nothing about the Enneagram. On the contrary: One can know a great deal about one's own type, astutely name one's structure &ndash; and yet remain deeply unconscious toward one's own life and fixation.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Knowledge about typology is not yet the same as awareness. You can know about yourself and still not truly see yourself.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">Mirrors for Maturation</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">From a deeper perspective, these overviews are not decorative charts, nor are they mere thinking aids. They are mirrors for maturation. They are not just meant to explain something, but to set something in motion &ndash; to enable self-knowledge and thereby initiate a process in which a person does not simply continue to settle into their familiar fixation.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Because life is not at a standstill. No one simply stays the same. Something changes every day, even when it is barely noticed. Lifetime changes a person, experience changes them, encounters change them, crisis changes them, insight changes them.</p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">You never step into the same river twice. This is precisely why it makes sense to keep letting yourself be reflected &ndash; not to judge yourself, but so as not to stand still.</blockquote>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">How Mirrors Work &ndash; Depending on the Inner State</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Whoever looks at such an overview not only sees something about human beings in general but is always confronted with themselves. This happens in very different ways: Some immediately feel defense. Others experience interest, but only as long as it doesn't get too personal. Still others recognize themselves with an inner shock. And some begin to suspect for the first time that their seemingly perfectly normal ways of reacting are in truth long-formed patterns &ndash; which shape their lives more strongly than they were aware.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">In a deeply unconscious state, such an overview is usually not experienced as a help, but as an imposition. The person then does not feel recognized, but rather observed, evaluated, or exposed. They do not read calmly, but selectively. They do not seek truth, but protection.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The higher the level of consciousness, the more open the encounter with the mirror: curiosity instead of defense, insight instead of justification, movement instead of standstill.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="margin:2.5rem 0 0;">
        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.4rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.75rem;">Not a Judgment &ndash; A Mirror</h2>
        <img
          src="../assets/enneagramm-als-spiegel.jpg"
          alt="Das Enneagramm ist ein Spiegel, kein Urteil. Wer mit Typen etikettiert, verletzt die Freiheit des Anderen."
          style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:0.75rem 0 1.25rem;"
        />
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">The Enneagram is an instrument for self-knowledge &ndash; not a classification system for others. This distinction sounds self-evident but is frequently blurred in everyday life. Anyone who assigns a type to someone based on a first impression, who dismisses reactions from friends or colleagues as &bdquo;typical Seven&ldquo; or &bdquo;that's just the Eight in him,&ldquo; is using the Enneagram as a judgment &ndash; and is thus doing exactly what it is supposed to prevent.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">Because a label shuts things down. It seemingly explains what a person does &ndash; and thereby takes away their openness to be different in this moment. The other becomes a category, not a person. What was intended as understanding becomes a limitation.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">A mirror works differently. It shows &ndash; to the one looking into it. It gives feedback about one's own pattern, one's own reaction, one's own fixation. The Enneagram as a mirror means: I look at myself, not at the other. I don't ask &bdquo;What type is he?&ldquo; but rather &bdquo;What does he trigger in me &ndash; and what does that say about my pattern?&ldquo; In this way, the freedom of the other remains untouched. And this is how genuine understanding arises &ndash; not through categorization, but through encounter.</p>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The ultimate chart book: Who you truly are &ndash; in colorful, illustrative overviews. The direct entry into the visual world of the Enneagram.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
      ${bookTip("nichts-und-alles", "A quiet book about looking inward &ndash; consciousness, space, and inner freedom. The ideal companion for holding one's own mirror more steadily.", "Nichts und Alles")}
      ${bookTip("du-bist-nicht-dein-zweifel", "Truly seeing oneself &ndash; not just knowing about oneself. A guide to letting go of one's own fixation.", "Du bist nicht dein Zweifel")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"neun-logismoi", label:"9 Logismoi according to Evagrius"},
        {route:"interessante-erkenntnisse", label:"Interesting Insights"},
        {route:"bewusstseinsuebungen", label:"Awareness Exercises"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function neunLogismoiPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("neun-logismoi")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Intellectual History & Self-Knowledge</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Logismoi According to Evagrius Ponticus</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">What do we fill our inner emptiness with? As early as the 4th century, Evagrius Ponticus described nine inner movements that pull human beings away from their center &ndash; showing an astonishing proximity to the Enneagram.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/neun-logismoi.jpg"
        alt="The 9 Logismoi According to Evagrius Ponticus"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />

      <div class="vb-section">
        <p class="vb-intro">No human being is completely quiet inside. Something within us seeks support, comfort, validation, security, or vitality. And at precisely this point, the question becomes important: What do we try to fill this inner emptiness with?</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">Evagrius Ponticus, a Christian thinker and spiritual teacher of the early Church, made subtle and surprisingly precise observations about this. He called them inner movements of thought &ndash; Logismoi &ndash; that urge human beings in specific directions. They often seem sensible and even useful. That is precisely what makes them so effective. Because they promise something they ultimately cannot deliver.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">The Nine Movements</h2>

        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Anger</strong> gives strength for a moment. Someone who is hurt can feel strong rather than weak through anger. But this strength doesn't last &ndash; anger often binds us even more tightly to what hurt us.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Pride</strong> is the attempt to make oneself internally greater by placing oneself above others. Behind it usually lies no real strength, but a hidden insecurity.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Vainglory</strong> goes outward. The person lives off the gaze of others, off recognition and admiration. One's sense of self depends on being seen and confirmed.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Sorrow</strong> shows how a person can lose themselves in pain. Sadness is part of life &ndash; but sometimes one remains so stuck in suffering that it becomes the defining force.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro"><strong>Greed</strong> tries to gain security through possession. <strong>Fearfulness</strong> tries to make life manageable through control. <strong>Gluttony</strong> stands not just for food, but for the attempt to calm emptiness through consumption. <strong>Lust</strong> refers to escaping into stimulation and pleasure when inner tension cannot be borne otherwise.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">Especially profound is <strong>despondency &ndash; acedia</strong>. This is not mere laziness. It is a weariness of the soul: Nothing truly touches anymore, nothing tastes good anymore, the essential loses its attraction. The person still functions, but is internally empty and cut off. This description sounds surprisingly modern.</p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">A person does not become free by immediately filling every inner unrest with something. They become free when they learn to grow stiller and not let themselves be ruled by every inner impulse.</blockquote>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">Evagrius Ponticus &ndash; A Man of His Time Who Points Beyond It</h2>

        <p class="vb-intro">Evagrius lived from about 345 to 399 AD. He originated from Pontus (Asia Minor), received an ecclesiastical education, and later went to the Egyptian desert &ndash; to the famous Desert Fathers of early Christianity. As a monk, ascetic, and writer, he developed a sharp eye for the soul's struggles. His descriptions of the Logismoi are considered precursors to the later &bdquo;catalog of vices,&ldquo; which became the seven deadly sins in the Western Church.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">Interesting for Enneagram connoisseurs: Evagrius originally counted <em>nine</em> Logismoi &ndash; not seven. This number nine is telling. It reflects a deep observation that fundamental human patterns express themselves in nine typical forms &ndash; an insight that the Enneagram later continued in a different language.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">The Proximity to the Enneagram</h2>

        <p class="vb-intro">Evagrius did not know the modern psychological Enneagram &ndash; it did not exist in this form back then. And yet he describes patterns that can be recognized in the nine types: the anger of the Eight, the pride of the Two, the vainglory of the Three, the sorrow of the Four, the avarice of the Five, the fearfulness of the Six, the gluttony of the Seven, lust in the Sexual type, and acedia as the shadow of the Nine.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">This shows: Some truths about humanity do not become obsolete. They reappear across different times, cultures, and languages because they touch upon something fundamental about human life. Both systems &ndash; the Logismoi and the Enneagram &ndash; look at the same question: What makes a human being unfree? And what leads them back to their truth?</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">This overview invites self-knowledge: Where do you pull toward when you become internally empty? What do you calm yourself with? What could happen if you don't immediately flee, but become stiller &ndash; and look more honestly?</p>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The Enneagram as a path to self-knowledge &ndash; what makes us unfree and what leads us back to the truth.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
      ${bookTip("du-bist-nicht-dein-zweifel", "Letting go of what is not truly you &ndash; a guide out of inner fixations.", "Du bist nicht dein Zweifel")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"gifte-des-geistes", label:"Poisons of the Mind"},
        {route:"schaubilder-als-spiegel", label:"Charts as Mirrors"},
        {route:"spirituelle-uebungen", label:"Spiritual Practices"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function gaslightingPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("gaslighting-enneagramm")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Relationship Psychology</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Gaslighting and the Enneagram</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Gaslighting is a form of psychological manipulation in which another person's perception, experience, or memory is systematically called into question &ndash; with the goal of creating uncertainty and gaining interpretive sovereignty over shared reality.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/gaslighting-enneagramm.jpg"
        alt="Gaslighting and the Enneagram"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />

      <div class="vb-section">
        <p class="vb-intro">In the context of the Enneagram, this is not about labeling certain types as inherently manipulative in a blanket way. That would be too simplistic and would contradict the system. Rather, the crucial factor is: immature expressions of individual types can foster specific patterns that make them more susceptible to gaslighting-like dynamics. Some people control reality through guilt, others through emotional fusion, and still others through fear, moral pressure, or the control of narratives.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">The highest tendency appears where attachment, identity, and control are tightly intertwined &ndash; where one's own experience is set as the benchmark for a shared reality, and the other person is meant to be held within that reality.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Very High Intensity &ndash; Attachment, Identity, and Control</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">This can manifest particularly intensely in the <strong>SX2</strong>: when attachment is steered via guilt and emotional reality, a system emerges in which the other person permanently feels they are failing or are not enough. The <strong>sexual Four</strong> also belongs to this range &ndash; here, personal feeling is easily equated with truth, and strong identity fusion makes it difficult to distinguish between subjective experience and shared reality. The <strong>SX6</strong> can generate highly unsettling dynamics through anxiety, attachment pressure, and a distorted perception of threats. For the <strong>SO3</strong>, control of the narrative stands more in the foreground: reality is shaped via image and presentation &ndash; what matters is what looks good on the outside. The <strong>SX8</strong>, in turn, can exert massive influence on others' experience of reality through dominance and relational power. What these patterns share is that, in immaturity, they can deeply intrude into the counterpart's sense of self.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">High Intensity &ndash; Subtler, But Psychologically Effective</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">A very high, but often subtler tendency is found in types that control less openly &ndash; acting with greater psychological precision instead. The <strong>SO2</strong> frequently works with guilt, relational pressure, and moral appeal. The <strong>sexual Three</strong> can strongly harness reality in the service of attachment and desired mirroring. The <strong>SO6</strong> tends to exert influence via threat narratives and group norms &ndash; whoever does not agree jeopardizes cohesion. The <strong>SX1</strong> can charge moral interpretations so intensely that their own view appears as the only legitimate one. The <strong>SO4</strong> often brings a strong identity narrative in which emotional truth is placed above sober clarification. The <strong>SX5</strong> usually appears quieter, but can cause considerable uncertainty through subtle cognitive destabilization.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Medium Intensity &ndash; Rationalization and Context Shifting</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">In the middle range are patterns that function more via rationalization, projection, or context shifting. The <strong>SO8</strong> combines power with narrative control, but usually remains less identity-bound. The <strong>SP6</strong> frequently constructs a security reality in which caution and threat dominate the picture. The <strong>SX7</strong> can blur reality through trivialization and emotional shifting without openly controlling it. The <strong>SO5</strong> tends toward cognitive standardization: their own thinking becomes the benchmark. The <strong>self-preservation Two</strong> works more indirectly with guilt, whereas the <strong>SX9</strong> can make clarity difficult through fusion and obfuscation &ndash; without consciously manipulating harshly.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Low Intensity &ndash; Avoidance Instead of Distortion</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">In the lower range are types where avoidance, mitigation, or rationalization are more pronounced than true reality distortion. The <strong>self-preservation Three</strong> is indeed image-oriented, but usually less focused on deep identity fusion. The <strong>SP4</strong> possesses a subjective reality, but rarely attempts to systematically trap others within it. The <strong>SO7</strong> tends to downplay rather than purposefully reinterpret reality. The <strong>SP8</strong> relies more frequently on direct power than on subtle psychological distortion. The <strong>SP1</strong> orients themselves around rules instead of manipulative interpretation, and the <strong>SO9</strong> tends more toward conflict obfuscation than targeted destabilization.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Lowest Intensity &ndash; Withdrawal Instead of Control</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The lowest tendency appears where people tend to withdraw or evade rather than actively reinterpret reality. The <strong>SP5</strong> retreats instead of mentally destabilizing others. The <strong>SP7</strong> flees unpleasant realities instead of controlling them. The <strong>SP9</strong> pacifies, dampens, and lulls conflicts to sleep rather than manipulating through targeted reinterpretation. Naturally, these types can also exhibit gaslighting-like behaviors &ndash; but usually not as systematically and not as close to identity as other constellations.</p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">No type is automatically toxic &ndash; and no type is automatically harmless. What matters is the level of maturity, self-reflection, and the willingness to take responsibility for one's own behavior.</blockquote>

        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram describes structures and tendencies, not moral judgments. The value of such a classification does not lie in prematurely condemning people. It lies in better recognizing manipulation patterns, naming them more clearly &ndash; and protecting oneself more consciously against them.</p>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("hinter-der-leidenschaft", "The deeper driving forces behind the nine patterns &ndash; why people act the way they do, and what that has to do with their wound.", "Hinter der Leidenschaft")}
      ${bookTip("wenn-die-stille-brennt", "When inner exhaustion meets the Enneagram &ndash; burnout, loss of boundaries, and the path back to oneself.", "Wenn die Stille brennt &ndash; Burnout")}
      ${bookTip("du-bist-nicht-dein-zweifel", "Letting go of what isn't truly you &ndash; a guide out of inner fixations and dependencies.", "Du bist nicht dein Zweifel")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"interessante-erkenntnisse", label:"Interesting Insights"},
        {route:"wunden", label:"Wounds & Protective Strategies"},
        {route:"leidenschaft-und-wunde", label:"Passion and Wound"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function interessanteErkenntnissePage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("interessante-erkenntnisse")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Type Psychology</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Neun interessante Erkenntnisse</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">What many people believe they know about the nine Enneagram types &ndash; and what is really behind it. Nine observations from decades of therapeutic and diagnostic practice.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/interessante-erkenntnisse.jpg"
        alt="Neun interessante Erkenntnisse zu den Enneagrammtypen"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />

      <div class="vb-section">
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram is a precise system &ndash; but also one that is easily misunderstood. Many type profiles oversimplify and create caricatures instead of genuine self-knowledge. The following nine observations clear up some common shortcuts and sharpen the perspective on what truly drives us.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 1 &ndash; Not Perfection, but Inner Accusation</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Ones are frequently described as perfectionistic &ndash; but that only hits the core halfway. The decisive factor is not external order, but the permanent inner corrective impulse: a kind of moral muscle tension that never fully lets up. Some Ones outwardly appear quite relaxed and tolerant &ndash; yet still carry an unrelenting inner judge within them that evaluates every action, every thought, and every decision in retrospect. The suffering of the One arises not from disorder around them, but from the never-fully-silenced voice saying: <em>That is still not good enough.</em></p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 2 &ndash; Helping as a Relationship Strategy</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Twos help &ndash; not always out of pure love. Frequently, their generosity also serves to establish attachment and make themselves indispensable. In most cases, this happens unconsciously: the Two feels genuine warmth, a genuine desire to give &ndash; while failing to notice that they are simultaneously building a relational structure in which the other person can hardly feel free anymore. Twos can thus appear more controlling than they are willing to admit to themselves. The real question they avoid is: <em>Am I still lovable if I give nothing?</em></p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 3 &ndash; Self-Confusion with the Role</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Threes do not always deceive consciously. That would be too simple. Frequently, they identify so completely with their function, their role, or their efficiency that they partially lose contact with their actual inner experience. It is not a mask they put on &ndash; it is a confusion: they mistake the performance for themselves. This does not make them a liar, but someone who honestly shows what they believe themselves to be. The actual therapeutic process for the Three begins only when they learn to ask: <em>Who am I if I am not performing?</em></p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 4 &ndash; Precision, Not Just Emotional Depth</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Fours are considered the emotional beings of the Enneagram &ndash; but that falls short. Often, they are astonishingly precise observers of subtle inner differences: they perceive what is not quite right, what is missing, what is slightly skewed in a situation. One could call it <em>fine-tuning awareness</em>. Their suffering stems primarily not from emotional depth alone, but from this almost artistic sensitivity for the unfinished and the inauthentic. Because they perceive so precisely what is missing, they are also the hardest to satisfy.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 5 &ndash; Stinginess with Energy, Not Just Knowledge</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Fives are often described as knowledge collectors who dispense their knowledge with restraint. That is part of the truth. The core problem runs deeper: Fives primarily hold back their energy, their access, and their availability. For them, knowledge is frequently a means to protect their own inner space &ndash; a space they experience as limited and precious. The fear that drives them is not social coldness, but the deep apprehension of being innerly drained hollow by the demands and expectations of others. Withdrawal is not a luxury for them, but self-protection.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 6 &ndash; Fear Disguised as Attack</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Sixes are not simply fearful in the classical sense. Many appear rather combative, provocative, or paradoxically resistant. Fear is then warded off via counter-control, targeted testing, skepticism, and sometimes open confrontation. The sexual Six (SX6) in particular develops an attitude that can strongly resemble an Eight or a Three on the outside. Anyone who only knows Sixes as fearful, adapted types will find this variant puzzling &ndash; until they grasp that attack and control are also forms of coping with fear.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 7 &ndash; Flight from Unambiguity</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Sevens do not merely flee from pain. That is known &ndash; but it does not fully explain them. At least equally significant is the flight from limitation through unambiguity: committing oneself means not only giving up alternatives for a Seven, but experiencing an existential loss of identity. Choosing one possibility means letting all others die. For Sevens, this is not a rhetorical image, but an inner reality. Their restlessness, their planning, their eternal keeping-open of options is therefore frequently of an existential nature &ndash; something they themselves admit to less often than is actually the case.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 8 &ndash; Strength as Protection Against Powerlessness</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Eights appear powerful &ndash; and in many respects, they are. But their true sensitivity lies in a place barely visible from the outside: the impossibility of allowing powerlessness. Many Eights endure physical pain and emotional burden better than the state of helplessness and being at the mercy of others. Their hardness is therefore frequently less aggression than armor they have erected precisely against this experience. Anyone who truly wants to encounter an Eight must understand that behind the strength lies a very early experience: <em>Being weak is dangerous.</em></p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.8rem 0 .5rem;">Type 9 &ndash; Self-Forgetfulness of the Will</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Nines are often described as comfortable or conflict-avoidant. But that captures only the surface. The deeper pattern is subtler: Nines gradually lose themselves in adaptation, routines, and trivialities &ndash; not because they are lazy, but because their own center becomes increasingly diffuse. They can accomplish a great deal for others while wondering what they themselves actually want. The Nine's main problem is therefore not laziness in the banal sense, but a kind of <em>self-forgetfulness of the will</em>: their own impulse, their own desire, their own direction becomes harder and harder to perceive amid constant accommodation.</p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">The Enneagram only becomes truly useful when it no longer describes the drawer, but the force sitting inside it.</blockquote>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The nine types in charts &ndash; from core structures to the subtle differences often lost in brief descriptions.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
      ${bookTip("hinter-der-leidenschaft", "What really lies behind the nine passions &ndash; the wounds that drive them and the remedies that can resolve them.", "Hinter der Leidenschaft")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"gaslighting-enneagramm", label:"Gaslighting & Enneagram"},
        {route:"lookalike-typen", label:"Lookalike Types"},
        {route:"differenzierung", label:"Differentiation"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function bedeutungSubtypesPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("bedeutung-27-subtypen")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Subtypes &amp; Typing</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Why the 27 Subtypes Are Essential</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Anyone who seriously engages with the Enneagram cannot bypass the 27 subtypes. It is a fundamental misconception to believe that one can initially &bdquo;just start with the nine types&ldquo; and add the subtypes later &ndash; because without them, the picture remains too crude for serious typing.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/bedeutung-27-subtypen.jpg"
        alt="Die Bedeutung der 27 Subtypes im Enneagramm"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />

      <div class="vb-section">
        <p class="vb-intro">The nine types alone create caricatures too quickly. Type 8 becomes &bdquo;the strong one,&ldquo; Type 2 &bdquo;the helper,&ldquo; Type 5 &bdquo;the thinker,&ldquo; Type 4 &bdquo;the deep-feeling one.&ldquo; That may suffice for an initial overview &ndash; but it is not enough for serious type determination. Human beings do not live as stencils. They live as concrete, differentiated, often surprising expressions of a pattern.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">The Nine Types Without Subtypes Lead Astray</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Those who work exclusively with the nine basic types run the risk of categorizing people too quickly. One recognizes certain behavioral traits, certain core themes &ndash; and thinks one has reached the goal. In reality, one often only holds a rough map. The actual landscape only reveals itself when the three instinctual drives are added: self-preservation, social, or sexual.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Only then does it become clear why two people of the same basic type can seem so different that at first glance, one would take them for two completely different types. A self-preservation Four can appear more objective and stoic than the common image of Type 4 would suggest. A social Eight can appear more caring and community-oriented than many expect from an Eight. A sexual Six can appear like an Eight. A self-preservation Two can easily be mistaken for a Six or Four.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">The 27 Subtypes Are Not a Refinement for Specialists</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">To put it plainly: In practice, we do not encounter nine types, but 27 manifestations of these nine types. The basic type remains central. But it never appears in the abstract. It always manifests within a specific instinctual dynamic, in a specific way of organizing oneself in the world. Anyone who considers only the basic type and ignores the subtypes is working with a skeleton &ndash; but not yet with a living human being.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Subtypes are therefore not a later remedy for advanced students, but a necessary part of understanding right from the start. They show how a type actually embodies itself, where its energy flows, and what colors its passion.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">Typing Requires Agility</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">A good understanding of the Enneagram does not thrive on stubbornly holding onto one level. It requires the ability to zoom back and forth between different levels. Sometimes you have to zoom far out: Is this person anchored more in the heart, head, or gut center? Then zoom in closer: Which of the three types within this triad could it be? And then look even closer: In which instinctual coloring does this type manifest?</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">This is not a luxury, but a genuine necessity. The number 3 remains important because of the triads. The number 9 remains important because of the basic types. But the number 27 is indispensable in concrete work with real human beings.</p>

        <blockquote class="pull-quote">The real question is not: Which type is this? But rather: In what concrete form does this person live out their type?</blockquote>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">Subtypes Protect Against Simplification</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Many people experience studying the Enneagram for a long time, reading a lot, knowing many type descriptions &ndash; and yet making no progress in determining their own type. They remain uncertain, waver between types, or feel that no description really fits. Often, this is simply because the work was too coarse.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Subtypes do not make the Enneagram unnecessarily complicated. They are what makes it truly realistic in the first place. They protect against the temptation to label people prematurely. They protect against type caricatures and superficial attributions. And they bring the Enneagram out of abstraction back into life &ndash; because a person is not simply &bdquo;a Four&ldquo; or &bdquo;a Six.&ldquo; They always live their type in a specific way. And this way is highly significant psychologically, energetically, and diagnostically.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">Considering subtypes from the very beginning does not mean getting bogged down. It is about providing the right inner picture from the start: There are nine basic types, and each of these types appears in three fundamental instinctual variants. This knowledge alone changes one's entire mindset. Instead of learning nine rigid stencils, one learns a living, differentiated system right from the start.</p>

        <p class="vb-intro">Only in the 27 subtypes does a person reveal themselves in their true soulful form. Whoever stops at the nine basic types sees only the rough outline.</p>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("die-verborgene-dynamik-der-27-subtypen", "The 27 subtypes in detail &ndash; normal, reinforcing, and countertype of each Enneagram type explained extensively.", "Die verborgene Dynamik der 27 Subtypes")}
      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The ultimate chart book: the nine types in colorful, illustrative overviews &ndash; the direct entry into the visual Enneagram.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"antriebskraefte", label:"Driving Forces of Humanity"},
        {route:"subtypen-schaubilder", label:"Subtype Charts (All 27)"},
        {route:"horney-triaden", label:"Horneyvian Triads & Subtypes"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function identifikationPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("identifikation")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts · Consciousness & Self-Knowledge</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The Tighter the Identification, the Louder the Pattern</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">A sentence about the connection between consciousness and passion – and why the Enneagram is not a labeling system, but a path to inner freedom.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/identifikation.jpg"
        alt="Identification &ndash; The Tighter the Identification, the Louder the Pattern"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">

        <p class="vb-intro">This sentence contains a paradox that is not immediately obvious at first. When someone asks: »How do you break free from your pattern?«, the obvious answer is often: »By working on yourself.« But the Enneagram shows something different – and deeper: The pattern does not become quieter through effort, but through changing distance.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">Was Identifikation bedeutet</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Identification means: I mistake the pattern for myself. I do not experience my passion as something happening within me – but as who I am. The Type Two person does not say: »I notice that I tend to help unasked.« They experience helping as their core, as their worth, as their identity. The Type Eight person does not experience their power as a pattern manifesting – but as who they are.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">As long as I identify with the pattern, there is no observer for it. And without an observer, there is no freedom.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">Why the Pattern Becomes Louder</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Tight identification leads to an amplification of the pattern. This is because the pattern runs unfiltered and unchecked when no one is there to observe it. There is no inner witness saying: »Ah, there is my passion again.« Instead, the pattern translates directly into behavior, reaction, and thought.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The less distance a person has to their pattern, the more directly it shows itself: The anger of the One erupts without being chosen. The pride of the Two controls relationships without them noticing. The hunger of the Seven drives from experience to experience without ever landing. This is not a moral failure – it is the mechanical result of tight identification.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">What Loosening Identification Means</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Loosening identification does not mean erasing or fighting the pattern. It means creating an inner space – between impulse and reaction, between feeling and behavior, between automatism and choice.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">This space is not an achievement. It arises through awareness. Through simple noticing: »There is my passion again. That is me – but I am not only that.« In that moment, the pattern itself does not transform, but its power over the person changes. It turns from law into observation, from compulsion into possibility.</p>

        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--ink);margin:1.6rem 0 .5rem;">The Enneagram as a Mirror, Not a Cage</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Precisely here lies the deepest danger when working with the Enneagram: turning it into a new frame of identification. Whoever says »I am a Four« instead of »I am currently showing the pattern of a Four« has used the Enneagram incorrectly. They have built a new box, larger than all previous ones – but still a box.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram is most effective when used as a mirror, not a label. The mirror shows: Here a pattern is running. Here passion is at work. Here is identification. And where something can be shown, the possibility arises to differentiate – myself and the pattern. That is the beginning of freedom.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:2rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">The tighter the identification, the louder the pattern. The more conscious the distance, the quieter it becomes &ndash; and the more visible the person becomes who was there before the pattern began. &ndash; Detlef Rathmer</blockquote>

        ${bookTip("der-raum-hinter-allem", "Beyond all patterns: the quiet space that remains when identification recedes – a meditative work on consciousness and freedom.", "Der Raum hinter allem")}
        ${bookTip("nichts-und-alles", "Being without holding on – a quiet book about the space beyond personality and the inner vastness that arises when we stop defining ourselves.", "Nichts und Alles")}
        ${bookTip("hinter-der-leidenschaft", "What lies behind the pattern: the nine wounds of the Enneagram and the remedies that go to the root.", "Hinter der Leidenschaft")}
        ${bookTip("leidenschaft-und-heilung", "Passions, virtues, and paths of healing of all 27 Subtypes – from therapeutic practice.", "Leidenschaft und Heilung")}
        ${relatedLinks([
          {route:"schaubilder-als-spiegel", label:"Charts als Spiegel"},
          {route:"ego-fixierungen", label:"Ego-Fixierungen"},
          {route:"bewusstseinsuebungen", label:"Awareness Exercises"},
        ])}
      </div>
    </div>
  `);
}

function schmerzschutzPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("schmerzschutz")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Consciousness & Self-Knowledge</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The Type Protects Against Pain, But It Prevents Intimacy</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">A sentence on the dual function of the Enneagram type &ndash; and why what protects us simultaneously separates us from ourselves and from others.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/schmerzschutz.jpg"
        alt="Pain Protection &ndash; The Type Protects Against Pain, But It Prevents Intimacy When We Mistake It for the Truth"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <p class="vb-intro">The full sentence reads: <em>&bdquo;The type protects against pain, but it prevents intimacy when we mistake it for the truth.&ldquo;</em> It contains two truths at once &ndash; and the crucial difference lies in the last clause.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">The Type as a Protective Structure</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Every Enneagram type arose as a response to early pain. The child felt: It is not safe to be as I truly am. So it developed a strategy &ndash; a kind of inner armor that helped make the pain bearable. The Two became helpful to secure love. The Six became vigilant to detect threats early. The Nine withdrew to avoid conflict. These strategies are clever. They worked. And they saved us &ndash; back then.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">This is not a metaphor: The type is a sophisticated system for pain avoidance. It directs attention, filters perception, and controls behavior so that the original painful wound is re-triggered as little as possible.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">What It Prevents</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">The problem does not begin with the protection itself &ndash; but with mistaking the protection for reality. If I believe I <em>am</em> the type, then every step out of the pattern is a step into danger. I cannot open up, because opening up means pain to my nervous system. I cannot allow genuine intimacy, because genuine intimacy means: The other person sees me &ndash; not my strategy, but me.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">Intimacy arises precisely where we stop performing. Where we no longer play the role we consider safe. And that is exactly the spot the type blocks &ndash; not out of malice, but simply because it is a protection.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">&bdquo;When We Mistake It for the Truth&ldquo;</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">That is the key sentence. As long as the type is used as a protection &ndash; consciously, flexibly, appropriately for the situation &ndash; it is valuable. It becomes a problem when we equate it with our identity. When I say: That's just who I am. When the strategy becomes a conviction: The world is like this, people are like this, intimacy is dangerous.</p>
        <p class="vb-intro">The Enneagram shows: This mistaking-for-truth is not a weakness &ndash; it is the mechanism of fixation itself. And it is not resolved through willpower, but through awareness: by recognizing that a structure is at work &ndash; and that I am more than this structure.</p>

        <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--gold);padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin:2rem 0;font-style:italic;color:var(--copper);font-size:1.05rem;background:var(--ivory);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">The type protects against pain, but it prevents intimacy when we mistake it for the truth. &ndash; Detlef Rathmer</blockquote>

        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "What remains when the protective structure recedes? An exploration of identity beyond type &ndash; who we truly are before the pattern begins.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
        ${bookTip("die-sprache-unserer-beziehungen", "Cultivating intimacy with the Enneagram &ndash; how the nine types experience, seek, and unconsciously prevent relationships.", "Die Sprache unserer Beziehungen")}
        ${bookTip("hinter-der-leidenschaft", "The nine wounds behind the patterns &ndash; and the remedies that get to the root of pain protection.", "Hinter der Leidenschaft")}
        ${relatedLinks([
          {route:"identifikation", label:"Identification & Patterns"},
          {route:"naehe", label:"Intimacy"},
          {route:"wunden", label:"Wounds"},
        ])}
      </div>
    </div>
  `);
}

function blickqualitaetPage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("blickqualitaet")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Typing & Profiling</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The Primary Gaze Quality of the 9 Enneagram Types</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">The gaze reveals more than words. Every Enneagram type carries a characteristic gaze quality &ndash; a way of looking that is deeply connected to their inner world, motivation, and energy center.</p>
      <img
        src="../assets/blickqualitaet.jpg"
        alt="The Primary Gaze Quality of the 9 Enneagram Types"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2rem;border-radius:12px;cursor:zoom-in;"
      />
      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <p class="vb-intro">The chart shows at a glance: Each of the nine types has a typical way of looking at the world. This <em>primary gaze quality</em> does not arise by chance &ndash; it is the direct expression of a type's inner passion, motivation, and energy processing. Whoever can read it has a typing tool that needs no words.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;color:var(--ink);">What Gaze Quality Reveals</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Behind every gaze quality lies a specific flow of energy. The chart distinguishes between three centers &ndash; gut, heart, and head &ndash; and three forms of energy processing: <em>transformed</em> (energy flows integrated), <em>excessive</em> (too much energy outward), and <em>blocked</em> (energy is congested). These nine combinations create the nine different gaze qualities.</p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">The Nine Gaze Qualities at a Glance</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">
          <strong>Type 1 &ndash; Focused-rigid gaze quality:</strong> precise, distant, critical. The gaze is focused and controlled &ndash; as if scanning the world for deviations from the ideal.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 2 &ndash; Warm gaze quality:</strong> loving, caring, connecting. A gaze that actively seeks out who currently needs help &ndash; inviting and outwardly directed.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 3 &ndash; Focused-calculating gaze quality:</strong> focused, confident of victory, successful. The gaze is goal-oriented, self-assured &ndash; it signals: I know where I want to go.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 4 &ndash; Deep-dreamy gaze quality:</strong> melancholic, sad, lost. A gaze that wanders inward or into the distance &ndash; as if what is essential is always elsewhere.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 5 &ndash; Distant gaze quality:</strong> thoughtful, intellectual, empty/distant. The gaze observes without revealing itself &ndash; withdrawn behind an invisible pane of glass.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 6 &ndash; Boundary-drawing gaze quality:</strong> anxious, cautious, skeptical. The gaze constantly checks: What could become dangerous? It is vigilant and seeks reassurance.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 7 &ndash; Button-eyed gaze quality:</strong> lively, cheerful, optimistic. A bright, darting gaze that rushes from possibility to possibility &ndash; curious and open.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 8 &ndash; Controlling gaze quality:</strong> dominant, controlling, intimidating. The gaze measures the room, tests boundaries, and signals: I am here.<br><br>
          <strong>Type 9 &ndash; Peripheral gaze quality:</strong> gentle, peaceful, relaxed. A soft, often slightly diffuse gaze that does not fixate on anyone directly &ndash; perceiving everything simultaneously without judging.
        </p>

        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;margin:1.8rem 0 1rem;color:var(--ink);">Gaze Quality in Practice</h2>
        <p class="vb-intro">Eye quality is one of the most reliable typing features because it is harder to control than words or gestures. It can be observed in the first few seconds of an encounter &ndash; even before anyone has said anything. Those trained to distinguish these qualities gain direct access to a person's inner world. However, caution is advised: A single observation is never enough for a reliable typing. Eye quality is a clue, not proof.</p>

        ${bookTip("die-praxis-der-typbestimmung-taschenbuch", "How do you recognize the Enneagram type based on external features? A practical guide to typing &ndash; with a focus on body language, facial expressions, and gaze quality.", "Die Praxis der Typbestimmung")}
        ${bookTip("die-verborgene-dynamik-der-27-subtypen", "How do the three subtypes of a basic type differ in their body language and gaze quality? The standard work on the 27 subtypes.", "Die verborgene Dynamik der 27 Subtypes")}
        ${bookTip("meta-intelligenz", "Sharpening nonverbal perception &ndash; how we learn to read the inner world of other people without needing words.", "Meta-Intelligenz")}
        ${relatedLinks([
          {route:"enneagramm-profiling", label:"Enneagram Profiling"},
          {route:"mikroimpressionen", label:"Micro-impressions"},
          {route:"differenzierung", label:"Differentiation"},
          {route:"blickqualitaeten-atlas", label:"Gaze Quality Atlas of the 27 Subtypes"},
        ])}
      </div>
    </div>
  `);
}

function beruhmtePhilosophenPage() {
  const phil = [
    {
      nr: 1, name: "Aristotle", jahre: "384–322 BC", subtyp: "Self-Preservation Subtype",
      text: "Aristotle was the systematizer par excellence. He classified plants, animals, virtues, forms of government, and poetic genres &ndash; laying the foundation of Western science in the process. Typical of Type 1's Self-Preservation subtype: he did not direct his standard outward at society, but first at himself &ndash; with the quiet, persistent question of whether one is really doing it right. His golden mean (Mesotes) is not a rule for others, but a principle of one's own conduct: virtue as daily habituation, not a single insight. The Nine wing shows in his calm, undogmatic breadth &ndash; he did not just order a single field, but the entire body of known knowledge into one coherent system."
    },
    {
      nr: 2, name: "Søren Kierkegaard", jahre: "1813–1855", subtyp: "Self-Preservation Subtype",
      text: "Kierkegaard wrote about love with an intensity few have matched. Yet he did not love easily &ndash; he loved with tension, self-torment, under the constant question of whether he was worthy to give and receive. Type 2's Self-Preservation subtype is the introverted Helper: giving not openly and theatrically, but in secret, often with inner conflict. Kierkegaard broke off his engagement because he believed he could not give the other person what she deserved &ndash; a deeply typical Two gesture that turns caregiving into a burden."
    },
    {
      nr: 3, name: "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz", jahre: "1646–1716", subtyp: "Social Subtype",
      text: "Leibniz was a diplomat, mathematician, philosopher, and inventor &ndash; all at once. He devised differential and integral calculus, developed the binary number system, wrote on metaphysics, and negotiated at European royal courts at the same time. Type 3's Social subtype strives for success within a social framework: recognition through achievement, visibility through impact. Leibniz always wanted to be useful &ndash; on the largest possible stage. No type embodies the pursuit of social influence through competence more clearly."
    },
    {
      nr: 4, name: "Arthur Schopenhauer", jahre: "1788–1860", subtyp: "Sexual Subtype",
      text: "Schopenhauer was a master of cultivated suffering. He saw the world as a blind will that drives us without ever satisfying us &ndash; and wrote about it with a passion that contradicted his own principle. Type 4's Sexual subtype seeks intense connection and suffers deeply from disappointment. Schopenhauer had a strained relationship with his mother, lived in isolation, had few close bonds &ndash; and compensated for his longing for depth with a philosophical pessimism he cultivated with almost aesthetic devotion."
    },
    {
      nr: 5, name: "René Descartes", jahre: "1596–1650", subtyp: "Social Subtype",
      text: "\"I think, therefore I am\" &ndash; no sentence embodies Type 5 better. Descartes withdrew in order to think in complete silence. He lived for years in the Netherlands, moved frequently to work undisturbed, and published only hesitantly. Type 5's Social subtype seeks knowledge through exchange with intellectual circles &ndash; but on his own terms. Descartes corresponded extensively with the scholars of his time, building networks of thought &ndash; always at a safe distance from physical closeness and emotional demands."
    },
    {
      nr: "6a", name: "Immanuel Kant", jahre: "1724–1804", subtyp: "Phobic Social Subtype",
      text: "Kant lived in strict order: he barely ever left Königsberg, and his daily routine was so regular that neighbors could set their clocks by him. Type 6's phobic Social subtype seeks security through rules, systems, and reliable structures. Kant's Categorical Imperative is the philosophical project of a Six: find a universal rule everyone can follow &ndash; an ethic that creates security because it knows no exceptions. His concept of duty breathes the Six's fear of the chaos that arises when people act as they please."
    },
    {
      nr: "6b", name: "Plato", jahre: "427–347 BC", subtyp: "Counterphobic Sexual Subtype",
      text: "Plato is the counterphobic counterpart to Kant. Where Kant hedges, Plato attacks. Type 6's counterphobic subtype moves toward fear &ndash; fighting it by confronting it head-on. Plato designed the rule of philosopher-kings, an ideal state with clear hierarchies &ndash; an attempt to set something unshakeable against the chaos of Athenian democracy. The Sexual subtype shows in the intensity of his bonds: Socrates was, for Plato, not merely a teacher but a life's theme."
    },
    {
      nr: 7, name: "Epicurus", jahre: "341–271 BC", subtyp: "Social Subtype",
      text: "Epicurus is regarded as the philosopher of pleasure &ndash; which has led to him being thoroughly misunderstood. He did not teach gluttony, but the art of choosing what is pleasant and avoiding pain. That is the Seven principle in its rawest form: joy as a guiding point, pain avoidance as a way of life. Type 7's Social subtype seeks enjoyment together with others: Epicurus founded his famous Garden in Athens, living there in community with friends and students &ndash; a kind of shared household of the good life, where intellectual exchange and convivial joy were inseparable."
    },
    {
      nr: 8, name: "Socrates", jahre: "469–399 BC", subtyp: "Social Subtype",
      text: "Socrates provoked. He asked questions that cornered people, exposed false certainties, and insisted that not-knowing was the beginning of all wisdom. Type 8's Social subtype fights for justice in the public sphere &ndash; and accepts conflict as the price. Socrates was indicted, sentenced to death, and refused the escape his friends offered him: he preferred to die rather than betray his life's work. This uncompromising strength, which fights not for effect but for the cause itself, is the core of the Eight."
    },
    {
      nr: 9, name: "David Hume", jahre: "1711–1776", subtyp: "Self-Preservation Subtype",
      text: "David Hume was one of the most influential thinkers of the Enlightenment &ndash; and one of the most agreeable. Contemporaries described him as cheerful, benevolent, without bitterness. He doubted almost everything &ndash; causality, the existence of the self, God &ndash; and remained strangely unruffled throughout. Type 9's Self-Preservation subtype seeks inner peace and outer comfort. Hume wrote to understand, not to argue. His radical skepticism is the calm wonder of a Nine who lets go of all certainties &ndash; and stays more relaxed doing so than most who cling to them."
    },
  ];

  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("beruhmte-philosophen")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Interesting Insights &middot; History</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Famous Philosophers in the Enneagram</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">The Enneagram describes nine fundamental structures of human personality. Flipping through the history of philosophy, you find all of them again &ndash; in the thinkers who shaped the Western world.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1.25rem 0;">Philosophers are in a sense ideal Enneagram subjects: they have dedicated their lives to thinking, pouring their core beliefs, fears, and longings into words &ndash; often with a directness that has occupied biographers for centuries. What a philosopher holds to be true often reveals more about their inner structure than about the world. Aristotle classifies, Schopenhauer suffers, Epicurus enjoys, Socrates provokes: the types don't just think &ndash; they philosophize in a type-specific way.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0 1.75rem;">Important Note: The subtypes (self-preservation, social, sexual) are three variants within each type that differentiate the core pattern more finely. &bdquo;Subtype&ldquo; and &bdquo;Untertyp&ldquo; designate the same thing: the instinctive primary orientation that shapes the personality once more with greater precision. For Type 6, even two philosophers appear &ndash; one phobic, one counterphobic &ndash; because this type is the only one that splits so clearly into two directions.</p>

      <img
        src="../assets/beruhmte-philosophen.jpg?v=2026-08-10-se1fix"
        alt="Berühmte Philosophen im Enneagramm"
        style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:0 0 2rem;"
      />

      ${phil.map(p => `
        <div style="background:var(--cream);border-left:3px solid var(--gold);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
          <p style="font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold-dark);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Type ${p.nr} &middot; ${p.subtyp}</p>
          <p style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.1rem;font-weight:600;">${p.name}</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.78rem;color:var(--gray-mid);margin:0 0 0.7rem;">${p.jahre}</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0;">${p.text}</p>
        </div>
      `).join("")}

      ${bookTip("die-verborgene-dynamik-der-27-subtypen", "The hidden dynamics of the twenty-seven subtypes &ndash; the standard work for understanding instinctive primary orientations.", "Die verborgene Dynamik der 27 Subtypes")}
      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The nine types in their depth &ndash; from the core structure to the essence.", "Wer du wirklich bist")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"interessante-erkenntnisse", label:"Interesting Insights"},
        {route:"hunderassen", label:"The Enneagram Types Gone to the Dogs"},
        {route:"rumi-zitate", label:"Rumi Quotes for the 9 Types"},
        {route:"beruehmte-aristoteles", label:"Portrait: Aristotle (SE1w9)"},
        {route:"beruehmte-david-hume", label:"Portrait: David Hume (SE9w8)"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function hoellenkreiseDantePage() {
  const kreise = [
    { typ: 1, kreis: 1, farbe: "#c98a8a", titel: "1st Circle", text: "Here dwell the wise, poets, and heroes of antiquity. As a poet, Dante placed himself here, in this first circle, by his own self-understanding – likely because a sexual Four, at its point of integration, moves toward One and there, more integrated and self-aware, can formulate and write with great linguistic precision. In better phases of his life he may well have felt he belonged to this universal principle of Type 1 – in fact, as his portrait shows, he was clearly a sexual Four with a Five-wing (SX4w5)." },
    { typ: 2, kreis: 2, farbe: "#9b3fa0", titel: "2nd Circle", text: "In the hell of lust, the sinners of love are whipped by terrible storms – driven restlessly onward, never coming to rest." },
    { typ: 3, kreis: 9, farbe: "#8fd6cf", titel: "9th Circle", text: "In the ice, traitors, fratricides, and patricides are frozen in place and suffer bitter cold – the opposite of the warmth they themselves denied others." },
    { typ: 4, kreis: 7, farbe: "#3fae3f", titel: "7th Circle", text: "(Self-)murderers are tormented in the desert under a rain of fire – violence once directed at oneself or others returns as an eternal burning." },
    { typ: 5, kreis: 4, farbe: "#e79a9a", titel: "4th Circle", text: "The greedy and the wasteful roll stone burdens against each other, wailing – two forms of the same unhealthy relationship to possession and resources." },
    { typ: 6, kreis: 8, farbe: "#f2c6cf", titel: "8th Circle", text: "Corrupt officials, priests, hypocrites, and evil counselors suffer elaborately devised cruelties – broken trust answered with calculated harshness." },
    { typ: 7, kreis: 3, farbe: "#e8c93f", titel: "3rd Circle", text: "Beaten down by cold rain, the gluttonous drag themselves through filth – the former pursuit of pleasure turned into bleak repetition." },
    { typ: 8, kreis: 5, farbe: "#8a1f1f", titel: "5th Circle", text: "In the foul swamp, the wrathful tear at one another – unchecked force that, in the end, turns only against its own fellow sufferers." },
    { typ: 9, kreis: 6, farbe: "#f0e14a", titel: "6th Circle", text: "Sinners of weakness and passivity lie in burning tombs – the inertia that meant standstill in life becomes burning idleness." },
  ];

  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("hoellenkreise-dante")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Circles of Hell &middot; Literature</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The 9 Circles of Hell According to Dante's Inferno</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Dante Alighieri (SX4w5) designed a Hell of nine concentric circles in his "Divine Comedy" – a structure of encyclopedic precision that transformed his personal exile from Florence into a literary world tribunal. Each circle punishes a specific human failing, and a striking number of these failings map onto the nine passions of the Enneagram.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1.25rem 0;">Dante conceived of Hell as a funnel-shaped pit divided into nine concentric circles, at whose center sits Satan, evil personified. Descending from circle to circle means encountering an ever more radical form of the same failing – from the comparatively mild circle of the unbaptized to the icy center reserved for betrayal. The overview below matches each of the Enneagram's nine universal principles to the circle of Hell that corresponds to it most closely.</p>

      <img
        src="../assets/schaubilder/hoellenkreise-dante/hoellenkreise-dante.jpg"
        alt="The 9 Circles of Hell According to Dante's Inferno"
        style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:0 0 2rem;"
      />

      ${kreise.map(k => `
        <div style="background:var(--cream);border-left:3px solid ${k.farbe};border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
          <p style="font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold-dark);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Type ${k.typ} &middot; ${k.titel}</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0;">${k.text}</p>
        </div>
      `).join("")}

      <p style="font-size:0.82rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--gray-mid);margin:1.5rem 0 2rem;">The Italian poet and philosopher Dante Alighieri (1265–1321) designed nine circles of Hell in his work "The Divine Comedy." Hell is thereby a pit arranged into nine concentric circles, at whose center sits Satan, evil personified. The individual themes of the circles of Hell correspond to the nine universal principles of the Enneagram.</p>

      <div style="background:var(--cream);border-left:3px solid var(--gold);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1.5rem 1.75rem;margin:0 0 2rem;">
        <h2 style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.35rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 1rem;">Dante, the Inferno, and the Enneagram: a fascinating parallel</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 1rem;">That Dante's nine circles of Hell map so seamlessly onto the nine passions of the Enneagram is more than a pleasant coincidence – it is a finding that has long fascinated Enneagram researchers and teachers as well. Dante's "Inferno" is, at its core, a literary cartography of human abysses, blind spots, and destructive passions: the structure of the nine circles orders wrongdoing by severity and kind, from uncontrolled desire to icy betrayal. The Enneagram describes essentially the same thing: nine fundamental fixations and passions (wrath, pride, vanity, envy, avarice, fear, gluttony, lust, and sloth) that can drive a person into destructive patterns.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 1rem;">Dante, of course, knew nothing of Claudio Naranjo, of modern psychology, or of the Enneagram as it is taught today. But he possessed something else: a deep, intuitive anthropological understanding. Great poets and philosophers often have a razor-sharp perception of the universal patterns of the human psyche – observing the same human weaknesses across generations. The historical roots of the Enneagram, especially the seven deadly sins and passions it partly builds upon, overlap strongly with the Christian scholasticism and ancient philosophy that Dante had deeply internalized. He drew on the same foundation of archetypal vices that, centuries later, would also flow into modern typologies.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0;">This is precisely what is so remarkable: throughout the whole of world history there have been people who saw more deeply than their era might suggest – not because people in the past were less intelligent, but because great thinkers, poets, and polymaths of every age possessed a profound, holistic wisdom about human nature that ranked among the greatest cultural achievements of their time. That Dante's circles map so precisely onto modern psychological types and behavioral patterns shows above all one thing: how precisely he was already able to capture the darkest corners of human nature in image and metaphor. This very timelessness of the nine principles also deeply occupied Claudio Naranjo – and likewise the author of this app, Detlef Rathmer, whose ongoing engagement with historical sources such as Dante's work shows that the nine basic patterns of human entanglement are not a product of modernity, but a truth about being human that has been rediscovered, again and again, across millennia.</p>
      </div>

      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The nine types in their depth – defense patterns, passions, and the path to essence.", "Wer du wirklich bist – Band 1")}
      ${bookTip("die-verborgene-dynamik-der-27-subtypen", "27 subtypes: passions, defense strategies, and healing paths from therapeutic practice.", "Die verborgene Dynamik der 27 Subtypen")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"schaubilder", label:"All charts"},
        {route:"beruehmte-dante-alighieri", label:"Portrait: Dante Alighieri (SX4w5) – author of the Divine Comedy"},
        {route:"beruehmte-thomas-von-aquin", label:"Portrait: Thomas Aquinas (SE9w8) – whose theology shaped Dante"},
        {route:"beruhmte-philosophen", label:"Chart: Famous Philosophers in the Enneagram"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function dreiLebenskraeftePage() {
  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("drei-lebenskraefte")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Basic Principles &middot; Triads</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The Three Basic Life Forces</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">Behind the nine Enneagram types operate three fundamental energy directions: active, balancing, and passive. They do not describe character, but rather the way a type fundamentally makes contact with the world.</p>

      <img
        src="../assets/drei-lebenskraefte.jpg"
        alt="Die drei grundlegenden Lebenskräfte des Enneagramms"
        style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.5rem 0 2rem;"
      />

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">The Enneagram does not just recognize nine types &ndash; it recognizes three fundamental directions of movement in life. These three forces &ndash; active, balancing, passive &ndash; have been described in various wisdom traditions: as Tamas, Rajas, and Sattva in Yoga, as the three Gunas in Sankhya, as Yang, Yin, and the balancing middle in Taoism. In the Enneagram, they become visible as the basic energetic orientation of each type.</p>

      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:1.25rem;margin:1.75rem 0;">

        <div style="background:var(--cream);border-left:4px solid #8b2014;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;">
          <p style="font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#8b2014;margin:0 0 0.3rem;">Active &middot; Types 3, 7, 8</p>
          <p style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.1rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.6rem;font-style:italic;">Outward-going &ndash; asserting, expanding, determining, free</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0;">The active types move toward the world. They shape, move, expand. Type 8 does it with power and directness, Type 7 with enthusiasm and ingenuity, Type 3 with determination and impact. What all three have in common: They do not seek, they do. The energy flows outward &ndash; into action, influence, result. The risk of this force lies in overextension: when doing becomes more important than being.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--cream);border-left:4px solid var(--gold-dark);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;">
          <p style="font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold-dark);margin:0 0 0.3rem;">Balancing &middot; Types 1, 2, 6</p>
          <p style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.1rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.6rem;font-style:italic;">Bringing into balance &ndash; harmonizing, reconciling, mediating, agreeable</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0;">The balancing types sense the imbalance &ndash; and do everything in their power to resolve it. Type 1 wants to establish what is right, Type 2 to create closeness and harmony, Type 6 to create security and reliability. They are the mediators, the bridge builders, the peacekeepers of the Enneagram. Their strength is sensitivity to what is missing. Their challenge: They cannot rest as long as something is out of alignment &ndash; even if the balance is only missing within themselves.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="background:var(--cream);border-left:4px solid #2e7d6e;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;">
          <p style="font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#2e7d6e;margin:0 0 0.3rem;">Passive &middot; Types 4, 5, 9</p>
          <p style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.1rem;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.6rem;font-style:italic;">Inward-going &ndash; reserved, waiting, withdrawing, calm, still</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0;">The passive types receive, observe, withdraw. Type 9 seeks stillness and peace, Type 5 withdrawal and knowledge, Type 4 depth and inner experience. This energy is not weak &ndash; it is a different kind of power. Being passive creates space for perception, contemplation, and processing. The challenge lies in withdrawal as a pattern: when the world gets too loud and they retreat further and further inward until contact with life is lost.</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1.5rem 0;">These three forces are not a judgment. None is better than the other. They describe how life expresses itself through different types &ndash; and why some people naturally lead the way, others mediate, and still others pause. A balanced system needs all three.</p>

      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The nine types in their depth &ndash; foundational forces, passions, and the path to essence.", "Wer du wirklich bist")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"drei-zentren", label:"The Three Centers of Intelligence"},
        {route:"triadendefizite", label:"Triad Deficits"},
        {route:"horney-triaden", label:"Horneyvian Triads"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function sucheNachLiebePage() {
  const typen = [
    { nr: 1, ersatz: "Perfection & correctness instead of love", halt: "Held together by self- and other-improvement.", text: "Type 1 believes that love may only exist once everything is right &ndash; themselves, circumstances, others. So the search for love becomes a search for flawlessness. But love does not wait for perfection." },
    { nr: 2, ersatz: "Conditional love & giving instead of love", halt: "Held together by manipulating and charming their surroundings.", text: "Type 2 gives so that they will be loved &ndash; and calls it love. But giving as a strategy is not yet love. As long as they twist themselves into shape to be needed, true affection never truly arrives." },
    { nr: 3, ersatz: "Success & achievement instead of love", halt: "Held together by deceiving themselves and others into believing they can manage alone.", text: "Type 3 works, performs, achieves &ndash; and hopes that it counts. They deceive others about their true face, sometimes themselves too. But love is not an award for performance." },
    { nr: 4, ersatz: "Individuality & creativity instead of love", halt: "Held together by denying the separation from being, and by controlling self and others.", text: "Type 4 longs for love more intensely than most others &ndash; and suffers more deeply from its absence. The separation from being, from what originally sustained them, is denied. Creativity and uniqueness become a substitute for the connection they truly seek." },
    { nr: 5, ersatz: "Knowledge & observation instead of love", halt: "Held together by not feeling, by withdrawal, isolation, and avoidance.", text: "Type 5 keeps their distance &ndash; even from love. Knowledge protects, observing keeps them safe. What they might feel, they keep at arm's length. The warmth of connection appears as a threat to their inner autonomy." },
    { nr: 6, ersatz: "Security & loyalty instead of love", halt: "Held together by fear, mistrust, and defensive, paranoid behavior.", text: "Type 6 seeks, above all, security in love. But fear and mistrust poison the very thing they are meant to protect. They test, doubt, hedge their bets &ndash; and don't notice that real love needs no safety net." },
    { nr: 7, ersatz: "Fun & joy instead of love", halt: "Held together by planning to make everything right again (the return to paradise lost).", text: "Type 7 flees the pain of longing into pleasure and distraction. Joy is meant to replace what's missing. The idea that everything can still be made right keeps them in motion &ndash; and prevents the stillness in which love could grow." },
    { nr: 8, ersatz: "Power & control instead of love", halt: "Held together by anger, fighting, control, justice, and revenge.", text: "Type 8 has learned: whoever shows themselves gets hurt. So they show strength instead. Power and control secure them. But love requires vulnerability &ndash; exactly what they protect most fiercely." },
    { nr: 9, ersatz: "Harmony & self-forgetting instead of love", halt: "Held together by smoothing things over, acting as if all is well, living on autopilot.", text: "Type 9 keeps the peace &ndash; at their own expense. They balance things out, suppress tension, live on autopilot. What looks like love is often just the absence of conflict. Real love would wake them up &ndash; and that is exactly what they fear." },
  ];

  return shell(`
    ${pageHeader("suche-nach-liebe")}
    <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem 3rem;">
      <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Relationships &middot; Development</p>
      <h1 class="section-title">The Search for True Love</h1>
      <p class="psycho-intro">All nine types seek love. But each seeks it in their own way &ndash; and each has developed their own way of standing in their own way.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1.25rem 0;">That sounds paradoxical, but it isn't. Every Enneagram type experienced early in life that love &ndash; affection, being seen, connection &ndash; seemed attached to certain conditions. And so they developed a strategy: replacing the original with something achievable. Perfection instead of love. Achievement instead of love. Control instead of love.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">These substitute satisfactions are not a weakness &ndash; they were clever answers to real experiences. The problem is that they block the true search. You get something &ndash; recognition, security, harmony &ndash; but not what you are truly looking for. And because the pain of longing remains, the strategy is intensified: more perfection, more giving, more control.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0;">The Enneagram points out these patterns not as an accusation, but as an invitation: Recognize what you are replacing love with &ndash; and what holds you back from going further.</p>

      <img
        src="../assets/suche-nach-liebe.jpg"
        alt="Die Search nach der wahren Liebe – alle 9 Enneagrammtypen"
        style="width:100%;border-radius:8px;margin:1.75rem 0 2rem;"
      />

      ${typen.map(t => `
        <div style="background:var(--cream);border-left:3px solid var(--gold);border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
          <p style="font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold-dark);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Type ${t.nr}</p>
          <p style="font-family:'EB Garamond',serif;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:600;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.25rem;">${t.ersatz}</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.82rem;color:var(--gray-mid);font-style:italic;margin:0 0 0.6rem;">${t.halt}</p>
          <p style="font-size:0.88rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0;">${t.text}</p>
        </div>
      `).join("")}

      ${bookTip("die-sprache-unserer-beziehungen", "Cultivating intimacy with the Enneagram &ndash; how the nine types experience, seek, and sometimes avoid love.", "Die Sprache unserer Beziehungen")}
      ${bookTip("die-sprache-unserer-sexualitaet", "In partners, we often search for the love we will not find there &ndash; an honest look at desire, attachment, and what we truly seek.", "Die Sprache unserer Sexualität")}
      ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The core structure of the nine types &ndash; from passion to essence.", "Wer du wirklich bist")}
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"beziehungen-schaubild", label:"Relationships – Love and Protection"},
        {route:"leidenschaft-und-wunde", label:"Passion & Wound"},
        {route:"mangelgefuehle", label:"Feelings of Lack"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

function humorDer9TypenPage() {
  const HUMOR_IMG = "https://res.cloudinary.com/ymooybdl/image/upload/v1783720175/kompass/stille-sounds/humor-elefanten-schaubild.jpg";
  const typen = [
    { nr:1, col:"#5f5f5f", titel:"Anger: Satirical Humor",
      art:"Satire is often used to denounce grievances and point out seemingly “wrong” behavior. Satirists frequently feel driven by a sense of what is “right” or “just.”",
      typ:"The Perfectionist strives for moral integrity and has a strong need to improve the world and themselves. Humor as satire allows them to channel anger over perceived imperfections without being directly destructive." },
    { nr:2, col:"#7a2fa8", titel:"Pride: Warm, Social Humor",
      art:"“Warm humor” encompasses witty remarks that foster closeness and belonging. It's less about mockery and more about a warm, sometimes self-deprecating way of creating connection and showing others that they are noticed.",
      typ:"The Helper is strongly focused on relationships and finds fulfillment in being needed. Through warm humor, they can make others laugh and thereby show that they know their feelings — which gives them the feeling of being important and valuable." },
    { nr:3, col:"#1fa688", titel:"Vanity: Confident, Quick-Witted Humor",
      art:"Quick-witted humor, often based on wordplay and self-presentation, is found in humorous personalities who like to put themselves in the spotlight and give fast, pointed responses.",
      typ:"The Achiever seeks recognition and often comes across as charming and humorous to support their self-image. Their humor frequently serves to inspire admiration — making them, in the joke, often the “star” of the moment." },
    { nr:4, col:"#3cbf1f", titel:"Envy: Melancholic, Ironic Humor",
      art:"Irony and melancholy can represent a kind of profound humor, in which serious themes are often treated in a bittersweet way. This type of humor appears simultaneously alluring and abysmal.",
      typ:"The Individualist often experiences feelings of deficiency and longing. Through ironic or melancholic humor, they can express their perception of life as complex and profound — while simultaneously creating a certain distance that protects them from being overwhelmed by intense feeling." },
    { nr:5, col:"#124fcc", titel:"Avarice: Dry, Intellectual Humor",
      art:"Dry humor, often with intellectual or subtle elements, appeals to those who prefer a certain distance and clarity. It is based on subtle, often understatement-laden punchlines.",
      typ:"The Observer likes to withdraw and analyze from a distance. Their humor often stays in the background and is a controlled form of expression that allows them to present their observations without emotional overload." },
    { nr:6, col:"#8a5222", titel:"Fear: Cautious, Absurd Humor",
      art:"Absurd humor is often paradoxical and plays with exaggeration or the irrational. It makes it possible to name fears and insecurities while simultaneously rendering them ridiculous.",
      typ:"The Loyalist struggles with doubt and seeks security. Through absurd humor, they can process the unpredictability of life by turning threats into something comical — which allows them to approach reality more relaxed." },
    { nr:7, col:"#d4a800", titel:"Gluttony: Enthusiastic, Playful Humor",
      art:"This humor is full of joie de vivre, energy, and is often quite lighthearted. Playful humor invites you to simply have fun and is frequently very contagious.",
      typ:"The Enthusiast loves enjoying life in all its facets and likes to distract themselves from negative thoughts. Their humor is a way of escaping — a way of taking life lightly and carrying others along with them." },
    { nr:8, col:"#a00802", titel:"Lust: Provocative, Dark Humor",
      art:"Provocative humor breaks taboos and often brings uncomfortable truths to light. It is powerful and often somewhat rough — a way of showing control and strength.",
      typ:"The Challenger values strength and refuses to be constrained by social norms. Dark humor gives them the opportunity to express their perception of the world as “hard” and often unforgiving — while simultaneously demonstrating their strength." },
    { nr:9, col:"#cc6e00", titel:"Sloth: Peaceful, Self-Deprecating Humor",
      art:"Self-deprecating humor has a calming effect and conveys a relaxed attitude toward life. Often it is light and warm, full of insight into one's own small flaws and quirks.",
      typ:"The Peacemaker seeks harmony and avoids conflict. Through self-deprecating humor, they can create a relaxed atmosphere in which others feel comfortable. This type of humor helps them playfully name their own “sloth” without really calling themselves into question." },
  ];

  const typenHTML = typen.map(t => `
    <div style="margin-bottom:2rem;padding:1.5rem 1.8rem;background:var(--surface-2,rgba(0,0,0,0.03));border-radius:14px;border-left:4px solid ${t.col};">
      <p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:${t.col};font-weight:700;margin:0 0 .2rem;">Type ${t.nr}</p>
      <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:700;color:${t.col};margin:0 0 .9rem;">${t.titel}</p>
      <p style="font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--muted);margin:0 0 .7rem;font-style:italic;">${t.art}</p>
      <p style="font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ink);margin:0;">${t.typ}</p>
    </div>
  `).join("");

  return shell(`
    <div class="page-container">
      ${pageHeader("humor-der-9-typen")}
      <div style="max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 1rem;">
        <p class="eyebrow">Charts &middot; Humor &middot; The 9 Types</p>
        <h1 class="section-title">Humor of the 9 Enneagram Types</h1>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1rem 0 0.8rem;">How can you get to know a person even better through their type of humor &mdash; alongside Rathmer's profiling system and the many other ways of reliable typing presented in this app?</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 0.8rem;">Humor is a complex mixture of emotional, cognitive, and social components. It is understood as a phenomenon that relieves tension, creates connections, and often serves as a valve for deeper psychological themes. At its core, it is about the ability to recognize contradictions, exaggerations, or discrepancies in reality and process them in a light, surprising, or joyful way.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.95rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:0 0 1rem;">The kind of humor a person prefers does not stem solely from rational thinking &mdash; it also reveals paradoxes and absurdities that this person perceives in the world, and is therefore closely tied to the ability for abstraction and perspective-shifting. Humor is thus often a <em>psychological opener</em>: it grants deep insights into personality, passions, and inner conflicts &mdash; sometimes more clearly than anything one generally reveals.</p>
        <div style="background:rgba(200,168,75,0.08);border-left:4px solid var(--gold,#c8a84b);border-radius:10px;padding:1.1rem 1.4rem;margin:0 0 1.5rem;">
          <p style="font-size:.85rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--gold-dark,#a07830);margin:0 0 .6rem;">A chuckle &mdash; even at oneself &mdash; can have various positive effects:</p>
          <ol style="font-size:.88rem;line-height:1.8;color:var(--ink);margin:0;padding-left:1.3rem;">
            <li><strong>Self-Acceptance:</strong> A self-deprecating smile helps you accept yourself better and view your own imperfection more positively.</li>
            <li><strong>Stress Reduction:</strong> Viewing yourself with humor reduces stress and helps you gain a lighter perspective on challenging situations.</li>
            <li><strong>Self-Worth:</strong> A healthy amount of self-irony can show that you don't take yourself too seriously and therefore don't let others define you.</li>
            <li><strong>Resilience:</strong> A humorous approach to oneself increases emotional resilience &mdash; it helps you cope more easily with setbacks and mishaps.</li>
            <li><strong>Social Connection:</strong> Self-deprecating humor can also ease interpersonal communication and foster likability, as it signals authenticity and a relaxed atmosphere.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <img src="${HUMOR_IMG}" alt="Die 9 Enneagramm-Elefanten &mdash; humorvolles Schaubild"
        style="width:100%;max-width:680px;display:block;margin:0 auto 2.5rem;border-radius:14px;" loading="lazy" />

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink);text-align:center;margin:0 0 1.5rem;">The Humor Styles of the 9 Types</h2>
        ${typenHTML}
        <p style="font-size:0.92rem;line-height:1.75;color:var(--anthracite);margin:1.5rem 0 0;padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.03);border-radius:12px;">This <em>humorous typology of the Enneagram</em> shows how specific personality patterns can find expression in the form of humor. For each type, humor offers the opportunity to release inner tensions, channel passions, and interact with the environment in a way that corresponds to their fundamental stance on life &mdash; a bridge to inner balance and self-expression.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="vb-section" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;">
        ${bookTip("wer-du-wirklich-bist-band-1", "The nine types in their entire depth &mdash; passions, wounds, and paths of development.", "Wer du wirklich bist &ndash; Band 1")}
      </div>
      ${relatedLinks([
        {route:"limericks", label:"The 9 Enneagram Limericks"},
        {route:"haiku-der-9-typen", label:"Haiku of the 9 Types"},
        {route:"tierentsprechungen", label:"Animal Equivalents of the 9 Types"},
      ])}
    </div>
  `);
}

